{
  "raw": {
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49039": {
      "title": "Category Manager – Hard Facilities Management  - Estates",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Commercial and Procurement; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00357 About us: UCL is London’s leading multidisciplinary university, with more than 18,000 staff and over 51,000 students from more than 150 countries. Alongside our global reputation for teaching and research, UCL has a significant and diverse commercial landscape, spending over £900 million each year with third-party suppliers. Our Commercial & Procurement function plays an important role in ensuring this spend delivers value for UCL. We are focused on effective sourcing, strong supplier management and better demand management, while supporting UCL’s Sustainable Procurement Strategy and creating the financial capacity to deliver our ambitious 2034 strategic plan. About the role: We are looking for a Category Manager – Facilities Management to join our Commercial & Procurement Services team within Estates. Reporting to the Head of Category – Facility Operations, you will manage procurement and commercial activity across the facilities categories including procurement of furniture, cleaning and logistics services. Working closely with stakeholders to understand their requirements and deliver effective commercial solutions. You will develop category plans, manage tenders and contract negotiations, support supplier and contract management, and monitor supplier performance. A key part of the role will be identifying opportunities to deliver efficiencies, cost savings and value for money. You will develop negotiation strategies, manage commercial arrangements and support the transition of new suppliers, while providing advice on contractual, commercial and compliance matters. You will also contribute to continuous improvement, innovation and UCL’s wider procurement and sustainability objectives. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will be a commercially minded procurement professional with the ability to build effective relationships and work confidently with a diverse range of stakeholders. You will have experience of delivering procurement and commercial activity and be comfortable balancing multiple priorities in a busy environment. You will bring strong planning, organisation and communication skills, excellent attention to detail and the ability to use evidence and data to inform your approach. Experience of negotiation and working within a large, complex and decentralised organisation would be particularly valuable. Please review our job description, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure these are covered in your application. What we offer: Alongside the exciting opportunity this role presents, we also offer some great benefits: • 41 Days of holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Hybrid working • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity,paternityand adoption pay • Employeeassistanceprogramme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48519": {
      "title": "2 x Clinical Lecturer (Teaching) in Implant Dentistry (0.2FTE)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11230 About us: The Department of Continuing Professional Development (CPD) was established in 1995 and offers comprehensive and structured CPD programmes for general dental practitioners, consultants, specialists and all other members of the dental team. Short educational programmes and longer part-time substantive degree programmes are also offered, together with revision courses and distance learning programmes. This type of education and training is facilitated by the emphasis on small group teaching which is evidence-based. Staff are experienced postgraduate teachers with high levels of clinical expertise. The Department delivers the Dental Manikin (DM) section of the Overseas Registration Examination (ORE) for the General Dental Council, as part of the ORE Consortium which consists of UCL Eastman Dental Institute, the Royal ENT and the Eastman Dental Hospital, Queen Mary University of London and the Royal College of Surgeons of England. About the role: The main purpose of the post is to ensure the development, coordination and smooth running of the prestigious part-time Masters in Implant Dentistry for Dental Practitioners. In addition, the post is involved in the design and delivery of short courses and teaching on other postgraduate programmes (including the supervision of MSc dissertation/research projects) and courses, in the fields of Prosthodontic Dentistry, including related training and mentoring. The MSc in Implant Dentistry runs over 3 years with over 82 contact days including compulsory induction/enrolment and examination days. Teaching takes place throughout the academic year on Tuesdays. We have two part-time posts available at CL8, each at 0.2 FTE, with a starting salary of £12,816.80–£14,402.20 per annum (20% of the full-time salary range of £64,084–£72,011), inclusive of London Allowance. Salary on appointment will be dependent on experience. This role does not meet the requirements for a Skilled Worker visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require a right to work in the UK. Interviews will be held on Tuesday 24 th November 2026 in the morning . The interview process will include a practical exercise, a short presentation, and an interview. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. UCL Terms and Conditions of Service: Research and Professional Services Staff Find out about the UCL Ways of Working: www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working . If you have any queries about the role, please contact Dr Dev Patel at dev.patel@ucl.ac.uk. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact Khadija Bouzgan at hr-eastman@ucl.ac.uk. About you: You must be a fully qualified dental practitioner and must be registered with the GDC, or be eligible to register. The post-holder will have experience of delivering postgraduate teaching in Implant and Prosthodontic Dentistry, and will also have experience of delivering continuing professional development courses. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49034": {
      "title": "Accommodation Advisor",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £31,904-£36,433; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00385 About us: UCL is one of the world's leading universities, providing an exceptional experience for students from across the globe. As part of UCL Estates, the award-winning Accommodation team manages more than 26 halls of residence, housing over 6,500 students across London. Our vision is to become the leading accommodation provider within the Higher Education sector by delivering an outstanding student experience. We believe our accommodation is more than just a place to live. It is where students build friendships, develop independence and become part of a thriving community. We're looking for a customer-focused Accommodation Advisor to help us create a welcoming, safe and supportive environment for our residents. About the role: As an Accommodation Advisor, you will be at the heart of the student experience, providing both front-of-house and administrative support across our accommodation sites. You will act as the first point of contact for students, visitors, contractors and stakeholders, delivering an exceptional level of customer service while ensuring the smooth day-to-day operation of our residences. This is a varied and rewarding role where no two days are the same. From welcoming new arrivals and supporting residents throughout their stay, to handling administrative processes and helping deliver community events, you will play a key role in ensuring students feel at home during their time at UCL. Please note: Core coverage is required between 8:00am and 7:00pm Monday to Friday , and 9:00am to 5:00pm on Saturdays , with shifts determined by the line manager. During holiday and summer periods, work will be undertaken on a rota basis across five days out of seven, which may include weekends. Occasional evening, weekend and bank holiday working will be required to support operational needs, with time off in lieu provided in accordance with UCL procedures. Due to peak operational activity, annual leave may be restricted during key accommodation periods. Please note we are likely to have two of these vacancies, one based in Bloomsbury and the other in King's Cross. About you: We're looking for someone who is passionate about customer service, enjoys working with people and thrives in a fast-paced environment. Essential Requirements Experience in a customer-facing role - experience in hospitality, accommodation, or purpose-built student accommodation would be particularly useful. Previous administration experience. Excellent verbal and written communication skills. Ability to remain calm, professional and objective when handling challenging situations. Strong relationship-building and stakeholder management skills. Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications. Excellent organisational skills, with strong attention to detail. Ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines. A customer-first mindset with strong empathy and problem-solving abilities. Positive, adaptable and collaborative approach to work. Ability to work independently and use initiative. Flexibility to work a rota and adapt to changing operational requirements. Desirable Requirements Experience within student accommodation, hospitality, retail or residential services. Experience in complaint handling and dispute resolution. Experience supporting marketing, communications or resident engagement activities. Knowledge of accommodation management systems such as StarRez. What we offer: Alongside the exciting opportunity this role presents, we also offer some great benefits: • 41 Days of holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Hybrid working • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity,paternityand adoption pay • Employeeassistanceprogramme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48960": {
      "title": "Accommodation Manager",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Estates, Facilities, Accommodation and Safety; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00384 About us: Shape an exceptional student living experience at UCL UCL Accommodation is looking for an experienced and inspiring Accommodation Manager to lead the delivery of outstanding residential services across a cluster of student accommodation sites. This is a key leadership role, combining people management, operational delivery, customer experience and commercial awareness. You will lead and develop accommodation teams, build strong relationships with colleagues and external partners, and ensure our residents experience a safe, welcoming and well-managed place to live. About the role: You will lead a team providing clear direction, coaching and support while creating a positive, collaborative and high-performing culture. You will take responsibility for performance, development and service delivery, ensuring teams understand how their work contributes to wider UCL Accommodation objectives. Working across multiple sites, you will oversee operational standards, customer satisfaction, health and safety, financial performance and supplier relationships. You will work closely with teams across UCL, including Estates, Human Resources, Marketing and Communications, Student Experience and Safety Services, as well as external contractors and suppliers. You will also lead and support key operational projects, including summer turnaround, refurbishment and commercial activity, while identifying opportunities to improve services through customer feedback, performance data and best practice. At the heart of the role is creating an environment where students feel safe, respected, supported and part of a community. You will engage with residents and student representatives, respond effectively to feedback and complaints, support community activities and ensure consistent student experience standards across your area. You will be a visible and approachable leader who understands the needs of students and is committed to delivering a responsive, professional and caring service. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will be an experienced people manager with a strong background in a customer-facing environment. You will have a proven ability to motivate and develop teams, manage performance and deliver consistently high standards of customer service. You will bring strong knowledge of health and safety, experience of managing budgets and costs, and the ability to build effective relationships with a wide range of stakeholders and suppliers. You will be confident managing challenging situations and complaints with professionalism, empathy and sound judgement. Highly organised and adaptable, you will be comfortable working at pace across a multi-site environment, balancing competing priorities while maintaining attention to detail. Strong communication skills and good IT literacy, including Microsoft Office, are essential. Experience in student accommodation, hospitality, retail, facilities management or a similar service environment would be advantageous. Please review our job description, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure these are covered in your application. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy Available documents"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49004": {
      "title": "Admissions & Visiting Electives Administrator (0.8 FTE, Fixed-Term maternity cover)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11330 About Us: Bringing together six UCL divisions and institutes - Cancer Institute, Eastman Dental Institute, Division of Medicine, Division of Infection and Immunity, Division of Surgery and Interventional Sciences and the UCL Medical School – the Faculty of Medical Sciences (FMS) offers a unique mix of pioneering research, best in class researchers and clinicians, cutting-edge facilities and links with world-renowned partner hospitals and institutions. The faculty is the top in the UK for research power in life and medical sciences (REF 2021) and is consistently ranked in the top 10 in QS rankings. The faculty is dedicated to delivering world-class medical education and developing translational research that benefits patients and society in the UK and globally. The Education Administration and Student Experience (EASE) staff across the Faculty work together in a close network of teams that support all of our students. The Medical School’s MBBS programme aims to qualify distinctive ‘UCL Doctors’ – highly capable, and patient-centred clinicians, equipped to practise medicine in a professional, inclusive, and sustainable way, grounded in science and best practice About The Role: The MBBS Admissions Administrator plays a key role in supporting the delivery of the Medical School’s admissions activities, including the coordination and administration of the Multiple Mini Interview (MMI) process, applicant communications and admissions events. The post holder will also support the Visiting Elective Programme, overseeing applications and pre-placement requirements and liaising with clinical supervisors, Trust administrators and relevant UCL teams to facilitate placements. Outside the main admissions period, the role provides administrative support to MBBS Student Support, as well as supporting the delivery of MBBS assessments and other Medical School events. The post holder will work closely with colleagues across the Medical School and wider UCL, contributing to the effective delivery of a responsive and high-quality service throughout the academic year. Appointment details: The post is graded at GRADE 6, the annual salary for which ranges from £36,433 - £42,891 (pro-rata) per annum inclusive of London Allowance. This is a fixed-term role (maternity cover) until October 2027. This role is 0.8 FTE (29.2 hours) and the role holder could work 4 days per week or 5 days a week with shorter days. This role is eligible for hybrid working, with a minimum of 40% on site. The usual on-site days for this role are Mondays and Tuesdays, although these may be subject to change. Flexibility is required between December and February, when the post holder will be expected to work on site as required to support the admissions cycle. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Application process: A full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Please use the personal statement section to explain how you meet each essential and desirable criteria outlined in the person specification. The advert will close on Thursday 22nd 2026 at 23:59 GMT/BST, however we may close the advert early if we receive a high volume of applications. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. Contact details: If you have any queries about the role or application process please contact Deborah Caleb ( D.Caleb@ucl.ac.uk ) If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact Deepak Singh ( Deepak.Singh@ucl.ac.uk). About You: You will have experience working in a professional services environment, ideally within higher education or a Medical School, and be confident managing high-volume administrative processes accurately and to time critical deadlines. You will have excellent written and verbal communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to provide a high-quality service and liaise effectively with applicants, students, academic and clinical colleagues, and other stakeholders. You will be highly organised, proactive and able to prioritise your workload, work independently and collaboratively, and remain effective when working under pressure. Strong IT skills are essential, including Microsoft Word, Excel, Outlook and Teams, alongside the ability to manage and manipulate data using spreadsheets and databases and to learn new systems quickly. What We Offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) pro rata Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance The full range of staff benefits can be found here Our Commitment To Equality, Diversity and Inclusion : As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. UCLMS holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48965": {
      "title": "Applications Specialist - Single Cell",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11253 About us: Our mission at the UCL GOS Institute of Child Health is to maximise and advocate for the holistic health of all children, young people and the adults they will become, through world-class research, education and public engagement. The UCL GOS ICH, together with its clinical partner Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children, forms the largest concentration of children’s health research outside North America. The 2024-29 GOS ICH strategy focuses on its five scientific programmes. GOS ICH’s activities include active engagement with children and families, to ensure that our work is relevant and appropriate to their needs. GOS ICH generates the funding for our research by setting out our proposals in high quality applications to public, charitable and industrial funding bodies and disseminates the results of our research by publication in the medical and scientific literature, to clinicians, policy makers and the wider public. The Institute offers world-class education and training across a wide range of teaching and life learning programmes which address the needs of students and professional groups who are interested in and undertaking work relevant to child health. GOS ICH holds an Athena SWAN Charter Gold Award. About the role: Applications are invited for an Applications Specialist position within the Sequencing team at UCL Genomics, UCL’s largest genomics facility based in the Zayed Centre for Research into Rare Disease in Children. UCL Genomics provides cutting-edge technologies, expert guidance, and comprehensive support to enable high-quality genomic research. The successful candidate will play an important role in delivering a high-quality genomics service provision within the Single Cell and Spatial Transcriptomic streams. The postholder will initially be responsible for supporting collaborators from experimental design through sample QC and processing. This will include generating single cell and spatial transcriptomic libraries, QC and generation of the data using Next Generation Sequencing (NGS). The role also includes the associated administration using LIMS, organisational and project planning activities required to support the efficient operation of the facility. This is a varied, predominantly lab-based position offering excellent opportunities to develop expertise across a range of sequencing technologies and contribute to the continuous improvement of a leading genomics core facility. This can involve testing and validating new solutions for scRNAseq and spatial transcriptomic assays from various solution providers. You will work closely with other Applications Specialists and colleagues to deliver reliable, high-quality data and an excellent service provision to our users. As single cell and spatial technologies are rapidly evolving, the responsibilities and scope of the role may develop over time. Collaboration is central to this role and a large proportion of the work involves supporting both internal and external users, and the postholder will work closely with researchers, principal investigators, clinicians, and industry professionals as well as other facility staff to ensure projects are delivered in a timely manner and to a high standard. For candidates appointed at grade 7, additional responsibilities will include leading the expansion and strategic development of the spatial transcriptomics stream, contributing to more complex operational and administrative tasks, and delivering presentations to internal and external audiences. This post is available initially until December 2027 with the possibility of extension. About you: We are seeking a highly motivated and enthusiastic individual with a strong interest in genomic technologies and considerable practical experience in molecular biology techniques such as PCR/qPCR and sample QC. Knowledge of scRNAseq and spatial transcriptomic assays would be essential to flourish within this role. The successful candidate should be confident in following detailed laboratory protocols. Experience of operating NGS sequencing platforms and performing scRNAseq or spatial transcriptomic assays would be highly desirable, although full training will be provided. We are particularly interested in candidates who are keen to develop their expertise within a collaborative genomics facility and contribute to the delivery of a high-quality service provision. As a key member of the team, we are seeking an effective team player who can work collaboratively with others but is also able to work independently and manage their own workload. A proactive approach, attention to detail, good communication skills and a commitment to providing an excellent service will be important attributes for success in this role. Candidates appointed at grade 7 will lead on the expansion and strategic development of the Spatial Transcriptomics stream. and will be expected to be able to deliver and organise presentations in internal facility, project and departmental meetings as well as external symposia and conferences as required. At GOS ICH we aim to always have a balanced interview panel that is representative of gender and ethnic diversity. A job description and full person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. If you have any queries relating to your application please contact ICH Human Resources at ich.hr@ucl.ac.uk quoting the job the reference. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Hybrid Final Salary Pension Scheme Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49066": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Clinical; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11368 About us: We are looking for a highly motivated and committed clinical research fellow. The post will principally support the quarantine phase of the international Mucosal Immunity in Human Coronavirus Challenge (MUSICC) project delivered at the Royal Free Hospital supported by the UCL Institute of Infection, Immunity and Transplantation. In addition to research activities, the post holder will join the Royal Free Hospital infection service which is a world-leading department providing outpatient and inpatient infectious diseases clinical care, and is the national referral centre for contact High Consequence Infectious Diseases (HCID). The post is ideally suited to, but by no means limited to, a doctor early in infection training seeking research experience, or an IMT doctor considering application to infection training. About the role: The post holder will act as the main study doctor supporting the safe and compliant delivery of the SARS-CoV-2 Omicron EG.5.1 human challenge study (COVHIC003) as part of the Royal Free-UCL Controlled Human Infection Model research team. This will include volunteer screening, consent, inoculation of SARS-CoV-2 challenge agent, clinical monitoring during the quarantine periods, and contribution to study documentation. The successful applicant will also join the Fink laboratory group at the UCL Institute of Infection, Immunity and Transplantation (IIT) based in the state-of-the-art Pears Building at the Royal Free Campus. The post holder will also have the opportunity to contribute to UCL MBBS infection and immunology teaching. The post holder will also join the clinical infection service at the Royal Free Hospital receiving training and supervised clinical experience in infectious diseases. About you: The post-holder will: Act as delegated study doctor for the SARS-CoV-2 human challenge study, supporting safe and compliant delivery of viral challenge, quarantine and follow-up procedures in accordance with the study protocol and Good Clinical Practice (GCP). Work collaboratively with the wider multidisciplinary human challenge study team, including project manager, nursing, and laboratory colleagues. Contribute to the preparation and review of study documentation, including study electronic data capture (using REDCap), protocols, participant information sheets, consent forms and case report forms. Participate in the general clinical infection service at the Royal Free Hospital, receiving structured training and supervised clinical experience in infectious diseases. Have the opportunity to allocate time for working within the IIT research laboratory, gaining exposure to translational immunology and infection research techniques. Have the opportunity to allocate time for UCL MBBS undergraduate teaching. Please see the attached job description and person specification for full details. Please also read the attached Candidate Guidance document. All applications must include a supporting statement, telling us, using examples, how you meet the essential criteria listed in the job description. Applications without a supporting statement will be rejected. If you have any queries about the role or application process, technical issues, or need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, please contact the staffing team at hr.ii@ucl.ac.uk . The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues in being successful and happy at UCL by sharing expectations around how we work. Please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to learn more. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48857": {
      "title": "Departmental Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07863 About us: The UCL Department of Electronic & Electrical Engineering is looking to recruit a Departmental Officer to join our Professional Services team. The post holder will be a key member of the team providing a reception and facilities management service in the department as well as administrative support to staff and students. About the role: The position comprises administrative support, reception and facilities management, and events, in addition to being responsible for a range of tasks which support staff and students in a large and busy department. The post holder will be required to establish good working relationships with departmental academic and professional services staff with whom they will work closely. The postholder will provide a welcoming front of house service to staff, students and all visitors to the department. This roles involves dealing with multiple tasks and customers at the same time and is key to the smooth running of the department. The post is available from 1 st December and is open-ended. This is an on-site role with an expectation of 100% on campus attendance, Monday – Friday. Attendance may vary depending on event requirements. About you: Experience of working within a higher education and/or service environment is essential, as is experience of working in a busy customer focused and customer facing environment. The post holder should possess excellent communication skills (written and oral) and good interpersonal skills and the ability to deal with a variety of people, both in in UCL and external agencies. They must be efficient, organised and take great care in attention to detail. A professional, friendly and helpful attitude is essential. The ideal candidate will be able to work independently as well as part of a small team. Experience of working in a reception and/or in an HR supporting role is desirable. Application details: To apply for the role, click the 'Apply Now' button at the bottom or top of the page Please upload a cover letter outlining how you meet the criteria in the person specification (document linked under 'Available documents') Applications close on 19th October at 23:59 A full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page Informal enquiries regarding this post and the application process can be addressed to Rebecca Thomas at becca.thomas@ucl.ac.uk. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48974": {
      "title": "Deputy Director of Operations",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Professional Services Leadership; London; £68,284-£78,386​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11275 About us: UCL is a world leading, global university with a community of more than 51,000 students from 150 countries and over 18,000 staff which pursues academic excellence, breaks boundaries, and makes a positive impact on real world problems. We are consistently ranked among the top ten universities in the world and are one of only a handful of institutions rated as having the strongest academic reputation. We are ambitious for change and are committed to taking ever bolder and more impactful interventions towards a becoming a more diverse, equitable and inclusive university. Faculty of Brain Sciences We are a global leader in research and education focused on the mind and brain. Our vision is to solve the greatest health and wellbeing challenges in brain sciences to reduce the global burden of disease and transform society. Established in 2011, the Faculty brings together world-class expertise in psychology, psychiatry, prion diseases, ophthalmology and vision, language sciences, audiology, deafness and hearing, neurology, and basic neuroscience. It comprises six academic departments: Division of Psychology and Language Sciences Division of Psychiatry Queen Square Institute of Neurology Institute of Ophthalmology Ear Institute Institute of Prion Diseases With over 1,900 staff and 4,000 students, we are one of UCL’s most research-intensive Faculties. For any questions relating to the role, please contact Geoff Dunk (Director of Operations) on g.dunk@ucl.ac.uk for an informal discussion. This is an internal recruitment advert, available to eligible UCL workers only . About the role: The Deputy Director of Operations will make an impact and support with establishing effective professional services provision across the Faculty of Brain Sciences. The role holder will work closely with the Director of Operations to establish functional service level provision for our academic departments and work to establish consistency in our ways of working, professional development and best practice amongst Faculty professional services teams. In addition to managing large teams as the need arises anywhere in the Faculty, the role holder will be tasked with initiating and autonomously managing a portfolio of short, medium and long-term projects to improve the efficiency and effectiveness of operations, and embedding strong identification with the Faculty and institution amongst staff. The Deputy Director of Operations will take a lead on identifying and supporting organisational change in any area of the Faculty, embedding strategic thinking and best practice across the whole of the Faculty. Close collaboration across the six academic departments in the Faculty is vital. This role has key responsibility to engage senior staff before and during change, ensuring all are equipped to support their staff in turn. The role holder will work in tandem with the Director of Operations on large projects, sometimes extending beyond the boundaries of the Faculty, and through involvement in strategic institutional projects relevant to Life and Medical Sciences or the university as a whole. The Deputy Director of Operations will report to the Director of Operations, but will work closely with the Dean and Deputy Dean, Vice-Deans and other senior academic and professional services colleagues across the Faculty and wider institution. The postholder will be a role model for the UCL Ways of Working, Dignity at Work and take an active role in Equality, Diversity and Inclusivity activities. A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: The role holder will be a senior operations manager who can thrive in a complex organisation and is motivated to make a positive impact across UCL. Experience of working in a large, complex academic environment is essential. You will be confident in providing high-quality operational leadership including leading and managing organisational change processes, translating strategic plans into operational processes, and managing business administration such as estates, budget and people planning. Alongside your experience, you will demonstrate excellent analytical skills, with the ability to interpret complex data insights and communicate insights clearly to diverse audiences. You will be a confident problem-solver who can develop and implement practical solutions, drawing on sound judgement and an evidence-based approach. You will be highly organised and able to manage a complex workload with competing priorities. Strong interpersonal and communication skills are key: you will be able to communicate complex ideas in writing and in person and will have experience of leading and managing teams, enabling team members to deliver to the best of their ability. We are looking for someone who is resilient and professional when faced with challenges, able to work independently but who also thrive as part of a team. Most importantly, you will contribute to a collaborative and inclusive culture, working closely with colleagues across the Faculty and able to build and maintain effective working relationships. This role does not meet the eligibility requirements for a Skilled Worker Visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require right to work in the UK to carry out this role. What we offer: Starting salary offered at a range between £68,284-£85,203 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48932": {
      "title": "Deputy Head of Operations (6months FTC) - Internal Applicants Only",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Professional Services Leadership; London; £54,931-£64,644; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07630 About us: For two centuries, the Department of Civil, Environmental and Geomatic Engineering (CEGE) has hosted pioneers of scientific approaches to complex problems. Our teaching and research are designed to bring positive change to human society and the natural environment. We do so by collaborating across disciplines and working with and for people. Our students are not passive learners: they get to address real-world challenges, whether local or global, in partnership with industrial stakeholders. Our success is built on the vast diversity in expertise and backgrounds of our staff and students, as well as our world-leading facilities. CEGE is based on the Bloomsbury campus and features extensive teaching and research laboratories and workshop facilities. The department also operates a structural and environmental testing facility for large-scale experiments and a large-scale robotics laboratory at UCL Here East in Stratford, East London. In Dagenham, CEGE hosts two unique research environments: the Person-Environment-Activity Research Laboratory (PEARL) , which investigates how people interact with their surroundings, and the Controlled Active Ventilation Environments (CAVE) laboratory , focused on innovative ventilation systems. About the role: We are seeking a Deputy Head of Operations (Internal Applicants Only) to support the Head of Operations in translating the department’s academic vision and strategic priorities into effective operational delivery. This is a broad senior role spanning planning, finance, people, space, governance, compliance, risk, communications and service improvement. You will work closely with the Head of Operations, Head of Department and senior leadership team, providing advice, analysis and operational leadership to support effective decision-making and the smooth running of the department. A particular focus of this fixed-term role will be providing continuity and resilience during a period of organisational change, including supporting transition and knowledge retention through EASE changes and Service Simplification. You will work across the department’s Bloomsbury, Here East and Dagenham East sites, build strong relationships across UCL’s matrix environment, and deputise for the Head of Operations as required. This is a fixed-term role until 28 th February 2027 in the first instance. This is an Internal recruitment advert (eligible UCL workers only) and also available as an internal secondment role (current UCL staff only). We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 40% on-site. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff . About you: As a Deputy Head of Operations , you will be an experienced operational leader who is comfortable working across a broad portfolio and navigating a large, complex organisation. You will combine strategic thinking with practical delivery, using sound judgement, evidence and organisational awareness to identify priorities, manage risk and maintain momentum. You will bring strong communication and relationship-building skills, with the confidence to work effectively with senior academic and professional services colleagues and to operate successfully within a matrix environment. You will be collaborative and inclusive in your approach, while also being comfortable making decisions, managing competing demands and providing clear advice where there may not be an obvious answer. Experience of organisational change, service improvement and operational planning will be particularly valuable. You will also have a strong commitment to creating a positive and inclusive working culture, supporting colleagues through change and helping teams work effectively across organisational boundaries. What we offer: A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact Kati Massey at k.massey@ucl.ac.uk If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR team at cege-hr@ucl.ac.uk . The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit UCL Ways of Working to find out more. In the event we get a high number of applications, we may close the advert early before the published closing date. As a minimum we will keep all adverts open for 2 weeks. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) (pro-rata) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit UCL Staff Benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here ."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48762": {
      "title": "Detection and Response Lead",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £68,284-£76,244; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05648 About us: Information Services Division (ISD) is the primary provider of IT services to UCL. We support and enhance learning, teaching, research and administrative processes by providing information- and technology-related services to over 50,000 staff and students of UCL and associated institutions. Our ambition is to be the leading IT services group in the Higher Education (HE) sector and we are growing our team’s capability in experience/UX, agile development, security, cloud, service management and partnering. We are modernising our technology foundations, digitising the processes of the university to transform experience for students and staff, and partnering across the university to drive differentiation in UCL education and research. UCL boasts a track record of ground-breaking research that transforms the lives of people around the world. Our teams provide dedicated, specialist support throughout the research and innovation lifecycle to help sustain and advance UCL’s position as a world-class, research-led university. About the role: Within ISD, this post is in the Information Security Group, which leads on all aspects of security at UCL. The role sits within Security Operations, which is responsible for security monitoring, incident response, vulnerability management and threat intelligence. You will lead UCL’s Detection and Response function, with responsibility for security monitoring, alert triage, investigations and security incident response. You will provide calm and accountable leadership during security incidents, develop the team’s capabilities, and ensure that effective processes, tooling, metrics and escalation arrangements are in place. Working closely with colleagues across Security Operations and the wider university, you will identify and address gaps in UCL’s detection and response capability, maintain and improve incident response plans and playbooks, and coordinate exercises to test UCL’s readiness. You will also build trusted relationships with senior stakeholders, service providers, government bodies and law enforcement, translating complex technical issues into clear operational and risk-based advice. About you: As the ideal candidate you will have a mix of the following skills and experience: Significant experience leading or managing security operations, SOC, detection and response, or incident response services in a complex organisation. Strong knowledge of security monitoring, alert triage, investigation workflows, incident declaration, incident response coordination and post-incident improvement. Experience working with relevant security tooling and platforms, such as SIEM, SOAR, EDR, NDR, identity protection, CSPM, CTEM and specialist incident response or forensic tools. Experience developing and improving incident response plans, playbooks, runbooks, exercises and operational process documentation. The ability to provide calm, decisive and evidence-based leadership during security incidents, including making timely decisions when information is incomplete. Experience leading, developing and supporting multidisciplinary technical teams, setting clear objectives and building capability, resilience and a culture of continuous improvement. Strong stakeholder management and communication skills, with the ability to explain technical security issues, operational impacts and risks to technical and non-technical audiences. Experience using metrics, evidence and operational data to improve services, prioritise risk and report outcomes to senior stakeholders. The ability to work collaboratively across internal teams, external suppliers and specialist partners to resolve telemetry, tooling, process and capability gaps. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48834": {
      "title": "Education Administrator (0.6FTE)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Education (B16) Reference: B16-02259 About us: About UCL Institute of Education Founded in 1902, UCL Institute of Education has been shaping policy and helping government, organisations and individuals navigate a changing society for the last 120 years. We embrace collaboration and excellence to create a future that is inclusive and just and have been ranked number one for education every year since 2014 in the QS World University Rankings by Subject. The Academic Programmes Office (APO) is a large vibrant and dynamic professional service support team at IOE. The APO provides student and programme support for all accredited academic programmes, (undergraduate, post graduate and teacher training) as well as for all research student activity. About the role: You will support the administration of learning and teaching on Undergraduate and/or Postgraduate Taught programmes at IOE. You will be expected to deliver a high-quality administrative service to students, colleagues and partners working in collaboration with academic and professional colleagues across the Faculty. You should be pro-active in your work and demonstrate flexibility in working with a number of individuals across different programme areas including working with other administrative colleagues as part of a team. This post is available on a part time basis at 0.6FTE (21.9 hours per week). For any informal queries about the role please contact Lorraine Fernandes - l.fernandes@ucl.ac.uk About you: You will have experience of working in a busy office environment, with excellent administration skills and an organised and flexible approach to work along with experience of working independently and as an active member of a team, supporting colleagues and contributing to the effective operation of the team. You will also have experience of developing, maintaining, reviewing and improving administrative, management and information systems. Your application form should address all the person specification points and should clearly demonstrate how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria. It is important that the criteria are clearly numbered and that you provide a response to each one. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro rata for part time staff) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49065": {
      "title": "Education Administrator Maternity Cover",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11322 About us: The UCL Faculty of Population Health Sciences (FPHS) brings together eight academic departments whose common aim is to explore and understand the underlying factors that cause ill-health and innovate to improve health worldwide. We reorganised our Education Administration and Student Experience (EASE) team in 2022 and have formed hubs to support our different types of students: Postgraduate Taught (PGT); Postgraduate Research (PGR); Undergraduate/Online; UCL East. Our EASE team is integrated across the Faculty and we work closely to complete annual administrative tasks together. We are continually looking for ways to increase efficiency, reduced duplication, and create a better experience for our staff. We are looking for an excellent Education Administrator to join our highly successful and supportive team. Based in Bloomsbury, the successful candidate will play a key role in supporting our postgraduate online students within the Undergraduate and Online programmes team. About the role: This role will provide specialist administration support to two of our established online programmes; Clinical Trials MSc and Master of Public Health MSc. The successful candidate will be responsible for ensuring effective procedures are adopted and documented for dealing with all aspects of the programmes administration. The position will support every part of the student lifecycle, including induction, attendance monitoring, assessments, student records, and teaching activities. They will play a crucial part in providing a high quality and timely service to students and staff. This role is offered on a part-time (0.85 FTE) basis. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 40% on site. This is a maternity cover position offered on a fixed term basis. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Application Details: This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-service… for more information. Application Process: A full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Please use the personal statement section to explain how you meet each of the essential and desirable criteria outlined in the person specification. Contact Details: If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact Andreas Opitz a.opitz@ucl.ac.uk If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact FPHS.HR@ucl.ac.uk About you: Applicants should have a minimum of five GCSEs at Grade C or above (including Mathematics and English) or have similar relevant work experience. You must have worked in a busy office environment, preferably within higher education. You should be very organised in your work, excellent at performing administrative duties, and be comfortable using a variety of software and systems. You will need good communication skills and have the desire to offer a high level of service. You will need to be able to work well as part of a team, liaise with a variety of people, and establish good working relationships with staff and students in a teaching and learning context. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave pro-rata, 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver Award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48505": {
      "title": "Full Time PhD Studentship- Mapping Spatial Plasticity Dynamics in Colorectal Cancer",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11314 About us: The Cell Communication Lab at the UCL Cancer Institute , led by Prof. Chris Tape, is dedicated to understanding how cancer cells communicate, adapt, and evolve within complex tumour environments. Our research combines cutting-edge experimental and computational approaches to investigate fundamental questions in cancer biology, with a particular focus on cancer cell plasticity and treatment resistance. Working closely with clinical colleagues at UCLH and partners across the Cancer Grand Challenges REWIRE-CAN programme , we bring together cancer biologists, clinicians, computational scientists, and drug developers to accelerate discoveries that can ultimately benefit patients. Our laboratory develops and applies innovative technologies including patient-derived organoids, single-cell analysis, spatial transcriptomics, and high-dimensional data science. We foster a collaborative, inclusive, and intellectually stimulating environment where researchers are encouraged to think creatively, work across disciplines, and contribute to impactful translational cancer research. About the role: This fully funded four-year PhD project will investigate how cancer cell plasticity varies across different regions of colorectal tumours. The successful candidate will explore a fundamental question in cancer biology: whether all tumour cells possess the ability to switch between drug-sensitive and drug-resistant states. You will establish and analyse patient-derived organoid models from multiple tumour regions, perform functional perturbation experiments, and study responses to chemotherapy. In parallel, you will use spatial transcriptomic technologies and advanced computational approaches to map plasticity dynamics within tumour tissue. Throughout the studentship, you will receive training in a range of state-of-the-art technologies, including organoid culture, single-cell mass cytometry, spatial transcriptomics, and high-dimensional data analysis. Working at the interface of discovery science and clinical research, you will collaborate with leading researchers across UCL and an international network of scientists within the REWIRE-CAN consortium. How to Apply Submit the following by 24th October 2026- (using the apply button.) CV including: Contact details of two referees (one academic). A short statement explaining how your experience aligns with the project and person specification. Academic transcripts and certificates (PDF format). Include official English translations if applicable. About you: You are a highly motivated and curious scientist with a strong interest in cancer biology and a desire to address important biomedical research questions. You enjoy problem-solving, are resilient when faced with experimental challenges, and are excited by the opportunity to work in a collaborative team-science environment. You will have, or be expecting to achieve, a First or Upper Second Class degree (or equivalent) in a relevant subject. A strong background in cell biology or cancer biology is desirable, along with an enthusiasm for learning new experimental and computational techniques. Experience in complex cell culture systems, such as organoids, single-cell technologies, or data analysis using Python and/or R would be advantageous. Most importantly, you will be passionate about advancing our understanding of cancer biology through innovative research and collaborative working. What we offer: This is a full time fully funded 4year PhD studentship , successful candidates will receive a non-taxable annual stipend of £27,800pa covering tuition fees at Home rate and meet UCL PhD admissions criteria. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people. Our division holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48719": {
      "title": "Governance and Benefits Analyst",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Strategy, Project and Change Management; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00382 About us: UCL Estates provides and manages the environment in which students and staff live, learn and work. Through our Estates team, we deliver sustainable facilities, high-quality buildings and essential services that support one of the world's leading universities. The Programme and Performance team provides the insight, assurance and reporting that helps ensure Estates achieves its strategic and operational objectives. About the role: We are seeking a Governance & Benefits Analyst to support effective governance and benefits management across a diverse portfolio of programmes and projects. Reporting to the Assistant Director - Performance, you will coordinate governance processes, manage committee workflows, monitor actions and ensure high-quality reporting for senior stakeholders. You will also play a key role in benefits management, working with project teams to identify, define, track and report benefits throughout the project lifecycle. The role involves maintaining benefits registers, analysing performance data, supporting post-implementation reviews and providing clear insight to support decision-making and continuous improvement. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will have experience working in governance, benefits management, performance reporting or a similar analytical role within a complex organisation. Comfortable engaging with stakeholders at all levels, you will be able to manage competing priorities, build effective relationships and communicate complex information clearly and confidently. With strong organisational skills, excellent attention to detail and a proactive approach, you will enjoy taking ownership of tasks and delivering high-quality outcomes. You will be proficient in Microsoft Office and SharePoint, with the ability to analyse data and present meaningful insights. Experience with Power BI and working within a large public-sector or complex environment would be advantageous. Above all, you will be committed to continuous improvement, collaboration and delivering best practice. Please read our job description, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure you address these in your application. We reserve the right to close our advert after two weeks if we receive an unexpectedly high level of interest, so would recommend you apply at the earliest opportunity. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48365": {
      "title": "Head of Commercial Contracts - IT&T",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Commercial and Procurement; London; See advert text; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00398 About us: UCL is one of the world’s leading multidisciplinary universities, with more than 18,000 staff and over 51,000 students from more than 150 countries. Alongside our global reputation for teaching and research, we operate a significant and complex commercial environment, spending more than £900 million each year with third-party suppliers. Commercial & Procurement Services plays an important role in ensuring this investment delivers maximum value for UCL. Our IT&T commercial activity supports the technology, infrastructure, systems and services that underpin UCL’s teaching, research and professional services. As we continue to strengthen our in-house commercial capability, we are looking for an experienced commercial contracts professional to help shape how some of our most important and business-critical IT&T supplier relationships and contracts are managed. About the role: As Head of Commercial Contracts – IT&T, you will provide senior commercial leadership across a portfolio of strategic, high-value and business-critical IT and technology supplier contracts. Reporting to the Director of Supply Chain – IT&T, you will lead the commercial and contract management of key suppliers, ensuring contracts are actively managed throughout their lifecycle and continue to deliver value for money, strong service performance and appropriate protection from commercial, operational and supply-chain risk. You will lead supplier governance and strategic supplier relationships, oversee complex commercial negotiations and contractual change, and use financial and performance insight to identify opportunities to improve value, manage cost and strengthen outcomes. You will also lead the resolution of supplier performance and commercial issues, ensuring appropriate contractual remedies and escalation routes are used where required. Working closely with Category Management, Finance, Legal, operational stakeholders and suppliers, you will help establish consistent, data-led commercial contract management practices and strengthen UCL’s approach to supplier performance, governance and commercial assurance. This is a senior and specialist role. Salary is likely to be £75,000-£95,000. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will be an experienced senior commercial or contract management professional with significant experience of managing, reviewing, negotiating and improving complex contracts within a large, complex or regulated organisation. You will bring strong knowledge of contract lifecycle management, supplier governance, commercial and financial management, risk and value-for-money delivery. Importantly, you will have a good understanding of contractual terms and mechanisms relevant to IT and technology services, which could include software, IT services, infrastructure or managed services contracts. You will be confident leading complex supplier negotiations, managing contractual change and resolving commercial disputes or performance issues. Strong analytical capability will enable you to interrogate commercial and financial information, challenge costs and supplier performance, and use data to provide clear insight to senior decision-makers. You will also be a credible and collaborative senior stakeholder manager, able to advise, influence and constructively challenge senior colleagues, suppliers and cross-functional teams. With strong commercial judgement and a continuous improvement mindset, you will help strengthen supplier accountability and build a high-performing commercial contract management capability across UCL. Please read our job description, paying particular attention to the essential and desirable criteria, and ensure these are covered in your application. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent.We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49074": {
      "title": "Head of Delivery (Estates)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £68,284-£76,244; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00327 About us: The UCL Estate comprises over 240 buildings, more than 6.5 million square feet of academic accommodation and around 4500 owned student bedrooms. The location, scale, variety and complexity of the estate are unparalleled in the sector, including world-leading research, teaching, offices and student accommodation within our Bloomsbury and UCL East campus, at Stratford Together, Campus Experience and Infrastructure and Estates Development (CE&I and ED) is responsible for managing all aspects of the UCL Estate from the strategic to the operational, including property asset management, master-planning, capital projects and seamless end to end operational support. The function comprises approximately 350 FM and property professionals, project managers and customer facing operational colleagues This key activity supports the student and campus experience for our 46,000 students and 13,000 staff across our sites. From providing student accommodation, security and catering, to delivering an estates masterplan and all forms of capital projects in a sustainable manner. We are a collective of technical professionals who work together to deliver a resilient, functional, and vibrant estate We have a proud history, and the ambition and drive to innovate. We strive to provide a coordinated and customer-oriented approach to both buildings and services, maintaining and transforming UCL’s Estate, so that it directly contributes to the creation of a truly inspirational environment for our academics, students and staff. We aim to provide the best possible experience, and responsive support, to our students and campus users in respect of the physical estate and all our campus services About the role: The Head of Delivery will set and lead the delivery culture and standards of the Capital Programme’s construction projects in delivery With oversight from supplier selection through pre-construction, construction to handing over to operations and academic occupation, the Head of Delivery will set and lead the delivery standard for capital programme works on behalf of UCL to objectively; Deliver a safe and respectful working environment for the workforce and work site adjacencies seeking industry leading-best practice in delivery Establish a high-quality interface with public realm focused on respecting and not interrupting a high-quality campus experience Ensure rigorous regulatory compliance for the works in delivery, setting the standard for how works are inspected and by whom Set a delivery model and standard for delivering world-class quality Ensure a seamless handover to operational and academic occupants, leading culture of engagement with operational colleagues and end users in the project delivery model Oversee project reporting to ensure rigorous command of works in delivery from a programmatic and resourcing lens, setting the standard for how we monitor and report on construction works in progress Oversee HSE reporting and near-miss and incident investigation, ensure robust and measured responses are actioned within the works in progress and lessons learned cascaded across the programme in delivery Ensure works in delivery meet existing UCL Standards and working closely with the UCL Health and Safety, Engineering and Operations teams to highlight if/where UCL standards do not delivery construction industry best practice and conceive pathways to agree and close any gaps The Head of Delivery will work with all respective Capital Programme Assistant Directors and UPOs, who will retain ultimate responsibility for their projects in delivery The Head of Delivery will assess skills gaps within the Capital Programme team and work will propose relevant training to close any skills gaps About you: With a focus is on delivery assurance and how we put people to work to deliver world-class places and spaces, the Head of Delivery will work with Capital Programme leadership team, the SLT and the Health and Safety and Operations team to set and define the aspired delivery culture for the programme and will review and drive enhancement in existing ways of working. Key considerations will include; Safety Culture and Project delivery leadership behaviours Roles and Responsibilities of our UPOs and PMOs in regard to health and safety and project delivery assurance Competency assessment and training/coaching to raise qualitive performance and confidence across programme Pre-Start checklists and establishing methods to assure project set up Quality of our CPPs and RAMS Training, review standards Quality of our site safety inspections Raising and closing out inspection and audit actions Monitoring and reporting progress on site Incidence response and reporting Quality of our monitoring and reporting Quality of how we put people to work daily Site set-up standards Welfare arrangements Logistics arrangements Interfaces with live academic spaces Communication with stakeholders and project environment neighbourhood What we offer: In return, you’ll have the opportunity to make a significant impact within Student Lifecycle portfolio, working alongside talented colleagues. We offer a collaborative environment where your ideas are valued, your growth is supported, and your work directly contributes to the university’s bold strategic goals Our people really are our greatest asset and we invest in them so that they can fulfil their potential. Equity, diversity and inclusion is important to us. We are committed to creating an environment where everyone is encouraged to give their best in a place where their unique experiences, perspectives and skills are seen as valuable assets As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Flexible working options Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department is working towards an Athena SWAN award We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Work at UCL pages: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48904": {
      "title": "Head of Operations",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Vice-President (External Engagement) (B23) Reference: B23-00229 About us: UCL is a radically different university. Founded in 1826 in the heart of London, we were the first university in England to welcome students of any religion and the first to welcome women to university education. Today, UCL has over 50,000 students and 15,000 staff and is one of the world’s top 10 universities. The Office of the Vice-President External Engagement (VPEE) leads UCL’s engagement with internal and external audiences, helping to build affinity with our staff and students, strengthen UCL’s public prominence and reputation, and demonstrate the public value of our research, education, innovation and partnerships. Our work spans brand, integrated marketing, internal communications, public affairs, media relations and events. We are looking for a Head of Operations to coordinate cross-team activity including finance and budgeting, HR processes, and team culture and wellbeing. About the role: The Head of Operations is a key role, that helps and enables leaders and colleagues across all teams in VPEE to work effectively. An expert in financial management, HR operations, you will support and train managers in VPEE on UCL policy and best practices in: staff recruitment, effective onboarding, people processes, and effective appraisal and development conversations. You will ensure a best in sector approach is in place for inducting new staff and introduce tools and activity to measure and increase colleague engagement. From a finance perspective, you will work with the Director of Operations, VPEE budget holders and Finance Business Partner, to coordinate planning and information gathering for UCL’s annual budgeting and reforecasting processes. You will also assist colleagues with procurement processes and develop training and information sessions on Finance processes and systems. Please note, this role is offered on a full-time basis (part-time applications of a minimum of 0.8 FTE would be considered), with hybrid working available - currently a minimum of 2-days per week onsite at our Bidborough House and Torrington Place offices (subject to change). Job Ref: B23-00229 Closing Date: 20-10-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) About you: We are looking for an experienced operations professional with a strong understanding of financial management, operational planning and organisational delivery, ideally with knowledge and experience of the higher education sector and the complex environment in which universities operate. You will understand the opportunities and challenges facing higher education and be able to apply your operational expertise effectively within a university setting. You will have experience of developing budgets, monitoring financial performance and using financial and people data to inform strategic decision-making. You will be confident working with senior colleagues and professional services teams to ensure resources are used effectively and organisational priorities are delivered. You will bring experience of managing change and improving processes, systems and ways of working in support of organisational objectives. You will be comfortable working across a complex organisation, managing competing priorities and bringing together different perspectives to achieve shared outcomes. You will be an experienced people leader who can motivate and develop colleagues and work effectively through both direct and matrix management. You will build strong, trusted relationships with senior colleagues and a diverse range of stakeholders, using your influencing and relationship management skills to achieve positive outcomes. Excellent communication skills are essential, including the ability to present complex information clearly to a wide range of audiences. You will be able to make sense of complex issues, including where information is incomplete, and use your judgement to establish priorities and respond effectively to changing demands. You will bring resilience, adaptability and a commitment to continuous improvement, alongside a genuine interest in delivering excellent professional services. You will demonstrate an active commitment to inclusion, diversity and intercultural sensitivity and will share UCL’s commitment to creating an environment where colleagues, students, partners and the wider community can thrive. Experience of analysing and presenting data to support evidence-based decision-making and proactive problem-solving would also be desirable. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=47736": {
      "title": "Head of Operations",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Professional Services Leadership; London; £68,284-£78,386​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11104 About us: The UCL Ear Institute is part of the Faculty of Brain Sciences and is internationally recognised as the UK’s premier multidisciplinary centre for research into hearing and deafness. It brings together some of the world's leading academics, clinicians and healthcare scientists working in fields related to hearing, balance and ENT care. In partnership with the Royal National ENT and Eastman Dental Hospitals, the Ear Institute forms Europe's largest single grouping of audio-vestibular researchers and clinical scientists, positioning it at the forefront of innovation and discovery in hearing health. The Ear Institute offers a genuinely collaborative research environment spanning fields as diverse as genetics, biophysics, cell biology, systems and computational neuroscience, human cognition and clinical neuroscience. Alongside its research activities, the Ear Institute delivers specialist undergraduate and postgraduate courses in audiology and hearing sciences; postgraduate research degrees; and a substantial portfolio of continuing professional development and short courses for healthcare professionals. Its thriving, vibrant community includes approximately 75 academic, research, technical and professional services staff, 25 PhD students and over 100 MSc and BSc students. About the role: UCL is seeking an exceptional operational leader to join the Ear Institute as its next Head of Operations. This is an exciting opportunity to join the Institute's Strategic Leadership Team and play a pivotal role in shaping its future. Working in close partnership with the Ear Institute Director, Professor Jennifer Linden, you will provide strategic and operational leadership across all aspects of the Institute's activities, supporting a community delivering research, education and clinical engagement. As Head of Operations, you will be responsible for the effective leadership of the Institute's professional services and operational functions. You will lead a professional services team of 11 staff, with direct line management responsibility for four team members, second-line management oversight of a further four staff through those managers, and matrix management responsibility for the remaining three team members. Working closely with academic, technical and clinical colleagues, you will help ensure the Institute continues to thrive in a complex and highly regulated environment. The role offers a diverse portfolio of responsibilities, spanning strategic planning, financial management, education and student support, human resources and people management, and the oversight of specialist facilities and infrastructure. It includes supporting major research initiatives, driving service improvement, and contributing to Faculty and UCL-wide priorities. As a key member of the Ear Institute's Strategic Leadership Team, you will work in partnership with colleagues to translate academic ambition into operational delivery, foster a positive and inclusive culture, support staff and student success, and ensure the long-term sustainability and success of the Institute The Ear Institute combines the reputation and ambition of a world-leading research institute with the culture of a collegiate and community-focused department. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to work closely with colleagues across the Institute, play a visible role in shaping its future direction and ways of working, and make a meaningful contribution to its research, education and operational success. The Ear Institute is committed to fostering an inclusive, supportive and equitable environment in which staff and students can thrive. It holds an Athena Swan Silver Award in recognition of its commitment to advancing gender equality and creating an inclusive culture for all members of its community. Environmental sustainability is also a core priority. The Ear Institute has achieved Gold accreditation through both Green Impact and the Laboratory Efficiency Assessment Framework (LEAF), and is one of a small number of UCL departments participating in the pilot of Green DiSC (Digital Sustainability Certification). These initiatives reflect the Institute's commitment to ensuring its activities remain environmentally, financially and operationally sustainable. Interviews will be held on 24 November 2026. If you have any questions about the role or would like to have an informal discussion, please contact the Director, Professor Jennifer Linden (j.linden@ucl.ac.uk) About you: We are seeking an experienced and collaborative leader who can operate confidently within a complex academic environment. You will have a strong track record of strategic planning, operational leadership, financial management and delivering organisational change. You will be able to build trusted relationships with a wide range of stakeholders, influence effectively at senior levels and provide clear, evidence-based advice to support decision-making. You will also bring excellent communication skills, strong analytical capability and the ability to balance strategic thinking with operational delivery. Experience of working within higher education, research, healthcare or another complex knowledge-intensive environment would be advantageous. Most importantly, you will be committed to supporting an inclusive and collaborative culture that enables world-leading research and education to flourish. The role is based at the Ear Institute, Gray's Inn Road, London, with hybrid working arrangements in line with UCL's Ways of Working framework. Given the operational complexity of the Institute and its specialist facilities, the postholder is expected to maintain a significant on-site presence. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance F ind out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48982": {
      "title": "Internships and Vacancies Administrator (Fixed-Term Contract)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Student Well-being and Registry; London; £31,904-£36,433; Vice-Provost (Education and Student Experience) (B06) Reference: B06-00213 About us: The UCL Higher Education Development & Support Institute (HEDS) brings together UCL Arena and UCL Careers within the Office of the Vice Provost (Education & Student Experience). Together, we support excellence in education and student experience, while helping students and graduates develop the skills, knowledge and opportunities they need for their futures. UCL Careers provides careers information, internships and vacancies services, guidance and events to current UCL students, researchers and recent graduates. Our work with employers and recruiting organisations helps connect our students and graduates with opportunities and supports their engagement with the labour market. We are looking for an Internships & Vacancies Administrator to join our Careers team on a secondment cover basis until July 2027. This is an opportunity to contribute to a busy, student-focused service, working with employers and colleagues across UCL to support the delivery of our central internship and vacancy services. About the role: As an Internships & Vacancies Administrator, you will provide administrative and customer-facing support across our central internships and vacancies services. You will be a key first point of contact for employers looking to advertise jobs and internships to UCL students and graduates, helping them understand our services and identifying the most appropriate ways for them to engage with our student community. Working as part of a collaborative team, you will manage enquiries through a shared inbox, provide advice to recruiters by email and Teams, and work with colleagues across employer engagement to ensure enquiries are directed appropriately. You will also review and approve employer and vacancy information on our careers management system, ensuring opportunities meet UCL's advertising policies and relevant requirements. You will maintain accurate employer and opportunity information on myUCLCareers (TargetConnect) , support vacancy reporting and help promote internships and other opportunities through our jobs board. You will also provide administrative support across specific services, including the UCL Talent Bank shortlisting service and the Careers Extra Summer Internship scheme. This is a varied role that would suit someone who enjoys working with people, has strong attention to detail and is interested in supporting students and employers within a higher education careers environment. Please note, this role is offered on a fixed-term contract basis until July 2027. This role does not meet the requirements for a Skilled Worker visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require a right to work in the UK Job Ref: B06-00213 Closing Date: 22-10-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) About you: We are looking for an organised and customer-focused professional who can communicate confidently with a range of audiences, both in writing and verbally. You will have excellent organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities, work accurately and remain calm when working to deadlines. You will have good IT skills, including experience of using Microsoft Office, spreadsheets and databases to manage information, contacts or recruitment-related activity. Strong attention to detail and a commitment to providing a high-quality service are essential. Experience of working in a higher education careers, placements, internships or graduate recruitment environment would be advantageous, as would experience of administering internship or placement schemes or working with recruiters. An understanding of the graduate labour market and experience of liaising with employers would also be desirable. Above all, you will be committed to equality, diversity and inclusion and enjoy working collaboratively as part of a team. You will understand how your work contributes to the wider service and the experience of our students, graduates and employer partners. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48725": {
      "title": "Lab Manager (Maternity Cover)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Laboratory Technical and Lab Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11238 About us: Help shape the future of hearing and balance research Are you an experienced laboratory professional looking for an opportunity to lead laboratory operations within a world-leading research environment? The UCL Ear Institute is seeking a proactive and highly organised Laboratory Manager (Maternity Cover) to play a pivotal role in supporting our internationally recognised research and teaching activities. Working at the heart of one of the world's leading centres for hearing, deafness and balance research, you will help ensure our laboratories, facilities and research infrastructure operate safely, efficiently and to the highest professional standards. This is an exciting opportunity for someone with strong laboratory experience who is ready to broaden their leadership and operational management expertise within a complex and dynamic higher education environment. UCL Ear Institute is part of the Faculty of Brain Sciences and is internationally recognised as the UK's premier multidisciplinary centre for research into hearing and deafness. We bring together leading researchers, clinicians, educators and professional services staff to deliver groundbreaking discoveries and innovations that improve lives worldwide. Through our partnerships with the Royal National ENT and Eastman Dental Hospitals, we form Europe's largest grouping of audio-vestibular discovery and clinical scientists. The Institute is home to approximately 75 staff and supports a diverse portfolio of hearing science, auditory neuroscience and clinical research, underpinned by specialist laboratory facilities and shared research infrastructure. We are proud of our strong commitment to sustainability, inclusion and operational excellence, holding Athena Swan Silver accreditation together with Gold accreditation through Green Impact and LEAF. About the role: Reporting to the Head of Operations, with a dotted line to the Institute Director, you will lead the day-to-day management of shared laboratories and associated research facilities across the Institute. You will work closely with academic, technical and professional services colleagues to ensure laboratories remain safe, compliant, well-equipped and fully operational. Key responsibilities include: Managing all shared laboratory facilities and identifying opportunities for service improvement. Overseeing laboratory equipment procurement, maintenance, servicing and calibration. Acting as a key contact for laboratory refurbishment, relocation and infrastructure projects. Leading on laboratory health and safety compliance, audits, inspections, inductions and risk management. Supporting sustainability initiatives, including LEAF and Green Impact activities. Managing laboratory databases, records and inventory systems. Overseeing laboratory-related procurement and expenditure, ensuring value for money and compliance with UCL procedures. Line managing a Cell and Molecular Laboratory Technician. This is a fixed-term maternity cover position for 13 months. The post has been created to provide cover during a period of maternity leave and is expected to end on the return of the substantive post-holder or at the end of the fixed-term period, whichever is sooner. The post is also available as a secondment opportunity for eligible internal applicants, subject to agreement from their current line manager. For an informal discussion, please contact Aisling Muller at aisling.muller@ucl.ac.uk About you: We are looking for an individual who combines strong scientific understanding with excellent operational and relationship-management skills. You will have: A degree (or equivalent experience) in a relevant scientific discipline. Practical experience working within a research laboratory environment. Strong knowledge of molecular and cell biology and laboratory techniques. Experience managing stakeholders across research teams and external partners. A good understanding of laboratory health and safety regulations and compliance requirements. Excellent organisational, communication and problem-solving skills. The ability to manage multiple priorities and work independently in a fast-paced environment. Experience in laboratory management, line management, project management, higher education and the use of university finance systems would be advantageous This maternity cover role offers a unique opportunity to gain leadership experience within a complex research-intensive institute while contributing directly to cutting-edge scientific research and education. You will work closely with senior academic and professional services leaders and benefit from being part of the wider technical and professional services community across UCL's Faculty of Brain Sciences. What we offer: Starting salary offered at £43,981 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Gold award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49029": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching) in Business and Sustainability- maternity leave cover",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07937 About us: Set within The Bartlett Faculty, The Bartlett School of Environment, Energy and Resources (BSEER) is comprised of four world-class institutes, providing expert knowledge, collaborative research, and teaching. This role is within the Institute for Sustainable Resources, whose mission is to provide evidence, expertise and training to respond to climate change and support sustainable transitions for people and planet. About the role: The UCL Institute for Sustainable Resources is looking for a maternity cover for a Lecturer (Teaching) in Business, Sustainability and Society. The successful candidate will play an important role in the delivery of our MSc programme, Business and Sustainability (BaS), which is committed to transformation across business, environment and society. They will act as interim Deputy Course Leader, significantly contributing to running the programme and associated managerial and administrative tasks, in collaboration with the Course Leader. They will lead/co-lead two taught modules, and contribute teaching on other modules as required. They will also act as supervisor and marker to student Capstone projects. The role will involve being a key contact for students, and working closely with administration team on delivery of the programme. This post is available from 1 st December 2026 and is funded until 30 April 2028 in the first instance; further funding to support the post may become available. UCL welcomes applications from international applicants and has licence to sponsor individuals who require a visa. This is dependent on the post and candidate meeting eligibility requirements for visa sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. About you: A track record of managing and delivering postgraduate taught programmes and/ or modules on topics related to business sustainability, and of successful postgraduate research supervision (at Masters level), is essential. An ability to deliver high-quality teaching, excellent communication, and enthusiasm for research and teaching related to business sustainability is also required. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit Work at UCL | Work at UCL - UCL – University College London to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48870": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching) in Computational Linguistics",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11339 About us: Based within the Division of Psychology and Language Sciences in UCL’s Faculty of Brain Sciences, the Department of Linguistics brings together theoretical, experimental and computational approaches to the study of language, and has a track record of innovative and excellent research in formal theoretical linguistics and the cognitive science of language. UCL is the top-ranked university in the UK for research power in psychology, psychiatry and neuroscience according to the UK’s Research Excellence Framework 2021. The Division is ranked fifth in the world for psychology in the QS World University Rankings by Subject 2026, and UCL is ranked joint eighth in the QS World University Rankings. About the role: The postholder will deliver and develop teaching in computational linguistics, while also undertaking original research and publication, master’s student supervision in areas related to their research expertise, contributions to doctoral supervision where appropriate, and academic service. They will teach the existing Introduction to Computational Linguistics and Advanced Computational Linguistics modules on the MA Linguistics with a Specialisation in Computational Linguistics. They will also design Foundations of Maths and Programming for Computational Linguistics for the same postgraduate programme and teach it from 2027–28. This is a fixed-term role for two years from the start date. The salary range is from Grade 8, SP 40 - 42 (£56,431 - £59,571 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance). This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research, Teaching and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. About you: The successful candidate will hold a relevant PhD by the start date and can evidence independent research in computational linguistics, desirably with high-quality research outputs commensurate with career stage. They will have mathematical expertise and relevant university teaching experience, with the ability to develop accessible postgraduate teaching and assessment in mathematical and programming foundations. They will organise teaching and administrative work independently and contribute to an inclusive academic environment. Further details The advert will close on 26 October 2026 at 23:59 GMT. A Job Description and Person Specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Mario Giulianelli at m.giulianelli@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact PALS HR at pals.hr@ucl.ac.uk . What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Athena Swan Status Statement Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48892": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching) in European Prehistory",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03295 About us: The UCL Institute of Archaeology is recognised as one of the leading academic departments of Archaeology and Heritage in the world. It is the largest department within its field in the UK. It offers a uniquely stimulating environment with more than 50 academic staff pursuing high-quality research across five continents. We are at the forefront of global archaeology, archaeological science, conservation, and heritage studies. To find out more about the Institute of Archaeology, visit our webpage at UCL Institute of Archaeology . About the role: We are seeking an innovative archaeologist to appoint as a full time (36.5 hours per week) Lecturer (Teaching) available from 1 January 2027 to 31 December 2027. We are looking for applicants with research expertise in the European Mesolithic, Neolithic, Bronze Age and/or Iron Age, and in leading-edge methods associated with aDNA and/or palaeoecology. Applicants should also be able to contribute to teaching in regional survey courses on prehistory and the coordination of core teaching in introductory archaeology. Some contribution to the Institute’s active UK field training programmes (either in the field or post-excavation) will also be expected. The successful candidate will be expected to undertake undergraduate and postgraduate teaching, as well as supervise Masters’ and Undergraduate dissertations, and undertake some administrative duties. About you: The successful candidate will have a PhD in Archaeology (relevant to some aspect of European Prehistory) and experience of research-led teaching in Archaeology. They will also have experience of designing, coordinating, and delivering teaching and assessments at undergraduate level in fields relevant to the role. Field and/or collections experience in European Prehistoric Archaeology and willingness to support Institute of Archaeology student fieldwork training and post-excavation supervision and research experience with either aDNA or palaeoecology is also essential. Further details about the post and a person specification can be found in the Job Description (linked below). Queries about the post may be addressed to Professor Kevin MacDonald at kevin.macdonald@ucl.ac.uk . If you have any queries about the application process, please contact Louisa Goldsmith (HR Administrator) at l.goldsmith@ucl.ac.uk . To apply for the vacancy, please click on the Apply Now button. Candidates are asked to also upload the following documents: A full CV (including academic and employment history, and any additional relevant skills and the contact details of two referees, one of whom should be the current or most recent line manager) A cover Letter that states how you meet each of the essential and desirable criteria as listed in the person specification within the job description Evidence of qualifications Please refer to the application process within the job description for guidance on completing your cover letter. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days' annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=47822": {
      "title": "Lecturer - x2",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Academic; London; £56,431-£66,437​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07695 About us: The Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering is one of the largest Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering departments in the UK. The Department is proud to host internationally leading researchers covering a broad range of activities, spread over several sites. Our staff and students have a diverse range of interests and expertise, encompassing many areas of physics, biomedical engineering, medicine, physiology, computer science, and mathematics. Our focus is on excellent academic research to identify new technologies and methods for the diagnosis, treatment and management of diseases. Affiliated with 8 major London hospitals, the Department is a key partner of UCL’s Institute of Healthcare Engineering and the Hawkes Institute, and works closely with other departments from the Faculty of Engineering Sciences, including Mechanical Engineering, Electronic and Electrical Engineering, and Computer Science. We offer a range of undergraduate and postgraduate degrees and are home to over 700 students. These programmes include a range of departmental and cross-faculty modules, incorporating problem and scenario-based learning at all stages. This creates a highly engaging learning environment where students put their theoretical knowledge to practical application. More information on the department’s research and teaching activities can be found at: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/medical-physics-biomedical-engineering . About the role: We are looking for two new Lecturers (Assistant Professors) to lead their own research groups within the department, ideally complementary to existing activities. We seek applicants with a programme of research within biomedical engineering and medical physics disciplines aligned with the interests of the department. Apart from offering mentorship and support to their own research teams, the new lecturers will contribute to teaching on our BEng/MEng in Biomedical Engineering programmes, act as a personal tutor for students on our taught programmes, supervise student projects and contribute to the effective running of the department. They will be expected to teach one or more disciplines, which may not align directly with their research areas. This is a permanent position. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 40% on site. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Academic Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies/2021/jun/conditions-service-academic-staff for more information. Please upload Cover Letter, Research Proposal, Teaching Proposal and summary of publications. About you: You will have an exciting vision for your research group and be able to communicate how this research group could flourish within UCL. You will be, or have the potential to be, an effective and approachable leader, demonstrating mentorship and support for your team. You will have a PhD level degree or equivalent in Engineering, the Physical Sciences, or relevant field, and must have experience in producing independent, original, impactful and rigorous research contributions in your field. You will be familiar with the research funding process and grant writing. You will have experience of supervising BSc, MSc and PhD level students and of delivering university level teaching at either BSc or MSc level, in a relevant topic. Your application should include: a full CV including publications. a Cover Letter. Please evidence how you meet the essential and desirable criteria in the Person Specification part of the Job Description. a Research Proposal (maximum length 2 pages). Please describe your vision and research topic, how you will build your team and how you will start to fund your research programme. a Teaching Proposal (maximum length 2 pages). Please outline how you would contribute to teaching in our department. Summary of publications (maximum length 1 page). Please include your 6 most important papers, explaining your contribution and impact of each paper. The Cover Letter, Research Proposal, Teaching Proposal and summary of publications should be compiled into a single document. The minimum acceptable font size is 11 and margins in all directions must be at least 2cm. Your application may be rejected if these documents are not provided or the above size restrictions are not respected. (By including a Cover Letter, you can leave blank the 'Why you have applied for this role' field in the online application form, which is limited in the number of characters it will allow.) What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . The advert will close on 16 th October 2026 at 23:59 GMT. Interview Date(s) are scheduled for: 9 th – 13 th November 2026. A Job Description and Person Specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Professor Terence Leung ( t.leung@ucl.ac.uk ) or Head of Department Professor Peter Munro (p.munro@ucl.ac.uk) If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact medphys.hr@ucl.ac.uk . This role meets the requirements for a Skilled Worker certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible.” UCL Terms and Conditions of Service: Academic Staff Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48750": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Contemporary Moving Image 0.5FTE",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Academic; UCL East; £56,431-£66,437​; School of Education (B16) Reference: B16-02254 About us: Founded in 1902, UCL Institute of Education has been shaping policy and helping government, organisations and individuals navigate a changing society for the last 120 years. We embrace collaboration and excellence to create a future that is inclusive and just, and have been ranked number one for education every year since 2014 in the QS World University Rankings by Subject. The Department of Culture, Communication and Media (CCM ) is a department of IOE, UCL’s Faculty of Education and Society. It is committed to excellence in teaching, research and consultancy in the areas of: Media and Cultural Studies, Art, Design and Museology; Academic writing; English education: Applied Linguistics; Music Education; Multimodality studies; Learning with Digital Technologies. We are looking to appoint an exceptional Lecturer in Contemporary Moving Image. UCL's BA Media blends creative media production and critical thinking through practice-as-research. Unlike purely practical film production or game development programmes, students critically engage with theories of media and technology, addressing these concepts directly within their practical work. Students explore digital and neural media, interactive storytelling, worldbuilding, fine art, programming, and animatics through making, experimentation, and reflection. About the role: The Lecturer in Contemporary Moving Image will support students in developing experimental work at the frontier of 21 st century moving image practices, while demonstrating a nuanced and critical understanding of the relationship between art-making, technology, and culture. The postholder will bring experience of creating inclusive and equitable learning environments, including the decolonisation of thought and the promotion of diversity within art and technology. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/prospective-students/undergraduate/degrees/media-ba The postholder will play an important role in the delivery of the BA Media. In addition to teaching at undergraduate level, the post-holder may contribute to Masters and PhD programmes in media, and to research, knowledge exchange and consultancy activities. About you: You will be a creative practitioner with a recognised practice in contemporary moving image fields, and demonstrable experience in directing and presenting ambitious public-facing moving image productions. You will teach moving image as an experimental and critical medium, making use of film and video technologies, animation, virtual production, storytelling alongside key competencies in camera and lighting, NLEs (Adobe Premiere Pro, DaVinci Resolve), and direction of projects involving cast and/or crew, from development through to delivery/presentation. With a doctorate (completed or close to completion) or comparable professional practice or/and industry experience, in games development, art and technology or creative computing, you will also have evidence of potential to produce internationally excellent, original and significant practice and research. For further information please contact Dane Sutherland – dane.sutherland@ucl.ac.uk Your application form should address all the person specification points and should clearly demonstrate how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria. It is important that the criteria are clearly numbered and that you provide a response to each one. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave, 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro rata for part time staff) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48752": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Digital Media Practice - 0.5",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Academic; UCL East; £54,931-£64,644; School of Education (B16) Reference: B16-02209 About us: Founded in 1902, UCL Institute of Education has been shaping policy and helping government, organisations and individuals navigate a changing society for the last 120 years. We embrace collaboration and excellence to create a future that is inclusive and just, and have been ranked number one for education every year since 2014 in the QS World University Rankings by Subject. The Department of Culture, Communication and Media (CCM ) is a department of IOE, UCL’s Faculty of Education and Society. It is committed to excellence in teaching, research and consultancy in the areas of: Media and Cultural Studies, Art, Design and Museology; Academic writing; English education: Applied Linguistics; Music Education; Multimodality studies; Learning with Digital Technologies. About the role: We are looking to appoint an exceptional Lecturer in Digital Media Practice. UCL's BA Media blends creative media production and critical thinking through practice-as-research. Unlike purely practical film production or game development programmes, students critically engage with theories of media and technology, addressing these concepts directly within their practical work. Students explore digital and neural media, interactive storytelling, worldbuilding, fine art, programming, and animatics through making, experimentation, and reflection. You will support students in developing technical fluency in experimental web and mobile creative programming, while demonstrating a nuanced and critical understanding of the relationship between art-making, technology, and culture. The postholder will bring experience of creating inclusive and equitable learning environments, including the decolonisation of thought and the promotion of diversity within art and technology. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/prospective-students/undergraduate/degrees/media-ba In addition to teaching at undergraduate level, the post-holder may contribute to Masters and PhD programmes in media, and to research, knowledge exchange and consultancy activities. About you: You will be a creative practitioner with a practice engaging contemporary digital and computational fields, and demonstrable experience of public-facing work. This is a post for someone who will teach coding (building browser-based, interactive, and generative work using JavaScript and libraries such as three.js, p5.js, and WebGL/GLSL) alongside key tools such as Unreal Engine, Blender, and Stable Diffusion, treated as both creative and critical media. With a doctorate (completed or close to completion) or comparable professional practice or/and industry experience, in games development, art and technology or creative computing, you will also have evidence of potential to produce internationally excellent, original and significant practice and research. For further information please contact Dane Sutherland – dane.sutherland@ucl.ac.uk Your application form should address all the person specification points and should clearly demonstrate how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria. It is important that the criteria are clearly numbered and that you provide a response to each one. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave, 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro rata for part time staff) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48634": {
      "title": "Lecturer in Quantitative Social Science",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Academic; London; £56,431-£66,437​; School of Education (B16) Reference: B16-02206 About us: Founded in 1902, UCL Institute of Education has been shaping policy and helping government, organisations and individuals navigate a changing society for the last 120 years. We embrace collaboration and excellence to create a future that is inclusive and just and have been ranked number one for education every year since 2014 in the QS World University Rankings by Subject. The Social Research Institute , formerly the Department of Social Science in the UCL Institute of Education, is one of the leading centres for social research in the UK. It is home to seven internationally renowned research units: Centre for Longitudinal Studies Centre for Time Use Research Cohort & Longitudinal Studies Enhancement Resources (CLOSER) Evidence for Policy & Practice Information Centre (EPPI) Quantitative Social Science (QSS) Social Science Research Unit Thomas Coram Research Unit Our strategy is to exploit and promote our key characteristics: All our centres are multidisciplinary, with staff drawn variously from sociology, social policy, demography, economics, psychology, anthropology and social statistics. We are working across centres/units to promote cross-methodological, problem-solving interdisciplinary research on particular themes where we have outstanding scholarship and critical mass, e.g. gender, families, work, inequalities, migration, bio-social interactions. We are tackling major social challenges, e.g. on how societies support family wellbeing, gender equality and economic prosperity. We have a central role in sociological studies at UCL with staff working on empirically and theoretically rich research. Our work is increasingly transgressing the boundaries of academic social science, taking us into life sciences (health, social epidemiology, biosocial research) natural sciences (information science, environmental science) and the humanities (peacebuilding, creative arts). Our staff actively engage with public priorities in policy and practice (as seen in our many Impact Case Studies) and with networks for knowledge exchange. We are active in the collection and curation of data as a public resource and in the development of research methods as well as in substantive research. We have a globally central position in longitudinal research, time use research, and systematic reviews. More than 180 academic, research, teaching, technical and professional service staff work in the department. In addition to the seven research centres, it is home to three undergraduate degrees, three master’s programmes and a thriving doctoral community. About the role: We are now seeking a Lecturer (Assistant Professor) in Quantitative Social Science to contribute to our research and teaching. Relevant fields include sociology and economics. A research agenda on the environment and/or climate change is welcome. Applicants with the ability to teach advanced quantitative methods, particularly causal inference and proficiency in R/Python are especially welcome. The post holder will contribute to and help shape our interdisciplinary research portfolio by applying advanced quantitative social science methods to pressing real-world challenges around the environment and climate change. They will be expected to develop an ambitious research agenda, seek external funding from a diverse range of sources and produce work with demonstrable impact beyond academia. The successful candidate will teach and lead modules on our BSc Social Sciences with Data Science, MSc Social Research Methods with Data Science or MSc. Sociology programmes and may be invited to (co-)lead these programmes over time. We are particularly interested in applicants with a commitment to open and collaborative scholarship, innovative pedagogy, and inclusive curriculum design. Appointees will also be expected to contribute actively to the intellectual and collegial life of the department. The post is available from 01 September 2027. For further information contact Professor Kirstine Hansen, Director, QSS Research Centre and chair of the recruitment panel (k.hansen@ucl.ac.uk). About you: You’ll have a PhD in sociology or economics where advanced quantitative methods have been applied, completed by time of appointment. A research agenda around the environment and/or climate change. A clearly articulated publication strategy and pipeline targeting internationally leading journals in sociology and economics. Your application form should address all person specification points and clearly demonstrate how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria. It is important that the criteria are clearly numbered and that you provide a response to each one. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro-rata for part-time staff) • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. For a full job description and to apply for this role please visit UCL’s online recruitment portal (https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs)"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48384": {
      "title": "Lecturer/ Associate Professor",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Academic; London; See advert text; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03266 About us: The Department of Philosophy at UCL is one of the leading philosophy departments in the UK. In the 2021 REF, 60% of our overall profile received the top grade (4*), the highest of any UK philosophy department. We offer several undergraduate degree programmes. Undergraduate first-year modules usually consist of two hour-long lectures plus an hour-long seminar each week; second-year modules usually involve a one-hour lecture plus a one-hour seminar each week; third year modules usually consist of a weekly two-hour seminar. We offer taught master’s programmes, and a prestigious research master’s and PhD. Postgraduate-level modules usually consist of a weekly two-hour seminar. In collaboration with the Department of Computer Science, we run a BA in Philosophy and Computer Science, and in 2026 introduced an MASc in Philosophy, Logic and Artificial Intelligence. The department has great advantages as a centre of research activity. The many research seminars, talks and lectures that take place in the various London colleges, the Institute of Philosophy in the University of London, the Institute of Classical Studies, the Aristotelian Society and the Royal Institute of Philosophy, all offer a wealth of opportunities. The post is offered on an open-ended, full-time basis, with an ideal start date of 1 September 2027. As London’s Global University, we are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates whose backgrounds are underrepresented in Philosophy. About the role: Main purpose of the job We are looking for a candidate whose main research lies in the philosophy of computer science and/or artificial intelligence, and who will contribute to the teaching and development of our two joint degrees with Computer Science and help build research connections between the departments. We welcome candidates working anywhere at the interface of philosophy and computing, including the philosophy of cognitive science, the foundations of machine learning, and the ethical and social dimensions of AI. We are open to a wide range of approaches. This appointment is intended to complement our existing strengths in logic and the philosophy of mathematics. The appointment will be made at either Lecturer (Grade 8) or Associate Professor (Grade 9) level. Application Applications will be made online. When you apply, you will need to submit: • a CV; • a cover letter, of no more than two sides; • two writing samples, totalling no more than 20,000 words. Important: You will also need to arrange for two referees to submit letters of reference prior to the published closing date . Letters should be addressed to Professor Daniel Rothschild at philosophy.letters@ucl.ac.uk. About you: A PhD in a relevant field, awarded before the start date. Clear evidence of excellence in philosophical research at a level commensurate with the grade of the job applied for (Lecturer or Associate Professor). What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=47450": {
      "title": "Lecturer/Associate Professor in Artificial Intelligence for Biomolecular Design & Molecular Interactions",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Academic; London; £68,284-£76,244; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-10648 About us: The Division of Biosciences at UCL is a major centre for research and teaching in the biological sciences, fostering curiosity-driven research that spans molecules to ecosystems. Within Biosciences, the Research Department of Structural & Molecular Biology (SMB) provides a stimulating and diverse research environment where important and exciting areas of modern biology, biophysics, biochemistry, biotechnology and medicine are investigated at the atomic, molecular, cellular and organism level. We are recruiting a Lecturer (Assistant Professor equivalent) or Associate Professor in AI for Biomolecular Design & Molecular Interactions to expand our strengths in Computational Biology and Bioinformatics as applied to research surrounding Modelling and Design of Life. This role also forms part of AI for Life, a major programme across Biosciences in computational and artificial intelligence-driven life sciences. About the role: An exciting development in AI-driven biosciences is the development of predictive and generative models of biomolecular interactions, spanning protein–ligand, protein–protein, and protein–nucleic acid systems. Next-generation approaches that integrate structural, thermodynamic and sequence-level information to model binding specificity, affinity, and conformational dynamics, and on translating these insights into the rational design of novel molecules and interaction networks, will enable precise control of biomolecular interactions and functions. This is an exciting opportunity to become a Lecturer/Associate Professor at UCL. The successful candidate will take an active role in the Research Department of Structural & Molecular Biology (SMB), developing international standard, competitive research programme, conducting teaching and training of undergraduate and postgraduate students, and supporting the Departmental vision of creating an impact on the life of the University and the society. The successful candidate will establish (at Grade 8) or expand (at Grade 9) a research and teaching programme centred on original, AI-driven approaches for biomolecular design, with a focus on molecular recognition and interaction engineering. We are particularly interested in candidates with a track record of developing innovative approaches combining elements of structural biology, biophysics, quantitative biology and machine learning, to understand and design interactions between proteins, small molecules, and nucleic acids. This new research programme will be fully embedded within research activities of SMB, providing opportunities of fruitful interactions between experimentalist and theoreticians, shaping and expanding new research directions. The postholder will play a pivotal role in SMB’s teaching activities, contributing teaching in relevant areas (e.g. computational biology, protein-nucleic acid/small molecule interactions, protein/biomolecular design) at both undergraduate and postgraduate levels, including a new cross-division MRes AI for Bioscience. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Academic Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies/2021/jun/conditions-service-academic-staff for more information. Interviews will be provisionally scheduled for early January 2027 For informal queries about the post, please contact Professor Franca Fraternali (f.fraternali@ucl.ac.uk). For queries about the application process, or to request reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for the job online, please contact the FLS HR team on fls.hr@ucl.ac.uk About you: You will have a PhD or equivalent qualification in a relevant field and an innovative research agenda in AI for Biomolecular Designs and Molecular Interactions, Computational Biology applied to Molecular Biosystems or in a closely related area with clear quantitative and data science strength. You will have experience of research-led teaching at undergraduate and/or postgraduate level, with a strong interest in interdisciplinary working, external engagement and translational impact. You will also show the potential to build collaborative partnerships, attract research funding, and contribute to an inclusive and ambitious academic environment. A full list of essential and desirable criteria is provided in the accompanying person specification at the bottom of this page with further details of how to apply. What we offer: The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know that diversity and inclusion are fundamental drivers of creativity, innovation, and excellence, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world's talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong, thrive, and reach our full potential. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian, and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women’ Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48789": {
      "title": "Maintenance Operative (CHP)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Estates, Facilities, Accommodation and Safety; London; £36,433-£41,833; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00322 About us: UCL is a radically different university. Founded in 1826 in the heart of London, we were the first university in England to welcome students of any religion and the first to welcome women to university education. Today UCL has over 50,000 students and more than 18,000 staff and is one of the world’s top universities. We are ranked second in the UK for research power and first in London (REF 2021). UCL Estates plays a vital role in supporting this community, managing a large and complex estate that underpins world-leading research, teaching and public engagement. The Maintenance Operations team ensures that buildings, systems and infrastructure are safe, reliable and operating effectively across the university. About the role: We are looking for a Maintenance Operative specialising in Combined Heat and Power (CHP) and steam system operation to join our Estates Maintenance Operations team. This is a hands-on technical role focused on the delivery of both planned and reactive maintenance across critical plant and infrastructure. You will work on systems including CHP plant, district heating, steam and hot water systems, as well as associated controls and building services equipment. A BOAS qualification would be desirable. You will be responsible for diagnosing faults, responding to breakdowns and carrying out repairs to ensure minimal disruption to services. The role will involve working across multiple disciplines, including mechanical, electrical and plumbing maintenance, supporting the safe and efficient operation of a complex estate. You will also be involved in planned maintenance programmes, testing and inspection activities, and preparing plant and equipment for statutory compliance. Working closely with colleagues, supervisors and contractors, you will play a key role in maintaining high standards of safety, performance and customer service. About you: You will have completed a recognised apprenticeship or equivalent training in a mechanical and or electrical discipline, with practical experience working in maintenance operations, ideally within a complex environment. You will have strong fault-finding skills and be confident working with plant, equipment and systems such as CHP, heating, and associated controls. You are comfortable interpreting drawings, manuals and system data, including using building management systems to monitor and diagnose issues. You will be a collaborative team player who can also work independently, managing your own workload and responding effectively to changing priorities. You take a methodical approach to your work, maintaining high standards of accuracy, safety and compliance at all times. Strong communication skills are important in this role. You will be able to engage effectively with colleagues, contractors and building users, providing clear updates and maintaining a strong focus on customer service. You will also bring a proactive and flexible approach, with the willingness to work across different sites and adapt to the needs of a busy operational environment, always contributing positively to the wider team. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49071": {
      "title": "Management Accountant (12- Month Secondment)\n\nInternal Applicants only",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Finance; London; £56,431-£66,437​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11324 About us: Are you an experienced finance professional looking to broaden your strategic financial management experience in a complex and research-intensive environment? The UCL Cancer Institute is seeking a proactive and collaborative Management Accountant to join its Professional Services team on a 12-month secondment. This role offers an excellent opportunity to work at the heart of one of the world's leading cancer research institutes, supporting senior decision-making and contributing to the effective financial management of a dynamic and diverse organisation. The UCL Cancer Institute is the hub for cancer research at UCL and brings together world-leading scientists and clinicians working to improve outcomes for cancer patients through innovative research and collaboration. The Institute has strong links with UCL's partner hospitals and includes the Cancer Research UK & UCL Cancer Trials Centre, one of the largest cancer trials centres in the UK. About the role: Reporting to the Institute Manager, the Management Accountant will play a key role in supporting the financial management of the UCL Cancer Institute. Working closely with senior leaders, budget holders and colleagues across UCL Finance, you will provide high-quality financial advice, management information and business planning support. Responsibilities include leading the Institute's budgeting, forecasting and long-term financial planning processes, monitoring income and expenditure, and producing clear financial reports to support decision-making. You will provide financial advice to academic and professional services colleagues, support business case development and financial modelling, and ensure compliance with UCL financial regulations and procedures. The role is also responsible for managing month-end and year-end processes, maintaining robust financial controls, and supporting the effective management of a diverse portfolio of accounts. In addition, you will line manage and develop two Finance Administrators, helping to ensure a responsive and high-performing finance service. This is an excellent opportunity for a proactive finance professional to develop their experience in a complex and dynamic environment while contributing to the delivery of world-leading cancer research. You will also play an important role in identifying opportunities for process improvement and enhancing financial services across the Institute. This vacancy is available as an internal secondment opportunity. Applicants interested in applying on a secondment basis should discuss the opportunity with their line manager and obtain agreement in principle before submitting an application. Any appointment will be subject to formal approval from the individual's substantive department About you: We are seeking a qualified or part-qualified finance professional with experience of budgeting, forecasting and financial reporting in a complex organisation. You will have strong analytical, organisational and communication skills, with the ability to build effective relationships and explain financial information clearly to a wide range of stakeholders. You will be customer-focused, proactive and detail-oriented, with experience of supporting or developing staff and managing competing priorities. Experience within Higher Education and familiarity with Oracle, MyFinance or Axiom planning tools would be advantageous. What we offer: UCL is one of the world's leading universities and offers staff an exceptional working environment, including: Opportunities for professional development and career progression. Generous annual leave entitlement. Competitive pension scheme. Family-friendly policies. Employee wellbeing support. Flexible and hybrid working arrangements where operationally appropriate. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48889": {
      "title": "Moving Image Practice 1: Camera, Sound and Edit Postgraduate Teaching Assistant (Arts and Sciences)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; UCL East; £36,433-£42,891​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03305 About us: University College London (UCL) is a multi-faculty college of the University of London with a population of over 52,000 students, from more than 150 different countries. Degree programmes are provided in Arts and Humanities, Social and Historical Sciences, Education, Architecture, Building, Environmental Design and Planning, Laws, Life Sciences and Clinical Sciences (including Medicine), Mathematical and Physical Sciences, and Engineering Sciences. UCL is among the world’s top universities, as reflected in performance in a range of rankings and tables, and is ranked 9th in the world, 5th in Europe and 2nd in London in the QS World University Rankings 2025. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and minority ethnic backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ people, and women. The UCL Arts and Sciences (UASc) department is the home for the Bachelor of Arts and Sciences (BASc) which is the UK's leading interdisciplinary degree, covering arts, social sciences and sciences degree. BASc combines the breadth of interdisciplinary study across the arts and sciences with the opportunity to specialise in one or two key subject areas. There is also postgraduate taught sister programme to UCL’s Bachelor of Arts and Sciences (BASc), the new MASc in Creative Health which combines the breadth of interdisciplinary study across the arts and sciences, with the opportunity to specialise in the role of non-clinical, community-led, socially engaged approaches to public health. UCL Arts and Sciences also launched a new undergraduate degree, the Bachelor in Creative Arts and Humanities (BACAH) , with its first intake 2023/24. This degree is homed at UCL East and brings together critical humanities thinking with practices of writing, performance and moving images. BACAH further develops the UASc departments’ interdisciplinarity by bringing together critical humanities thinking with critical practice. The Department has a doctoral programme in Interdisciplinary Societies, Arts and Sciences which accepted its first students in the 2023/24 academic year and is aiming to grow to 20 doctoral students. About the role: The Department of Arts and Sciences wishes to make an appointment for a Postgraduate Teaching Assistant to teach on the BASC0062 Moving Image Practice 1: Camera, Sound and Edit module running in Term 2 of the 2026/27 academic year. This appointment is required to work on the BA Creative Arts and Humanities degree based at the UCL East campus in Stratford. The purpose of this job is to support teaching and learning in our BASC-coded modules, working with the academic modules leads through the delivery of small group teaching for students taking this module in the Arts and Sciences Department. Main duties of the post are tutorial class teaching, regular meetings with the course lecturer, provision of feedback to students, attendance reporting, coursework assessment and examination marking. PGTAs will be given training in support of their roles. PGTAs are expected to be able to commit be present on the UCL East campus for the full duration of the term and during the examination period as required. We will not be offering any online seminar classes in 2026/27. This role does not meet the eligibility requirements for a Skilled Worker Visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require right to work in the UK to carry out this role. About you: Applicants must be in at least the second year of their PhD studies at the start of any contract with us (4 January 2027). PhD students from outside UCL can also apply, but priority for appointments will go to current UCL PhD students. Please check the job description and person specification for additional knowledge or experience required for the module. All contracts are inclusive of teaching hours, preparation hours, office hours and marking. Previous experience of undergraduate teaching is desirable. Please note that we can only consider applications from students still completing their PhD and registered as a student at the time a contract would begin (see date above). Applicants with a completed and awarded PhD are not eligible to apply. The deadline to apply is 23.59 (BST) on Sunday 25 October 2026. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, need reasonable adjustments, a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact Cristy Meadows, Head of Operations, UCL Arts and Sciences at uasc-operations@ucl.ac.uk . What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49079": {
      "title": "Notarial Practice Private International Law Convenor",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03312 About us: UCL’s Faculty of Laws is a dynamic, highly respected and friendly community, which enjoys global recognition for the quality of its teaching and research. For almost 200 years, UCL Laws has been one of the leading centres of legal education in the world. Recognised as offering an outstanding educational experience to our students, we combine a strong theoretical foundation in the law with practical teaching from world-leading academics and practitioners. About the role: We are looking to appoint an outstanding candidate to lead the Private International Law module of the Notarial Practice Course, who will work closely with the Course Director in managing teaching and learning materials, maintaining a Moodle page, leading workshops, preparing assessments to monitor students' progress, and marking formative and summative assessments. The module is taught partly through online materials and partly through classes held in London. This role is expected to start in early January 2027, and is fixed-term until 31 July 2027. We anticipate this part-time role (0.2 FTE) will work on average one day per week, but exact working pattern and contracted hours will be agreed with the successful candidate. The successful candidate will be appointed at Grade 8 on UCL's salary scale, and the full-time equivalent salary (£57,570-£67,776 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance) will be pro-rated for part-time appointments (£11,514-£13,555.20 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance). About you: The successful candidate will hold, or be close to completion of, a PhD in Private International Law or a related subject area, with evidence of successful teaching in this area at Higher Education level. As an excellent communicator with strong interpersonal skills, the role holder will be an engaging educator, work effectively as a member of a team, and be able to convey complex ideas clearly and to support students’ learning through constructive and developmental feedback. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy, please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for this vacancy, please click on the 'Apply Now' button. You will be required to upload your CV and supporting statement as part of your application; please note that the online application portal may prompt you to upload other documents, and we ask you not to do so at this stage. The deadline for applications is 25 October 2026, and interviews will likely take place in week(s) commencing 16 or 23 November 2026. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact Dr David Foster, Notarial Practice Course Director at david.foster@ucl.ac.uk. If you have any queries about accessibility or reasonable adjustments please contact lawshr@ucl.ac.uk. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48887": {
      "title": "One Desk HR Manager (ELIGIBLE FOR UCL INTERNAL CANDIDATES ONLY)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05752 About us: The Professional Services (PS) Hub brings together key customer-facing and transactional services across HR, Finance, IT and Business Operations, with a strong focus on delivering high-quality, efficient services and an excellent customer experience. Within the Hub, One Desk provides a single point of contact for UCL students, staff, suppliers and partners, responding to thousands of enquiries every day through telephone, email, live chat and other digital channels. Our team provides timely, accurate and helpful advice while continually looking for ways to improve our services and the customer journey. About the role: As One Desk Manager, you will provide operational and people leadership to Team Leaders, Senior Advisors, Advisors and Assistants, ensuring the team is well supported, appropriately resourced and equipped to deliver an exceptional customer experience. You will be responsible for monitoring and improving performance against agreed SLAs and KPIs, using data and customer insight to identify opportunities for improvement. You will oversee quality monitoring across calls, chats and tickets, providing coaching and feedback to support high standards of service. If you have any queries about the role or the application process please contact Passant Khalifa (Senior Recruiter) via p.khalifa@ucl.ac.uk About you: We are looking for an experienced people manager with a strong customer service focus and the ability to lead teams in a busy, complex environment. You will have experience managing a team that provides high-quality advice and guidance, alongside a good working knowledge of HR legislation and customer-focused practices. You will bring excellent interpersonal, communication and organisational skills, with the ability to prioritise competing demands while maintaining accuracy and service quality. You will be confident using data and KPIs to monitor performance, identify trends and drive continuous improvement. You will also have experience working with HR, Finance and Payroll systems, as well as customer service technologies such as ticketing and live chat platforms. Experience within a large, complex organisation would be advantageous. Above all, you will take pride in delivering an excellent service, sharing knowledge and experience, building strong relationships and making timely, evidence-based decisions. A customer service qualification or training, and Lean Six Sigma or similar certification, would be desirable. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49026": {
      "title": "Operations Administrator",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07831 About us: The Centre for Advanced Spatial Analysis (CASA) is an interdisciplinary research department focusing on the science of cities, applying it to city planning, policy and architecture in the pursuit of making our cities better places to live in. It is part of The Bartlett, UCL's Faculty of the Built Environment. CASA was established in 1995 to lead the development of a science of cities drawing upon methods and ideas in modelling, sensing the urban environment, visualisation and computation. It seeks to examine and offer solutions to the problems of resource efficiency and effective planning, and governance shared by all cities. Further information can be found on our website at https://www.ucl.ac.uk/bartlett/casa/ About the role: The Operations Administrator will work alongside colleagues who provide departmental professional services. The postholder will act as first point of contact for internal and external routine departmental operations queries, and deliver a wide range of administrative duties including, servicing committee meetings, event organisation, conference travel arranging, routine financial administrative support, support with maintaining a safe and compliant office environment, support with departmental facilities, access and sustainability processes, and deliver day-to-day office and space management activities. A proactive, flexible and customer-focussed approach is always required to ensure a high level of service to staff, students and visitors. This is a fixed-term or secondment post available from 11th January to 10th July 2027. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research, Teaching and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Find out about the UCL Ways of Working: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/organisational-development/career-development-and-progression/ways-working. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. For any queries about the role please contact Susan Francis (s.francis@ucl.ac.uk). A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. About you: The successful candidate will be educated to A level standard or have experience working in a similar administrative role in a Higher Education Institution, or similarly complex organisation. The successful candidate should also demonstrate excellent administrative skills and a proactive approach to providing a comprehensive administrative service. They should be able to take direction and to work independently, have excellent communication skills and attention to detail, as well as a have high level organisational and time management skills. We are looking for someone who takes initiative and is willing to take on new tasks, who is a problem solver and who works well in a team. For full list of essential and desirable criteria, please see a job description and person specification at the bottom of this page. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days); Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave; Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE); Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan; Relocation support for eligible posts; On-site nursery and gym; Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay; Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme); Discounted medical insurance. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy (https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/hr-policies-procedures-and-advice/z-policies/prevention-bullying-harassment-and-sexual-misconduct) and Personal Relationships Policy (https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/hr-policies-procedures-and-advice/z-policies/personal-relationships-policy)"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48937": {
      "title": "Operations Administrator - 0.6 FTE",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11346 About us: Join one of the country’s leading Clinical Trials Units. The UCL Institute of Clinical Trials and Methodology brings together five expert research units: UCL Innovative Clinical Trials Unit (InCTU, formerly MRC Clinical Trials Unit at UCL), the Comprehensive Clinical Trials Unit at UCL, the MRC Centre of Research Excellence in Clinical Trial Innovation, and virtual partners:- Cancer Research UK/UCL Cancer Trials Centre, the PRIMENT CTU Across the Institute we design and deliver high-quality clinical trials, meta-analysis and epidemiological studies, across a range of diseases, that inform clinical practice globally. We are committed to improving local, national and global health. Key areas of focus are continuing to deliver innovations in trial methodology to improve outcomes for patients, and training and developing the next generation of trialists. We work with academic collaborators in more than 60 countries worldwide and a significant number of commercial partners. Within the Institute, the Innovative CTU leads research addressing internationally important questions, primarily in infectious diseases, cancer and neurodegenerative diseases. A key goal is to deliver swifter and more effective translation of scientific research into patient and public health benefits. We achieve this by carrying out challenging and innovative studies. We work closely with MRC Centre of research excellence in Clinical Trial Innovation to develop and implement methodological advances in study design, conduct and analysis. InCTU is a UKCRC registered trials unit. We are seeking to recruit an Operations Administrator to support the work of this research unit. About the role: An enthusiastic committed and professional individual is sought for the role of Operations Administrator. You will provide work closely with the Director of the Innovative Clinical Trials Unit as well as two other members of the leadership team. You will work within a multi-disciplinary team and will be expected to provide administrative support across a wide range of core InCTU activities as the need arises, developing their understanding of the research environment in which the post will operate as well as promoting a positive working environment and being a team player. Key Programme tasks will include, organising and minuting meetings and events, monitoring project milestones, assistance with procurement, expense and budgetary management, diary management and travel arrangements as required. This post is funded for 2 years in the first instance. About you: No two days are ever the same in the role we offer. This is a development level role for a highly organised, flexible candidates who is prepared take on a variety of tasks and manage their portfolio of work working directly with Senior Staff. You should have strong communication skills (written and verbal) and be willing to work with personnel across the Unit ranging from Professorial and Clinical down to Trial Managers and operational staff. In return we offer a supportive Team environment and the opportunity to develop your skills and ambitions. Please attach a covering letter, with your CV, explaining how you meet the criteria in the person specification that is included in the Job Description. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, to arrange an informal discussion regarding the post please contact Nav Kaur (n.kaur@ucl.ac.uk) ICTM HR (ictm.hr@ucl.ac.uk) What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: · 41 Days holiday pro rata (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) · Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme · Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) · Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan · Immigration loan · Relocation scheme for certain posts · On-Site nursery · On-site gym · Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay · Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service · Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48835": {
      "title": "PA & Administrative Assistant to Vice-Deans and DESE",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11105 About Us: The Faculty of Medical Sciences is an exciting and dynamic environment driving forward ambitious and challenging plans for future development. This is a pivotal post within the Faculty team and provides an excellent opportunity for the right applicant to develop a career in university administration. We're a recognised world leader in Medical Education, helping students to become leaders in their fields. The faculty is the top in the UK for research power in life and medical sciences (REF 2021) and is ranked sixth in the world for Medicine and number one in London (QS World University Rankings by Subject 2023). The Faculty is dedicated to delivering world class medical education and developing translational research that benefits patients and society in the UK and globally. Bringing together six UCL divisions and institutes the Faculty of Medical Sciences (FMS) offers a unique mix of pioneering research, best in class researchers and clinicians, cutting edge facilities and links with world renowned partner hospitals and institutions. About the Role: The role requires interaction with many people within and external to UCL. A professional attitude, willingness to help, and an ability to remain tactful and assertive are required in order to adequately execute the duties outlined. This permanent post which will be based at the Bloomsbury Campus. Appointment details: The post is graded at GRADE 6, the annual salary for which ranges from £36,433 - £42,891 (pro-rata) per annum inclusive of London Allowance. Application process: A full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Please use the personal statement section to explain how you meet each essential and desirable criteria outlined in the person specification. The advert will close on 18 October 2026 at 23:59 GMT/BST, however we may close the advert early if we receive a high volume of applications. Interviews are expected to take place on Tuesday 3 rd and Wednesday 4 th The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. Contact details: If you have any queries about the role or application process please contact Marion Atkinson ( Marion.Atkinson@ucl.ac.uk ). If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact Deepak Singh ( Deepak.Singh@ucl.ac.uk). About You: An enthusiastic, proactive and resourceful individual is sought for this part-time post. The post holder will provide dedicated executive support, including diary management, arranging meetings and committee servicing to the Faculty Vice-Deans and DESE. The post holder will assume general and specific administrative support duties to progress the operational activities of the FMS Faculty Office, and where appropriate LMS Faculties. You will be educated to A level standard, or NVQ Level IV administration, or have substantial relevant experience. You will have an experience of working in an administrative position, incorporating an EA/PA role, in a large or complex organisation, experience in event management, servicing high-level committees, working groups and similar including meeting preparation as well as minute taking. Experience of organising own work and taking responsibility for meeting targets, drafting emails, letters, papers and presentations. The more extensive list of skills is included within Job Description and Person Specification attached to this job. What We Offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) pro rata Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance The full range of staff benefits can be found here Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. UCLMS holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48784": {
      "title": "PGT Senior Employer Engagement Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-Provost (Education and Student Experience) (B06) Reference: B06-00210 About us: UCL is a global university with a distinctive commitment to innovation, collaboration and positive change. Our community brings together students and staff from around the world, creating an environment where different perspectives, ideas and experiences can thrive. The Higher Education Development & Support Institute (HEDS) brings together UCL Arena and UCL Careers, supporting excellence in education, student experience and graduate outcomes. UCL Careers provides careers education and support to current students and graduates, alongside recruitment solutions and employer engagement activity. UCL School of Management is home to world-leading research and education in business and management, with more than 2,000 students, over half of whom are from outside the UK. The School is currently undertaking an ambitious expansion as it delivers a strategy to double in size over the next five years. This is an exciting opportunity to join the School of Management Careers team and play a key role in developing connections between our postgraduate students, employers and alumni. About the role: As Senior Employer Engagement Officer, you will lead the development of employer and alumni relationships that create valuable opportunities and experiences for postgraduate students across the UCL School of Management. You will build and maintain strong relationships with a diverse range of stakeholders, including employers, alumni, academic colleagues, Programme Directors, senior managers and professional services teams. You will use your understanding of stakeholder needs, labour market insight and student feedback to identify opportunities, secure engagement and develop activities that deliver meaningful outcomes. A key part of the role will be designing and delivering a programme of employer and alumni activity, which could include employer-led events, skills workshops, mentoring opportunities, internships and other experiences that connect students with the world of work. You will work collaboratively with colleagues and external partners to shape content, agree objectives and ensure activities are delivered to a high standard. You will also take responsibility for project managing activity from initial idea through to delivery and evaluation. This will involve coordinating stakeholders, managing competing priorities, monitoring engagement and using qualitative and quantitative feedback to assess impact and identify opportunities for improvement. Working as part of the wider School of Management Careers team, you will contribute to the development of the School's Employer Engagement Strategy, share labour market intelligence and help strengthen connections across the School and wider UCL Careers community. The role offers an opportunity for someone who enjoys working with people, building partnerships and turning ideas into well-delivered activity. You will have scope to develop new approaches, respond to changing employer and student needs, and contribute to the continued development of careers provision within a growing Business School. Please note this role is offered on a full-time basis with hybrid working available. As a student-facing role, attendance on campus (Canary Wharf School of Management) is required for currently a minimum of one day per week during term-time but may be more according to workload and business need. Job Ref: B06-00210 Closing Date: 25-10-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) Interviews: Interview are likely to take place onsite at our Canary Wharf campus from 20th November. About you: We are looking for a collaborative and proactive professional with experience of building and managing effective relationships with a wide range of stakeholders. You will be confident engaging with people at different levels and able to establish credibility, understand different perspectives and influence others to achieve shared outcomes. You will have experience of working in a stakeholder-facing, partnership, engagement or professional services environment, with the ability to manage multiple relationships and projects simultaneously. You will be comfortable taking an idea from initial concept through to delivery, coordinating others, managing deadlines and evaluating the impact of your work. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills are essential, alongside the confidence to present, facilitate discussions and engage stakeholders both individually and in group settings. You will be able to use evidence and data to inform your approach, make recommendations and identify opportunities for continuous improvement. Knowledge or experience of graduate recruitment, employer engagement, careers or relevant employment sectors such as professional services, consulting, technology, financial services, entrepreneurship or innovation would be valuable. We would also welcome applications from individuals who have developed strong stakeholder management and partnership experience in another professional services, higher education or business environment. You will be organised, adaptable and comfortable working in a busy environment where priorities can change. Most importantly, you will be committed to providing a responsive, high-quality service and to developing inclusive opportunities and positive outcomes for our students. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49072": {
      "title": "Post Graduate Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £36,433-£41,833; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07405 About us: The Centre for Advanced Spatial Analysis is offering a range of teaching opportunities for postgraduate research students to assist on its Masters’ level courses as Post Graduate Teaching Assistants. Both full and part time students are eligible to apply. Please see the Job Description, at the bottom of this page, for full details. Students may apply to assist on any of the modules, and only one application is required. Please specify which module/s you are applying for when completing application form. About the role: Post holders can be expected to take on some or all of the following duties: • Teaching assistance through the facilitation of seminars and/or workshops; • Class preparation and meeting with lecturers/module organisers; • After-class contact time with students, including time-tabled office hours; • Marking students' work; • Giving feedback to students. Each post is unique and the duties will vary depending on arrangements of teaching in each module. Please note that PGTA roles are dependent on student numbers and these are to be confirmed for individual modules. In the event of student numbers being lower than expected, the work may be not required or reduced. PGTAs will be notified if work is withdrawn on this basis. Please note that this is an internal opportunity for UCL students. Only if there is no suitable UCL PhD student available, applications from external PhD students at other universities would be considered. For any queries about the role or about the application process please contact Sandra Starosta (s.starosta@ucl.ac.uk). To apply please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below and complete application form. Please remember to attach your CV. About you: You must be registered as a PhD student at the start of the academic year 2026/2027 to be eligible to apply. Any PGR student wishing to undertake teaching or assessing responsibilities at UCL must have attended the Arena One: Gateway workshop. You must have the right to work in the UK. Other requirements are: • Working towards a postgraduate research degree (PhD/EngD). • A desire to gain teaching experience in a built environment discipline. • Disciplinary knowledge and, if applicable, specialist skills of the subject area of the programme unit/module. • Ability to communicate clearly, both orally and in writing, with students and staff. • Excellent organisational and time-management skills. • Ability to be flexible in terms of research workload and other commitments. • Ability to work independently for short periods and as part of a team, recognising when advice / input needs to be sought. • Available for the three hour mandatory UCL Arena One Gateway Workshop, if not previously attended. • Commitment to UCL’s policy of equal opportunities and the ability to work harmoniously with colleagues and students of all cultures and backgrounds. • The ability to meet deadlines. What we offer: The appointments will be made on UCL’s salary structure at grade 6. PGTAs will be paid for training, preparation time, attending lectures and meetings, office hours and marking. The estimated total teaching hours across each module is listed in the Job Description, at the bottom of this page. Payments will be spread evenly for the duration of each module. Hours of work vary across modules. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more about the benefits on offer. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy (https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/hr-policies-procedures-and-advice/z-policies/prevention-bullying-harassment-and-sexual-misconduct) and Personal Relationships Policy (https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/hr-policies-procedures-and-advice/z-policies/personal-relationships-policy)"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48954": {
      "title": "Post Graduate Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £36,433-£41,833; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-06449 About us: About UCL & the Faculty of Engineering Join a top-tier global university ranked #1 in the UK for research strength, home to 30 Nobel laureates and a vibrant community from over 150 nationalities. As part of the UCL Faculty of Engineering - the UK’s largest and second most impactful in Europe - you will be embedded in an environment defined by academic excellence, world-class industry partnerships, and state-of-the-art facilities across Central London and the Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park. About the Department (CEGE) Ranked in the top 50 worldwide, the Department of Civil, Environmental & Geomatic Engineering delivers pioneering, people-focus research and teaching. Building on a 200-year legacy of innovation, our multidisciplinary team tackles real-world global challenges alongside industrial partners. We thrive on diversity, sector-leading inclusivity, and cutting-edge experimentation in our world-class living laboratories, including PEARL and CAVE. Driven by engineering for people, breaking boundaries across disciplines, empowering a diverse community, and continuing a legacy of pioneering leadership. Learn more about our Values. About the role: We seek Postgraduate Teaching Assistants to support teaching and learning activities for the upcoming academic year 2026-2027. As a PGTA, you will work closely with academic module leads to support the delivery of teaching for students enrolled in modules within the Civil, Environmental & Geomatic Engineering Department (CEGE). For more details, please review the Job descriptions for the module(s) you are interested in in the attachment below. Further information on each module is also available via UCL’s Module Catalogue . This role is offered on a 15 guaranteed hours contract for the 2026-2027 academic year. UCL PhD students will have priority, but students from other universities may also apply, subject to agreement with their Supervisor. PGTAs are employed at Grade 6 on the UCL non-clinical salary scale , on an hourly basis, in line with the UCL PGTA Code of Practice . About you: In order to be eligible, you need to: Be a registered Post Graduate Researcher (PGR) student at UCL or another UK university, or have recently completed a PGR degree; Have the right to work in the United Kingdom; Have the approval of your primary PhD supervisor. All PGTAs must enroll and complete UCL’s dedicated PGTA training programme, ‘Arena One’. Further in-house induction and informal training will be provided. Further Information: For more information about your experience as a PGTA, please visit : Getting started as a PGTA at UCL For queries about the application process, please contact the UCL CEGE HR team at cege-hr@ucl.ac.uk . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. If we receive many applications, we may close the vacancy before the published closing date.We will keep the advertisement open for at least two weeks and recommend submitting your application early. For more information about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion, please read further."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48731": {
      "title": "Post Graduate Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £36,433-£41,833; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07534 About us: The Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering at UCL is one of the UK’s largest and most diverse departments. We are proud to host internationally renowned research groups across multiple sites, with staff and students working at the intersection of physics, engineering, medicine, physiology, computer science, and mathematics. As a global leader in Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering research and education, our department fosters close collaborations with clinicians, practitioners, and scientists in major teaching hospitals. This enables our researchers to remain at the forefront of healthcare engineering and allows our students to learn about the latest innovations directly from leading experts in the field. Our mission is to advance cutting-edge research that develops new technologies and methods for diagnosing, treating, and managing diseases through medical imaging, physiological monitoring, and implantable devices. Additionally, we offer a wide range of undergraduate and postgraduate degree programs. The department holds an Athena Swan Bronze Award, recognizing our ongoing commitment to supporting and advancing women’s careers in STEM (Science, Technology, Engineering, and Mathematics). About the role: We seek Postgraduate Teaching Assistants to support teaching and learning activities for the upcoming academic session. The roles are varied and may include: • Facilitating laboratory practical sessions • Supporting small-group project work and tutorials • Assisting with marking and providing feedback • Helping to prepare teaching materials Specific responsibilities will vary depending on the assigned module(s) and departmental needs. About you: We invite applications from highly motivated individuals who are current or prospective doctoral (PhD) students at UCL. Students must be enrolled on an MPhil programme to be eligible to apply. Applicants must: • Be registered UCL research students at the time of appointment. • Have the right to work in the UK. Some roles do not require specialist skills, and training can always be provided, however some of the skills (though not all) that we are looking for are listed below – having a range of them increases the chance of being appointed: • Analogue Electronics to 1st year undergrad level and/or degree level electrical engineering • Experience using CAD software (Solidworks, Fusion 360) and experience of 3D printer use • Experience in technical drawing • Experience of measuring bio-impedance signals • Matlab and/or Simulink experience. • Arduino type programming experience • Proficiency in Python programming • Design and DIY skills • Physics background with knowledge of optics, waves, thermodynamics, solid state physics, nuclear medicine • Mechanical Engineering background – in particular, knowledge of materials and dynamics, using an Instron machine Before confirming your appointment, you must provide valid right-to-work documentation, such as a current passport and any applicable visa (sharecode). What we offer: PGTA roles are offered under an average-hour contract, meaning your total agreed hours will be evenly distributed over the duration of the contract. This arrangement ensures that you receive consistent, prorated monthly payments throughout your employment period. All new PGTAs will start at the first spinal point of Grade 6. PGTAs with prior experience from the previous academic session will advance by one spinal point. At UCL, employed PGTAs are entitled to sick leave and pay, as set out in the Conditions of Service for Research, Teaching and Professional Services Staff . PGTAs qualify for sick pay, which is automatically included in their normal monthly salary as they are on an Averaged Hours contract. Additionally, annual leave pay is also included automatically. HR Services will automatically calculate the payment due for any leave accrued for the hours worked monthly for Averaged Hours contracts. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London's Global University, we recognize that diversity enhances creativity and innovation.We strive for our community to reflect the diversity of global talent. We are committed to providing equal opportunities, fairness, and inclusion, ensuring everyone feels they belong here.Therefore, we particularly encourage applications from underrepresented candidates in UCL's workforce, including individuals from Black, Asian, and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled individuals, and those from the LGBTQI+ community. If we receive many applications, we may close the vacancy before the published closing date.We will keep the advertisement open for at least two weeks and recommend submitting your application early."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48657": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03281 About us: The Department of Greek and Latin at UCL is looking to appoint a Postdoctoral Research Fellow in Papyrology to work on the AHRC-funded project Christian Books from Oxyrhynchus . The appointment is expected to commence on 1 December 2026 and will end on 31 October 2030 . The post is part-time at 0.8 FTE . About the role: This Postdoctoral Research Fellowship forms part of the AHRC-funded project Christian Books from Oxyrhynchus , led at UCL by N. Gonis in collaboration with colleagues at the University of Birmingham and the University of Oxford. The project investigates unpublished Oxyrhynchus papyri containing fragments of the Septuagint, the New Testament, and other early Christian literature, with the aim of advancing our understanding of the history of these texts and providing new insights into the production and use of Christian books in Roman and late antique Egypt. The Research Fellow will contribute to this wider programme of research by working on papyri containing fragments of the Septuagint. Main purpose of the job, duties, and responsibilities To contribute to the research programme of Christian Books from Oxyrhynchus under the direction of the Project Lead and Co-leads. To carry out high-quality independent research. To edit unpublished Oxyrhynchus papyri containing fragments of the Septuagint. To contribute to the revision of editions by other scholars. To participate in regular project meetings. To assist in organising project events. To help maximise the scholarly impact and visibility of the project. To participate in research-support and outreach activities as directed by the line manager. In addition to the above the post-holder is expected to: Actively follow UCL policies, including those relating to equal opportunities, and give consideration within their role to how they can advance equality of opportunity and foster good relations between people who share a relevant protected characteristic and those who do not share it. Maintain an awareness and observation of Fire and Health and Safety Regulations. Carry out their duties in a resource-efficient manner and actively support UCL’s Sustainability Strategy, policies, and objectives within the remit of their role. Carry out any such other duties as are within the scope, spirit and purpose of the post as requested by the line manager. About you: You will have completed a PhD in a relevant field and possess specialist expertise in Greek literary papyrology. You should have an excellent command of Hellenistic Greek, the ability to decipher fragmentary and damaged papyrus texts, and a reading knowledge of modern scholarly languages relevant to the discipline. Familiarity with the society and culture of late Roman Egypt, the textual history of the Greek Bible, Biblical Hebrew, and/or other languages relevant to Hebrew Bible scholarship would be advantageous. You should also possess excellent written and interpersonal communication skills in English, strong attention to detail, and the ability to work both independently and collaboratively within a research environment. Further requirements can be found in the job description. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit UCL rewards and benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48426": {
      "title": "Principal Research Infrastructure Developer (Lead Cyber Security Developer)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £68,284-£78,386​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05695 About us: UCL is a multi-disciplinary university with a population of over 13,000 staff and 42,000 students from 150 different countries. For more information see www.ucl.ac.uk/about . The UCL Centre for Advanced Research Computing (ARC) is UCL’s institute for infrastructure and innovation in digital research. We are an innovative hybrid: a professional services department that delivers reliable and secure infrastructure and services to UCL research groups, and a laboratory for research and innovation. We are a home for the research technology professionals - research software engineers, HPC systems engineers, dev-ops specialists, data scientists and data stewards - who support and collaborate in the delivery of UCL research. Further information about working in ARC can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/arc . About the role: We are seeking a highly motivated cyber security expert with experience (or a strong interest) in Research Computing/HPC environments. This post will help develop secure research infrastructure alongside providing support and guidance on cyber security best practice to staff across our Research Infrastructure, Data and Software Engineering domains. . You’ll play a vital role in not only shaping our policies and processes, but developing tooling to help secure our hardware and software. Salary range is £68,284 to £78,386 (including London Allowance) depending on experience. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 20% on site. Key responsibilities: Research and deploy cyber security tooling Liaise with UCL’s central Information Security Group to ensure ARC’s infrastructure meets central requirements Audit infrastructure deployments to ensure compliance with security requirements Provide guidance and recommendations on security best practice to all teams across ARC Develop tooling and processes to ensure new platforms comply with ISO 27001 and CAF Contribute to documentation, knowledge sharing, and continuous service improvement About you: The successful candidate will need to demonstrate the following criteria. Essential: Experience working in Linux environments Background in cyber security Background in research computing, HPC, or a related technical field Strong problem-solving skills Ability to communicate security recommendations effectively to technical members of staff who aren’t security experts Experience auditing/monitoring cyber security compliance Desirable: Experience with Kubernetes Experience with DevOps tooling (Terraform, GitHub Actions) Familiarity with private/public cloud platforms Experience with secrets management in a large organisation Please see the attached job description and person specification for full details. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or application process, please contact Mrs. Jodie Jackson, via e-mail (jodie.jackson@ucl.ac.uk). What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48849": {
      "title": "Project Manager (ASAP)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11207 About us: The Department of Clinical and Movement Neurosciences at UCL Queen Square Institute of Neurology is a world-leading centre for research into movement, movement disorders and neurological disease. Bringing together clinical and basic neuroscience, the Department uses advanced imaging, computational, cellular and molecular approaches to understand brain function and develop new approaches to neurological disorders. With strong links across UCL, the National Hospital for Neurology and Neurosurgery and the Royal Free London, we offer a highly collaborative, multidisciplinary research environment with excellent facilities and opportunities for impactful translational research. About the role: We are seeking a Project Manager to join a newly funded international research programme supported by Aligning Science Across Parkinson’s (ASAP). The programme brings together researchers from five leading institutions: the MRC Laboratory of Molecular Biology, Van Andel Institute, University College London, the University of Cambridge, and the University of Manchester. The project will investigate the structure, composition and biological behaviour of alpha-synuclein filaments across Parkinson’s disease, Parkinson’s disease dementia, dementia with Lewy bodies, multiple system atrophy and related neurodegenerative conditions. Using a range of cutting-edge approaches, including cryo-electron microscopy, mass spectrometry, biochemical assays, cell models, human organoids, and mouse models, the team will explore how different filament structures arise and whether they can drive disease-associated pathology. You will coordinate the day-to-day delivery of this multidisciplinary programme, working closely with researchers across the partner institutions. You will coordinate meetings and milestones, support project reporting and facilitate the exchange of samples, protocols and data between teams. You will also have the opportunity to contribute to research data generation and analysis, as well as the preparation of presentations and scientific manuscripts. This is an exciting opportunity to play a central role in a collaborative, international programme addressing important questions in Parkinson’s disease and related neurodegenerative disorders. The post is available immediately and funded by ASAP to 31 May 2029 in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). Informal enquiries regarding the role can be addressed to Dr Zane Jaunmuktane ( z.jaunmuktane@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You will have a postgraduate qualification and/or research experience in neuroscience, neurodegeneration or a related biomedical discipline and experience of managing and coordinating complex research projects or programmes, with the ability to work effectively across multiple teams and institutions. You will have excellent communication and stakeholder management skills, and be proactive and resourceful, with strong problem-solving skills and the ability to manage competing priorities, monitor progress, and deliver to deadlines. An interest in research and a commitment to supporting high-quality research are essential. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa . What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits; v isit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48805": {
      "title": "Projects Health and Safety Manager",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Estates, Facilities, Accommodation and Safety; London; See advert text; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00375 About us: Our estate is one of the most diverse and complex in the higher education sector, encompassing listed and historically significant buildings, state-of-the-art research facilities, laboratories, teaching spaces, student accommodation and new-build developments. This unique environment presents a range of health and safety challenges and opportunities, making UCL an exceptional place for safety professionals to develop and apply their expertise. The Campus Experience & Infrastructure and Estates Development (CE&I and ED) team is responsible for the strategic planning, development, maintenance and operation of the UCL estate. Together, these functions oversee everything from long-term estate strategy, property and asset management, and capital development programmes through to the day-to-day delivery of facilities and operational services. UCL Safety Services provides expert advice, assurance and leadership to support the university's commitment to a safe and healthy environment for all. We work in partnership with faculties, departments and professional services to embed a positive safety culture, ensuring that health and safety is integrated into decision-making, planning and day-to-day activities across the institution. About the role: As our Projects Health and Safety Manager, you will play a pivotal role in ensuring CDM requirements are embedded throughout the design and construction lifecycle. You will provide expert health and safety leadership across a diverse capital projects programme, including minor works, fit-outs, refurbishments, heritage buildings, infrastructure projects, public realm improvements and major new developments. You will lead on the development and implementation of policies, frameworks and procedures to support duty holders, improve safety performance and champion best practice. You will work collaboratively with project teams, designers, contractors and health and safety colleagues across the organisation to ensure a consistent, high‑quality approach to compliance. Regular site monitoring, audits and reviews will underpin your responsibility to maintain safe, legally compliant working environments. Salary range: £68,284 - £78,386 per annum (including London Allowance), depending on experience, pro-rata for part-time staff. About you: To do this job you'll need: Chartered Member of IOSH (CMIOSH) with evidence of ongoing CPD. NEBOSH Diploma, health and safety degree-level qualification, or equivalent. Extensive, up-to-date knowledge and practical experience of CDM 2015 in construction and project delivery. Experience of implementing and auditing health and safety management systems, ideally aligned to ISO 45001. Up-to-date knowledge of UK health and safety legislation, including CDM 2015, the Building Safety Act 2022, fire safety legislation, and other statutory requirements. Experience of leading incident investigations, including root cause analysis and embedding lessons learned. Excellent stakeholder engagement, communication, and influencing skills. Experience of commissioning or delivering health and safety training and supporting competency development. Confident user of Microsoft 365 and digital reporting tools, including Excel, Word, Outlook, and Power BI. Specialist knowledge of construction and M&E risks, including fire, asbestos, legionella, and gas. Experience of producing clear health and safety performance reports for senior management. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion. Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48934": {
      "title": "Recruitment and Selection Advisor",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Student Well-being and Registry; London; £36,433-£41,833; Vice-Provost (Education and Student Experience) (B06) Reference: B06-00207 About us: UCL is a global university, committed to transforming education, research and innovation to create positive change. Through the Higher Education Development & Support Institute (HEDS), we bring together expertise in education development and careers to enhance the student experience and support students and graduates to achieve their ambitions. UCL Careers provides careers education and guidance to current students, graduates and staff, alongside recruitment solutions for employers. Our core service supports students in navigating the recruitment and selection process, from developing effective CVs and cover letters through to preparing for interviews and securing opportunities in the UK and globally. As part of HEDS, UCL Careers works closely with faculties, departments, employers and other partners to ensure our careers and employability provision reflects the changing graduate labour market and the needs of our diverse student community. About the role: We are looking for a Recruitment & Selection Advisor to join our team in our careers team. This is an excellent opportunity for a careers and employability professional who is passionate about helping students and graduates develop the confidence and skills they need to succeed in the recruitment process. You will provide expert, practical advice to students and graduates applying for internships, graduate roles, jobs and further study. This will include delivering one-to-one appointments, providing constructive feedback on CVs, cover letters, application forms, motivation statements and professional profiles, and helping individuals understand how to tailor their applications effectively to different opportunities. You will also design and deliver engaging small-group workshops on employability and recruitment, including topics such as CV tailoring, cover letter writing and application forms. You will use your knowledge of recruitment and selection to make these sessions practical, interactive and relevant to the needs of UCL students and graduates. A strong understanding of graduate recruitment and selection practices will be important in this role. You will draw on your experience of how employers recruit and assess candidates, alongside current labour market and sector knowledge, to help students understand what employers are looking for and how to navigate recruitment processes successfully. You will work with a diverse student population across UCL's faculties, supporting undergraduates, taught Masters students and research students, including PhD students. You will also contribute to research into recruitment practices and graduate labour market trends, sharing insights with colleagues and incorporating them into student advice, workshops and online careers resources. The role offers the opportunity to collaborate widely across UCL Careers, faculties and departments, as well as with employers and alumni. You may also contribute to careers events, sector-focused activities and wider Careers projects. Please note, the activities in this role follow the main employer recruitment season and therefore in the Autumn term you will be expected to undertake approximately 5 hours of one to one student application advice appointments per day. This will decrease in the Spring and Summer terms in line with student demand. Please note, this is an internal recruitment advert, available to eligible UCL workers. The role offers hybrid working with currently a minimum of 2-days per week in our Bloomsbury offices (subject to change). Please draft your application and include all essential/desirable criteria in your supporting statement detailing how you meet each individual criterion. Job Ref: B06-00207 Closing Date: 28-10-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) Interviews: Interviews are due to take place from 24th November About you: You will have professional experience in careers and employability, graduate recruitment and selection, or a closely related area, with a strong understanding of the challenges students and graduates face when entering the labour market. You will have experience of advising students, graduates or other early-career individuals on a one-to-one basis, with the ability to quickly build rapport and provide clear, specific and constructive feedback. You will be confident discussing applications and helping individuals identify how they can strengthen and tailor their CVs, cover letters and other application materials. You will also have experience of planning and delivering small-group workshops or training sessions on employability, ideally covering areas such as CV writing, CV tailoring, cover letters, applications, interviews or other aspects of graduate recruitment. You will be able to create an engaging and inclusive learning environment that encourages participants to develop their own skills and confidence. Your experience will include an understanding of graduate recruitment and selection, enabling you to provide credible, labour-market-informed advice on what employers are looking for and how recruitment processes operate across different sectors. Experience of engaging with employers, undertaking labour market research or keeping up to date with recruitment trends would be particularly valuable. You will be an excellent communicator who can deliver advice and feedback with diplomacy and tact, including when working with students from diverse backgrounds and those whose first language may not be English. You will be comfortable managing a busy workload, particularly during peak graduate recruitment periods, while maintaining a high standard of service. Above all, you will take a facilitative and educational approach, helping students and graduates to develop the knowledge, skills and confidence to navigate recruitment and selection processes independently. You will be collaborative, organised and committed to delivering an inclusive careers and employability service that makes a meaningful difference to our students and graduates. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48445": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11244 About us: Join the Developmental Diversity (DevDiv) lab, a collaboration between the Department of Clinical, Educational & Health Psychology and the Institute of Cognitive Neuroscience at UCL, the UK’s top-ranked university for research in Psychology, Psychiatry and Neuroscience. We investigate how genetic, neural, cognitive and environmental factors shape social, emotional, and wellbeing outcomes for people with neurodevelopmental conditions. You will join a supportive research environment that works in partnership with individuals, families, clinicians and educators to advance understanding, inform policy and practice, and improve lives. About the role: You will support the CHEER project (Characterising Humour Enjoyment, Expression and Role in children with and without neurodevelopmental conditions associated with intellectual disabilities), funded by the Baily Thomas Charitable Fund. This novel strengths-based study explores what types of humour are understood, enjoyed and used by children with and without fragile X syndrome or autism, and whether humour can support social engagement. Working with Dr Katherine Ellis, Dr Sarah White, Dr Jo Moss and Prof Sophie Scott, you will contribute to and develop expertise in the design, collection, management, analysis and dissemination of research data from children and their families. The advert will close on 16/11/2026 at 23:59 GMT, however we may close applications early if we receive a high volume of applications. Early application submission is recommended. Interview Date(s) are expected to be in the first week of December 2026 A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Salary is £39,807 per annum. UCL does not believe that this role is eligible for sponsorship and cannot provide individual immigration advice. Applicants who believe they may be eligible for a visa for this role should visit https://www.gov.uk/browse/visas-immigration/work-visas to check their individual circumstances. About you: The ideal candidate will hold a master’s in psychology or a related discipline or have equivalent research experience. They will have experience working with children with intellectual disability and their families, collecting research data, and managing research datasets. Candidates should be highly organised, able to work independently and collaboratively, and possess excellent communication skills. A keen interest in developmental psychology, intellectual disability and neurodevelopmental conditions is essential. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Athena Swan Status Statement Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48695": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11274 About us: Our mission is to maximise and advocate for the holistic health of all children, young people and the adults they will become, through world-class research, education and public engagement. The UCL GOS ICH, together with its clinical partner Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children, forms the largest concentration of children’s health research outside North America. The 2024-29 GOS ICH strategy focuses on its five scientific programmes. GOS ICH’s activities include active engagement with children and families, to ensure that our work is relevant and appropriate to their needs. GOS ICH generates the funding for our research by setting out our proposals in high quality applications to public, charitable and industrial funding bodies and disseminates the results of our research by publication in the medical and scientific literature, to clinicians, policy makers and the wider public. The Institute offers world-class education and training across a wide range of teaching and life learning programmes which address the needs of students and professional groups who are interested in and undertaking work relevant to child health. GOS ICH holds an Athena SWAN Charter Gold Award. Please only attach relevant documents to your application (qualifications, cover letters, supporting statements) and avoid attaching large files e.g. research papers, thesis, publications etc. About the role: We are seeking a highly motivated Research Assistant to join the Chatzeli group at the UCL Great Ormond Street Institute of Child Health (GOS ICH). This role offers an exciting opportunity to contribute to a multidisciplinary project funded by the Wellcome Trust, investigating how branching morphogenesis scales across species. The Research Assistant will work closely with the PhD students, collaborators and postdocs in the Chatzeli lab to aid their experiments and allow smooth running of the lab. Working with both mouse and human developing salivary glands, we will uncover how organs grow, branch and pattern themselves over different timescales and sizes. You will be involved in establishing and maintaining organoid and whole organ cultures, imaging whole organs, and supporting the quantitative analysis of branching behaviour using advanced microscopy and computational tools. The role also involves supporting computational analysis of transcriptomic datasets and assisting with the day‑to‑day running of the laboratory and animal maintenance. This project sits at the interface of developmental biology, quantitative imaging, and computational analysis, offering an excellent opportunity for someone interested in interdisciplinary research. The salary offered is £39,807 per annum and is funded for 2 years in the first instance. About you: You will have a degree in a relevant biomedical or life sciences and practical experience in laboratory research (e.g. biomedical science, genetics, biochemistry, biology). You will have experience working in a laboratory setting. Experience with mouse models, organoids, primary tissue culture and imaging is advantageous. A strong interest in developmental biology, quantitative imaging and computational analysis will be advantageous for this role. Excellent organisational skills and attention to detail are crucial, along with enthusiasm, curiosity and teamwork. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48287": {
      "title": "Research Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11265 About us: The mission of the UCL GOS Institute of Child Health is to maximise and advocate for the holistic health of all children, young people and the adults they will become, through world-class research, education and public engagement. The UCL GOS ICH, together with its clinical partner Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children, forms the largest concentration of children’s health research outside North America. The 2024-29 GOS ICH strategy focuses on its five scientific programmes. GOS ICH’s activities include active engagement with children and families, to ensure that our work is relevant and appropriate to their needs. GOS ICH generates the funding for our research by setting out our proposals in high quality applications to public, charitable and industrial funding bodies and disseminates the results of our research by publication in the medical and scientific literature, to clinicians, policy makers and the wider public. The Institute offers world-class education and training across a wide range of teaching and life learning programmes which address the needs of students and professional groups who are interested in and undertaking work relevant to child health. GOS ICH holds an Athena SWAN Charter Gold Award. About the role: We are seeking Research Assistant to contribute to an experimental programme investigating skeletal muscle responses to mechanical and metabolic stress. The postholder will be responsible for conducting and supporting animal studies including surgical procedures, animal monitoring, endpoint assessments, tissue collection and processing. The role will also involve a range of histological and molecular analyses, including tissue sectioning and staining, RT-qPCR, ELISA and RNAscope. The postholder will analyse experimental data and undertake statistical analyses under the supervision of the research team. Post is available for one year in the first instance from 1/12/26. About you: The successful candidate will have an MSc in a relevant biological or biomedical science and experience of working in a research laboratory. Previous experience of animal experimentation, particularly mouse handling and tissue collection, and/or molecular or histological techniques would be highly desirable. You will be organised, reliable and meticulous, with the ability to follow experimental protocols accurately and maintain high standards of record keeping and data quality. You will be able to work independently while also contributing effectively as part of a multidisciplinary research team. A willingness to learn new techniques, good analytical skills and a genuine interest in skeletal muscle biology and experimental research are essential. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact Madhur Sharma on ich.hr@ucl.ac.uk quoting job reference"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48843": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - Clinical & Affective Neuroscience",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11341 About us: UCL (www.ucl.ac.uk) is a welcoming university situated at the heart of London. UCL is a top-ranked university in the UK for research in psychology; psychiatry & neuroscience. UCL’s Institute of Cognitive Neuroscience is an inter-disciplinary research institute, bringing together different disciplines with common interests in the human mind and brain. The ICN has over 120 staff, including a vibrant body of postdoctoral and postgraduate (PhD) researchers, plus clinical fellows, organised into distinct research groups. The post-holder will be part of the Clinical and Affective Neuroscience Group About the role: This is an exciting opportunity to join a team led by Prof. Sarah Garfinkel and Prof. Camilla Nord (University of Cambridge). We are recruiting a research assistant to contribute to the running of a clinical trial. The post-holder will be responsible for conducting behavioural, interoceptive and emotion-based tasks and will contribute to the recruitment, tracking and retention of participants undergoing a mindfulness- and decentring-based intervention for depression and anxiety. This work aims to understand the interoceptive mechanisms that help us perceive, understand and regulate our emotions. About you: We are looking for an RA with a background in psychology and/or cognitive neuroscience, with experience in behavioural and psychophysiological testing and in clinical research and/or working on a clinical trial. Ability to code (python/R) and experience working with individuals with mental health conditions (anxiety/depression). Someone who enjoys teamwork, highly organised, self-directed, interested in the biological basis of emotion. Valid right to work in the UK covering at least one year (we are unable to sponsor a visa for this post). What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48812": {
      "title": "Research Assistant in Neuroimaging",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11246 About us: The Department of Imaging Neuroscience is an internationally recognised centre for research into the human brain in health and disease. Home to the Functional Imaging Laboratory (FIL), the Department combines cutting-edge neuroimaging technologies, including 7 T and 3 T MRI, MEG and wearable OPM systems, with world-leading research in cognitive neuroscience. The Department hosts major collaborative research programmes and works closely with clinicians, scientists and researchers across UCL and internationally to advance understanding of brain function and develop new approaches to the diagnosis and treatment of neurological and psychiatric disorders. About the role: We are seeking a Research Assistant to support high-quality human neuroimaging research across the Department’s range of imaging platforms. You will contribute to the safe and effective collection of data across a variety of cognitive neuroscience studies, with a particular focus on supporting study participants and the safe operation of the MRI environment. Your responsibilities will include consenting study participants, operating neuroimaging equipment following appropriate training, managing and curating data from imaging sessions, carrying out routine equipment checks and supporting quality assurance procedures. You will work closely with researchers and colleagues across the Department to ensure that data are collected accurately, safely and to a high standard. This role offers an excellent opportunity to develop practical experience in human neuroimaging within a world-renowned research environment. You will receive training in the use of the Department’s scanners and related procedures and gain experience across a range of neuroimaging platforms and research studies. The post is available immediately and is funded for two years in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). Informal enquiries regarding the role can be addressed to Daniel Bates (email: daniel.bates@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You will have an Honours degree in neuroscience, psychology, radiography, physics, engineering or a related discipline, together with hands-on experience of working with a neuroimaging modality, such as MRI, MEG or EEG. You will have an understanding of scientific research and MRI safety principles, as well as experience of working with databases and a strong working knowledge of data protection and GDPR. You will have excellent interpersonal, communication and IT skills and be able to work effectively and cooperatively within a multidisciplinary research environment. You will be a confident problem-solver who can learn new skills quickly, work resourcefully and use your own initiative while maintaining a high level of care and consideration for research participants. What we offer: Starting salary offered in the range £39,807 - £42,891 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Gold award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48978": {
      "title": "Research Assistant, Early Psychosis Informatics into Care (EPICare)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11209 About us: The UCL Division of Psychiatry, located within the Faculty of Brain Sciences, is a world-renowned centre of excellence in mental health research. Our mission is to improve mental health through cutting-edge research and education. By leveraging insights from basic science, we address clinical problems with the aim of improving patient outcomes and public health. Moreover, we take pride in delivering innovative teaching that is directly relevant to clinical practice and informed by our research. Join our team and help us drive forward the field of mental health research and education The EPICare programme is a large NIHR- and Mental Health Mission–funded study to develop, implement and evaluate the first national digital psychosis registry and NHS Trust-based clinician dashboard in Early Intervention in Psychosis (EIP) services across England. The programme aims to provide timely insights into EIP service care, and patient outcomes to support clinical decision-making and improvements in EIP care. EPICare is organised across seven work packages spanning scientific evaluation, programme management, public and patient involvement, data integrity and research capacity development. We are seeking a Research Assistant to support activity during the evaluation stage of EPICare (work packages 5 and 6) which is supported by an NIHR Programme Grant for Applied Research. The post is funded at Grade 6B for 12 months at 0.4 FTE as part of this four-year NIHR Programme Grant. The programme will assess how accurately and reliably demographic, treatment and clinical outcome data are captured in the digital registry and examine the clinical effectiveness of the registry by investigating variations in psychosis incidence, inequalities in care, and the real-world effectiveness of treatments on patient outcomes. It will also evaluate whether the clinical dashboard improves data quality and adherence to NICE standards, and whether both the registry and dashboard are cost-effective. Within this quantitative research support role, the research assistant will lead data quality, integrity and management activities with the support of the Programme Manager, Chief Investigator and the investigator team, including preparing large datasets for analysis and developing and implementing data management procedures to support reproducible, high-quality analyses. The postholder will also assist in delivering EPICare platform training for clinical staff and provide administrative support to the Programme Manager. The role is based at UCL with occasional travel to study sites. This post suits a candidate with strong data management and organisational skills who is motivated to contribute to high-quality applied mental health research. For more information about EPICare, please visit: www.epicaremh.org About the role: Duties and responsibilities The Research Assistant will support the management of the EPICare registry dataset, working closely with the Programme Manager (Melanie Lafanechere), Chief Investigator (Prof. James Kirkbride) and wider research team. They will clean, process and prepare data for analysis; collaborate with team members to resolve data quality issues; be involved in the development, implementation and maintenance of data management documentation; and support programme evaluation activities. The postholder will also support the development of EPICare platform training materials and delivery of training to clinical staff, providing support to stakeholders across the participating NHS pilot Trusts. Additionally, the Research Assistant will provide administrative support to the Programme manager and EPICare team, coordinating meetings and communicating with multiple stakeholders. The postholder will be expected to communicate with stakeholders across academic, clinical, digital, governance and lived experience groups to help ensure the smooth running of the EPICare programme. Appointment details: The post is graded at Grade 6B, with an annual salary ranging from £39,807 to £42,891 pro rata, including London Allowance. The appointment is available from 1 November 2026 and is funded for a fixed duration of 12 months at 0.4FTE About you: You will have a strong academic background (Bachelor’s and Master’s degrees) in psychology, epidemiology, public health or a related field and an interest in applied mental health research. You will have excellent organisational skills, attention to detail and strong written and verbal communication skills. You should have experience of conducting data analysis using statistical software, managing and maintaining study documentation and coordinating meetings. You will be comfortable working both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary research team to meet agreed deadlines, and should uphold the highest ethical, governance and professional standards in all aspects of the research. The ideal candidate will also have a good understanding of governance requirements pertaining to sensitive health data, GDPR and Good Clinical Practice, and may have experience working with large datasets similar to the EPICare registry or within mental health research or services. Application process: A full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. In your personal statement, please explain how you meet each of the essential and desirable criteria outlined in the job description. You should include the following documents in your application: (1) Maximum of 2-page CV, (2) Maximum of 2-page personal statement Applications will be anonymised, therefore please ensure your personal statement is fully completed. Please also ensure to include your degree class within your personal statement if applicable Please do not upload any additional documents (transcripts, your publications, or other reports). Interviews will be held in mid-October (exact date TBC, online via Microsoft Teams) Contact details: If you have any queries about the role, please contact Melanie Lafanechere at m.lafanechere@ucl.ac.uk If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact dop.hr@ucl.ac.uk What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance The full range of staff benefits can be found here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/pay-benefits/staff-benefits Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. The Division of Psychiatry prides itself for operating in an all-inclusive environment. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and there is a commitment to advancing the careers of everyone, regardless of gender or role. We aim to provide a family friendly environment where both women and men feel able to take the time they need for family. The Athena SWAN Charter recognises commitment to advancing women's careers in science, technology, engineering, maths and medicine (STEMM) employment in academia and the Division is delighted to have an Athena Swan Silver Award since 2022. All staff are invited to contribute to EDI initiatives within the Division to contribute to improving working conditions and opportunities for all. Mentoring is a crucial part of supporting career progression and mentoring schemes are available for staff in the Division. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/psychiatry/about/equality-diversity-and-inclusion https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48425": {
      "title": "Research Data Scientist",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05696 About us: UCL is a world-leading teaching and research university, often ranked in the top ten in the world with an annual turnover of well over £1 billion. Part of UCL’s vision is to take on the hardest global challenges. The UCL Centre for Advanced Research Computing (ARC) is UCL’s new institute for infrastructure and innovation in digital research - the supercomputers, datasets, software and people that make computational science and digital scholarship possible. We are an innovative hybrid: a professional services department that delivers reliable and secure infrastructure and services to UCL research groups, and a laboratory for research and innovation in the application of advanced computational and data intensive research methods, working in partnership with academics from all fields. We are a home for the research technology professionals - research software engineers, HPC systems engineers, dev-ops specialists, data engineers, data scientists and data stewards - who support and collaborate in the delivery of UCL research About the role: Would you like to work with complex data and bridge cutting-edge AI with domain research? Research Data Scientists (also known as AI Engineer, Data Scientist, Machine Learning Specialist, Computational Statistician): Conduct collaborative research to extract knowledge and insight from datasets using a variety of mathematical, statistical and computational approaches. Develop software and scripts to implement these analyses in robust and reproducible ways. Work with research infrastructure developers to deploy these to production. You will, in all subject areas, provide data science expertise and services to world-leading research teams, training researchers in best practices, and working with scientists and scholars to build workflows, analyses and software to meet new research challenges. With such a varied job we don’t expect our candidates to know it all from the start. It is an excellent opportunity to develop new skills, spending time to study both the research areas we support and the specialist technologies applied About you: We are specifically looking for candidates with hands-on experience in Agentic AI and Large Language Models (LLMs) , along with a strong foundation in mathematics and a deep understanding of deep learning architectures . The successful applicant will also have experience of analysing, researching, and solving complex computational data analysis problems. You will be able to communicate well with researchers, and present complex or technical information to a range of stakeholders. A relevant degree or equivalent professional experience is required according to seniority. We are looking for candidates who have addressed complex data science problems in at least one field, but more importantly, who can build an understanding of new scientific questions, and see how to translate research needs to technology requirements. We are deeply committed to data science best practices and their application in research contexts. You should share our enthusiasm for reproducible, reliable computational research, and be able to help to spread that to our research community. In addition, ARC aims to help you develop your own research ideas in data science and connected areas. You will be able to set up research initiatives, bid for funding, exchange/collaborate within ARC themes across a range of novel digital research. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49050": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11259 About us: The mission of the Department of Neuroinflammation is to transform the lives of people living with diseases underpinned by neuroinflammation, including multiple sclerosis and Alzheimer’s disease, for the better, by identifying, understanding, and correcting or preventing the underlying mechanisms. Our vision is to build upon the excellence and breadth of our research, seamlessly integrating basic and clinical science. The Physics group, led by Professor Claudia Wheeler-Kingshott, develops quantitative MRI approaches to investigate neurological disease, particularly multiple sclerosis. The group has access to a 3T Philips Ingenia Cx MRI scanner and develops applications spanning microstructure, physiology and functional measurements across the central and peripheral nervous systems. The group has close collaborations with the University of Pavia in Italy, including research into brain digital twin technologies. Professor Wheeler-Kingshott also leads international research exploring ultra-low-field MRI and other accessible neuroimaging technologies in the UK, Italy and Uganda. About the role: We are seeking an enthusiastic and motivated researcher to join Professor Wheeler-Kingshott’s Physics group within the Department of Neuroinflammation and contribute to a UKRI-funded project investigating the physics of brain digital twins and their potential application in low-resource settings. You will work with Dr Antonio Ricciardi and Professor Wheeler-Kingshott to understand the limits of this technology using the 3T MRI scanner and EEG signal recordings. You will also learn about fast neural electrical impendence tomography (fnEIT) developed by Professor David Holder, which in future may be a better option for detecting brain function in low resource settings. The post is available from 01 November 2026 and funded for 1 year in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). For informal enquiries about the role please contact Professor Claudia Wheeler-Kingshott (email: c.wheeler-kingshott@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You should hold a PhD in physics, mathematics, engineering, neuroscience or related discipline, or be close to obtaining, with demonstrable experience in quantitative MRI acquisition/processing and demonstrable experience/understanding of computational modelling. Experience/understanding of virtual brain modelling platforms and a good knowledge of neuroanatomy and advanced neuroimaging analyses such as imagine and signal manipulation for parameter’s extraction is essential, as is knowledge of machine learning / artificial intelligence applications in neuroscience, and strong programming skills, including Python and MATLAB. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Gold award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48957": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11291 About us: A Research Fellow post is available to work with Senior Research Fellow Dr Paul Nderitu at the UCL Institute of Ophthalmology at UCL, ranked the best place in the world to study ophthalmology (CWUR Rankings by Subject 2017). UCL Institute of Ophthalmology conducts cutting-edge research, attracting principal investigators, post-doctoral fellows and MD/PhD students of the highest international calibre to a stimulating research environment. About the role: The successful Research Fellow will have an exciting opportunity to play a leading role in a project that will develop state-of-the art AI clinical support tools for the detection and prediction of two of the most common causes of sight-loss; diabetic eye disease (diabetic retinopathy) and sight-threatening age-related macula degeneration (wetAMD). The successful candidate will gain experience in developing AI models using longitudinal multimodal retinal imaging data from Moorfields Eye Hospital, a world-leading institution for eye care. The post is funded from the 1 January 2027 until 31 December 2027, in the first instance. The salary for this role will be on Grade 7, between spine points 32-34 (£46,261 - £48,679 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance.) Appointment as a Research Fellow at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD. If this is not the case, an initial appointment will be made as a Research Assistant at Grade 6b with payment at Grade 7 backdated upon award of a PhD to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis (with corrections). About you: The successful candidate will hold an PhD in a relevant topic, such as computer science. They will have significant experience in computer vision, deep learning tools (e.g. PyTorch), large-data analysis, statistical analysis and familiarity with manuscript writing and working in cluster or cloud-based environments. Experience with longitudinal data and/or healthcare datasets, particularly in ophthalmology, foundation models, and domain adaptation techniques, will be advantageous. The applicant should also have excellent communication skills and a commitment to conducting high-quality research that will benefit healthcare. Application Process If you have any queries regarding the vacancy please contact Dr Paul Nderitu (p.nderitu@ucl.ac.uk) If you have any queries regarding the application process please email: ioo.hr@ucl.ac.uk. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the 'Apply Now' button below. Please ensure you attach your CV and highest academic qualification. In addition, please ensure you provide a personal statement outlining how you meet the criteria to support your application. Please DO NOT attach research papers. What we offer: Working with some of the greatest intellects in the world, UCL also offers a generous pension scheme and holiday allowance, plus closure and bank holidays as well as enhanced parental pay and more. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48899": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07906 About us: The Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering is one of the largest Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering departments in the UK. The Department is proud to host internationally-leading research groups covering a broad range of activities and spread over several sites. Our staff and students have a diverse range of interests and expertise, covering many areas of physics, engineering, medicine, physiology, computer science, and mathematics. Our focus is on excellent academic research in identifying new technologies and methods for diagnosis, treatment and management of diseases using medical imaging, physiological monitoring, and the development of implanted devices. We also offer a range of undergraduate and postgraduate degrees. The Advanced X-Ray Imaging (AXIm) group is a multi-PI group, hosted within the Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering, that develops novel X-ray image formation techniques and deploys them with the latest developments in source and detector technology. We have an opportunity to recruit a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to the AXIm group. The post is part of an international collaboration with cutting-edge x-ray industry from the Republic of Korea, with options to travel to Seoul and engage directly with the industrial collaborators. The aim of the project is to develop novel x-ray microscopy methods that go beyond current capabilities. AXIm has recently achieved world-first results in the 3D imaging of intact, unstained biological tissue where every single cell and cell nucleus is visualized. This project will significantly expand these capabilities into much larger volumes and across different materials. About the role: Applications are invited for a Research Fellow position to join a collaborative effort with leading x-ray industry in the Republic of Korea and other partners to develop innovative, laboratory-based x-ray microscopy that goes beyond the currently available capabilities. The post offers a unique opportunity to conduct research and development at an international leading university and contribute to the developments of advanced x-ray methods for the life and physical sciences. UCL’s AXIm group is internationally recognised as a leading group in the development of x-ray phase contrast and other cutting-edge x-ray imaging methods. The appointed person will be formally part of the team led by Prof Sandro Olivo and be co-supervised by him and Dr Michela Esposito, who leads the microscopy activities. The postholder will be required to design, build and commission an innovative x-ray instrument, and to validate it on cutting-edge scientific and industrial applications. The role will also involve preparing and presenting findings of research activity to colleagues for review purposes, contributing to the drafting and submitting of papers to appropriate peer-reviewed journals, preparing progress reports on research for funding bodies amd other stakeholders, and contributing to the overall activities of the research team and department as required. For more information about this role, please contact Prof Sandro Olivo, a.olivo@ucl.ac.uk , or Dr Michela Esposito, michela.esposito@ucl.ac.uk About you: Applications are invited for a Research Fellow position to join a collaborative effort with leading x-ray industry in the Republic of Korea and other partners to develop innovative, laboratory-based x-ray microscopy that goes beyond the currently available capabilities. The post offers a unique opportunity to conduct research and development at an international leading university and contribute to the developments of advanced x-ray methods for the life and physical sciences. UCL’s AXIm group is internationally recognised as a leading group in the development of x-ray phase contrast and other cutting-edge x-ray imaging methods. The appointed person will be formally part of the team led by Prof Sandro Olivo and be co-supervised by him and Dr Michela Esposito, who leads the microscopy activities. The postholder will be required to design, build and commission an innovative x-ray instrument, and to validate it on cutting-edge scientific and industrial applications. The role will also involve preparing and presenting findings of research activity to colleagues for review purposes, contributing to the drafting and submitting of papers to appropriate peer-reviewed journals, preparing progress reports on research for funding bodies amd other stakeholders, and contributing to the overall activities of the research team and department as required. For more information about this role, please contact Prof Sandro Olivo, a.olivo@ucl.ac.uk , or Dr Michela Esposito, michela.esposito@ucl.ac.uk A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. We may close applications early if we receive a high volume of applications. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working If you have any queries about the role, please contact a.olivo@ucl.ac.uk . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48912": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11039 About us: The Division of Biosciences at UCL is one of the world's foremost centres for research and teaching in the biological sciences. We have an outstanding international reputation and a community of over 500 staff and more than 300 PhD students engaged in cutting edge research and collaboration. Further information can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/biosciences This is an exciting opportunity to join the Amoyel lab in the Department of Cell and Developmental Biology at UCL on a Wellcome Trust-funded project. The lab studies how local and systemic environmental inputs influence reproduction. We use a variety of approaches, from genetics and imaging, RNA sequencing and metabolomics, to mathematical modelling. We use the Drosophila testis as a model system, exploiting the many tools available in flies, as well as developing new tools when needed. Our research aims to bridge scales to understand how cell behaviour is coordinated both at the local level and with organismal physiology and how this is underpinned by changes in cell biology. The lab aims to be an inclusive and supportive environment; all applicants are welcome and informal enquiries are encouraged. About the role: The post holder will investigate how diet impacts on male fertility in Drosophila. They will join a team within the lab studying systemic physiology and its regulation by diet, and in turn, how stem cells in the gonad respond to these changes. Initially, they will contribute to ongoing work in which we seek to identify inter-organ signals that regulate the response of testis stem cells to nutrient levels. Over time, the successful candidate would be expected to develop independent lines of research and original contributions within this broad topic. This role is an open-ended contract with a funding end date of 28/02/2028 in the first instance. Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be at Grade 6B (salary £38,357 - £41,005 per annum) with payment at Grade 7 being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD Thesis. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Successful candidates will be expected to adhere to the Wellcome Trust’s policies on the Responsible Conduct of Research ( https://wellcome.org/research-funding/guidance/policies-grant-conditions/responsible-conduct-research ), as well as other Wellcome Trust policies ( https://wellcome.org/research-funding/guidance/policies-grant-conditions ), including promoting a positive research environment and a commitment to continuing personal development. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Marc Amoyel at marc.amoyel@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR Administrator. About you: You will be expected to lead a research project, through designing and conducting experiments in consultation with the supervisor. You will be able to work independently and as part of a team, to present your work to colleagues and to a wider audience, and to critically evaluate your work and the scientific literature. You must have a PhD in a relevant field (eg developmental or stem cell biology, cell biology or metabolism), or be working towards obtaining one. You should have a track record in producing high quality research, and be in a position to apply for research fellowships. Experience with Drosophila work is desirable but not essential, but you should have experience of biochemical or ‘omics techniques and a good understanding of metabolic pathways and their influence on cell behaviour. What we offer: The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know that diversity and inclusion are fundamental drivers of creativity, innovation, and excellence, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world's talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong, thrive, and reach our full potential. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian, and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women’. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ ."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48847": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11301 About us: The Institute of Ophthalmology is committed to a multi-disciplinary research portfolio that furthers an understanding of the eye and visual system linked with clinical investigations targeted to specific problems in the prevention and treatment of eye disease. The combination of the Institute’s research resource with the resources of Moorfields Eye Hospital, which has the largest ophthalmic patient population in the Western World, opens the way for advances at the forefront of vision research and provides an unparalleled setting for research and training in Ophthalmology. About the role: Inherited retinal diseases (IRDs) are the commonest cause of blindness amongst working age adults. There are over 360 causative genes, but for the majority of patients, no treatments are available. Professor Mariya Moosajee and her team are working to advance our understanding of IRDs by generating human disease models using induced pluripotent stem cell technology to derive patient retinal organoids. These can be studied to identify disease pathophysiology and to test new treatments. Working in close collaboration with Professor Mariya Moosajee, the Post Doctoral Fellow will focus on the development of non-viral approaches, using mRNA and non-viral gene therapy, as potential treatment options for two genes of interest MYO7A and GUCY2D-retinopathy. The Post Doctoral Fellow will use a range of techniques to facilitate the group’s work, including: stem cell culture, generation of iPSC colonies and differentiation into target retinal cells, histology, electron and confocal microscopy, molecular biology such as western blots, and immunocytochemistry. Additional skills may be required for testing in vivo models, and necessary training will be provided where necessary. Candidates with a PhD is essential, experience in the aforementioned techniques, and who are bright, independent and highly motivated, but willing and able to work effectively as part of a collaborative team are encouraged to apply. About you: The candidate must be a good communicator, both in lay and scientific terms, with strong writing skills. They must be competent with literature searches, published papers, and have background understanding of genetic eye disease. The candidate should be organised, hardworking and enjoy writing. What we offer: Working with some of the greatest intellects in the world, UCL also offers a generous pension scheme and holiday allowance, plus closure and bank holidays as well as enhanced parental pay and more. Visit h ttps://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Application Processs If you have any queries regarding the vacancy please contact Professor Maria Moosajee, (email: m.moosajee@ucl.ac.uk ). If you have any queries regarding the application process please email: ioo.hr@ucl.ac.uk . A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Please ensure you attach your CV, highest academic qualification . Also, please ensure you provide a personal statement outlining how you meet the criteria to support your application. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48613": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07841 About us: The UCL School of Management is home to UCL’s business and management research and education programmes. The School has forged a reputation for world-leading research and teaching in the areas of innovation management, the creation and growth of technology-intensive organisations, business analytics, and entrepreneurship. The research undertaken at the UCL School of Management has been rated as ‘world-leading’ and ‘internationally excellent’ in the Research Excellence Framework 2021 (REF), placing us 2nd in the UK for Business and Management. The School is accredited by both the AACSB and AMBA, and is a member of the Chartered Association of Business Schools. The School is home to over 2,300 students, with more than 55% from outside the UK, who study across a range of undergraduate, postgraduate, executive and PhD programmes. These programmes prepare our students for leadership roles in the next generation of tech-intensive organisations and new ventures, as well as in finance and consulting firms. This is an exciting time to join the UCL School of Management, which, since 2025, has been undergoing a rapid expansion to deliver a new strategy that will see staff and student numbers double over the coming years. This growth includes new degree programmes, additional staff, and enhanced facilities at our Canary Wharf campus, supporting our mission to educate socially conscious entrepreneurial leaders equipped to tackle the world’s greatest challenges. Further information about the School can be found here: www.mgmt.ucl.ac.uk/ About the role: UCL School of Management Operations and Technology group is seeking a motivated, independent, and highly skilled Postdoctoral Research Fellow to contribute to a research agenda in the following two potential directions: • Led by Dr. Kenan Arifoglu, Dr. Aras Selvi: research on end-to-end synthetic data generation and decision making under uncertainty. The work will develop new methods for generating privacy-preserving data that remain useful for the operational decisions ultimately made from them, rather than treating data generation and decision making as separate stages. A particular focus will be on high-stakes healthcare settings, where sensitive data must support reliable planning, capacity, and resource-allocation decisions under uncertainty despite privacy restrictions. • Led by Dr. Yi-Chun Akchen and Dr. Mengzhenyu Zhang: projects on examining how firms, platforms, and market designers can use information and assortment strategically. Two related research projects are possible. The first project studies information disclosure combined with mechanism design in markets with private information. The second studies dynamic information and assortment policies in markets where customers arrive over time and do not observe the history of previous decisions. Both contribute to a broader understanding of how limited, carefully designed communication and menus can improve allocation, coordination, and revenue. This post is a fixed‑term, 12‑month contract. The role is based at the School’s Canary Wharf campus, and the start date is flexible, with appointments possible from January 2027. About you: The successful candidate will need to evidence the following in their application. They must hold a PhD, or be close to completing a PhD, in Operations Research, Operations Management, Economics, Computer Science, or a related discipline. If the PhD has not been formally awarded before the employment start date, the post holder will be appointed at the Research Assistant Grade 6B level. They must demonstrate clear interest in one of the two research agendas presented as core to this role, as set out in the Job Description. This interest must be evidenced in the Cover Letter, where applicants are required to specify which agenda they intend to pursue and how they plan to use the 12‑month appointment to meet the duties and responsibilities of the post. They must also demonstrate advanced knowledge and experience in optimisation methods or game theory, mechanism design, economic theory, and strong mathematical and analytical skills, including experience with formal proofs. This expertise should be evidenced through the CV and writing samples. In addition, applicants must show that they can propose a research plan that fits within one of the two research directions described in the Job Description, and this should be clearly articulated in the Cover Letter. Please see the attached Job Description and Person Specification for full details. When completing your supporting statement, please tell us, using examples, how you meet the essential criteria listed in the Job Description. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits, some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and, for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion here."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48880": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11327 About us: The mission of the Department of Clinical and Experimental Epilepsy is to transform the lives of people living with epilepsy for the better by identifying, understanding, and correcting or preventing the underlying mechanisms leading to the epilepsies and associated comorbidities in each individual person. Our vision is to build upon the excellence and breadth of our research, seamlessly integrating basic and clinical science. Based within the department, the Experimental Epilepsy Group conducts research spanning the fundamental mechanisms of vesicle fusion and synaptic transmission, ion channel biology, experimental models of epilepsy, neural circuit function, neurological diseases modelled in rodents and flies, and advanced therapies for epilepsy and related disorders. Professor Kirill Volynski’s group investigates the molecular mechanisms underlying neurotransmitter release, using quantitative imaging and computational approaches to study presynaptic function in health and disease. About the role: We are seeking a Research Fellow to join Professor Kirill Volynski’s laboratory and contribute to an interdisciplinary research programme investigating the molecular and cellular mechanisms underlying neurodevelopmental disorders caused by mutations in synaptic vesicle release machinery genes, known as SNAREopathies. You will investigate the functional consequences of disease-linked variants using advanced electrophysiological and optical approaches, including quantitative fluorescence imaging of neurotransmitter release at individual synapses. The research will draw on complementary expertise in synaptic physiology, molecular biology, biophysics and structural biology. The role involves collaboration with Professor Dimitri Kullmann at UCL Queen Square Institute of Neurology and Dr Shyam Krishnakumar at Yale University, providing an opportunity to work across complementary research environments and contribute to research that will advance understanding of SNAREopathies and inform potential therapeutic and gene therapy approaches. The post is available from 01 November 2026 and is funded by an MRC grant awarded to Professor Volynski for one year in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). We expect to hold interviews on Thursday, 22nd October. A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You’ll have a PhD in a relevant discipline within neuroscience or biophysics (or be close to obtaining) and a track record of experimental laboratory research. Experience of quantitative optical microscopy and/or patch-clamp electrophysiology, molecular biology, and working with neuronal cultures is essential. Knowledge of scientific computer programming and a strong publication record are desirable. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. What we offer: Appointment as Research Fellow is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be as Research Assistant (salary £39,807 - £42,891 per annum) with appointment as Research Fellow being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits; visit This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Silver award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on EDI initiatives; visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/ion/equality-diversity-inclusion for more information about what we’re doing. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48357": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11300 About us: University College London Interaction Centre (UCLIC) is a world leading Centre of Excellence in Human-Computer Interaction teaching and research, studying interactions between people and technology, drawing on the best scientific traditions in Computer Science and Human Sciences, and working collaboratively with the research community and industry. UCLIC is unique in this space since it straddles Computer Science and Psychology. We are proud of our work in EDI and outreach, promoting equity, diversity and inclusion across our community and aiming to inspire, empower and engage people from all backgrounds. With Athena Gold and Silver awards in Computer Science and Psychology respectively, we are committed to creating a fair, inclusive and supportive environment for all staff and students. About the role: This exciting research position is part of XR-SENSE: Next-Generation Visuohaptic Media Representation, Coding and Interactive Rendering for Immersive and Inclusive XR , a major EU Horizon-funded project bringing together leading research and industry partners across Europe. XR-SENSE aims to develop the next generation of immersive technologies, creating realistic, scalable, inclusive and affect-aware visuo-haptic experiences. The project combines advanced sensing, streaming and rendering technologies with human-centred affective computing and interaction models. The successful candidate will play a key role in developing affect recognition and modelling approaches to enable personalised XR experiences . They will work within a highly interdisciplinary European consortium, collaborating with researchers and industry partners across multiple countries and contributing directly to the development and evaluation of innovative immersive technologies. This post-doctoral researcher position is full time (1 FTE) for 2 years starting in November 2026 or soon after. About you: You will be a collaborative and motivated researcher with a PhD, or equivalent experience, in Affective Computing, Machine Learning, Computer Vision, body gesture recognition or a related discipline. You will have experience developing affect recognition models, analysing multimodal sensor data, and designing human-participant studies, alongside a strong track record of peer-reviewed research. You will bring expertise in computer vision and machine learning for analysing human affective behaviour and non-verbal signals, as well as experience using LLMs for verbal data analysis. You will be an effective communicator and team member, able to work independently, manage competing priorities and contribute effectively to a multidisciplinary research environment What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits such as: .41 Days holiday including bank holidays .Hybrid working .Final Salary Pension Scheme .Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan .On-Site nursery .On-site gym .Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay .Employee assistance programme: .Staff Support Service .Discounted medical insurance. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact nadia.berthouze@ucl.ac.uk Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Support Staff."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48514": {
      "title": "Research Fellow / Senior Research Fellow in Artificial Intelligence for Cancer Digital Twins",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11142 About us: This post is based in Professor Jasmin Fisher’s laboratory at the UCL Cancer Institute . The UCL Cancer Institute is a world-leading centre for cancer research, bringing together more than 400 scientists and clinicians who work collaboratively to understand cancer and translate discoveries into improved diagnostics, treatments and patient outcomes. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/cancer/ . We are seeking a highly motivated and talented Senior Research Fellow in Artificial Intelligence to join an ambitious and multidisciplinary research programme focused on understanding cancer using AI to generate digital tumour twins. This programme aims to transform cancer research and precision oncology by developing a new generation of transparent, interpretable and trustworthy AI technologies that integrate machine learning, mechanistic modelling, formal verification and large-scale biomedical data. Cancer is a complex, multi-scale disease involving interacting processes across the molecular, cellular, tissue and organ levels. At the same time, advances in genomics, molecular profiling, pathology, imaging and clinical data collection have generated unprecedented volumes of multimodal cancer data. A major challenge is to integrate these diverse data sources into coherent computational frameworks that can generate biological insights, support clinical decision-making and accelerate therapeutic discovery. The Fisher laboratory addresses this challenge through the development of tumour digital twins, which are executable mechanistic models that capture the biological behaviour of individual tumours and can be used to predict disease progression and treatment response. A central goal of the programme is to develop novel neurosymbolic AI methodologies that combine the power of large language models, and machine learning with formal reasoning, mechanistic modelling and formal verification to automatically construct and validate transparent, interpretable biologically grounded models directly from large-scale cancer datasets and scientific knowledge. About the role: The successful candidate will play a central role in the design and development of a neurosymbolic AI platform for cancer. They will contribute to the integration and analysis of large-scale multi-omics and clinical cancer datasets, the development of AI models and the creation of explainable and verifiable computational frameworks for tumour digital twins. Working at the intersection of artificial intelligence, formal methods and cancer research, the postholder will collaborate closely also with Professor Mateja Jamnik and her group in the Department of Computer Science and Technology at University of Cambridge, alongside biologists, clinicians and computer scientists from partner organisations. The research will contribute to fundamental advances in our understanding of cancer biology while supporting the development of innovative approaches for patient stratification, therapeutic target discovery, treatment optimisation and precision oncology. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to publish in leading journals, present their work at major international conferences and contribute to the development of transformative AI technologies with significant scientific and clinical impact. This is an exceptional opportunity for an ambitious AI researcher who is passionate about applying cutting-edge AI technologies to challenging real-world problems in biomedicine. The position offers the chance to work at the forefront of trustworthy AI, neurosymbolic reasoning, computational biology and cancer systems medicine, within a world-leading research environment committed to scientific excellence and translational impact. Appointment at Grade 7 and Grade 8 is dependent upon the successful award of a PhD . Candidates who have not yet been awarded their PhD may be appointed initially at Research Assistant Grade 6B (with progression to Grade 7 and backdated salary adjustment upon submission of the final corrected PhD thesis. Appointment at Grade 8 is contingent on the candidate’s previous experience. For more details about the Fisher Lab, please visit: www.ucl.ac.uk/cancer/fisher-lab This post is funded for 3 years in the first instance, with a probationary period of 9 months. The position is available from 1st October 2026, and early availability would be advantageous. Interviews will be held in November 2026. Applications should include a CV and a Cover Letter: In the Cover Letter, please provide evidence of the essential and desirable criteria in the Person Specification part of the Job Description. (By including a Cover Letter, you can leave blank the 'Why you have applied for this role' field in the application form, which is limited in the number of characters it will allow.) About you: You will have a PhD, or be close to completing one, in Computational Biology, Bioinformatics, Cancer Biology, Systems Biology, Genomics or a related discipline, with experience analysing large-scale biomedical datasets, including genomic, transcriptomic and multi-omics data. You will have strong programming skills in R, Python and MATLAB, alongside expertise in statistical analysis, data integration and reproducible research practices. You will be an innovative and highly motivated researcher with excellent analytical and problem-solving skills, capable of working independently while contributing effectively to a multidisciplinary team. Experience in cancer biology, computational oncology, bioinformatics or related fields is essential, and a strong publication record demonstrating high-quality research outputs is expected. You will be an excellent communicator with the ability to present complex scientific concepts to a range of audiences and collaborate effectively with biologists, clinicians, computational scientists and AI researchers. Organised, proactive and committed to scientific excellence, you will be excited by the opportunity to contribute to pioneering research at the forefront of computational cancer biology, tumour digital twins and precision medicine. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance For rewards and benefits at UCL please visit: Staff Benefits | UCL People & C ulture - UCL – University College London Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: Equality, Diversity & Inclusion: Think differently, do differently. | Office of the President and Provost (Equality, Diversity & Inclusion) - UCL – University College London"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48522": {
      "title": "Research Fellow / Senior Research Fellow in Software Development for Cancer Digital Twins",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11143 About us: This post is based in Professor Jasmin Fisher’s laboratory at the UCL Cancer Institute . The UCL Cancer Institute is a world-leading centre for cancer research, bringing together more than 400 scientists and clinicians who work collaboratively to understand cancer and translate discoveries into improved diagnostics, treatments and patient outcomes. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/cancer/ . We are seeking a highly motivated and talented Senior Research Fellow in Software Development to join an ambitious and multidisciplinary research programme focused on understanding cancer using AI to generate digital tumour twins. This programme aims to transform cancer research and precision oncology by developing a new generation of transparent, interpretable and trustworthy AI technologies that integrate machine learning, mechanistic modelling, formal verification and large-scale biomedical data. Cancer is a complex, multi-scale disease involving interacting processes across the molecular, cellular, tissue and organ levels. At the same time, advances in genomics, molecular profiling, pathology, imaging and clinical data collection have generated unprecedented volumes of multimodal cancer data. A major challenge is to integrate these diverse data sources into coherent computational frameworks that can generate biological insights, support clinical decision-making and accelerate therapeutic discovery. The Fisher laboratory addresses this challenge through the development of tumour digital twins, which are executable mechanistic models that capture the biological behaviour of individual tumours and can be used to predict disease progression and treatment response. A central goal of the programme is to develop a novel neurosymbolic AI platform that combine the power of large language models and machine learning with formal reasoning, mechanistic modelling and formal verification to automatically construct and validate transparent, interpretable biologically grounded models directly from large-scale cancer datasets and scientific knowledge. About the role: The successful candidate will play a central role in the design and development of a neurosymbolic AI platform for cancer. They will contribute to the integration and analysis of large-scale multi-omics and clinical cancer datasets, the development and fine-tuning of AI models and the creation of explainable and verifiable computational frameworks for tumour digital twins. Working at the intersection of artificial intelligence, formal methods and cancer research, the postholder will collaborate closely also with Professor Mateja Jamnik and her group in the Department of Computer Science and Technology at University of Cambridge, alongside biologists, clinicians and computer scientists from partner organisations. The research will contribute to fundamental advances in our understanding of cancer biology while supporting the development of innovative approaches for patient stratification, therapeutic target discovery, treatment optimisation and precision oncology. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to publish in leading journals, present their work at major international conferences and contribute to the development of transformative AI technologies with significant scientific and clinical impact. This is an exceptional opportunity for an ambitious research software development engineer (RSDE) who is passionate about applying cutting-edge formal verification and AI technologies to challenging real-world problems in biomedicine. The position offers the chance to work at the forefront of trustworthy neurosymbolic AI, formal reasoning, computational biology and cancer systems medicine, within a world-leading research environment committed to scientific excellence and translational impact. Appointment at Grade 7 and Grade 8 is dependent upon the successful award of a PhD . Candidates who have not yet been awarded their PhD may be appointed initially at Research Assistant Grade 6B (with progression to Grade 7 and backdated salary adjustment upon submission of the final corrected PhD thesis. Appointment at Grade 8 is contingent on the candidate’s previous experience. For more details about the Fisher Lab, please visit: www.ucl.ac.uk/cancer/fisher-lab The position is available from 1st October 2026, and early availability would be advantageous. Interviews will be held in November 2026. This post is funded for 3 years in the first instance, with a probationary period of 9 months. Applications should include a CV and a Cover Letter: In the Cover Letter, please provide evidence of the essential and desirable criteria in the Person Specification part of the Job Description. (By including a Cover Letter, you can leave blank the 'Why you have applied for this role' field in the application form, which is limited in the number of characters it will allow.) About you: You will hold a PhD in Computer Science, Software Engineering, Machine Learning, Mathematics, Physics or a related quantitative discipline, with a strong track record in software development and/or research. You have extensive experience designing, building and maintaining robust, scalable software systems, and are proficient in modern software engineering practices, including version control, automated testing, documentation and CI/CD workflows. You have experience developing and deploying AI-enabled applications, including machine learning, large language models and generative AI technologies, and are comfortable working with cloud platforms, distributed computing environments and data-intensive systems. You are an analytical problem-solver with excellent communication skills, able to explain complex technical concepts to both specialist and non-specialist audiences. Most importantly, you thrive in collaborative, multidisciplinary environments and are motivated by the opportunity to develop trustworthy, transparent AI technologies with real-world impact. You bring a proactive approach, a commitment to technical excellence and reproducibility, and a genuine enthusiasm for working at the intersection of artificial intelligence, software engineering and cancer research. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance For rewards and benefits at UCL please visit: Staff Benefits | UCL People & C ulture - UCL – University College London Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: Equality, Diversity & Inclusion: Think differently, do differently. | Office of the President and Provost (Equality, Diversity & Inclusion) - UCL – University College London"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48583": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in  Evolution and Development",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11225 About us: The Division of Biosciences at UCL is one of the world's foremost centres for research and teaching in the biological sciences. We have an outstanding international reputation and a community of over 500 staff and more than 300 PhD students engaged in cutting edge research and collaboration. Further information can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/biosciences We are seeking an enthusiastic researcher with a background in evo-devo/functional genomics to join the group of Dr. Sean Stankowski. The position is well funded through a UKRI Future Leaders Fellowship. About the role: The Research Fellow will play a lead role in an ambitious project to reconstruct the evolutionary pathway between different reproductive modes in Littorina. ( https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.adi2982 ). Specifically, they will lead a strand of the project focused on understanding the molecular and developmental basis underlying a recent transition from egg-laying to live-bearing in this system. They will take responsibility for designing and implementing the experimental strategy using various approaches, potentially including bulk and single cell RNAseq, in situ hybridisation, chromatin accessibility assays, and proteomics. The project will be supported by a research technician. The researcher will work as part of a team and in collaboration with project partners from UCL (Paola Oliveri), the Sanger Institute (Dr. Joana Meier), the Institute of Science and Technology Austria (Dr. Nick Barton), The University of Gothenburg (Dr. Kerstin Johannesson and Dr. Erical Leder) and the University of Göttingen (Dr. Daniel Jackson). This role is an open-ended contract of three years with the possibility of extension. A PhD is an essential criterion for this role in relevant subject area; Where the successful candidate is close to completion and awaiting their formal PhD award, the initial appointment will be made at Research Assistant Grade 6B (salary £39,107–£42,891 per annum). The appointment will transfer to Grade 7 following confirmation of the PhD award, with the Grade 7 salary backdated to the date of final thesis submission. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Sean Stankowski at s.stankowski@ucl.ac.uk. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR Administrator (fls.hr@ucl.ac.uk) stating the advert reference number in the subject line. About you: Applicants must hold a PhD in a relevant field and have a strong theoretical understanding of molecular and developmental biology, and experience using approaches such as single-cell and spatial transcriptomics, in situ hybridisation, differential expression analyses, chromatin accessibility assays, and proteomics. The ideal candidate will have a demonstrated ability to design and conduct research independently, and be organised, creative, and collaborative. Experience in integrating multiple data types to address biological questions, is highly desirable. An interest in the developmental and genetic basis of reproductive differences , experience working on molluscs would be advantageous . What we offer: We will support your professional development to help you achieve your long-term goals and will provide access to training courses and international and domestic conferences. The position will include time to develop and peruse your own research interests, and includes opportunities to work with and visit project partners at other research institutions. VISA The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefit s to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our division holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48955": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in AI for Neonatal Retinal Imaging",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11293 About us: A Research Fellow post is available to work with Senior Research Fellow Dr Siegfried Wagner at the UCL Institute of Ophthalmology, ranked the best place in the world to study ophthalmology (CWUR Rankings by Subject 2017). UCL Institute of Ophthalmology conducts cutting-edge research, attracting principal investigators, post-doctoral fellows and MD/PhD students of the highest international calibre to a stimulating research environment About the role: This is an exciting opportunity for a motivated researcher with a background in machine learning, computer science or a related quantitative discipline to engage in high-impact, translational research in medical AI. The post holder will play a central role in building the world's largest, most diverse neonatal retinal imaging dataset and in developing a self-supervised foundation model for neonatal retinal images. The role combines large-scale data curation and annotation with methodological development in foundation models, applied to a globally important, underserved area of paediatric ophthalmology: retinopathy of prematurity (ROP) and associated systemic disease in preterm infants. The post is funded from the 1 December 2026 until 30 November 2028 in the first instance. Salary will be on Grade 7, Point 32 (£46,261 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance.) Appointment as a Research Fellow at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD. If this is not the case, an initial appointment will be made as a Research Assistant at Grade 6b with payment at Grade 7 backdated upon award of a PhD to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis (with corrections.) About you: The successful candidate will hold an PhD in a relevant topic, such as computer science. They will have demonstrable experience in AI for healthcare or medical imaging; the development, training and fine-tuning of deep learning models; working with large-scale imaging or medical datasets; and a strong publication record in relevant journals. Knowledge of software engineering standards such as Python, and experience with neonatal/paediatric retinal imaging and disease will be advantageous. The applicant should also have excellent communication skills and a commitment to conducting high-quality research that will benefit healthcare. Application Process If you have any queries regarding the vacancy please contact Mrs Polly Rawlinson (p.rawlinson@ucl.ac.uk) If you have any queries regarding the application process please email: ioo.hr@ucl.ac.uk. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the 'Apply Now' button below. Please ensure you attach your CV and highest academic qualification. In addition, please ensure you provide a personal statement outlining how you meet the criteria to support your application. Please DO NOT attach research papers What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer a generous pension scheme and holiday allowance (27 days), bank holidays (8 days), plus closure (6 days) and more. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewardand-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49053": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Algal Biomineralisation and Excitability",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11372 About us: The Division of Biosciences at UCL is one of the world's foremost centres for research and teaching in the biological sciences. We have an outstanding international reputation and a community of over 500 staff and more than 300 PhD students engaged in cutting edge research and collaboration. Further information can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/biosciences This is an exciting opportunity to join the McClelland lab, which studies how marine algae build mineral structures and how electrical signalling at the cell membrane shapes their physiology. You will join a small, interdisciplinary team that combines algal cell biology with electrophysiology, advanced imaging, structural biology, geochemistry and mathematical modelling. Ideas and methods move freely between disciplines. About the role: You will investigate biomineralisation and electrical excitability in marine algae, applying specialist expertise in one of three areas: electrophysiology (patch clamp and sharp electrode), fluorescence lifetime imaging (FLIM), or cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM). You will design and carry out experiments, analyse and publish results, and present your work within the group and at conferences. Alongside the core project, you will be encouraged to develop your own research ideas. You will get extensive support to apply for independent fellowship or grant funding, including mentoring and feedback on applications. Salary: £ 46,261 – £ 54,019 per annum, Grade 7. Depending on experience. This is a fixed-term post until 30 th September 2027, for up to 12 months depending on the start date. Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon PhD award. If not yet awarded, initial appointment will be at Grade 6B, with backdating to Grade 7 upon submission of PhD thesis. Visa sponsorship This role meets the requirements for a Skilled Worker certificate of sponsorship or Global Talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. For guidance on eligibility, see Gov.uk Skilled Worker Visa guidance Flexible working and conditions We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. UCL Terms and Conditions of Service: Research and Professional Services Staff About you: You will hold a PhD (or have submitted your thesis) in a relevant discipline, with extensive hands-on experience of algal biology, including culturing algae and manipulating them in experiments. You will also have extensive demonstrable expertise in at least one of: • electrophysiology, including patch clamp and sharp-electrode recording; • fluorescence lifetime imaging microscopy (FLIM); • cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM). You will have a publication record appropriate to your career stage. You will be able to work independently while contributing to a collaborative, interdisciplinary team, and you will communicate your work clearly to specialist and non-specialist audiences. Experience with calcifying or silicifying marine algae, quantitative data analysis or programming (e.g. Python, MATLAB), or writing funding applications would be an advantage. Key Applicant Information Closing date: 25/10/26 at 23:59 GMT (applications may close early if volumes are high, so early submission is recommended). Interviews scheduled for: TBC Job description and person specification available at the bottom of the advert. Queries about the role: contact Dr Harry McClelland [h.mcclelland@ucl.ac.uk]. Please use the Subject line: PDRA enquiry-xxxxx (replacing xxxxx with your name). Queries about the process, accessibility, or reasonable adjustments: contact the Faculty HR Team on fls.hr@ucl.ac.uk What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . ‘Why UCL?’ the impact UCL has had in the world and the impact people can make working with us. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – visit UCL Ways of Working to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy Our Faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion here: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion ."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48760": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Bioinformatics",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07896 About us: UCL’s Department of Computer Science (CS) is a top-ranked Computer Science Department in the UK. In the 2021 Research Excellence Framework (REF) evaluation, UCL Computer Science was ranked second in the UK for research power and first in England. The Bioinformatics Group has a world-class reputation in the area of bioinformatics and particularly the applications of AI in protein bioinformatics. About the role: The post sits within the Department of Computer Science and the Jones group, contributing to a BBSRC-funded project developing next-generation protein language models and domain-centric machine-learning frameworks for large-scale sequence-structure analysis and functional prediction. The role involves designing, implementing, and benchmarking computational models; developing and maintaining open-source software and web resources; running and analysing experiments; and documenting, presenting, and publishing research outcomes in line with project objectives. The post also includes contributing to project planning and reporting, supporting group activities, representing the work at scientific meetings, and helping with supervising students where appropriate. Funding is available for this post until 31/01/2031 in the first instance. About you: The ideal candidate is a research-focused computational scientist with a PhD and a strong background in bioinformatics, computational biology, or a closely related quantitative discipline, with experience in protein sequence or structure analysis, machine-learning method development, and modern software engineering practices. They should be proficient in Python and Linux-based development, familiar with deep learning approaches in structural biology, and comfortable working with large-scale datasets and model benchmarking. Strong analytical, organisational, and communication skills are essential, along with the ability to collaborate effectively within a team, contribute to publications and presentations, and support the development of web-based bioinformatics tools. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact Professor David Jones (d.t.jones@ucl.ac.uk). What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part-time staff). Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro rata for part-time staff). Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan. On-Site nursery Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay. Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service. Discounted medical insurance. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London's Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world's talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ . Our department holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49014": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Biopharmaceutical Economics & Sustainability Modelling",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07923 About us: The “Next-Gen Biopharma Manufacturing 5.0” Prosperity Partnership is a 5-year collaborative programme between UCL Biochemical Engineering and AstraZeneca, jointly funded by EPSRC and AstraZeneca. It aims to transform antibody manufacturing using AI, automation, digital twins and advanced analytics to improve production of therapies for cancer, obesity and autoimmune conditions. Delivered through the UCL–AstraZeneca Centre of Excellence, the programme will appoint six postdoctoral researchers to work across UCL and AstraZeneca sites, combining computational and experimental approaches. This Research Fellow position sits at the heart of this transformation. Your economic and sustainability research will directly inform and guide the strategic decisions that shape how we reimagine biopharmaceutical manufacturing. By developing robust models that evaluate cost, environmental impact and resource efficiency, you'll provide the evidence base that drives innovation from concept to implementation. Your work will bridge cutting-edge science and real-world application, ensuring that the next generation of manufacturing technologies are not only technically advanced but economically viable and environmentally responsible. UCL Biochemical Engineering is a global leader in bioprocess engineering research and education, with a mission to develop innovative biomanufacturing solutions for health and a sustainable bioeconomy. The department combines world-class facilities, strong industrial links, and an interdisciplinary research culture. AstraZeneca is a global, science-led biopharmaceutical company with a focus on the discovery, development, and commercialisation of medicines in Oncology, Rare Diseases, and BioPharmaceuticals. About the role: We are seeking a highly motivated postdoctoral researcher to join this ambitious Prosperity Partnership between UCL and AstraZeneca and work in a cross-disciplinary team across both UCL (London) and AstraZeneca (Cambridge) sites. This role will evaluate the economic and environmental impact of next-generation biomanufacturing technologies, supporting more sustainable and cost-effective development pathways for advanced biologics through data-driven decision-making. You will lead the development of integrated techno-economic and life cycle assessment models, incorporating uncertainty analysis and multi-objective optimisation to evaluate and optimise the cost, carbon footprint and resource impacts of emerging bioprocess strategies. These decision-support tools will be applied to real-world case studies in collaboration with AstraZeneca, generating insights that inform strategic choices and guide industrial implementation. This role is funded for 2 years in the first instance, with potential for extension. About you: You will hold (or be near completion of) a PhD in a relevant discipline such as biochemical engineering, chemical engineering, industrial engineering, environmental sustainability, or a related field. Essential to this role is demonstrated expertise in both techno-economic analysis and life cycle assessment, supported by strong quantitative modelling and analytical skills. While prior experience in the pharmaceutical or biopharmaceutical sector would be beneficial, it is not essential—you will have the opportunity to develop industry-specific knowledge through close collaboration with AstraZeneca colleagues and exposure to real manufacturing and process development challenges. What matters most is that you bring solid foundations in sustainability assessment and economic modelling, along with the ability to apply these methods to complex industrial systems. You will have experience working on interdisciplinary research projects and be motivated to deliver practical, real-world impact. Excellent communication skills and a collaborative approach are essential, as is the ability to work independently and take ownership of your research. Experience developing decision-support tools, familiarity with LCA software, and skills in optimisation and uncertainty analysis would be advantageous. A commitment to UCL's values and to promoting equality, diversity and inclusion is essential. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, UCL also offer some great benefits. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. Our department holds an Athena Swan Gold award in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48779": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Climate Change and Public Health",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11251 About us: The UCL Institute for Global Health (IGH) is at the heart of UCL’s Grand Challenge of Global Health. The Institute’s vision is for a world where international policy on global health is informed by world-class research. IGH aims to support the development of robust solutions to aspects of the world’s major global health challenges through scholarly outputs, education, public engagement, translational research, and ultimately by influencing public policy and professional practice. The Lancet Countdown: tracking progress on health and climate change, is an independent, international research collaboration, that works to track the evolving links between health and climate change globally, regionally and nationally, in partnership with The Lancet and the World Health Organization. Its aim is to inform a robust response to climate change that protects and promotes human health. We are looking for an experienced Research Fellow in Climate Change and Public Health to join our team. About the role: The successful candidate will have experience in climate change and global health and/or epidemiology, with strong quantitative skills and the ability to apply this expertise across climate change and health research. This will include supporting the development and evaluation of indicators of the health impacts of climate change, policies and actions to address climate change, and use of findings to inform real-world impact. The postholder will support the delivery of the health research and academic initiatives of the Lancet Countdown, including the collation of health and epidemiological evidence for the annual Global Lancet Countdown Report, Regional Reports, special issues, and additional academic and research outputs. They will also support the development of research to quantify the impacts of climate on health, and the health opportunities of climate change mitigation and adaptation action. This role offers the opportunity for an early career researcher to develop a research portfolio in the intersection of climate change and health, in support of the overall Lancet Countdown aims and their personal academic career development. This is a full-time role (i.e. 1.0 FTE, 36.5 hours per week) and it is available until 30 April 2029 in the first instance. Please note that the successful candidate must be living in the UK or willing to relocate to the UK for the duration of the contract. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Salary range: £46,261 - £54,019 per annum (including London Allowance), depending on experience. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Dr Jennifer Israelsson (Senior Research Fellow) on j.israelsson@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the IGH HR Team on IGH.HR@ucl.ac.uk . About you: It is essential that the successful candidate has a PhD or equivalent working experience in the intersection of climate change, and global health, epidemiology, or a closely related discipline. They will also have: Research experience in climate change and global health, epidemiology, or a closely related discipline Experience in epidemiological modelling, ideally environmental epidemiological models Intermediate proficiency in R or similar coding language Experience working with multidisciplinary academic teams Experience communicating scientific and technical evidence to non-academic audiences Track record of leading or contributing to academic peer-reviewed publications Excellent communications skills including ability to express ideas clearly through multiple modes Strong analytical and critical thinking skills What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48811": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Data-Parallel Proof Checking for Monoidal Theories",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07928 About us: UCL's Department of Computer Science (CS) is a top-ranked Computer Science Department in the UK. In the 2021 Research Excellence Framework (REF) evaluation, UCL Computer Science was ranked second in the UK for research power and first in England. The post is held within the Programming Principles, Logic and Verification (PPLV) research group. About the role: The post is held in connection with the ARIA Safeguarded AI programme project “Data-Parallel Proof Checking for Monoidal Theories”, led by Professor Fabio Zanasi within the Programming Principles, Logic and Verification (PPLV) group at UCL. The post holder will strengthen the Metacat proof-checking core and develop a reinforcement-learning theorem-proving prototype, while continuing research on monoidal theories and diagrammatic proof systems, contributing both software and theoretical outputs to the Safeguarded AI platform. About you: Applicants should hold, or be close to completing, a PhD in Computer Science, Mathematics, or a closely related field, with research expertise in category theory / monoidal categories and string diagrams, and strong software development skills applicable to building and maintaining research-grade proof-checking or verification software. Experience with proof assistants, automated reasoning, or reinforcement learning is desirable, as is a track record of scientific publication and presentation at international venues. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the 'Apply Now' button below. If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact Professor Fabio Zanasi (f.zanasi@ucl.ac.uk). What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff). Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro rata for part time staff). Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan. On-Site nursery Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay. Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service. Discounted medical insurance. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London's Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world's talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=47954": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Defect-Mediated Charge Transport",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07825 About us: The London Centre for Nanotechnology (LCN) is an interdisciplinary enterprise between University College London, King’s College London and Imperial College London. In bringing together world-class infrastructure and leading nanotechnology research activities, the Centre aims to attain the critical mass to compete with the best facilities abroad. The LCN has strong relationships with the broader nanotechnology and commercial communities, and is involved in much major collaboration. As the world’s only such facility to be located in the heart of a metropolis, the LCN has superb access to corporate, investment and industrial partners. It is at the forefront of training in nanotechnology, and has a strong media presence aimed at educating the public and bringing transparency to this emerging science LCN and UCL Physics and Astronomy are at the forefront of research into complexity in materials science, particularly in the area of emergent properties in strongly correlated systems. Particularly, how charge moves these compounds is of paramount importance, but how it interacts with the defects found in real materials is poorly understood and difficult to reveal. Single crystals can offer unparalleled insights into material properties, charge transport and defect chemistry. The Facility for Advanced Crystals for Emerging Technologies (FACET) at the Research Complex at Harwell has recently opened to allow access to these powerful scientific platforms for device physics applications. About the role: The post holder will be required to carry out research on charge transport in energy materials – specifically iron oxide (a candidate photocatalyst, battery electrode and supercapacitor material) and nickel oxide (an emerging supercapacitor electrode material and hole transport layer for photovoltaics) – with the goal to elucidate the effects of dopants on their antiferromagnetic structure and electron small polaron hopping. In particular, they will apply advanced neutron and X-ray scattering techniques at national facilities to reveal short-range correlated disorder, invisible to long-range structure probes. This project will use a state-of-the-art, laser-heated, high-pressure image furnace to synthesise metal oxide single crystals of extremely high purity. These will be precisely oriented and a suite of transport measurements used to determine the nature of hopping transport. Finally, three-dimensional delta-pair-distribution-function analysis will isolate correlated magnetic and atomic disorder to build a full picture of charge transport in these important materials. The role is a fixed term position, post duration is until 28.02.2029. About you: The ideal candidate will be a motivated and collaborative researcher who is either completing or has recently obtained a PhD in Chemistry, Physics, Materials Science, or a closely related discipline. You will have a strong academic background and expertise in at least one key area of inorganic crystalline materials research, such as synthesis and characterisation, with knowledge of techniques including crystal growth, charge transport measurements, X-ray or neutron scattering, or powder X-ray diffraction being advantageous. The successful candidate will demonstrate the ability to conduct high-quality independent research, critically evaluate scientific information, and analyse and interpret complex data to produce impactful research outcomes. They will have a track record of contributing to peer-reviewed publications and be confident communicating complex scientific concepts to both specialist and non-specialist audiences. Strong interpersonal, written and verbal communication skills are essential, together with the ability to work effectively within a multidisciplinary research environment and build positive working relationships with academic and professional services colleagues. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our long-term commitment and 'beacon' status in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48990": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Disruptive Additive Manufacturing Microstructures via Beam-Shaping",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07903 About us: University College London (UCL) UCL is a multi-disciplinary university with a population of over 13,000 staff and 42,000 students from 150 different countries. Degree programmes are provided in Arts and Humanities, Built Environment, Brain Sciences, Engineering Sciences, Education, Laws, Life Sciences, Mathematical & Physical Sciences, Medical Sciences, Population Health Sciences and Social and Historical Sciences. For more information, please visit http://www.ucl.ac.uk/about UCL Mechanical Engineering UCL Mechanical Engineering has been pioneering the development of engineering education, having taught the core discipline for over 165 years. UCL was home to the UK’s first ever Professor of the Mechanical Principles of Engineering, Eaton Hodgkinson, in 1847. It was also where Sir Alexander Blackie William Kennedy introduced organized laboratory practicals in university education training; a world-leading educational innovation at the time. About the role: The Multiscale X-ray Imaging Lab (MXI Lab) at the Research Complex at Harwell, Oxfordshire, uses bespoke in situ replicators and ultra-fast synchrotron imaging to reveal the physics of additive manufacturing, working with Diamond Light Source and the ESRF. You will lead the adaptation of our Blown Additive Manufacturing Process Replicator (BAMPR-2) to apply non-Gaussian beam shaping and ultrasound to directed energy deposition (DED), and use synchrotron imaging to show how this transforms solidification microstructures and reduces defects. You will hold a PhD (or equivalent experience) in materials science, physics or a related engineering discipline, with in-depth knowledge of X-ray imaging and analysis, physical metallurgy and advanced powder processes. Role specific details: This post is funded in the first instance for 18 months. If you have any queries about the role, please contact ruikang.xue@ucl.ac.uk A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. About you: You will be accountable for the scientific goals of the project: adapting the in situ DED rig, with a Design Engineer, for a non-Gaussian ring beam and ultrasonic excitation; running laboratory and synchrotron imaging campaigns; and analysing the resulting large datasets to quantify melt pool hydrodynamics, solute transport and microstructure evolution. You will also coordinate the MXI Lab’s DED researchers, mentor PhD students, contribute to beamtime proposals, and publish your findings. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian, and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people and LGBTQI+ people."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48854": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Heritage and Environmental Risk- Internal UCL Candidates Only",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07879 About us: BSEER brings together four world-leading institutes to generate sustainable solutions and real-world impact across the built environment, energy, resources and heritage. Our Institute for Sustainable Heritage (ISH) delivers ground-breaking, cross-disciplinary research and teaching to sustain cultural heritage for future generations. About the role: The main purpose of this job is to support ISH’s contribution to the Centre of Excellence in Green Heritage Science. The Green Heritage Science Centre of Excellence (GreenHer) is a new European research hub coordinated by the University of Ljubljana, bringing together institutions across Slovenia and Italy to advance conservation, sustainable heritage management, participatory engagement, and digital heritage. To enable this, the post holder will be required to support the implementation of pilot research projects in collaboration with Slovenian and Italian collaborators on the project and will also be responsible for coordinating and developing collaborative bids for research funding. This post is available from 1 December 2026 and is funded for 36 months in the first instance; further funding to support the post may become available. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. UCL welcomes applications from international applicants and has licence to sponsor individuals who require a visa. This is dependent on the post and candidate meeting eligibility requirements for visa sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. About you: You will hold a PhD (or equivalent research experience) in heritage science, archaeology, conservation, sustainability or a related discipline, with a track record of designing and delivering successful research projects. Experience applying Living Labs or other participatory research methodologies would be an advantage. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48275": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Human Genetics (Rare Cardiovascular Disease)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11243 About us: UCL established the Institute of Health Informatics (IHI) in August 2014 in the Faculty of Population Health Sciences. The aim of IHI is to conduct high quality data-intensive research to improve health at local, national, and international levels. The Faculty of Population Health Sciences has established itself as UCL’s largest research-based faculty, encompassing eight institutes. More information available at www.ucl.ac.uk/population-health-sciences/. About the role: Institute of Health Informatics is looking to appoint a Research Fellow in Human Genetics (Rare Cardiovascular Disease) to join our welcoming and vibrant Institute. This position presents a unique opportunity to join a team of experienced health data scientists, informaticians, statisticians and clinical epidemiologists and contribute significantly to the statistical analysis and subsequent presentation of results from large scale national and rich local health record data resources. The Research Fellow in Human Genetics will work across partners to integrate clinical and genetic data within relevant secure data environments for research; carry out data management tasks to prepare data for analyses, including data extraction and data quality assurance; and generate efficient and shareable code which can be used in different datasets; amongst other duties. A full range of duties can be found on the attached job description. The post is full-time (36.5 hours per week) and the post is funded until 30 September 2029. Further funding may become available. Appointment at Grade 7 (£46,261 - £54,019 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance) is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be at Research Assistant Grade 6B (£39,807 – £42,891 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance) with payment at Grade 7 being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. For an informal discussion please contact Professor Tom Lumbers at t.lumbers@ucl.ac.uk For any queries regarding the recruitment process please contact Anita Gorasia at a.gorasia@ucl.ac.uk. Please only submit a CV and complete the questionnaire. No additional information is required or will be considered at this point. About you: If you believe you meet the requirements why not come and be part of this unique and exciting opportunity and be part of something where you feel included, valued and proud. The Research Fellow in Human Genetics must have (PhD in Statistics, Epidemiology, Genetics, Computer Science or a related numerate discipline, or relevant working experience as well excellent programming and data visualisation skills in Python and R; and ability to write code that clearly expresses its purpose, and is readily used by others maintained and extended. The role holder will have experience with large-scale data processing, including data collection, cleaning, and preprocessing (e.g. SQL, PySpark), and analysis. Please review the job description before applying, paying particular attention to the essential / desirable criteria, and ensure your experience in these areas is addressed in the questionnaire section of the application. What we offer: We will consider applicates to work on a part-time, flexible and job share wherever possible. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are:, 41 Days holiday (pro rata for part time staff) (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days), cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, on-site gym and employee assistance programme Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=47847": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Mass Spectrometry",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07792 About us: The Chemistry Department at University College London is the oldest in England, ranked 3 rd in the UK for research excellence in REF 2021. Our department is situated in Bloomsbury, at the heart of London, and we offer an exciting and vibrant environment in which to study at one of the UK's top universities. The Department has an annual research income of around £15 million and is excellently equipped for chemical biology, nanomaterials and biophysical research and characterisation as a result of >£4M of investments in laboratories for synthetic and biological chemistry. This includes peptide synthesisers, extensive HPLC equipment, a suite of 5 high field NMRs and a mass spectrometry suite. The UCL Mass Spectrometry Science Technology Platform (MS-STP: academic lead Prof Kostas Thalassinos) provides access to a world-class portfolio of mass spectrometry and sample preparation technologies supporting structural biology, proteomics, single-cell, and spatial biology research. The facility houses a Waters Select Series Cyclic IMS, Xevo G2-XS QTof with Acquity UPLC M-Class, Waters BioAccord System, Orbitrap Eclipse Tribrid Mass Spectrometer, and an Orbitrap Astral Zoom platform enabling next-generation single-cell proteomics. In addition, the hub hosts a CellenOne system for automated single-cell isolation and preparation, and a Syncell Microscoop platform for subcellular proteomics, allowing proteome measurements to be performed from defined cellular structures and regions. http://www.ucl.ac.uk/chemistry https://www.ucl.ac.uk/science-technology-platforms/mass-spectrometry About the role: We are seeking to appoint a Postdoctoral Researcher in Mass Spectrometry to contribute to a multidisciplinary project investigating cyclic antimicrobial peptide–lipid interactions at molecular resolution. This project is part of an EPSRC Open Plus Fellowship led by Professor Alethea Tabor in collaboration with Professor Kostas Thalassinos. The overall goal of the project is to develop and apply advanced structural mass spectrometry methodologies, especially XL-MS, to understand how cyclic antimicrobial peptides interact with their target lipids, and to support the development of new antimicrobial strategies against multidrug-resistant bacteria. In this project, you will lead the development and application of structural mass spectrometry approaches alongside complementary biophysical techniques using model lipid bilayers and contribute to the development of computational models of peptide–lipid complexes. You will work in a highly collaborative, multidisciplinary environment embedded within both research groups, partnering closely with a Research Fellow in Chemical Biology and a PhD student who will design and synthesise bespoke photoreactive peptides for these studies. You will also engage with collaborators across UCL and partner institutions including Queen’s University Belfast, University of Nottingham, Utrecht University, and Uppsala University, spanning expertise in biophysics, microbiology, NMR, and lipid synthesis. Strong teamwork, communication skills, and the ability to deliver high-quality results in a timely manner will be essential for success in this role. The appointment will be for a fixed term of 2 years initially, with potential for extension subject to funding and/or project requirements. About you: You will have a PhD (or be close to completion) in Mass Spectrometry, Chemistry, Biochemistry, Biophysics or a related discipline, with experience of structural mass spectrometry techniques and computational analyses. Expertise in handling membrane proteins or peptides and studying biomolecular interactions in model membranes, and experience in cross-linking mass spectrometry, would be advantageous. You will be able to work effectively both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team, demonstrating strong organisational, communication, and problem-solving skills. The ability to manage your time, deliver high-quality results to agreed deadlines, and engage constructively with collaborators is essential. You will also be willing to contribute to the wider research environment, including supporting student supervision, participating in teaching and public engagement activities, and contributing to the safe and effective operation of laboratory facilities. A track record of contributing to publications in peer-reviewed journals, or clear potential to do so, is expected. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. Please upload a CV, list of publications and covering letter stating why you are interested in the role and how you meet the criteria. For informal enquiries please contact Alethea Tabor ( a.b.tabor@ucl.ac.uk ) or Kostas Thalassinos ( k.thalassinos@ucl.ac.uk ). Any queries on the application process can be directed to Rezwana Akhter ( r.akhter@ucl.ac.uk ), as we wish to encourage online applications. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. At UCL Chemistry we are dedicated to the Athena SWAN Charter for women in science, recognising the commitment to advancing women’s careers in science, technology, engineering, maths and medicine (STEMM) employment in higher education and research. The department was successfully awarded a Bronze Award in 2016 and Silver Award in 2020, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48822": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Molecular Beam Epitaxy of III-V/Si Materials and Devices (2 positions)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07884 About us: We are seeking to appoint two outstanding Research Fellows to join our internationally recognised semiconductor photonics research programme within the Department of Electronic & Electrical Engineering at University College London (UCL). The successful candidates will contribute to the EPSRC Programme Grant Quantum Dot on Silicon Systems for Communications, Information Processing and Sensing (QUDOS). The programme brings together world-leading researchers from UCL, Cardiff University, the University of Cambridge and the University of Southampton to develop next-generation integrated photonic technologies based on monolithically integrated III-V compound semiconductors on silicon. The QUDOS Programme Grant is a major EPSRC investment aimed at developing high-performance quantum dot materials, photonic integrated circuits and optoelectronic devices on silicon for future communication, sensing and information-processing technologies. Building on pioneering demonstrations of directly grown telecommunications-wavelength lasers on silicon, the programme seeks to establish scalable monolithic integration technologies that combine the superior optical performance of compound semiconductors with the manufacturing advantages of silicon platforms. The research has the potential to transform photonic integration in a manner analogous to the impact that silicon integrated circuits have had on modern electronics, enabling unprecedented functionality, scalability and cost-effectiveness. About the role: The Research Fellows will play a leading role in the epitaxial growth and development of advanced III-V and silicon-based semiconductor materials and optoelectronic devices using molecular beam epitaxy. The role will involve conducting independent research, developing new device structures, operating and maintaining advanced MBE facilities, and contributing to the broader research objectives of the QUDOS programme. The roles are available immediately until 30 th April 2027 in the first instance. Further funding to support the posts may be available. Subject to project requirements, an extension may be possible through a subsequent Programme Grant, Boundless light: Fibre optic systems unconstrained by glass properties (BOUNDLESS). About you: Applicants should have a PhD in a relevant area of physical sciences or engineering. A first degree in theoretical physics, applied mathematics, materials sciences or electrical engineering is essential. You should strong experience on the operation and maintenance of III-V solid-source MBE and/or Group-IV MBE facility, as well as a strong track-record on the research outcomes of MBE-growth of III-V compound semiconductor materials and/or Group-IV materials. The successful candidate will have in-depth knowledge of the mechanism of QD lasers and strong experience on the heteroepitaxial growth of QD lasers. The ability to analyse and write up data in the form of journal papers and reports is desirable. If the successful candidate has not yet been awarded their PhD, appointment will be made as a Research Assistant (Grade 6B). Payment at Grade 7 will be backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis including corrections, once the PhD has been awarded. Application details: To apply for the role, click the 'Apply Now' button at the bottom or top of the page Applications close on 16th October at 23:59 A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page Informal enquiries regarding this post can be addressed to Prof Huiyun Liu huiyun.liu@ucl.ac.uk. For questions regarding the application process please contact Rebecca Thomas at becca.thomas@ucl.ac.uk. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48579": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Molecular Quantum Sensing for Biomedical Applications",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07874 About us: Ben Miller’s research group at UCL’s London Centre for Nanotechnology develops quantum-sensing approaches for biological applications with a particular interest in nanoparticle probes and sensitive optical detection. The group brings together materials chemistry, spin physics and biological experimentation. Current projects explore organic sensing materials and fluorescent nanodiamonds for biological and diagnostic applications. The post is funded through the UK Quantum Biomedical Sensing (Q-BIOMED) Research Hub and involves collaboration with researchers in chemistry, physics and biomedicine, including Professor Vijay Chudasama’s group in UCL Chemistry. The London Centre for Nanotechnology (LCN) is an interdisciplinary enterprise between University College London, King’s College London and Imperial College London. In bringing together world-class infrastructure and leading nanotechnology research activities, the Centre aims to attain the critical mass to compete with the best facilities abroad. The LCN has strong relationships with the broader nanotechnology and commercial communities, and is involved in much major collaboration. As the world’s only such facility to be located in the heart of a metropolis, the LCN has superb access to corporate, investment and industrial partners. It is at the forefront of training in nanotechnology, and has a strong media presence aimed at educating the public and bringing transparency to this emerging science. About the role: The post holder will develop diagnostic and other biomedical sensing methods using the optical and spin properties of molecular quantum materials in nanoparticle form. Specific objectives involve the development, characterization and integration of molecular nanocrystals into diagnostic tests. The work will involve preparing and characterising nanoparticles, developing coatings and functionalisation methods, and testing their behaviour in biological environments. This will include improving particle dispersibility and stability, attaching biological recognition molecules, and evaluating optical and spin properties and performance in model assays. There will be scope to propose and pursue new materials, chemistries and biomedical applications. Working with Ben Miller and collaborators, the postholder will develop their ideas into experiments, use the results to guide subsequent work and lead the preparation of research papers. They will also contribute to intellectual property arising from the research. The is a fixed term role for 12 months, with the possibility to extend subject to securing further funding. About you: The ideal candidate will hold a PhD in chemistry, physics, materials science, biochemistry, biophysics or a related discipline. You will have practical experience of working with nanoparticles and modifying or functionalising their surfaces. A background in synthetic chemistry would be welcome, especially where this has involved surface modification, coatings or bioconjugation. You will have a strong publication record, and be able to demonstrate your own contributions. You will enjoy working closely with colleagues and collaborators, contributing your expertise to connect new sensing materials with biological applications. You should be comfortable using microscopes and experienced or motivated to learn relevant optical and biological methods. Experience of bioassay development or optical characterisation of molecular materials or nanoparticles would be advantageous. Previous experience in quantum sensing is not essential What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our long-term commitment and 'beacon' status in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https: //www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49059": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Next-Generation Urban Climate Modelling",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07907 About us: The UCL Department of Risk and Disaster Reduction (RDR) inspires innovation and evidence-based practice focused on improving disaster risk reduction (DRR) through our research, education, and partnerships. This creates a better life for many people by reducing the negative impacts of disasters. The RDR is an exciting cross-UCL department, which leads research, knowledge exchange and teaching in the fields of risk and disaster reduction and humanitarian action. By providing a focus for UCL’s activities the RDR, with its breadth of disciplinary emphasis, promotion of novel multidisciplinary research and translation into practice, aims to assume a role of leadership both in the UK and internationally. The Department has 30 members of academic and teaching staff, 12 members of research staff ranging from research assistants to professorial research fellows and 9 members of professional services staff. The Department is in the Faculty of Mathematics and Physical Sciences (MAPS), but works across all UCL’s faculties, and contributes to UCL’s Grand Challenges. The RDR strives to produce world-leading research in disaster risk reduction through a multidisciplinary perspective for the long-term benefit of humanity. The RDR has established the Humanitarian Institute, Centre for Digital Public Health in Emergencies, Centre for Gender and Disaster, and jointly with UCL Department of Science and Technology Studies the Warning Research Centre. The RDR conducts research around the themes of disaster risk reduction and resilience, cascading crises, natural hazards, climate change risk and adaptation, humanitarian crisis response, GIS and remote sensing, law and economics, conflict and migration, catastrophe modelling, warning, digital health in emergencies, and inclusivity including gender responsive resilience, in order to integrate education, research, innovation and enterprise for the long-term benefit of humanity. The RDR aims to develop leaders and experts in disaster risk reduction research, education, and practice. We run PhD and MRes programmes in Risk and Disaster Reduction, MSc programmes in Risk, Disaster and Resilience and Risk and Disaster Science, and an undergraduate BSc programme in Global Humanitarian Studies, all with a diverse and cross-disciplinary international intake. We have about 25 PhD students, 90 masters students and will have about 180 students across the three years of the BSc programme from 2023-2024 academic year. The RDR seeks to empower all to break down barriers to global DRR knowledge. This knowledge exchange is a core part of the mission of the RDR, which it seeks to fulfil by publishing high-impact reports, engagement with the media and building long-term partnerships. RDR members have joined and led several field missions and contributed to multidisciplinary reports. These reports have been drawn on by the UK Cabinet Office and other government bodies in advice to ministers. RDR staff contributed substantially to the UCL-Lancet Commission on Migration and Health. The RDR has close links with London First, the business representative grouping, private companies, London Resilience and UK Government, including the Cabinet Office, and is developing links with intergovernmental organizations such as the International Migration Organization, Plan International, UN Women and other NGOs. UCL RDR runs regular events to engage the public and potential partners in issues relating to risk and disaster reduction and humanitarian action, such as its Annual Conference and Humanitarian Summit. About the role: The Research Fellow is responsible for the scientific integrity and the institutional memory of SUEWS through a period of substantial technical change. They will establish the evidence that the model is correct. A working benchmark already tests each release against observations; the postholder takes it from one reference site to a set spanning contrasting climates, decides what counts as a pass, and sets out in one place, for the first time, what the model assumes, where those assumptions hold and where they do not. They will also capture reasoning that has never been written down, through a structured interview programme with the model's developers and long-standing users: why particular parameterisations were chosen, what was tried and abandoned, and how the science was actually done. That material becomes the training and onboarding route through which new contributors join the project. The postholder will work closely with the PI and with the Research Software Engineer at Reading, and is expected to publish from this work and present it internationally. About you: You will hold a PhD in a discipline relevant to urban climate, environmental modelling or scientific computing, and be able to move confidently between observational data and model output. You will be confident working with scientific code and data, and will have a track record of publishing your own research. We care more about whether you can tell a right result from a wrong one than about how much code you have written, and we expect and welcome the use of AI coding assistants. You will write clearly for readers of very different backgrounds, from model developers to local authority users, and you will be comfortable drawing knowledge out of experienced colleagues and recording it faithfully. We are interested in capability rather than a particular career path: urban climate expertise reaches this field from architecture, public health, engineering and computing as often as from meteorology, and we welcome applications from any of those routes. Please note that this post leads validation, documentation and knowledge transfer rather than core code development, which is led by the Research Software Engineer at the University of Reading. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days per annum) per annum • Additional 5 days' annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance. To find out more, visit www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: We know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL's workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48390": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Reactor Design, Operation and Monitoring",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07844 About us: UCL Department of Chemical Engineering is one of the top research and teaching departments in the UK and has world-class standing. The department offers undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, and has an extensive research portfolio across a wealth of areas, from molecular scale to complex systems. About the role: The successful candidate will conduct experimental and theoretical/computational research for the Sustainable Open-loop Large-scale Inter-seasonal Thermochemical Heat Storage (SOLIS) project. The research will implement reactor design (up to pilot scale) with heat integration, and multiphase flow analysis. The post holder will lead the design and construction of a pilot scale reactor, collaborate with other researchers within the SOLIS project and International Partners, to extend the impact of the research to other problems related to sustainable development. This is a fixed term role until from 1st January 2027 to 30th of September 2029 in the first instance. Salary is £46,261-£48,679 per annum inclusive of London allowance. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. About you: The successful candidate will hold a PhD in chemical or mechanical engineering, materials science, physical sciences or related. The post holder will have experience in reactor design & construction, P&ID, experimentation and analysis, as well as experience in either fluidization, powder technologies and reaction engineering. Hands on experience on reactor assembling and risk assessment is essential. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, please contact massimiliano.materazzi.09@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact chemeng.safety@ucl.ac.uk . The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our long-term commitment and 'beacon' status in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48554": {
      "title": "Research Fellow – Extragalactic Observations of the High-Redshift Universe",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07866 About us: The Astrophysics Group is one of the largest in the UK, comprising about 130 members of academic, research and support staff, of which 21 are academic staff members. Our research areas cover galaxy formation and evolution, cosmology, gravitational waves, massive stars, star formation and stellar evolution, interstellar and circumstellar processes, exo-planetary and planetary science, atmospheric physics and instrumentation. UCL's Department of Physics & Astronomy has a strong track record of investment and scientific leadership in facilities such as JWST, DES, DESI, 4MOST, Euclid, Ariel, Rubin-LSST, PFS, JCMT, Litebird as well as participation at co-I level of instruments on large explorer mission concepts such as Cassini and JUICE with participation in many other mission and instrument concept designs. UCL is one of the world's top universities based in the heart of London. It is a modern, outward-looking institution. UCL's staff and former students have included 33 Nobel prize winners. It is a truly international community: more than one-third of our student body – more than 50,000 strong – come from 150 countries and nearly one-third of staff are from outside the UK. The Department of Physics &; Astronomy is located in the heart of the historical area of Bloomsbury. Scientific research and study has been a strong feature of UCL since its inception in 1826 and the Department is currently one of the top ranked Physics departments in the country, and indeed the world. We have forged an internationally renowned reputation for being at the forefront of world leading research and providing high class teaching. Those joining us can expect to be part of a dynamic community of Physicists and Astronomers whose enthusiasm for science and innovation is exceptional. About the role: This position is for a 3-year PDRA (starting date between 1st January and 1st April 2027, by mutual agreement) in the Astrophysics Group of UCL’s Department of Physics & Astronomy, working in Dr. Guido Roberts-Borsani’s group on galaxy formation and evolution in the first billion years with the James Webb Space Telescope, as part of the OMEGA project funded by a European Research Council (ERC) Starting Grant. The successful applicant will carry out high-impact research on the interstellar medium, stellar, and black hole properties of high-redshift galaxies, using JWST spectroscopy from the PI's approved programmes (in particular DAJE-JWST and OMEGA-JWST) to advance our understanding of galaxy properties and chemical enrichment from cosmic reionization to cosmic dawn. Depending on the project's needs and the candidate's expertise, the research will address one or more of OMEGA's core themes: the ionisation structure of the interstellar medium and robust metallicity measurements; the physical origins of carbon, nitrogen, and oxygen (CNO) abundance ratios; the burstiness and modes of star formation at high redshift; and the pace at which early galaxies built up their chemical reservoirs. In addition to producing first-author publications, the PDRA is also expected to develop their own research ideas and telescope proposals, engage and coordinate with the PI’s existing collaborations, contribute to the social and intellectual life of the wider Astrophysics Group, and support the supervision of PhD students working on related themes. All applications require the following documents compiled as a single PDF (submitted as the mandatory CV attachment in the application portal): Cover letter CV Research statement (max 3 pages) List of publications All applications also require 2 letters of reference to be sent separately by the referees via email to Guido Roberts-Borsani (g.robertsborsani@ucl.ac.uk) For any enquiries related to the position, please contact Guido Roberts-Borsani (g.robertsborsani@ucl.ac.uk) or Kay Nakum (k.nakum@ucl.ac.uk). About you: The successful candidate will hold (or be close to completing) a PhD in astrophysics, with a strong track record of observational extragalactic research and publications in leading peer-reviewed journals. They will have experience analysing spectroscopic and/or photometric data from JWST (experience with other major observatories such as HST, Keck, VLT, or ALMA, is a bonus), and strong working knowledge of ISM physics of high-redshift galaxies. Programming and data-analysis skills are essentials, as is the ability to plan and prioritise their own research, work well under tight deadlines, coordinating between international collaborations, and deliver high-quality results. Experience presenting published works at conferences and preparing successful telescope proposals is considered an advantage. What we offer: Starting salary offered in the range £46,261 – £54,019 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion. Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48546": {
      "title": "Research Fellow:  3DTreePrint Project",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03278 About us: University College London (UCL) is one of the world's top universities. Based in the heart of London, it is a dynamic, outward-looking institution. At its establishment in 1826, UCL was radical and responsive to the needs of society, and this ethos – that excellence should go hand-in-hand with enriching society – continues today. The UCL Department of Geography is a leading UK and global department, with a heritage of excellence in research, renowned for its innovative and interdisciplinary research to understanding complex global challenges. It brings together expertise in physical and human geography to explore critical issues such as climate change, sustainable development, and social justice. The department fosters an interdisciplinary approach, equipping students and researchers with the skills to critically engage with the complex interactions between people, places, and environments. About the role: This post sits within ARIA's The Resilient Climate and Ecosystems opportunity seed route. ARIA (Advanced Research + Innovation Agency) is a UK research funding agency that supports ambitious research at the edge of the possible. Its opportunity spaces identify underexplored areas where new scientific and engineering capabilities could lead to major breakthroughs. This post sits within the ARIA's The Resilient Climate and Ecosystems opportunity seed route. Opportunity seeds provide funding of up to £500,000 for individual research teams to pursue high-potential, early-stage ideas aligned with an opportunity space but outside the scope of a larger programme. The seed mechanism is intended to give research teams the flexibility to explore new technical pathways that might otherwise fall between conventional funding routes. We are seeking a research fellow who will join the team and will work on the 3DTreePrint project led by Dr Martin Mokroš at UCL Geography. The 3DTreePrint project pairs low-cost LiDAR with automated workflows to make detailed 3D measurement of trees affordable, repeatable and scalable, building “3D structural fingerprints” to track ecosystem resilience over time. Main partners of the project are Forest Research, Forestry England, Earthwatch Europe and Polish Academy of Sciences. The full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. This is a full time, fixed term post with funding available until 31 May 2028 . The appointment is available to start on the 1st of December 2026 . Funding is available at Grade 7, spine point 32 (including London Allowance). Informal enquiries may be made to Dr Martin Mokroš (m.mokros@ucl.ac.uk) If you have any queries regarding the application process, or if you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, please contact the department’s research administration team: geog.research@ucl.ac.uk About you: The successful candidate will have a PhD (awarded or close to submission) in computer science, robotics, geomatics, remote sensing, forest science or a closely related discipline, with a substantial computational component. You will also need to demonstrate : • Substantial experience of processing point-cloud data from terrestrial and/or mobile laser scanning in forest, woodland or other structurally complex vegetated environments. • Advanced programming skills in Python and/or C++, including the ability to read, adapt, extend, debug and test existing open-source codebases rather than only applying them as supplied. Please see the full job description and person specification at the bottom of this page full more information. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum time in site to be determined between successful candidate and line manager. We will consider applications to work on a flexible and job share basis wherever possible . This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave plus 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Department is committed to the values of equality, diversity and inclusion and we have an active Equality, Diversity and Inclusion Committee, and networks for BME, LGBTQ, and disabled/neurodiverse staff and students. The Department recognises the impor tance of family - friendly working, and we try, as far as possible, to organise meetings and events with staff responsibilities as parents and carers in mind. In 2022, the Department was awarded an Athena SWAN Silver award in recognition of our advancement of gender equality: representation , progression, and success. Above all the Department aims to be a supportive environment for both its staff and students. More information can be found here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/social - historical - sciences/geography/about/equality - diversity - and - inclusion"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48690": {
      "title": "Research Fellow: UCL Atmospheric Chemistry Research Group",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03294 About us: University College London (UCL) is one of the world's top universities. Based in the heart of London, it is a dynamic, outward-looking institution. At its establishment in 1826, UCL was radical and responsive to the needs of society, and this ethos – that excellence should go hand-in-hand with enriching society – continues today. The UCL Department of Geography is a leading UK and global department, with a heritage of excellence in research, renowned for its innovative and interdisciplinary research to understanding complex global challenges. It brings together expertise in physical and human geography to explore critical issues such as climate change, sustainable development, and social justice. The department fosters an interdisciplinary approach, equipping students and researchers with the skills to critically engage with the complex interactions between people, places, and environments. About the role: This post will be part of a team of researchers at UCL and MIT investigating the ability to improve estimates of pollutant emissions from rocket launches to better constrain the atmospheric impacts of this emerging pollution source. The position is funded by a Leverhulme Research Project Grant awarded to Prof Marais in June this year ( https://www.leverhulme.ac.uk/listings?field_grant_scheme_target_id=15 ). The Research Fellow will have the exciting opportunity to learn to use and adapt an existing plume model to simulate pollution from rockets during launch. This will then provide the synthetic observations needed to validate top-down emission inversion estimates from space-based remote sensing instruments. The Research Fellow will also gather datasets of launch locations and timings, and of rocket characteristics to (i) mine satellite data for coincidences between launches and instrument overpasses or scans, (ii) interpret key factors affecting pollutant emissions from launches, and (iii) maintain UCL’s world-leading online tracker of rocket launch emissions ( https://orbitalemissions.org/ ). The full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. This is a full time, fixed term post with funding available for 2.5 years. The appointment is available to start from 1st January 2027. Funding is available at Grade 7, spine point 32-33 (including London Allowance). Informal enquiries may be made to Prof Eloise Marais ( e.marais@ucl.ac.uk ) If you have any queries regarding the application process, or if you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, please contact the department’s research administration team: geog.research@ucl.ac.uk About you: The successful candidate will have a PhD in a relevant research field and will need to demonstrate : • Fundamental science knowledge in a related discipline • Experience conducting independent research • Computer coding in a Unix environment Please see the full job description and person specification at the bottom of this page full more information. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum time in site to be determined between successful candidate and line manager. We will consider applications to work on a flexible and job share basis wherever possible . This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave plus 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Department is committed to the values of equality, diversity and inclusion and we have an active Equality, Diversity and Inclusion Committee, and networks for BME, LGBTQ, and disabled/neurodiverse staff and students. The Department recognises the impor tance of family - friendly working, and we try, as far as possible, to organise meetings and events with staff responsibilities as parents and carers in mind. In 2022, the Department was awarded an Athena SWAN Silver award in recognition of our advancement of gender equality: representation , progression, and success. Above all the Department aims to be a supportive environment for both its staff and students. More information can be found here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/social - historical - sciences/geography/about/equality - diversity - and - inclusion"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48920": {
      "title": "Research Nurse in Infection and Sexual Health",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11376 About us: The UCL Institute for Global Health (IGH) is at the heart of UCL’s Grand Challenge of Global Health. The Institute’s vision is for a world where international policy on global health is informed by world class research. IGH aims to support the development of robust solutions to aspects of the world’s major global health challenges through scholarly outputs, education, public engagement, translational research and ultimately by influencing public policy and professional practice. This post is based in the UCL Centre for Clinical Research in Infection and Sexual Health. The Centre has a research programme covering a wide range of studies from observational cohort studies, qualitative and mixed methods studies to intervention trials. Historically most of the work has focussed on HIV and other sexually transmitted infections including viral hepatitis. However, our portfolio of research extends to other infectious diseases research particularly TB, COVID-19, and Mpox. The centre collaborates with other centres in IGH to provide the support needed to conduct clinical research. We work closely with the Centre for Population Research in Sexual Health and HIV on research concerning digital health and pathways for care in sexual health. In the case of TB, our work is closely linked with the Centre for Molecular Epidemiology and Translational Research, the Centre for Pragmatic Global Health Trials and the MRC Clinical Trials Unit at UCL. These centres host a number of infectious disease studies (including TB) as part of a programme of transdisciplinary global health research. We have an exciting opportunity for an enthusiastic and highly motivated Research Nurse to join our dynamic team and gain broad experience in a clinical research setting. About the role: The postholder will be part of the clinical research team, currently comprised of six research nurses, five research assistants, doctors, and other non-clinical members. The post holder will work collaboratively with Principal Investigators, co investigators and all staff involved with clinical research across the Centre and affiliated NHS Trusts. The post holder will ensure oversight of studies, conduct risk assessments, contribute to education, training and clinical management within the clinical research team and support nurse revalidation. There is the need for visibility, flexibility, expert communication, and teaching skills to engender understanding and support for the research processes. Research nurses are responsible for the oversight of a portfolio of studies, and lead in all communication to support recruitment, the reporting of clinical events, the collection of quality controlled data and effective communication with external sponsors. The postholder will also contribute to networking across the trust as well the wider community to expand on recruitment into research. Research nurses in the team may be allocated to any of the portfolio of 15-20 current studies. They will have an honorary NHS contract and are able to remain on the NHS pension scheme if already a member. The role is full time (i.e. 36.5 hours per week) and is available immediately for 12 months in the first instance. An acceptable enhanced DBS check is required to carry out this role. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Salary range: £43,981 - £54,019 per annum (including London Allowance), depending on experience. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Ebun Taiwo (Senior Research Nurse) at e.taiwo@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the IGH HR Team on IGH.HR@ucl.ac.uk . About you: The post holder must have a current professional registration with the Nursing and Midwifery Council (NMC) and evidence of commitment to continuous professional development. They must have completed Good Clinical Practice training or commit to undertake this on appointment (training will be provided). They must also have experience of clinical research/clinical trials and/or gathering and analysing research data. Knowledge of sexual health, infectious diseases and blood-borne virus infection and understanding of data protection and confidentiality issues are essential. The ideal candidate will have experience of working in sexual health, infectious diseases and/or HIV/BBV and they will have excellent organisational, time management and prioritisation skills. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48561": {
      "title": "Research Software Engineer in Bioinformatics",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07809 About us: The UCL Bioinformatics Group is well-known for developing a widely-used suite of web-based tools based on a number of cutting edge protein structure prediction methods. Our web services have been online since 1998 and the methods available allow users to predict a variety of protein structural features, including secondary structure and natively disordered regions, protein domain boundaries and 3D models of tertiary structure. Recently we have been developing new services to assist users in prediction gene function structural domain segmentation - which we believe are vital and unique tool for biologists. Our flagship service, The PSIPRED Workbench, currently unites all our services e.g. predictive methods, online tutorials and common look and feel. Through prior BBSRC BBR grants, we have successfully integrated our suite of tools into a single web portal. In our upcoming BBSRC grant we wish to extend this integration philosophy across multiple resources. The BBSRC funded IntegraTED project is aimed at developing a new Bioinformatics web resource hosted at UCL. Over the past 30 years the UCL Bioinformatics Group (dept of Computer Science) and the CATH Project (dept of Structural & Molecular Biology) have developed world leading web resources in structural bioinformatics. In this project we are looking to bring together three of our most important resources to develop a single, highly integrated and streamlined web platform. The aim is to provide protein structure and function researchers access to an unrivalled online research platform. The three resources involved are; the PSIPRED workbench, an online suite of machine learning based tools for analysing protein structure, The CATH-Gene3D structural classification of protein domains and The Encyclopaedia of Domains (TED) our new resource cataloguing all detectable structural domains in AlphaFoldDB. About the role: In this project you will take the lead on the UCL Computer Science Bioinformatics Group’s portion of the IntegraTED project. There are three principle objectives in this portion of the project. In the first objective we wish to increase the reliability and performance of the existing ML workflows that underpin the PSIPRED workbench. One target here is to integrate novel bioinformatics foundation models to accelerate methods already available via the PSIPRED Workbench. The Second major objective is to work extensively on a new web interface for the IntegraTED resource. Here you will work closely with the CATH team on our planned unified web and data portal. The third major objective is to work on the TED data generation pipeline. The successful applicant for this project will work on all of these theme areas with the possibility of working on novel ML applications in this space and will importantly take the lead on developing and maintaining the PSIPRED Workbench. This role is funded until 31st August 2029 in the first instance. About you: The ideal applicant will be a developer with a strong interested in building Scientific Software and prior experience of software development within academia. You should have the ability and enthusiasm to acquire new skills across the ML-Ops and Bioinformatics fields. We expect good software development skills in a least one language regularly used in scientific computing (i.e. Julia, Python, etc) and you should have an interest in good practice in software development (e.g. source control, testing, etc). We focus mainly on Python but adaptability across languages is a bonus. Eventually, you will be expected to lead the development of large long standing software engineering project, so an interest in developing skill in application design is a must. What we offer: In return, you’ll have the opportunity to make a significant impact within Student Lifecycle portfolio, working alongside talented colleagues. We offer a collaborative environment where your ideas are valued, your growth is supported, and your work directly contributes to the university’s bold strategic goals. Our people really are our greatest asset and we invest in them so that they can fulfil their potential. Equity, diversity and inclusion is important to us. We are committed to creating an environment where everyone is encouraged to give their best in a place where their unique experiences, perspectives and skills are seen as valuable assets. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Flexible working options Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department is working towards an Athena SWAN award. We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Work at UCL pages: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48334": {
      "title": "Research Technical Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Laboratory Technical and Lab Support; UCL East; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11236 About us: UCL Division of Biosciences is one of the world’s foremost centres for research and teaching in the biological sciences and one of the largest Divisions within UCL, undertaking a significant amount of research and teaching. The Division has a diverse portfolio addressing all areas of biology from protein interactions to cell function, organism development, genetics, population studies, to ecology and the environment. Computational modelling approaches are frequently used alongside experimental research programmes and much of our research crosses traditional boundaries, including the relationship of biodiversity to the health of the planet. Activity is underpinned by high calibre science technology platforms and state of the art equipment. Educational activity includes a range of undergraduate programmes, an expanding number of Masters Programmes and a substantial number of postgraduate research students. We are now recruiting a Research Technical Officer position to provide technical support for the Agritech Platform and People & Nature Laboratory to support our cross-disciplinary research, teaching and external engagement based the new UCL East campus About the role: In this pivotal role, this position will provide technical support for teaching, research and public engagement activities related to biodiversity, environmental change and human health. The successful candidate will support a variety of complex projects, including those involving (1) vertical farming, greenhouse, bee labs and roof garden experiments; (2) eDNA and environmental genetics; (3) sensor technologies; (4) field-based ecological research and (5) public engagement through citizen science. Working closely with other technical and academic staff, the postholder will play a crucial role in developing and supporting the small technical team, physical spaces, digital assets, field sites, equipment, environmental genetics protocols and procedures at the UCL East Agritech Platform, the People & Nature Lab and wider engagement activities across the Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park. Being both a research and student-facing post, the post-holder will play a crucial role in delivering an excellent student experience and is ideally suited to someone who thrives in an interactive learning environment and is skilled in developing innovative practical-made solutions, while also maintaining safe and organised workspaces. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/humanresources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Dr Reshma Tilwani (reshma.tilwani@ucl.ac.uk). If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR Administrator. About you: You will have an undergraduate degree, or equivalent experience in a relevant Biological or Agricultural Sciences subject area, and have technical awareness of greenhouses, vertical farms, bee labs and a diverse range of ecological laboratory, data science and field techniques and equipment directly relevant to the UCL East Agritech Platform and People & Nature Lab. You would also have experience of supervising staff and in leading a team to deliver technical support provision, and experience of running greenhouses, vertical farms and diverse laboratory, data science and fieldwork environment and adherence to health and safety requirements. Experience in teaching, mentoring, and demonstrating skills or crafts to staff/students and the wider public would also be an advantage. What we offer: The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our division holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48238": {
      "title": "Research Technician",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11226 About us: The Division of Biosciences at UCL is one of the world's foremost centres for research and teaching in the biological sciences. We have an outstanding international reputation and a community of over 500 staff and more than 300 PhD students engaged in cutting edge research and collaboration. Further information can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/biosciences We are seeking an enthusiastic researcher with a background in evo-devo/functional genomics to join the group of Dr. Sean Stankowski. The position is well funded through a UKRI Future Leaders Fellowship. About the role: The Research Technician will contribute to research projects focused on understanding the genomic basis of adaption and speciation, primarily in intertidal snails from the genus Littorina . ( https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.adi2982 ). They will take the lead in implementing protocols developed by the Darwin Tree of Life project ( https://www.sanger.ac.uk/programme/tree-of-life/ ) to extract high molecular weight DNA and prepare long read sequencing libraries (ONT/PacBio), and assist with DNA/RNA extraction and DNA library prep for Illumina sequencing projects. Other tasks may include assistance with snail rearing, general organisation in the lab, and training of staff and students. Please note, this role is available on a full time (36.5 hours per week) basis for up to 2-years, or part time basis (18.25 - 27.4 hours per week) for 3-years, with possibility of extension. The researcher will work as part of a team that includes two Research Fellows, and also in collaboration with project partners from the Sanger Institute (Dra. Joana Meier), the Institute of Science and Technology Austria (Dr. Nick Barton), The University of Gothenburg (Dr. Kerstin Johannesson). A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Interviews will be held in November 2026. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Sean Stankowski at s.stankowski@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR Administrator (fls.hr@ucl.ac.uk) stating the advert reference number in the subject line. About you: The ideal applicant will have experience with the development and customisation of DNA extraction protocols, QC, and experience with long-read and illumina sequencing library preparation. Experience operating an ONT sequencer and the downstream processing of long and short-read sequence data would be a plus. What we offer: We will support your professional development to help you achieve your long-term goals. and will provide access to training courses and international and domestic conferences. The position will include time and funds to facilitate training, and opportunities to work with and visit project partners at other research institutions. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our division holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48876": {
      "title": "Senior Administrator",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; President and Provost (A01) Reference: A01-00098 About us: UCL’s Office of the President & Provost supports the President & Provost across the full range of their responsibilities as the academic leader of the university. The team provides high-level logistical, administrative, executive and policy support, enabling the President & Provost and their leadership team to deliver strategic priorities and lead change across UCL. The office operates in a fast-paced, high-profile and highly sensitive environment , working closely with colleagues across UCL and with external stakeholders. The President & Provost represents UCL nationally and internationally and engages with governments, organisations and other stakeholders across the higher education sector. The team works collaboratively with UCL’s Business Operations Service (BOS) and the wider network of Executive and Personal Assistants. Together, they provide coordinated, professional and effective support to senior leaders across the university. The environment requires professionalism, discretion, confidentiality, flexibility and excellent relationship-building skills , particularly when working with senior colleagues and high-profile internal and external stakeholders. About the role: This is a varied and challenging new role at the heart of the President & Provost's Team. As Senior Administrator, you will play a pivotal role in the smooth running of the team's executive support provision, providing high-level support to the Chief of Staff and Deputy Chief of Staff. You will take a strategic approach to their forward schedules. You will handle sensitive issues and correspondence, prepare briefings, and manage preparation for and follow-up from key meetings, committees and working groups. You will also coordinate their special projects and their engagement with the wider senior leadership team. You will manage projects, away days and events led by the Chief of Staff from start to finish. This includes research and horizon-scanning, stakeholder engagement, logistics (venues, catering, AV and travel), briefing papers and correspondence, agendas, records of decisions and actions, budgets, and evaluation of what worked. You will act as a conduit between the Chief of Staff and the team and maintain a forward look and yearly cycle of activity that maps governance deadlines, reporting cycles and key milestones. That way the team can anticipate peaks and clashes rather than react to them. You will also support the P&P core team's weekly meetings, the wider performance cycle (including senior leadership team objectives and appraisals), and, working with the governance team, the team's engagement with formal governance committees. You will assist the diary secretaries with strategic direction and line manage the Administrator, working in tandem with colleagues across the executive support function. This a chance to support the top level of the institution in a complex, fast-moving and highly sensitive environment, where you can make a real difference to how time and impact are used. This role is based at UCL's Bloomsbury Campus. If you have any questions about the role or the application process, please contact Passant Khalifa (Senior Recruiter) at p.khalifa@ucl.ac.uk . About you: You are a pragmatic, focused and highly organised professional with a can-do attitude and a keen eye for detail. You are used to providing senior administrative and/or secretarial support to leaders in a complex organisation, and you plan ahead by anticipating needs, dependencies and next steps rather than waiting to be asked. You are comfortable challenging a request when it's the right thing to do, and you stay calm and adaptable when priorities shift or something unexpected lands at the last minute. You are confident managing a demanding workload, and you bring analytical and problem-solving skills to it. You can work independently, make sense of complex issues even when information is incomplete, and are comfortable where there is no clear precedent. You draft clear, timely and fit-for-purpose communications, briefings and reports. You are proficient with standard office and productivity tools, including PowerPoint and a project or task management tool such as Microsoft Planner, and use them to produce high-quality materials and coordinate work effectively. Discretion and diplomacy are essential, and you will be trusted with confidential and sensitive information. You communicate tactfully and assertively, build effective relationships across UCL and externally, and enjoy working as a supportive, service-minded team player across different functions and ways of working. You have some experience of, or are comfortable with, line managing and working in tandem with diary secretaries and other executive support colleagues. Experience in line managing a diary secretary or manager, optimising office processes and digital tools, or delivering a welcoming front-of-house experience would be an advantage. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48270": {
      "title": "Senior Category Manager (Infrastructure)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Commercial and Procurement; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00349 About us: UCL is London’s leading multidisciplinary university and one of the world’s most successful research institutions, with more than 18,000 staff and over 51,000 students from more than 150 countries. Our Commercial & Procurement function manages more than £900 million of third-party expenditure each year and plays an important role in ensuring UCL achieves excellent commercial outcomes while supporting our wider strategic and sustainability ambitions. Within Estates Development, we support the continued investment, development and improvement of UCL’s diverse and complex estate. Effective infrastructure is fundamental to the operation of the university, supporting the environments in which our students learn, our researchers undertake world-leading work. We are strengthening our commercial capability and are looking for an experienced Senior Category Manager – Infrastructure to help shape how UCL procures and manages strategically important infrastructure requirements. About the role: As Senior Category Manager – Infrastructure, you will take ownership of developing and delivering commercial strategies across our maintenance and infrastructure category. Reporting to the Head of Category – Estate Infrastructure, you will work closely with senior stakeholders to understand their priorities and commercial challenges, translating these into effective commercial solutions. You will act as the framework manager for the maintenance and infrastructure contracts, working to ensure suppliers deliver performance and value for our Hard FM category. You will provide contract advice, while managing risk and supplier innovation You will lead complex procurement activity from initial strategy and market engagement through to competition, negotiation and contract award. This will include determining appropriate routes to market, developing contracting and negotiation strategies and leading the delivery of frameworks, competitions and other commercial solutions. You will take a strategic view of the category, using commercial insight and data to identify opportunities to improve value, drive efficiencies and deliver cost savings. You will also act as a trusted commercial adviser to senior stakeholders, providing guidance on procurement, contractual, commercial and compliance matters and ensuring commercial considerations are embedded into infrastructure planning. This is an opportunity to influence how a major organisation approaches infrastructure procurement and to help develop commercial solutions that balance value, quality, risk, sustainability and long-term operational requirements. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will be an experienced procurement and commercial professional with experience of leading complex procurement activity and delivering commercial outcomes. Ideally, your experience will include maintenance, infrastructure, engineering, utilities, or another technically complex environment, although we are particularly interested in the commercial capability and transferable experience you can bring. You will have experience developing and implementing robust procurements and contracts and be a confident negotiator who has delivered complex, large-scale commercial solutions. You will be comfortable operating within a large and complex organisation and working with a broad range of stakeholders. You will have the credibility to influence senior colleagues, constructively challenge requirements and build strong relationships. Strong analytical skills and commercial judgement will be important. You will be educated to degree level or have equivalent professional experience. Further professional development and accredited membership of the Chartered Institute of Procurement & Supply (CIPS) would be advantageous. If you are looking for an opportunity to combine strategic procurement expertise with the challenge of supporting a large, complex and evolving estate, we would be delighted to hear from you. Please read our job description, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure these are covered in your application. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent.We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48300": {
      "title": "Senior Category Manager (Major Works)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Commercial and Procurement; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00348 About us: UCL is London’s leading multidisciplinary university and one of the world’s most successful research institutions, with more than 18,000 staff and over 51,000 students from more than 150 countries. Our Commercial & Procurement function manages an annual third-party spend of more than £900 million and plays a critical role in ensuring UCL achieves excellent commercial outcomes while supporting our wider strategic and sustainability ambitions. Within Estates Development, we support the investment and development of UCL’s diverse estate. We are now looking for a Senior Category Manager – Major Works to help shape and deliver our commercial approach across this vitally important area. About the role: As Senior Category Manager – Major Works, you will take a leading role in developing and delivering procurement and commercial strategies that support UCL’s Estates Development priorities. Working closely with the Head of Category – Estates Development, you will build strong relationships with senior stakeholders, developing a detailed understanding of their requirements and translating these into effective category, sourcing and contracting strategies. You will lead complex procurement activity from strategy through to contract award, developing appropriate routes to market, commercial models, frameworks and competitions. You will provide expert commercial and contractual advice, lead negotiations and ensure solutions deliver strong operational outcomes, value for money and appropriate management of commercial risk. You'll also influence senior stakeholders and help ensure commercial considerations are embedded into the planning and delivery of major works activity. This is an opportunity to work client-side within a large and complex organisation and have a tangible impact on how UCL procures and delivers investment across its estate. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will be an experienced procurement and commercial professional with significant experience of leading complex, high-value procurement activity. You may currently be working as a Senior Category Manager, Procurement Manager, Commercial Manager, Senior Procurement Manager or in a comparable commercial role within construction, infrastructure, property or a major capital programme environment. You will have a strong track record of developing procurement and contracting strategies, running robust competitive procurement processes and negotiating complex commercial agreements. You will be commercially astute and able to identify opportunities, develop compelling cases for change and translate commercial strategy into successful delivery. You will be confident operating in a large, complex organisation and engaging with a diverse range of stakeholders. Experience of working with major works, large-scale construction, infrastructure or capital investment supply chains will be particularly valuable, alongside an understanding of the commercial considerations associated with complex projects and supplier markets. Please read our job description carefully, paying particular attention the the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure these are covered in your application. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent.We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49033": {
      "title": "Senior Category Manager - INTERNAL OPPORTUNITY OPEN TO UCL EMPLOYEES ONLY",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Commercial and Procurement; London; £68,284-£78,386​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00401 About us: UCL is London's leading multidisciplinary university, bringing together more than 13,000 staff and 42,000 students from over 150 countries. Renowned for excellence in teaching and world-leading research, UCL is committed to delivering an ambitious strategic vision for 2034. Our Commercial & Procurement function plays a vital role in supporting this vision, managing over £600 million in annual third-party expenditure. We aim to deliver an outstanding procurement service that achieves value for money, drives commercial excellence and supports UCL's Sustainable Procurement Strategy. We are looking for an experienced commercial leader who shares our commitment to innovation, sustainability and continuous improvement, and who can help transform our procurement approach to deliver lasting value for the university. About the role: As Senior Category Manager, you will lead the Travel, Meetings, Incentives, Conferences and Events (MICE), Contingent Labour, and External Engagement categories, including communications and marketing. You will develop and deliver category strategies that meet stakeholder needs, generate targeted savings and improve commercial and operational performance. Working closely with senior stakeholders across UCL, you will provide expert commercial advice, lead complex sourcing and negotiation activities, and identify opportunities to improve efficiency, manage demand and strengthen supplier relationships. You will also support the Director of Corporate Services in delivering the wider strategic roadmap. Key responsibilities include: Developing and implementing category strategies aligned with UCL's commercial objectives, stakeholder priorities and sustainability commitments. Building strong relationships with senior stakeholders and acting as the principal point of contact for your categories. Leading strategic sourcing, complex negotiations, contract development, supplier frameworks and preferred supplier lists. Identifying and delivering cost savings, efficiencies and innovative commercial solutions. Providing expert advice on commercial, contractual and procurement compliance matters. Using spend analytics and commercial data to identify opportunities, build business cases and support evidence-based decisions. Driving transformation, continuous improvement and new ways of working across procurement and stakeholder teams. Leading, coaching and developing team members, fostering a high-performing and collaborative culture. Supporting the delivery of UCL's wider Corporate Services strategic objectives. This is an opportunity to influence strategic decision-making, deliver measurable commercial benefits and contribute to the long-term success of a world-leading university. About you: You are a commercially astute procurement professional with significant senior-level experience in category management, strategic sourcing and commercial transformation. You can translate complex business needs into effective procurement strategies, deliver successful negotiations and build credible business cases that achieve measurable results. You will bring: Significant commercial and procurement experience, including leadership, transformation and change management. Experience across Corporate Services categories, ideally including contingent labour, travel, MICE and marketing. A proven track record of developing and implementing robust sourcing strategies and contracts, including complex, large-scale commercial solutions. Strong negotiation, influencing and stakeholder-management skills, with the confidence to engage effectively with senior leaders. Experience working within large, complex and decentralised organisations with diverse stakeholder groups. Strong commercial data literacy, including the ability to interpret spend analytics and use tools such as Excel and Power BI to inform decisions. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to present complex information clearly and persuasively. Strong organisational skills, sound judgement and the ability to manage competing priorities. A collaborative, innovative and evidence-based approach to problem-solving and continuous improvement. You will also be passionate about developing people, building high-performing teams and embedding sustainable, value-driven procurement practices. A relevant degree or equivalent professional experience is essential. Further professional development and membership of the Chartered Institute of Procurement and Supply (CIPS) would be advantageous. If you are ready to lead strategic procurement initiatives, influence senior stakeholders and deliver meaningful commercial impact, we welcome your application to join UCL and help shape the future of our university. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent.We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49061": {
      "title": "Senior Clinical Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11371 About us: The mission of the UCL GOS Institute of Child Health is to maximise and advocate for the holistic health of all children, young people and the adults they will become, through world-class research, education and public engagement. The UCL GOS ICH, together with its clinical partner Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children, forms the largest concentration of children’s health research outside North America. The 2024-29 GOS ICH strategy focuses on its five scientific programmes. GOS ICH’s activities include active engagement with children and families, to ensure that our work is relevant and appropriate to their needs. GOS ICH generates the funding for our research by setting out our proposals in high quality applications to public, charitable and industrial funding bodies and disseminates the results of our research by publication in the medical and scientific literature, to clinicians, policy makers and the wider public. The Institute offers world-class education and training across a wide range of teaching and life learning programmes which address the needs of students and professional groups who are interested in and undertaking work relevant to child health. GOS ICH holds an Athena SWAN Charter Gold Award. About the role: Applications are invited for a Senior Clinical Research Fellow to join the Molecular and Cellular Immunology Section at the UCL Great Ormond Street Institute of Child Health (UCL GOSICH). The post is funded for 12 months. This position is to provide clinical trial support to the internationally renowned advanced therapies group based in Molecular & Cellular Immunology section of Institute of Child Health. It will provide an opportunity to work with cutting edge clinical researchers and interact across inter-disciplinary groups. The gene and cell therapy group are involved in a portfolio of first in man trials involving rare diseases, strategies to improve immune responses to infection, leukaemia after haematopoietic stem cell transplantation, somatic gene therapy for primary immunodeficiencies, HIV and inherited skin disorders. The postholder must have paediatric experience and will be involved with the clinical care of gene therapy trial patients. They will work closely with the immunology and gene therapy research teams. About you: Applicants must have appropriate speciality in a paediatric setting and previous experience in clinical trials. Expertise in gene and cell therapy, molecular biology or immunology and experience working in clinical trials is desirable but not essential. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact Madhur Sharma on ich.hr@ucl.ac.uk quoting job reference"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=47993": {
      "title": "Senior Cloud Engineer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £54,931-£64,644; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05393 About us: Information Services Division (ISD) is the primary provider of IT services to UCL. We support and enhance learning, teaching, research and administrative processes by providing information- and technology-related services to over 50,000 staff and students of UCL and associated institutions. Our ambition is to be the leading IT services group in the Higher Education (HE) sector and we are growing our team’s capability in experience/UX, agile development, security, cloud, service management and partnering. We are modernising our technology foundations, digitising the processes of the university to transform experience for students and staff, and partnering across the university to drive differentiation in UCL education and research. UCL boasts a track record of ground-breaking research that transforms the lives of people around the world. Our teams provide dedicated, specialist support throughout the research and innovation lifecycle to help sustain and advance UCL’s position as a world-class, research-led university. About the role: We are looking for a Senior Cloud Engineer (Enterprise Storage) to provide technical expertise across our enterprise storage and backup services. The successful candidate will join a team that provides technical expertise across a range of core infrastructure platforms that underpin UCL’s critical managed services. These include virtual infrastructure, public cloud platforms, web platforms, load balancers, enterprise storage and backup solutions. In this role you will work with a broad set of technologies, contributing to the design, implementation and continuous improvement of our storage and data protection platforms, helping ensure high levels of resilience, reliability and performance. You will leverage your experience and passion for IT, storage and data protection to support the wider team in driving platform improvements and modernisation, whilst collaborating with product owners and stakeholders across the university to deliver reliable, scalable services that meet UCL’s business needs. About you: You will have practical experience administering Windows or Linux systems within a service-oriented infrastructure environment, with a strong focus on automation and operational efficiency. You will bring strong technical knowledge of enterprise storage and backup technologies, with experience administering and safeguarding resilient data protection platforms across private and public cloud environments. This will include supporting data protection strategies, platform resilience and disaster recovery capabilities. The successful candidate will be expected to demonstrate the ability to manage problems through to resolution, often with uncertain and incomplete information, with an eye for detail and accuracy and an aptitude for analytical and logical methodologies. Have experience of using their specialist knowledge to advise and support team members in their technical activities in both project and operational environments, with an ability to adapt to changing technologies. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48600": {
      "title": "Senior E-resources Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Libraries, museums, theatres and open science; London; £36,433-£42,891​; Vice-Provost (Faculties) (B25) Reference: B25-00302 About us: UCL is London’s global university. LCCOS – Library, Culture, Collections and Open Science – provides outstanding staff, services and resources to support the world-class research, education and enterprise that is undertaken in UCL, and connects the world with UCL through innovative programming and engagement activities. LCCOS is acknowledged as an international leader in Open Science and Scholarship. The LCCOS family comprises 4 museums and allied object collections, 13 libraries, the UCL Student Centre, Special Collections facilities, multiple additional learning spaces and a substantial off-site storage facility. We also provide comprehensive services to healthcare staff through our partnerships with 6 NHS Trusts. LCCOS holds the UK Customer Service Excellence (CSE) Award, The National Archives (TNA) Archives Service Accreditation, and Arts Council England Museum Accreditation. The combined staff in UCL LCCOS totals around 450. About the role: The E-Resources Team is responsible for managing a large and varied collection of e-resources (databases, e-journals and e-books). This includes acquisition, access management, discovery and review. The post holder will ensure UCL’s e-resource holdings and linking information in the library management system (Alma) are accurate and up to date, and they will use their excellent communication skills when responding to user enquiries, and when liaising with publishers, subscription agents and UCL IT support to resolve access issues. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 40% on site. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. This role does not meet the eligibility requirements for a Skilled Worker Visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require right to work in the UK to carry out this role. About you: We are looking for candidates who in particular have the following experience and skills: Experience of periodicals/journals or acquisitions work in a library environment. A demonstrable understanding of the issues involved in acquiring and managing electronic resources. Experience of responding to customer enquiries. Ability to work with accuracy and attention to detail. Further details The advert will close on the published date at 23:59 GMT. Interviews will be held at the Institute of Education Library. A job description and person specification can be accessed from the link at the bottom of this page. To apply, please provide: An up-to-date CV, including as a minimum detail of your current employment, salary and notice period, details of any former employment and your education history. A supporting statement, showing how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria marked ‘Application’ in the Person Specification found within the Job Description for this post. Your statement should be provided using the free text box under ‘Questionnaire’. If you have any queries about the role or have any queries about the application process, please contact lccos-jobs@ucl.ac.uk , quoting the job title and reference number. Further information about UCL LCCOS can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/lccos/ The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. What we offer: Annual leave & Work life balance 27 days annual leave + 6 closure days (Christmas and Easter) as well as statutory holidays Hybrid and flexible working Ability to buy 5 additional annual leave days Children & Family Enhanced maternity, adoption, paternity leave Carer’s leave Workplace nursery Other benefits We also offer some great benefits some of which are defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE); cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan; on-site gym etc. As part of the UCL community you can access free lunch hour lectures, exhibitions and museums and collections. On campus UCL has the Bloomsbury theatre hosting a range of performances and a series of bars, cafes and other facilities, which UCL staff can use. Visit Reward and Benefits | Work at UCL - UCL – University College London to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department is working towards an Athena SWAN award. We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48684": {
      "title": "Senior Finance Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05743 About us: UCL is a multi-disciplinary university with a population of over 18,000 staff and 51,000 students from 150 different countries. For more information see www.ucl.ac.uk/about . The UCL Centre for Advanced Research Computing (ARC) is UCL’s institute for infrastructure and innovation in digital research. We are an innovative hybrid: a professional services department that delivers reliable and secure infrastructure and services to UCL research groups, and a laboratory for research and innovation. We are a home for the research technology professionals - research software engineers, HPC systems engineers, dev-ops specialists, data scientists and data stewards - who support and collaborate in the delivery of UCL research. Further information about working in ARC can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/arc About the role: We are looking for someone to join our growing department. This role will work with departmental staff as well as with colleagues from across UCL and the wider research community. About you: This role would suit someone with experience of financial administration and a knowledge of accounting and financial procedures, particularly within a Higher Education setting. A relevant financial qualification or significant experience obtained in Higher Education would be essential for this role. The role holder should also be able to demonstrate excellent computer literacy, including efficient use of Excel and budgetary and reporting software (i.e. Axiom, Worktribe). Please attach a cover letter detailing why you have applied for this role, and the skills and experience you bring that are required in the person specification and job description. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48769": {
      "title": "Senior Information Security Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05649 About us: Information Services Division (ISD) is the primary provider of IT services to UCL. We support and enhance learning, teaching, research and administrative processes by providing information- and technology-related services to over 50,000 staff and students of UCL and associated institutions. Our ambition is to be the leading IT services group in the Higher Education (HE) sector and we are growing our team’s capability in experience/UX, agile development, security, cloud, service management and partnering. We are modernising our technology foundations, digitising the processes of the university to transform experience for students and staff, and partnering across the university to drive differentiation in UCL education and research. UCL boasts a track record of ground-breaking research that transforms the lives of people around the world. Our teams provide dedicated, specialist support throughout the research and innovation lifecycle to help sustain and advance UCL’s position as a world-class, research-led university. About the role: Within ISD, this post is in the Information Security Group, which leads on all aspects of security at UCL. The role sits within Security Operations, which is responsible for security monitoring, incident response, vulnerability management and threat intelligence. Working under the direction of the Cyber Threat Management Lead, you will lead day-to-day threat intelligence, threat hunting and detection engineering activity. You will help maintain UCL’s intelligence requirements and threat model, produce actionable intelligence, plan and conduct threat hunts, and identify opportunities to improve detection coverage against priority threats. You will work closely with analysts, technical teams, service providers and stakeholders across UCL to turn intelligence, hunt findings and incident evidence into practical improvements to detections, playbooks, tooling and security controls. You will also provide coaching and direction to colleagues, communicate complex threat information clearly, and support evidence-based decisions about UCL’s defensive posture. About you: As the ideal candidate you will have a mix of the following skills and experience: Significant experience in cyber threat intelligence, threat hunting, detection engineering, SOC operations, incident response support or related operational security work. Strong knowledge of intelligence requirements, threat modelling, intelligence cycles, analytical methods and the production of actionable intelligence. Strong knowledge of threat hunting and detection engineering methods, including hunt planning, telemetry analysis, detection coverage, custom detections and false-positive management. Experience using relevant security tooling and platforms, such as SIEM, TIP, SOAR, CTEM, intelligence collection platforms, reporting platforms and related monitoring or investigation tools. Experience using threat intelligence, hunt findings, incident findings or other operational evidence to improve detections, reporting, playbooks, tooling or controls. The ability to analyse complex or incomplete threat information and support timely, data-led decisions. Experience coordinating intelligence, hunting or detection engineering activity across analysts, technical teams, suppliers and stakeholders. Strong written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to explain threats, detection gaps, hunt findings and recommended actions to technical and non-technical audiences. The ability to organise and prioritise multiple activities, maintain accurate documentation, and ensure actions are clearly assigned, tracked and completed. Experience providing day-to-day leadership, coaching, knowledge sharing or technical direction to colleagues. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48820": {
      "title": "Senior OD Consultant (Employee Experience & Transformation)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; HR and Wellbeing; London; £68,284-£78,386​; People and Culture & PS Hub (B30) Reference: B30-00257 About us: UCL’s Organisational Development team supports more than 19,000 colleagues across academic, research, teaching and professional services roles. As a centre of expertise within People & Culture, we ensure that UCL has the learning, talent and organisational structures needed to deliver its strategy and People Plan. The Organisational Capability & Transformation team shapes the strategic capabilities UCL needs to achieve its academic mission and long-term ambitions. We bring together expertise in workforce capability and planning, employee experience and engagement, talent management, digital transformation and organisational change. Working collaboratively across People & Culture, Information Services, Faculties and Professional Services, we deliver institution-wide initiatives that strengthen organisational performance, improve colleague experience and build future workforce capability. UCL is deeply committed to equality, diversity and inclusion. Creating equity of opportunity is central to our approach to talent, learning, leadership development and organisational change. About the role: As Senior OD Consultant (Employee Experience & Transformation), you will lead the development of evidence-based approaches to employee experience, engagement and colleague voice across UCL. You will take strategic ownership of UCL’s Staff Experience Survey and wider employee-listening activities, translating colleague feedback and organisational data into clear insights and practical recommendations. You will develop a coherent listening strategy using approaches such as pulse surveys, focus groups and employee panels, helping UCL build a culture of continuous listening, learning and improvement. Working closely with senior leaders, People & Culture colleagues and transformation teams, you will provide expert organisational development and change consultancy across major university-wide programmes. You will lead people and organisational change workstreams, assessing change readiness, identifying risks and developing strategies that support successful adoption and sustainable impact. You will also help strengthen UCL’s Employee Value Proposition and improve the colleague experience across key stages of the employee lifecycle, including onboarding, career development, performance, learning, leadership and organisational change. A further focus of the role will be providing strategic leadership for the employee experience roadmap within UCL’s Thrive digital ecosystem. You will work with digital and product teams to translate employee insight into development priorities and explore how emerging technologies, including AI-enabled solutions, could enhance employee experience and workforce capability. As a trusted adviser, you will use evidence, organisational insight and specialist OD expertise to challenge constructively, influence decisions and shape strategic priorities across UCL. About you: You will be an experienced organisational development, employee experience or change professional with a strong record of delivering complex, organisation-wide initiatives. You will have expertise in employee engagement and listening, using qualitative and quantitative insight to develop clear, practical recommendations. An effective programme and stakeholder manager, you will be confident advising and influencing senior leaders, building relationships across multidisciplinary teams and supporting sustainable organisational change. You will also bring a strong commitment to inclusion, colleague voice and equity of opportunity, alongside an interest in how digital solutions and emerging technologies can improve employee experience and organisational capability. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48636": {
      "title": "Senior Optical Instrumentation Development Technician",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Laboratory Technical and Lab Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07800 About us: The Biomedical Optics Research Laboratory (BORL) within the Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering at University College London (UCL) is one of the world's leading centres for the development and clinical translation of optical technologies for monitoring and imaging human physiology and metabolism. The laboratory brings together physicists, engineers, neuroscientists and clinicians to develop innovative solutions that address major biomedical and healthcare challenges. The post will be based within Professor Ilias Tachtsidis' research programme, where he leads the MultiModal Spectroscopy Group and the MetaboLight Team, and is a senior member of BORL. Professor Tachtsidis is internationally recognised for pioneering broadband near-infrared spectroscopy (bNIRS), a unique optical technology capable of non-invasively quantifying tissue oxygenation, haemodynamics and mitochondrial metabolism through measurement of the oxidation state of cytochrome-c-oxidase. His multidisciplinary research spans optical technology development, neuroscience, computational modelling and clinical translation, with applications ranging from neonatal brain health and neurodevelopmental disorders to acute brain injury and neurological disease. About the role: Applications are invited for a Senior Optical Instrumentation Development Technician to join the Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering at University College London (UCL). The successful candidate will join the internationally renowned Biomedical Optics Research Laboratory (BORL) and work within the programme led by Professor Ilias Tachtsidis, who leads the MultiModal Spectroscopy Group and the MetaboLight Team and is a senior member of BORL. Professor Tachtsidis' work is internationally recognised for pioneering broadband near-infrared spectroscopy (bNIRS), a unique technology capable of non-invasively monitoring tissue oxygenation, haemodynamics and mitochondrial metabolism. The position is funded through the newly established Medical Research Council (MRC) Centre of Research Excellence for Human Neural Development, a landmark partnership between King's College London, University College London and the University of Manchester. The Centre aims to transform our understanding of early human brain development through the integration of advanced imaging technologies, experimental neuroscience, artificial intelligence and clinical research. This exciting technical role will focus on the development, integration, testing and deployment of next-generation optical neuroimaging technologies designed to characterise early brain health across multiple biological scales, from vascular function and oxygen delivery to cellular metabolism and mitochondrial activity. The successful candidate will contribute to the development of advanced hyperspectral near-infrared spectroscopy (NIRS) systems and associated instrumentation platforms for laboratory and clinical applications. The role will involve substantial hands-on work in electronic circuit and PCB design, software development for instrument control and data acquisition, optical system integration, rapid prototyping, 3D printing, testing, calibration and technical documentation. The post holder will work closely with engineers, researchers and clinicians to develop robust and reliable instrumentation suitable for deployment in research and clinical environments, including collaborations with King's College Hospital, University College London Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust and Manchester University NHS Foundation Trust. In addition to supporting the Centre's instrumentation activities, the post holder will provide technical support for undergraduate and postgraduate teaching laboratories within the Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering, including Biomedical Engineering and Medical Physics programmes. This post offers a unique opportunity to contribute to world-leading biomedical technology development within one of the UK's foremost biomedical optics groups. About you: This post offers a unique opportunity to contribute to world-leading biomedical technology development within one of the UK's foremost biomedical optics groups. This post offers a unique opportunity to contribute to world-leading biomedical technology development within one of the UK's foremost biomedical optics groups. The appointment will be offered on a fixed-term basis for seven years in the first instance, commencing on 1 October 2026, with the expectation that the role will transition into a permanent technical position within the Department, subject to satisfactory performance and departmental requirements. Key Requirements: Essential requirements include: Experience in the design, development and testing of electronic and instrumentation systems. Experience in electronic circuit and PCB design, prototyping and troubleshooting. Experience in software development for instrumentation control, automation and data acquisition. Experience in system integration involving electronic, optical and mechanical components. Experience using CAD software and rapid prototyping methods, including 3D printing. Experience using laboratory electronic test and measurement equipment. Knowledge of electrical safety testing and good engineering practice. Strong technical problem-solving skills and the ability to prepare technical documentation and reports. Ability to work effectively within multidisciplinary technical and scientific teams. Experience in biomedical optics, medical devices, clinical technologies, and knowledge of medical device, electrical safety or laser/light safety standards would be advantageous. For informal enquiries, please contact Professor Ilias Tachtsidis, Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering, UCL (i.tachtsidis@ucl.ac.uk). What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Find out about the UCL Ways of Working : www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronxze award in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48569": {
      "title": "Senior Post Graduate Teaching Assistants",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; See advert text; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07895 About us: This role is only available for current UCL-affiliated PhD students. The Department of Electronic and Electrical Engineering (EEE) at UCL was established by Professor Sir Ambrose Fleming, inventor of the thermionic valve and hence the founder of the discipline of electronics, in 1885 as the first department of electrical technology in England. That same pioneering tradition of innovation and excellence continues to underpin our work. We push in new directions across research, education and entrepreneurship, building on our long-established links with industry and benefitting from our loyal and supportive alumni community. We are committed to develop individuals and careers, across staff and students, developing and supporting our subject discipline. About the role: We are looking to appoint no more than 10 Senior Postgraduate Teaching Assistants (SPGTAs) in the department. In addition to normal PGTA activities, SPGTAs will also be able to assist with PGTA coordination and training and potentially take on more independent roles in teaching and learning support. The post holder will be invited to participate in improving PGTA experience in the department and help with the formation of a community of practice amongst departmental PGTAs to promote best practice. SPGTA appointment will be at grade 6, starting on spine point 25. About you: A suitable candidate should have already been appointed as a PGTA in the department for at least two academic years (candidates with less than 2 years of departmental PGTA experience may be exceptionally accepted provided that they have extensive prior relevant experience) and they should have experience with multiple aspects of teaching and learning support with demonstrated commitment to excellence in these activities. A suitable candidate should also be able to demonstrate in their application that they have adopted an evidence-based and reflective approach to supporting teaching and learning. Application details: Please upload the following with your application: A CV highlighting your teaching and learning support experience and the area of expertise A personal statement no longer than 400 words in which you should expand on your knowledge, approach and reflections in supporting teaching and learning in higher education A letter of support from an activity lead in the department you have assisted as a PGTA The deadline for receiving applications is 28 October 2026 at 23:59 . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48919": {
      "title": "Senior Programme Officer",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Strategy, Project and Change Management; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement) (B09) Reference: B09-01428 About us: UCL is a world-leading research university with a strong commitment to using its collective expertise to address global challenges and create positive impact locally, nationally and internationally. UCL Grand Challenges is a flagship programme bringing together expertise from across the university to stimulate cross-disciplinary research, education, innovation and knowledge exchange. Its themes include Climate Crisis, Mental Health & Wellbeing, Data-Empowered Societies, Inequalities and Intercultural Communication, supported by significant investment across their five-year lifespan. Based within the Office of the Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation & Global Engagement), the Grand Challenges team works across UCL to turn these ambitions into practical programmes, funding opportunities and collaborative activity. About the role: We are seeking a Senior Programme Officer to join the UCL Grand Challenges Programme Management Office (PMO). Please note this is a fixed-term internal only secondment position until 31st December 2028. Working closely with the Head of Programmes & Operations and colleagues across Grand Challenges, RIGE and the wider university, the role will help translate strategic priorities into effective and consistent ways of working across the programme. The postholder will coordinate a broad range of programme and operational activity, including planning, governance, financial monitoring, risk management, funding administration, data and reporting, and the development of processes and Standard Operating Procedures. There will be a strong focus on continuous improvement, identifying opportunities to make systems and processes more efficient and user-focused. The role will also support the effective operation and development of Grand Challenges’ grant management system, Flexi-Grant, and produce management information, reports and dashboards using tools including Power BI. The postholder will line manage the Programme Administrator and work with a wide range of stakeholders across UCL. About you: You will have experience of developing processes and delivering projects or funding schemes within higher education or another similarly complex organisation, alongside a good understanding of project, programme or portfolio management. Strong organisational, analytical and stakeholder management skills are essential, with the ability to manage competing priorities and work confidently across different teams and activities. Experience supporting the implementation, adoption or improvement of technology systems and identifying practical improvements to processes and ways of working will also be important. You will bring strong data and digital skills, including demonstrable proficiency in Power BI and experience of workflow and grant management systems such as Asana and Flexi-Grant, or equivalent platforms. Financial acumen, including experience of budget monitoring, forecasting and expenditure tracking, is also required. An effective communicator and collaborative colleague, you will be comfortable developing clear guidance, reports and training materials for different audiences, building positive relationships with stakeholders and working independently while knowing when to seek advice and input. What we offer: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48297": {
      "title": "Senior Project Manager",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Estates, Facilities, Accommodation and Safety; London; £68,284-£76,244; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00303 About us: The UCL Estate comprises over 240 buildings, more than 6.5 million square feet of academic accommodation and around 4500 owned student bedrooms. The location, scale, variety and complexity of the estate are unparalleled in the Higher Education sector, including world-leading research, teaching, offices and student accommodation within our Bloomsbury and UCL East campus, at Stratford. Together, Campus Experience and Infrastructure and Estates Development (CE&I and ED) is responsible for managing all aspects of the UCL Estate from the strategic to the operational, including property asset management, master-planning, capital projects and seamless end to end operational support. The function comprises approximately 350 FM and property professionals, project managers and customer facing operational colleagues. From providing student accommodation, security and catering, to delivering an estates masterplan and all forms of capital projects in a sustainable manner. We are a collective of technical professionals who work together to deliver a resilient, functional, and vibrant estate. About the role: Lead the transformation of one of London's most complex and historic estates. We are seeking an inspirational Senior Project Manager to lead a diverse portfolio of projects across UCL's Bloomsbury Capital Programme. Covering projects from £2m to £100m+, the portfolio includes major redevelopments, new-build projects, adaptation and reuse of historic and listed buildings, world-class research and teaching facilities, commercial developments and strategic estate investments. The role offers exposure to projects across Bloomsbury, UCL East at Stratford and other strategic locations supporting UCL's long-term ambitions. This is an opportunity to shape the places that support world-leading education, research and innovation whilst helping deliver one of the most ambitious and long-term capital investment programmes in the Higher Education sector. Working across a highly operational estate, you will lead projects that leave a lasting legacy for generations of students, researchers, staff and partners. The role combines strategic leadership, technical project delivery and team development. You will lead multidisciplinary project teams through all stages of the project lifecycle, bringing together stakeholders, consultants, contractors and specialist advisers to deliver successful outcomes in challenging and demanding environments. You will be responsible for managing multiple projects simultaneously, balancing competing priorities whilst maintaining a relentless focus on safety, quality, value, programme and user experience. You will work closely with senior leaders, academic stakeholders and professional services colleagues, translating technical information into clear recommendations and guiding projects through governance and approval processes. Success in this role will require an individual who is as comfortable challenging design assumptions and identifying better solutions as they are leading on-site construction activity. You will provide visible leadership throughout the project lifecycle, helping identify risks and opportunities early and ensuring that issues are resolved before they become barriers to delivery. Alongside project delivery, you will play an important role in developing capability within the wider team. You will help create an environment where project professionals can thrive, learn from experience and work collaboratively to deliver outcomes that exceed expectations. About you: Things you'll need in order to do this job include: Significant experience delivering major projects within occupied and operational environments where business continuity, user engagement and stakeholder management are critical to success. A strong track record of successfully delivering projects through design, procurement, construction and handover. Experience delivering projects through RIBA stages and equivalent project delivery frameworks. Demonstrable experience leading multidisciplinary project teams and managing external consultant and contractor organisations. The ability to lead confidently in complex and sometimes ambiguous situations, maintaining momentum and delivering at pace in a demanding environment. Strong construction and project delivery knowledge, with the ability to identify emerging risks and opportunities early and take decisive action before issues impact successful delivery. Excellent communication and influencing skills, with the confidence to engage senior stakeholders, governance groups and project teams. Experience balancing strategic objectives with practical delivery challenges. A collaborative and inclusive leadership style that inspires those around you, builds capability and brings out the best in project teams, consultants and contractors. A proactive, solution-focused mindset with the resilience, energy and drive to thrive in a fast-paced delivery environment. Strong commercial awareness, including experience administering major construction contracts such as NEC and JCT. Experience managing substantial project budgets and providing robust financial forecasting and reporting. Working knowledge of statutory and regulatory requirements including planning, building regulations, Building Safety legislation and CDM responsibilities. A passion for creating environments that support education, research and innovation. Relevant professional qualification or experiential equivalent is essential. The ability to work on-site in London at least 4 days per week. The following qualifications and experience, although not essential, would be highly advantageous: Experience delivering projects valued at £20m+. Experience of delivering teaching laboratories, research laboratories and other similar highly serviced functional spaces. Experience working within complex urban environments, occupied estates or major institutional portfolios. Experience managing projects with complex stakeholder groups and demanding operational requirements. Experience delivering projects within higher education, healthcare, science, cultural, commercial or public sector environments. Chartered status through a recognised professional body (for example CEng, ChPP, MCIOB, MRICS or equivalent). Please review the JD, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure these are addressed in your application. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48861": {
      "title": "Senior Recruiter - (APPLY IF YOU ARE A UCL INTERNAL & ELIGIBLE CANDIDATE ONLY)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; HR and Wellbeing; London; £56,431-£66,437​; People and Culture & PS Hub (B30) Reference: B30-00264 About us: UCL’s People & Culture team supports the university in attracting, recruiting and retaining the people who make its teaching, research and professional services possible. Our Talent Acquisition team works with hiring managers across UCL to deliver effective recruitment and a positive experience for candidates. About the role: We are seeking a Senior Recruiter to join our Talent Acquisition team. Reporting to the Talent Acquisition Manager, you will work with hiring managers to understand their recruitment needs, advise on approaches to attracting candidates and support campaigns from planning through to appointment. You will help ensure recruitment activity is well coordinated, inclusive and responsive to the needs of UCL’s varied teams. This is a Grade 8 role with hybrid working. About you: You will bring recruitment experience, strong stakeholder management skills and the ability to manage several priorities at once. You will be comfortable advising hiring managers, communicating clearly with candidates and colleagues, and using sound judgement to resolve problems as they arise. A collaborative approach, attention to detail and a commitment to delivering a positive recruitment experience will help you succeed. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49010": {
      "title": "Senior Research Fellow in Climate Change and Health",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11191 About us: The UCL Institute for Global Health (IGH) is at the heart of UCL’s Grand Challenge of Global Health. The Institute’s vision is for a world where international policy on global health is informed by world-class research. IGH aims to support the development of robust solutions to aspects of the world’s major global health challenges through scholarly outputs, education, public engagement, translational research, and ultimately by influencing public policy and professional practice. The Lancet Countdown: tracking progress on health and climate change, is an independent, international research collaboration, that works to track the evolving links between health and climate change globally, regionally and nationally, in partnership with The Lancet and the World Health Organization. Its aim is to inform a robust response to climate change that protects and promotes human health. We are looking for an experienced Senior Research Fellow in Climate Change and Health to join our team. About the role: The successful candidate will be responsible for leading the coordination and timely delivery of the health research and academic initiatives of the Lancet Countdown, including the collation of health and epidemiological evidence for the annual Global Lancet Countdown Report, Regional Reports, special issues, and additional academic and research outputs. This role is designed to provide specialist expertise on public health, global health and/or epidemiology to advance the Lancet Countdown’s academic work, and ensure the Lancet Countdown achieve the high standard of academic rigour and science. They will also support the development of research to quantify the effects of climate on health or the possibilities and health opportunities of climate change mitigation. The postholder will be an expert in climate change and global health and/or epidemiology, with strong quantitative skills and the ability to apply this expertise across climate change and health research, including development and evaluation of indicators of the effects of climate change and policies to address it on public health, and use of findings to inform real-world impact. Their public or global health disciplinary expertise will be essential to ensuring critical appraisal and interpretation of evidence as well as the integration of public and global health principles across all Lancet Countdown outputs. Their leadership and network understanding will be critical in the integration of expertise from across the climate and health fields. The postholder will also be encouraged to develop and expand a research programme at the intersection of climate change and health, in support of the overall Lancet Countdown aims and their personal academic career development. This is a full-time role (i.e. 1.0 FTE, 36.5 hours per week) and it is available until 30 April 2029 in the first instance. Please note that the successful candidate must be living in the UK or willing to relocate to the UK for the duration of the contract. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Salary range: £56,431 - £66,437 per annum (including London Allowance), depending on experience. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Clare Scully (Head of Operations) on c.scully@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the IGH HR Team on IGH.HR@ucl.ac.uk . About you: It is essential that the successful candidate has a PhD in the intersection of climate change, and global health, epidemiology, or a closely related discipline. They will also have: Postdoctoral experience in the intersection of climate change, and global health, epidemiology, or a closely related discipline Demonstrated experience in quantitative analysis, including the development of models and/or advanced data analysis in climate change and global health, ideally with expertise in epidemiological modelling Excellent technical knowledge of climate change – health intersection Experience managing and working with multidisciplinary academic teams, and participating in cross-disciplinary and international academic projects Experience in leading teams through complex programmes of work, delivering high-quality outputs against tight deadlines Track record of securing research grant income and to developing successful grant applications Track record of academic publications including some as first and/or senior author positions Strong analytical and critical thinking skills What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49069": {
      "title": "Senior School Administrator",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07868 About us: We are The Bartlett, UCL’s global faculty of the built environment. Individually, our schools and sections lead their fields. In partnership, they develop new responses to pressing world issues. As a whole, they represent a world-leading, multidisciplinary faculty, united by the radical spirit of UCL. Our vision is of a world where everything that’s built aims to add to the well-being of people and the environment. The Bartlett is unrivalled in its breadth and depth of disciplines, programmes, and departments. It is made up 12 Schools, Centres, and Institutes, providing teaching and research in a wide range of subject areas to over 4,000 students each year from across the world. Further information about The School can be found online. About the role: University College London (UCL) wishes to appoint a Senior School Administrator. The post holder will be based at The Bartlett School of Sustainable Construction at the Bartlett Faculty of the Built Environment, a world-leading faculty ranked #1 in the world for Built Environment studies and #1 in the UK for its research environment. The post holder will carry out administrative and operational duties within the Bartlett School of Sustainable Construction, consistent with the vision and mission of The School. This is an open-ended, full-time position. About you: The appointee is required to be educated to A-Level or have experience in a similar role, be articulate and able to flex their communication style appropriately for different audiences. The successful applicant will be expected to apply excellent administrative skills and a highly proactive approach to providing a comprehensive administrative service. The applicant will possess strong interpersonal and problem-solving skills including the ability to build successful relationships and collaborate, contributing towards maintaining a positive work environment. The successful candidate will be able to work harmoniously with staff and students of all cultures and backgrounds and possess excellent stakeholder liaison and/or public engagement skills. They will have experience of managing, supervising, or coordinating the work of a team as well as providing excellent support service to a wide range of tasks. Your application will be assessed based on how well the criteria set out in the person specification are met using examples to demonstrate this. Please read the Candidate Guidance document below. A cover letter is required which addresses the criteria in the person specification. For further details including a job description and person specification, please see the attachments towards the bottom of this webpage What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/pay-benefits/staff-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49055": {
      "title": "Service Improvement Officer",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement) (B09) Reference: B09-01408 About us: UCL is a world-leading, research-intensive university, consistently ranked among the top ten in the world, with an annual turnover of well over £1 billion. Our research and innovation continue to grow in scale and complexity, with record levels of research income and impact achieved last year. You'll join Research and Innovation Services (RIS) — the team that supports, advises and provides expertise to UCL's academic community across our extensive and varied portfolio of externally funded projects. RIS brings together four teams: Award Services, Contract Services, Planning, Insight & Improvement, and Compliance and Assurance. Within RIS, Planning, Insight & Improvement leads on short- and long-term business planning, the improvement of end-to-end processes and operational activity, and the stewardship and analysis of research administrative data — all to make research and innovation support better across the university. You'll be joining RIS at a genuinely exciting time. UCL has just launched its five-year strategy, in which RIS plays a key role, and we're investing in a number of strategic programmes across the directorate — bringing in new capacity, expertise and development opportunities that will help shape UCL's research environment and impact for years to come. About the role: Please note this role is currently available until October 2027 in the first instance and is available as a secondment opportunity. This is a great opportunity for a project management professional who enjoys getting close to the detail of how services actually work — and making them work better. You'll use your continuous improvement and business change expertise across a varied portfolio of projects, helping Research and Innovation Services keep pace with UCL's evolving needs. You'll work flexibly across multiple projects at once, and you'll have real scope to identify opportunities for improvement and see your solutions through to delivery. What you'll be doing Build relationships with colleagues across Research and Innovation Services and in academic departments, gathering feedback and spotting opportunities to improve services, processes, systems, policies and data. Log and help prioritise improvements to research and innovation services, processes, systems, policies and data. Lead and deliver small-scale projects that increase the value of the services RIS provides. Support the delivery of RIS workstreams, making sure discovery phases gather enough evidence and data to enable strong service design. Build your continuous improvement toolkit — defining requirements, driving process change, facilitating workshops and carrying out process mapping — with support along the way. Track and communicate the impact of improvements you help deliver. Understand stakeholder needs fully when designing and implementing improvements, including any dependencies or wider impacts. Work with other UCL divisions — including Change & Improvement, the Information Services Division, the Office of the Vice-President (Strategy) and the Research and Innovation Operations change portfolio — to deliver joined-up business change. Stay closely connected to the Planning, Insight and Improvement project portfolio, feeding into wider RIS discussions on service improvement. Identify when external resource or expertise is needed, and work effectively with external suppliers. Build strong working knowledge of UCL's research management systems (Worktribe, MyFinance, Data and Insight Service) and their related processes. Support the development and delivery of UCL's research management systems and interfaces, including aspects of testing on Worktribe Test systems. Develop and deliver training on research management systems and processes, and keep training materials fresh and up to date. Build a network of contacts at other institutions and research funders to keep on top of sector About you: You're a natural problem-solver who enjoys untangling complex processes and finding practical, workable solutions. You'll be comfortable managing multiple projects at once, building relationships with a wide range of stakeholders, and communicating clearly about the changes you're driving. You'll thrive in this role if you can bring: Experience of delivering process or service improvement activity in a previous role. Experience of developing and implementing end-to-end business processes, and understanding their impact on other service functions. Experience of capturing and developing business requirements, with the ability to prioritise effectively. Experience of using data to identify and analyse options for service improvement. Experience of working in a project and/or agile environment, with a good understanding of fundamental project management principles. A proven ability to analyse complex, challenging issues and develop innovative, creative solutions. Good written and oral communication skills, including presentation and report writing. An excellent attitude to customer service. Experience within a Higher Education research services or administration function is a bonus, as is experience advancing research management systems in a complex organisation, or familiarity with tools like Microsoft Project — though none of these are essential. Above all, we're looking for someone who's committed to providing a responsive, helpful service, who makes best practice a genuine priority, and who's happy documenting and sharing solutions so others can build on them too. What we offer: Hybrid working, with the flexibility to work remotely for up to 80% of the week if that suits you. 27 days' annual leave plus 6 university closure days and statutory bank holidays, with the option to purchase up to 5 additional days each year. Membership of one of the UK's leading pension schemes (USS or SAUL, depending on your role). A genuinely exciting moment to join RIS, with new investment, strategic programmes and development opportunities opening up across the directorate. Access to UCL's wider staff benefits, including season ticket loans, cycle-to-work schemes, staff discounts, wellbeing support and a strong culture of learning and development. The chance to shape how one of the world's leading research universities supports its academic community, working across a genuinely varied project portfolio. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London's Global University, we know that diversity fuels creativity and innovation — and we want our community to reflect the full diversity of the world's talent. We're committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where everyone truly belongs. We particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL's workforce, and we're happy to discuss any reasonable adjustments or flexible working arrangements you may need throughout the recruitment process and beyond."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48896": {
      "title": "Specialist Study Skills Tutor (12-Month Fixed Term Contract)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Student Well-being and Registry; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05767 About us: UCL Student Support and Wellbeing Services provides information, advice and welfare support to our diverse community of students, helping them to thrive and succeed throughout their time at UCL. Within the service, our Disability, Mental Health & Neurodiversity Support Services team provides specialist advice and support to enable students who identify as disabled to fully engage with their studies and make the most of their UCL experience. We are committed to inclusive practice and to removing barriers to learning, recognising the individual needs, experiences and perspectives of our students. About the role: As a Specialist Study Skills Tutor, you will provide specialist study skills support to students with specific learning differences and/or neurodivergence, helping them to develop strategies that enable them to engage confidently and effectively with their learning. You will deliver tailored one-to-one and group sessions, using information from diagnostic assessments, Educational Psychologist reports and other relevant professional reports to understand each student's needs and develop individual learning plans. You will work collaboratively with students to identify and overcome study-related barriers, monitor learning outcomes and evaluate the effectiveness of your support. You will also contribute to the wider Specific Learning Differences provision at UCL, including developing resources, delivering workshops and supporting the team to meet relevant Department for Education requirements and quality standards. You will maintain accurate student records and tutorial documentation, work closely with colleagues across Disability, Mental Health & Neurodiversity Support Services and contribute to the ongoing development of the service. This is a varied role where you will combine specialist knowledge with a student-centred approach, using your expertise to help create an inclusive learning environment for students across UCL. Please note, this role is offered on a fixed-term contract basis for 12-months in the first instance. This is an internal recruitment advert, available to eligible UCL workers. Hybrid working is available with currently a minimum of 2-days per week onsite (subject to change). If you have any queries about the role, please contact Lucy Smith at: lucy.smith@ucl.ac.uk Job Ref: B01-05767 Closing Date: 20-10-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) Interviews: Interviews are likely to place around 30 th October. About you: You will have a Level 5 or above qualification (or equivalent) from a recognised provider or professional training course relevant to specific learning differences/difficulties, together with membership of a relevant professional body such as PASSHE, Patoss or the Dyslexia Guild. You will have sound knowledge of specific learning differences and their manifestations within a Higher Education setting, alongside experience of delivering individual tuition and monitoring and evaluating learning outcomes. You will be confident interpreting diagnostic reports and other relevant assessments and using this information to design and deliver tailored interventions. You will have strong knowledge of study skills strategies appropriate to Higher Education and experience of using assistive technology and specialist software to support students with specific learning differences. You will be able to adapt your approach to work effectively with students with a wide range of disabilities and understand barriers from the student's perspective. You will be an excellent communicator, with the ability to build positive relationships, deliver presentations and workshops to a range of audiences, and work collaboratively with colleagues. You will be organised and able to manage your own workload, maintaining accurate records and meeting deadlines. Most importantly, you will demonstrate a commitment to inclusive practice and the social model of disability, with the patience, empathy and active listening skills needed to understand individual student needs and tailor your approach accordingly. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48492": {
      "title": "UCL INTERNAL ONLY Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11325 About us: The Division of Biosciences at UCL is one of the world's foremost centres for research and teaching in the biological sciences. We have an outstanding international reputation and a community of over 500 staff and more than 300 PhD students engaged in cutting-edge research and collaboration. Further information can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/biosciences This is an exciting opportunity to join the Insall lab, working on eukaryotic chemotaxis. You will work in a highly multidisciplinary team including mathematicians, engineers, cell and molecular biologists. About the role: The main purpose of this position in the Insall group is to study and learn equations that define chemotactic steering from a large dataset. The applicant will join an extremely multidisciplinary team, with microscopists, engineers and cell biologists, and will be expected to pitch in to other work while leading the math and machine learning. The is 3 month fixed-term role, due to research funding restriction. Research Fellows - 'Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be at Grade 6B (salary £39,148 - £41,833 per annum) with payment at Grade 7 being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD Thesis.' This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR Administrator. About you: We are a successful group in the field of quantitative and mathematical cell biology. The applicant will play a key role in driving the mathematical analysis of large datasets we have accumulated, ending with an equation that will help us understand the mechanisms of steering. Demonstrable knowledge of research techniques and methodologies, coupled with of a publication record in good quality publications. Experienced in high-level mathematical modelling with strong knowledge of the eukaryotic cell migration field What we offer: The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and LGBTQI+ people. Our division holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48223": {
      "title": "UCL Wellington Renal-Cardiovascular Clinical Fellowship Scheme",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11279 About us: University College London (UCL) and the Wellington Hospital are recruiting to a Clinical Fellowship scheme which offers candidates an opportunity to study for a PhD in UCL’s Institute of Cardiovascular Science, while working at the UK's largest private hospital providing specialist complex care. Clinically employed at The Wellington Hospital in St John’s Wood London, part of HCA Healthcare UK, these Clinical Fellows will commence a programme to undertake a period of basic and/or clinical cardiovascular research at UCL prior to continuation of specialist training. This is a tremendous opportunity to jointly develop a research career while working between two prestigious organisations. About the role: This Clinical Fellowship is available to Specialist Registrars in renal medicine looking to undertake a PhD with an interest in the cardio-renal syndrome. This research project will be based at The Hatter Cardiovascular Institute which undertakes both pre-clinical and clinical research. Clinical Fellows will register for their higher degrees at UCL (part-time). Their salary will be funded by The Wellington Hospital who will also cover their annual higher degree (PhD) registration fee and an annual allowance for consumables for a period of up to 3 years, within agreed limits. About you: We welcome all applicants who are medically qualified with the appropriate clinical experience, GMC registered and have MRCP. The scheme is open only to UK nationals due to funding restrictions. In addition, they must meet UCL’s entrance requirements for PhD registration. Those applicants who meet both standards will be invited to interview. Applicant must have a strong interest in Cardiovascular Science as well as a Strong understanding of Cardiovascular Physiology and Biology/Cell Biology/ Molecular Biology. What we offer: Applicants can apply either via the UCL or HCA websites and should be aware that relevant data will be shared between the two organisations as part of the recruitment process. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48794": {
      "title": "UCL – Wellington PhD Programme for UK Clinicians",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11362 About us: University College London (UCL) and The Wellington Hospital are recruiting to a Clinical Fellowship scheme which offers cardiology SpR’s an opportunity to study for a PhD in UCL’s Institute of Cardiovascular Science, while working at the UK's largest private hospital providing specialist complex care. About the role: This Clinical Fellowship will be available to Specialist Registrars looking to undertake a PhD grounded in cardiovascular science. They will have the opportunity to select a research project and a supervisor from across the range of disciplines in the UCL Institute of Cardiovascular Science (ICS). This includes The Hatter Cardiovascular Institute, Children’s Cardiovascular Disease and Population Science and Experimental Medicine, and the MRC Unit of Lifelong Health and Ageing. Clinical Fellows will register for their higher degrees at UCL (part-time). Their salary will be funded by The Wellington Hospital who will also cover their annual higher degree (PhD) registration fee and an annual allowance for consumables for a period of up to 3 years, within agreed limits. About you: We welcome all applicants who are GMC registered and have MRCP accreditation. The scheme is open only to UK nationals due to funding restrictions. In addition, they must meet UCL’s entrance requirements for PhD registration. Those applicants who meet both standards will be invited to interview. All interested applicants are invited to apply and submit both a Curriculum Vitae and Personal Statement. There is one Fellowship available and will commence in early 2027. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • On-site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Available documents"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/191749": {
      "title": "Faculty Position in Operations & Technology",
      "description": "Posted: Aug 18, 2026 Deadline: Nov 30, 2026 Department: University College London: School of Management Location: London UK University College London (UCL) is a leading global center for research and education, consistently ranked among the world’s top universities. In the 2025 QS World University Rankings, UCL was placed 9th globally. The university’s academic excellence is reflected in its community, which includes over 32 Nobel Prize winners among its staff and alumni. UCL is also deeply international, with more than one-third of its students representing over 150 countries and nearly one-third of its staff coming from outside the UK. The Operations and Technology Group at the UCL School of Management, University College London, invites applications for multiple tenure-track open-rank faculty position (Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor). The anticipated start date is 1 September 2027. The UCL School of Management is a world-leading business school, with a strong focus on research excellence. In the most recent Research Excellence Framework (REF), the School was ranked second among UK business schools for research output. Since 2016, it has been based in Canary Wharf, at the heart of London’s financial district. The School places a high value on academic research and provides strong institutional support, while maintaining a relatively light teaching load of just 60 hours per year. The UCL School of Management encourages diversity and a positive culture among all faculty, staff, and students. Our school is committed to building an intellectual community in which diversity, fairness, and inclusion are valued. UCL is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Successful candidates will demonstrate the ability or potential to conduct high-quality research in operations management, operations research, or business analytics. A doctoral degree in operations or a related discipline is required by 1 September 2027. Candidates are also expected to contribute to teaching in areas such as operations, analytics, or sustainability at the undergraduate, Masters and PhD level. To apply for this position, please submit a CV, a job market paper, and up to one additional research paper via https://apply.interfolio.com/191749. A cover letter and research statement are optional. If you are currently a PhD student or postdoctoral researcher, you should also arrange for three referees to submit their letters online. The application deadline is 30 November 2026 . To receive full consideration for the Assistant Professor positions, please submit your application by 9 October 2026 ."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49224": {
      "title": "Head of Student Lifecycle Services (Enrolment & Student Records)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Student Well-being and Registry; London; £68,284-£78,386​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05766 About us: Student and Registry Services (SRS) provides essential services across the student lifecycle, supporting UCL faculties and departments to deliver a high-quality student experience. Within SRS, Student Lifecycle Services is responsible for the operational management and assurance of taught student records from enrolment through to progression, award and the production of official academic records. As part of Student Operations, Student Lifecycle Services is led through two complementary Head of Student Lifecycle Services roles, with each postholder accountable for a defined service portfolio while sharing responsibility for a coherent, resilient and student-centred end-to-end student lifecycle service. We are looking for an experienced student lifecycle professional to join us as Head of Student Lifecycle Services (Progression and Awards). This is a senior leadership role with responsibility for services that are fundamental to the integrity of UCL’s academic administration and the student experience. About the role: As Head of Student Lifecycle Services (Enrolment and Student Records), you will provide strategic leadership for UCL’s enrolment and student records services across undergraduate, postgraduate taught, affiliate and other taught student populations. You will lead the Student Lifecycle Managers within your portfolio and provide strategic oversight of UCL’s annual enrolment and re-enrolment cycle, ensuring effective governance, operational readiness and coordinated delivery across faculties, departments and professional services. You will act as the senior business process owner for enrolment and student record management, ensuring student records, status and key lifecycle information are accurate, timely and appropriately controlled. Working collaboratively across UCL, you will lead service improvement and redesign, strengthen governance and resilience, and provide senior oversight of complex and sensitive student record matters. You will also shape the development of student lifecycle systems and processes, using data, user feedback and sector insight to improve service quality, compliance, efficiency and the student experience. Please note, this role is offered on a full-time basis with hybrid working available, currently a minimum of 1-day per week in our London Kings Cross office (subject to change) plus onsite events when required. Job Ref: B01-05766 Closing Date: 30-10-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) About you: You will be an experienced student lifecycle professional with substantial senior experience of enrolment, student status and student record management within a complex higher education environment. You will have a strong understanding of the UK higher education student lifecycle and the policy and regulatory environment affecting enrolment, student status and student records. You will have experience of leading complex, high-volume and deadline-critical services and managing managers, alongside a strong track record in strategic service leadership, service redesign and organisational or digital change. You will be confident owning complex processes, controls, risks and data quality within a regulated environment, and using management information and user feedback to improve services and inform decisions. A collaborative and inclusive leader, you will have excellent influencing and communication skills, strong strategic judgement and the ability to build effective relationships with senior stakeholders across organisational boundaries. You will be comfortable making evidence-based decisions in complex circumstances and remaining calm and accountable during high-pressure operational periods. Experience of SITS or another enterprise student information system, and of working with product, agile delivery or technology teams, would be desirable. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49261": {
      "title": "Assistant Management Accountant - INTERNAL OPPORTUNITY FOR UCL APPLICANTS ONLY",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Finance; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00407 About us: The UCL Finance department is divided into 4 Directorates who are responsible for the central management of UCL's financial performance and strategy and for the provision of services and advice. We currently have a vacant position for an Assistant Management Accountant within the Management Accounting team, which leads on management accounting, internal management reporting and compliance reporting to our regulators. About the role: The post-holder will be responsible for supporting the delivery of all aspects of the University’s management accounting, including cost management, cash flow management, variance analysis, budgeting and forecasting. They will support the provision of accurate financial information for use across the business. The post-holder will need to have a customer service focus with the ability to drive and deliver continuous customer service improvements including to financial systems and processes, workflows, management information and management and reporting of financial risks and issues. The post-holder will work closely with colleagues across UCL with responsibility for general accounting and will need to understand and deliver on their management accounting needs. This will include finance colleagues in the business areas, FP&A, the Financial Accounting team and the Finance Business Partners. A full range of duties can be found on the attached job description. About you: The successful applicant will need: • To be part qualified accountant actively studying for qualification with demonstrable experience in a management accounting or finance role • Experience of working in a service delivery environment • Experience of working collaboratively in a team environment • Excellent data analysis and reconciliation skills • Excellent IT skills including advanced knowledge of Excel and a good all round knowledge of Microsoft Office software Please read our job description carefully, paying particular attention to the essential and desirable criteria, and ensure these are covered in your application. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49258": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer (Teaching) in Finance and Technology - Corporate",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; UCL East; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07943 About us: UCL is a diverse global community of world-class academics, students, industry links, external partners, and alumni. Our powerful collective of individuals and institutions work together to explore new possibilities. Academic excellence is demonstrated by our 30 Nobel laureates and our top ranking in the UK for research strength. We are a global university with 14,300 employees and over 150 nationalities represented in a student body of 43,800 students. Our main location in Central London has recently been augmented by a campus on the Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park. The UCL Faculty of Engineering is the largest of its kind in the UK, ranked as the second most impactful engineering faculty in Europe and first for Research Strength in the Research Excellence Framework. Within Engineering, the UCL Department of Civil, Environmental & Geomatic Engineering is a multidisciplinary department renowned for innovation and excellence in research and teaching. We are ranked in the Top 50 worldwide for civil and structural engineering according to the 2021 QS World University and Subject rankings. About the role: The Institute of Finance and Technology (IFT) runs three MSc programmes which emphasise the analytical and quantitative foundations of finance and financial products, focusing on theory and methods and making extensive use of relevant practical applications. We are seeking to appoint an Associate Lecturer (Teaching) in Finance and Technology – Corporate to lead and teach from January 2027, at least three IFT modules in areas adjacent to corporate finance, banking, valuation, financial markets and digital finance . As an Associate Lecturer (Teaching) in Finance and Technology - Corporate , you will: Support teaching and learning across at least three IFT modules, and support other module delivery related to the same expertise Develop and maintain teaching materials that support students’ understanding Design and deliver assessment and feedback that enable students to apply theoretical knowledge Supervise MSc dissertations (10-12 students), including regular meetings with supervisees, reviewing and providing feedback on proposals, relevant literature, methodology and academic writing. Support, guide and encourage students to work independently to produce original work. This is a fixed-term role until 30 th September 2027, with the earliest start date in November 2026. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research, Teaching and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. About you: To be successful as an Associate Lecturer (Teaching) in Finance and Technology - Corporate , you will need: A completed PhD (or near completion) in finance, economics, financial technology, quantitative finance or another relevant subject area. Experience of teaching, tutoring or supporting learning in higher education, preferably in finance, financial economics, banking, investment and private equity and business innovations Strong knowledge of corporate finance, banking products and services, financial markets, securities, interest rates, exchange rates and valuation methods. An ability to connect academic theory, current research and industry practice in corporate finance, banking, valuation, financial markets and digital finance. What we offer: A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR team at cege-hr@ucl.ac.uk . We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit UCL Ways of Working to find out more. In the event we get a high number of applications, we may close the advert early before the published closing date. As a minimum we will keep all adverts open for 2 weeks. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (pro-rata) (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/about/our-people/equality-diversity-and-inclusion"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49265": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer (Teaching) in Finance and Technology – Innovation",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; UCL East; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07942 About us: UCL is a diverse global community of world-class academics, students, industry links, external partners, and alumni. Our powerful collective of individuals and institutions work together to explore new possibilities. Academic excellence is demonstrated by our 30 Nobel laureates and our top ranking in the UK for research strength. We are a global university with 14,300 employees and over 150 nationalities represented in a student body of 43,800 students. Our main location in Central London has recently been augmented by a campus on the Queen Elizabeth Olympic Park. The UCL Faculty of Engineering is the largest of its kind in the UK, ranked as the second most impactful engineering faculty in Europe and first for Research Strength in the Research Excellence Framework. Within Engineering, the UCL Department of Civil, Environmental & Geomatic Engineering is a multidisciplinary department renowned for innovation and excellence in research and teaching. We are ranked in the Top 50 worldwide for civil and structural engineering according to the 2021 QS World University and Subject rankings. About the role: The Institute of Finance and Technology (IFT) runs three MSc programmes which emphasise the analytical and quantitative foundations of finance and financial products, focusing on theory and methods and making extensive use of relevant practical applications. We are seeking to appoint an Associate Lecturer (Teaching) in Finance and Technology - Innovation to lead and teach from January 2027, at least three IFT modules each in areas in areas adjacent to venture capital and private equity, innovation finance and fintech. As an Associate Lecturer (Teaching) in Finance and Technology – Innovation , you will: Support teaching and learning across at least three IFT modules, and support other module delivery related to the same expertise Develop and maintain teaching materials that support students’ understanding Design and deliver assessment and feedback that enable students to apply theoretical knowledge Supervise MSc dissertations (10-12 students), including regular meetings with supervisees, reviewing and providing feedback on proposals, relevant literature, methodology and academic writing. Support, guide and encourage students to work independently to produce original work. This is a fixed-term role until 30 th September 2027, with the earliest start date in November 2026. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research, Teaching and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. About you: To be successful as an Associate Lecturer (Teaching) in Finance and Technology – Innovation , you will need: A completed PhD (or near completion) in finance, economics, financial technology, quantitative finance or another relevant subject area. Experience of teaching, tutoring or supporting learning in higher education, preferably in finance, financial economics, banking, investment and private equity and business innovations Strong knowledge of corporate finance, banking products and services, financial markets, securities, interest rates, exchange rates and valuation methods. An ability to connect academic theory, current research and industry practice in corporate finance, banking, valuation, financial markets and digital finance. What we offer: A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR team at cege-hr@ucl.ac.uk . We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit UCL Ways of Working to find out more. In the event we get a high number of applications, we may close the advert early before the published closing date. As a minimum we will keep all adverts open for 2 weeks. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (pro-rata) (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/about/our-people/equality-diversity-and-inclusion"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49173": {
      "title": "Associate Professor in Organisational Behaviour",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Academic; UCL East; £68,284-£76,244; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-10684 About us: UCL Global Business School for Health The UCL Global Business School for Health (GBSH) at UCL East campus is an innovative business school in preparing future healthcare leaders to disrupt and reimagine global health systems. GBSH is dedicated to training and developing the next generation of leaders in the global health economy by building on UCL’s proud tradition of cross-disciplinary academic excellence in education and research. GBSH develops talent from all sectors – policy makers, technologists and entrepreneurs – to challenge conventional thinking, to improve health outcomes and address health inequalities world-wide. With a vision for public good, GBSH is dedicated to health as the most important global issue in the 21st Century which is impacting communities and economies worldwide. Its ambition is aligned to the UN Sustainable Development Goal of ‘Universal Health Care for All’. At GBSH, we bring together global perspectives from diverse healthcare systems, industry partners and sector networks. GBSH is an interdisciplinary academic entity that is located within UCL’s Faculty of Population Health Sciences which is committed to innovating and improving health worldwide. About the role: This post will strengthen GBSH’s research and education in organisational behaviour, with a focus on leadership and/or workforce issues in health and healthcare. The post holder will make a substantial contribution to the Doctorate in Business Administration (DBA) Health programme, working closely with the Programme Director on doctoral education, supervision and programme development. We seek a scholar with a strong grounding in organisational behaviour and a research programme that advances understanding of people and organisations in the health field. Relevant interests may include leadership and team dynamics; workforce wellbeing, motivation and retention; professional identities and interprofessional working; and organisational culture, inclusion and change. We welcome different theoretical perspectives and methodological approaches within this remit. About you: The successful candidate will join the People and Organisation Management in Healthcare cluster. They will work across disciplines and engage with health organisations and professional communities to develop research that contributes to organisational scholarship and informs leadership and workforce practice. What we offer: Additionally, you can add information about benefits using the details below: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) •Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact hr.gbsh@ucl.ac.uk quoting job reference B02-10684."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48976": {
      "title": "AV & IT Support Officer (6 Month Fixed Term Contract)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; IT and Digital; London; £36,433-£42,891​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05758 About us: UCL is a world-leading teaching and research university, often ranked in the top ten in the world with an annual turnover of well over £1 billion. Part of UCL’s vision is to take on the hardest global challenges. The Information Services Division (ISD) is the primary provider of IT services to UCL. We support and enhance learning, teaching, research and administrative processes by providing information- and technology-related services to over 50,000 staff and students of UCL and associated institutions. Our ambition is to be the leading IT services group in the HE sector and we are growing our team’s capability in experience/UX, agile development, security, cloud, service management and partnering. We are modernising our technology foundations, digitising the processes of the university to transform experience for students and staff, and partnering across the university to drive differentiation in UCL education and research. About the role: The purpose of the AV and IT Support Officer role is to provide prompt, in-depth, customer-focused 2nd line support to staff, students and honorary contract holders. The post holder will provide field, phone and remote based support of UCL Managed Desktop, desktops/laptops, mobile devices, networking equipment, audio visual equipment, multimedia equipment, video conferencing, specialist equipment and other peripherals used by academic staff, administrative staff and students. The post holder will form part of a medium sized team and will be expected to work within virtual teams across other parts of the ISD. *Please note that this is initially a 6 month Fixed Term Contract (FTC)* About you: The post holder will possess deep and broad understanding in the following areas: Audio visual fundamentals Signal processing, switching and distribution Fundamentals of cabling conventions, standards and their applications Knowledge of Video Conferencing IT hardware including laptops, desktops, tablets and mobile devices Managed Desktop Services using Active Directory, Microsoft Windows, SCCM, Appsense and Ivanti Operating system support for Windows 10 and OSX Understanding of work place safety including carrying out informal risk assessments Experience of supporting large scale conferences and high profile events Experience of using fault logging system Experience of network switches, patching and Wireless networks Experience of manual handling regulations Additional skills: Excellent problem solving skills including an ability to diagnose complex hardware and software faults Excellent communication skills Be able to work under pressure Professional and supportive towards colleagues, staff, students and others The post holder will also be able to demonstrate the following ways of working indicators Responding to colleague, student and stakeholder needs with the intention to provide an excellent service Building rapport and being comfortable with others across different cultures and background Working methodically, prioritising and managing tasks What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49124": {
      "title": "Billing Manager",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Finance; London; £56,431-£66,437​; People and Culture & PS Hub (B30) Reference: B30-00270 About us: UCL’s Finance Services function supports the effective financial management of the university, providing robust, compliant and customer-focused services across UCL. The Billing team plays an important role in supporting departmentally led income-generating activity, working closely with Faculty Finance and colleagues across Credit Control, Legal, Compliance and Assurance, ISD, Audit and the wider Finance community. The team is focused on delivering consistent service standards, strong financial controls and effective ways of working across faculties. About the role: As a Billing Manager , you will lead the delivery of a consistent, compliant and user-focused revenue service, overseeing activity from initial triage through to billing and income recognition. You will manage and develop the Billing team, setting priorities, monitoring performance and ensuring accurate and timely service delivery. You will provide advice and escalation support on complex, high-value or higher-risk cases, while maintaining effective financial controls, audit trails and assurance requirements. A key part of the role will be building strong relationships across UCL, using management information to improve performance, and leading improvements to processes, systems, SOPs and service standards. If you have any questions about the role or the application process, please contact Passant Khalifa at p.khalifa@ucl.ac.uk About you: You will bring significant experience of finance operations, accounts receivable, billing, credit control or operational service delivery , ideally gained within a large or complex organisation. You will have experience of leading and developing a team, managing competing priorities and delivering a high-quality, customer-focused service. You will have a strong understanding of financial controls, audit and compliance requirements, alongside the analytical skills to use management information to identify risks and drive improvements. An effective communicator and relationship builder, you will be confident influencing and advising a range of stakeholders and comfortable using Excel, finance, CRM or case-management systems. You will also be able to demonstrate experience of improving processes, strengthening controls and standardising ways of working, alongside a commitment to equality, diversity and inclusion. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49127": {
      "title": "Billing Officer x2",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; People and Culture & PS Hub (B30) Reference: B30-00265 About us: Since our founding 200 years ago, UCL has blazed its own trail, fostering a vibrant, diverse community of world-class academics, staff, students, and alumni who aren’t afraid to think big and think differently. Together, we question the status quo, challenge assumptions, and tackle some of the world’s most complex problems head-on. Located in the heart of London, UCL is consistently ranked among the top ten universities in the world. With more than 16,000 staff and 50,000 students from 150 different countries, and an annual turnover of more than £1.2 billion a year, we are London’s leading multi-disciplinary university. The Professional Services (PS) Hub brings together key customer-facing and transactional services across HR, Finance and Customer Services, with a strong focus on delivering high-quality, efficient services and an excellent customer experience. The Billing Team sits within Accounts Receivable in Finance Services and supports UCL’s revenue activities by helping to ensure financial processes are consistent, accurate and compliant. Billing Officer - G6 About the role: We are hiring two Billing officers to work in our Billing team. As a Billing Officer , you will support the delivery of a consistent and compliant revenue process for departmentally led income-generating activity. You will review and triage revenue cases, maintain accurate records and audit trails, coordinate due diligence checks and customer setup, and support the preparation of accurate and timely sales invoices. You will also provide day-to-day guidance to academics, departments and faculty colleagues, working closely with teams including Credit Control, Compliance and Assurance, Legal and Faculty Finance. Through accurate case management and effective customer support, you will help improve consistency, reduce errors and strengthen financial controls across UCL. About you: You will have experience working in a service delivery, administrative, finance or customer-focused environment , with the ability to handle financial, billing, customer or operational information accurately. You will have excellent attention to detail, strong organisational skills and the ability to prioritise your workload effectively while responding to changing demands. You will be comfortable using Microsoft Office applications, including Excel, Word, Outlook and Teams, and able to maintain accurate records, trackers and audit trails. With effective written and verbal communication skills, you will be confident providing clear guidance to users and working both independently and collaboratively as part of a team. Experience within higher education or a complex organisation, and knowledge of billing, accounts receivable or finance systems such as Oracle Financials or MyFinance, would be advantageous. What we offer: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance “As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits.” Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: “As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion. Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49151": {
      "title": "Business Analyst Associate (Estates) - Internal Applicants Only",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-Provost (Strategy) (B24) Reference: B24-00095 About us: Please note these this role is open to Internal Applicants Only UCL is London's leading multidisciplinary university, with more than 16,000 staff and 50,000 students from 150 different countries. As well as being a global leader in teaching, UCL is also recognised as one of the most successful research institutions in the world. Based in the heart of London, UCL has a turnover of more than £1.2 billion a year. UCL's strategy sets our whole-university priorities for the next five years. This focuses investment on several shared priorities agreed through a consultation with our community and stakeholders. Enabling UCL to deliver a more cohesive set of services, we will be delivering a portfolio of change and improvement initiatives. Strategic Change will be at the heart of building this portfolio, working to support the new strategic objectives, our services and the enabling digital technologies. By providing leadership, support and hands on help we will be ensuring the delivery of fit for purpose solutions that drive value to all our users. About the role: Provide a business analysis service projects and change initiatives. Assist colleagues and other stakeholders to investigate operational issues, problems and new opportunities, contributing to business process and IT system improvements. Support a senior analyst or product owner with larger scale requests or deliver end to end analysis for small scale changes to existing services. About you: Support end users by reviewing, documenting and assessing requests for change. Gather, validate and document system and process information. Support the analysis of current ‘as-is’ business services and processes. Take part in meetings with stakeholders and assist in presenting issues and gathering requirements. Produce business analysis deliverables using relevant documentation styles in line with organisational standards and using appropriate tools. Document detailed to-be process models and requirements in line with agreed project deliverables, and communicate these to the wider team. Ensure engagement with customers and stakeholders is positive and delivers the right solution. Assist in defining holistic solutions that address organisational, people, process, information and technology aspects. Work closely with the product team to support design, delivery and testing phases of the project. Assist colleagues to define and undertake user acceptance testing for new or improved business processes and IT systems. Develop business analysis skills and expertise and attend relevant events related to business analysis to develop knowledge and proactively maintain networks with other professionals in the area. Develop agile delivery skills and techniques. What we offer: In return, you’ll have the opportunity to make a significant impact within Student Lifecycle portfolio, working alongside talented colleagues. We offer a collaborative environment where your ideas are valued, your growth is supported, and your work directly contributes to the university’s bold strategic goals. Our people really are our greatest asset and we invest in them so that they can fulfil their potential. Equity, diversity and inclusion is important to us. We are committed to creating an environment where everyone is encouraged to give their best in a place where their unique experiences, perspectives and skills are seen as valuable assets. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Flexible working options • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department is working towards an Athena SWAN award. We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Work at UCL pages: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49153": {
      "title": "Business Analyst Associate (HR) - Internal Applicants Only",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-Provost (Strategy) (B24) Reference: B24-00098 About us: Please note these this role is open to Internal Applicants Only UCL is London's leading multidisciplinary university, with more than 16,000 staff and 50,000 students from 150 different countries. As well as being a global leader in teaching, UCL is also recognised as one of the most successful research institutions in the world. Based in the heart of London, UCL has a turnover of more than £1.2 billion a year. UCL's strategy sets our whole-university priorities for the next five years. This focuses investment on several shared priorities agreed through a consultation with our community and stakeholders. Enabling UCL to deliver a more cohesive set of services, we will be delivering a portfolio of change and improvement initiatives. Strategic Change will be at the heart of building this portfolio, working to support the new strategic objectives, our services and the enabling digital technologies. By providing leadership, support and hands on help we will be ensuring the delivery of fit for purpose solutions that drive value to all our users. About the role: Provide a business analysis service projects and change initiatives. Assist colleagues and other stakeholders to investigate operational issues, problems and new opportunities, contributing to business process and IT system improvements. Support a senior analyst or product owner with larger scale requests or deliver end to end analysis for small scale changes to existing services. About you: Support end users by reviewing, documenting and assessing requests for change. Gather, validate and document system and process information. Support the analysis of current ‘as-is’ business services and processes. Take part in meetings with stakeholders and assist in presenting issues and gathering requirements. Produce business analysis deliverables using relevant documentation styles in line with organisational standards and using appropriate tools. Document detailed to-be process models and requirements in line with agreed project deliverables, and communicate these to the wider team. Ensure engagement with customers and stakeholders is positive and delivers the right solution. Assist in defining holistic solutions that address organisational, people, process, information and technology aspects. Work closely with the product team to support design, delivery and testing phases of the project. Assist colleagues to define and undertake user acceptance testing for new or improved business processes and IT systems. Develop business analysis skills and expertise and attend relevant events related to business analysis to develop knowledge and proactively maintain networks with other professionals in the area. Develop agile delivery skills and techniques. What we offer: In return, you’ll have the opportunity to make a significant impact within Student Lifecycle portfolio, working alongside talented colleagues. We offer a collaborative environment where your ideas are valued, your growth is supported, and your work directly contributes to the university’s bold strategic goals. Our people really are our greatest asset and we invest in them so that they can fulfil their potential. Equity, diversity and inclusion is important to us. We are committed to creating an environment where everyone is encouraged to give their best in a place where their unique experiences, perspectives and skills are seen as valuable assets. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Flexible working options • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department is working towards an Athena SWAN award. We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Work at UCL pages: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49087": {
      "title": "Careers Consultant: Technology & Analytics Specialist (UCL School of Management)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-Provost (Education and Student Experience) (B06) Reference: B06-00214 About us: UCL Careers supports our students and recent graduates to build their futures, make informed career decisions and successfully navigate an increasingly complex global labour market. Alongside our university-wide careers provision, we work in partnership with academic departments to provide specialist, tailored careers and employability support. This role sits within our dedicated careers provision for the UCL School of Management, one of the UK’s leading business and management schools. Home to more than 2,000 students from across the world, the School has particular strengths in innovation, technology, business analytics and entrepreneurship and is currently delivering an ambitious programme of growth. Our postgraduate students have ambitious career goals, with many looking to build careers across technology, data, AI, business analytics and marketing science. We are looking for a careers professional with strong knowledge of these areas who can help them understand the opportunities available, develop their skills and translate their academic experience into successful careers. About the role: As Careers Consultant: Technology & Analytics Specialist, you will be our lead careers specialist for students interested in careers across Technology, Data Science, AI, Business Analytics and Marketing Science. You will work primarily with postgraduate students, joining a collaborative team of Careers Consultants and Employer Engagement professionals to design and deliver a comprehensive careers and professional development programme. Through workshops, group sessions, one-to-one coaching and application support, you will help students explore their options, develop their career management skills and prepare effectively for competitive recruitment processes. You will bring specialist insight into the technology and analytics labour market, helping students understand different career pathways and the skills employers are looking for. This could include careers such as Data Scientist, Data Engineer, Business Intelligence Analyst, Business Analyst, Product or Customer Insights Analyst, Quantitative Analyst and Analytics Consultant. A key part of the role will be keeping close to developments in the sector. You will use labour-market information, graduate outcomes and student insight to shape careers activity, while maintaining an up-to-date understanding of recruitment practices across technology, analytics, data and related areas. You will also build strong relationships with academic colleagues, employers and other stakeholders, working collaboratively to embed relevant employability support across the School. Our students are highly international and pursue careers across the UK and major global graduate markets, so you will also help them understand how recruitment practices and opportunities differ internationally. Please note this role is offered on a full-time basis with hybrid working available. As a student-facing role, attendance on campus (Canary Wharf School of Management) is required for currently a minimum of one day per week during term-time but may be more according to workload and business need. Job Ref: B06-00214 Closing Date: 3-11-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) Interviews: Interview are likely to take place onsite at our Canary Wharf office from mid-November About you: You may already be working in university careers or employability and have developed specialist expertise supporting students pursuing careers in technology, data or analytics. Alternatively, you might bring experience from recruitment, talent development, coaching or industry and be looking for an opportunity to use that expertise to support the next generation of talent. You will have a strong understanding of careers and recruitment across Technology and Data/Analytics, gained either through professional experience in these areas or through substantial experience supporting similar cohorts within a university or business school. You will understand the rapidly evolving graduate market for areas such as AI, machine learning, big data and data science, and be able to explain how different roles fit across the analytics lifecycle – for example, the differences between a Data Scientist, Data Engineer and Business Analyst. Just as importantly, you will enjoy working with people. You will be able to build rapport with students from a wide range of backgrounds, provide effective one-to-one careers coaching and turn specialist knowledge into engaging and accessible careers education for larger groups. You will be confident building relationships with colleagues and stakeholders, comfortable using evidence and data to evaluate the impact of your work, and able to manage projects and competing priorities effectively. You will also be curious about new approaches and technologies and keen to continue developing your own professional knowledge. Knowledge of technical recruitment methods such as coding assessments, case interviews and portfolio reviews, or familiarity with tools such as SQL, Python, Power BI, Tableau, Google Analytics or Salesforce, would be valuable but is not essential. A professional qualification in careers and employability is also desirable; we welcome applications from candidates without one who are willing to work towards an approved qualification once in post. If you are passionate about helping people navigate careers in technology and analytics, and would enjoy combining specialist labour-market knowledge with coaching, careers education and relationship building, we would be delighted to hear from you. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49212": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11247 About us: The Department of Neuroinflammation focuses primarily on research into multiple sclerosis (MS), employing a broad range of basic and clinical methodologies, including clinical therapeutic trials. The department is internationally recognised as a centre of excellence for MS research, building on the legacy of Professor Ian McDonald, whose contributions are memorialised in the widely accepted McDonald Criteria for the diagnosis of MS. Based within the department, the Queen Square Multiple Sclerosis Centre (QSMSC) operates across multiple domains of MS research and integrates research with clinical care across UCL, UCLH, and the National Hospital for Neurology and Neurosurgery (NHNN). About the role: We are seeking a Clinical Research Fellow to support the clinical delivery of the REFINE-MS trial, a multi-centre, phase 3 study led by Professor Olga Ciccarelli and Professor Max Parmar at UCL. The trial aims to identify the optimal dosing frequency of ocrelizumab in patients with stable relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis (RRMS). You will play a key role in patient recruitment, clinical assessments, and data collection, working closely with colleagues at UCLH and the MRC Clinical Trials Unit. Responsibilities will also include coordinating blood sample analysis, contributing to data interpretation, and supporting public and patient engagement activities. This academic post offers opportunities to participate in additional research projects and the clinical care of people living with MS. An honorary contract will be sought from UCLH NHS Foundation Trust, and other Trusts where the postholder may provide input, for which a DBS check will be required. The post is available immediately and funded by the National Institute for Health and Care Research - Efficacy and Mechanism Evaluation (NIHR EME) for two years in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). Informal enquiries regarding the role can be addressed to Professor Olga Ciccarelli (email: o.ciccarelli@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You will be a registered medical practitioner with a licence to practise in the UK (i.e., GMC registration) and have completed the MRCP or hold an equivalent qualification from an international institution. You will have a good understanding of the biology of neurological conditions and/or clinical assessments (e.g., EDSS, patient-reported outcome measures), with experience of working with Multiple Sclerosis patients, and the ability to provide clinical supervision. Excellent communication, interpersonal, and organisational skills, along with the ability to work independently and use your own initiative, are essential for this role. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. What we offer: The role is offered in the range of £52,748 - £82,026 per annum including London Allowance, on the CL7 or Resident Doctors Pay Scale, dependent on NHS contract and/or stage of clinical training. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Silver award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49129": {
      "title": "Continuous Improvement Manager (ELIGIBLE FOR UCL INTERNAL CANDIDATES ONLY)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; People and Culture & PS Hub (B30) Reference: B30-00262 About us: This is an exciting opportunity for a Continuous Improvement Manager to join UCL’s Service Excellence Team to support the delivery of improvement work with the UCL community. Our Service Excellence function is part of the Professional Services Hub which brings together key customer facing and transactional services across different functions: HR, Finance, Commercial, Faculty, Research, Estates, IT & Business Operations. The PS Hub is committed to delivering high quality & efficient services, with a real emphasis on customer experience. The University’s Professional Services, which the Hub is a part of, brings together colleagues who perform their work while sitting in the Hub, but also outside, by binding them together in a common framework of tools, methods, technologies and performance standards. About the role: This role is eligible for internal candidates only at UCL. Please don't apply if you are an external candidate to UCL. You will deliver positive change by engaging, encouraging and supporting colleagues across UCL to collaboratively develop and deliver user-centred and measurable solutions that address the underlying causes of common process problems. You will use data, evidence and user experience to define and understand the problems and identify the root causes. You will be at the heart of developing an improvement mindset at UCL and will champion and embed Continuous Improvement across the institution. The role is also available as a one-year secondment to internal applicants. About you: We are looking for people who have led on improvement initiatives, preferably within a service environment, using Lean Six Sigma (and/or other relevant CI methodologies) and with experience of working with stakeholders to build a continuous improvement mindset. You will have a strong track record of leading the facilitation of improvements to processes, services or systems, in a highly organised and collaborative way. We are seeking individuals who invest in their personal development and are team players to support the ongoing development of the Continuous Improvement team and UCL more widely. You will need to have the right to work in the UK because this role does not meet the eligibility requirements for a Skilled Worker Visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require right to work in the UK to carry out this role. Interviews will take place in person at Bidborough House on Wednesday 4 November 2026. If you have any queries about the role please email Paula Lewis at paula.m.lewis@ucl.ac.uk If you have any questions about the application process please email Passant Khalifa at p.khalifa@ucl.ac.uk What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49094": {
      "title": "Contracts Manager (Research and Innovation)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement) (B09) Reference: B09-01431 About us: UCL is a world-leading research-intensive university, with a thriving research and innovation community and an extensive portfolio of externally funded projects. Research and Innovation Services (RIS) supports academics throughout the research and innovation lifecycle, from securing and managing funding through to providing specialist advice on collaborations and contracts. Within RIS, Contract Services provides a single point of contact for research and innovation-related contracts, helping ensure agreements meet UCL requirements, external regulations and funder and partner terms. This is an opportunity to join a specialist team supporting research with real-world impact across UCL. About the role: As a Contracts Manager, you’ll represent UCL in the review, drafting and negotiation of a broad range of research and research-related agreements. You’ll work closely with academics, departments and professional services colleagues, providing clear advice on contractual obligations, responsibilities and risks. Your portfolio will include agreements covering research funding, collaborations, sub-contracts, studentships, confidential information, materials, data and equipment. You’ll negotiate commercially appropriate positions on areas such as intellectual property, publication rights, liability and indemnity, licensing and commercialisation, while helping ensure compliance with relevant legislation and contractual requirements. Alongside managing your own portfolio, you’ll build effective relationships across UCL, contribute to the continuous improvement of processes and best practice, and support training and guidance for departments. About you: We’re looking for someone with experience of reviewing, drafting and negotiating research contracts and/or legal agreements, together with a strong understanding of key contractual issues such as intellectual property, confidentiality, publication, liability and indemnity, data protection and governing law. A relevant degree-level qualification or equivalent is also required. You’ll bring a commercial and pragmatic approach to negotiation, with the judgement to identify contractual risks and provide clear, practical advice. Strong interpersonal and communication skills are important, particularly the ability to explain complex information to non-specialist audiences and manage stakeholder relationships and expectations effectively. The role requires someone comfortable taking ownership of a large and varied workload, balancing competing priorities and progressing multiple pieces of work at the same time. Experience or knowledge of research funding, support and contracts within higher education or a commercial research environment would be an advantage. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay •Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong.We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49277": {
      "title": "Departmental Administrative Officer (Maternity Cover)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11353 About us: The Department of Translational Neuroscience and Stroke is home to a collaboration of interlinked neurological research areas, each one focused on advancing translational scientific knowledge to further patient recovery and management and educating through teaching and academic supervision. The Department of Neuroinflammation is recognised worldwide as a centre of excellence for multiple sclerosis (MS) research. The Department employs a broad range of basic and clinical methods, including clinical therapeutic trials to undertake research into multiple sclerosis (MS). About the role: We are recruiting an experienced administrator to support both the Translational Neuroscience and Stroke and Neuroinflammation departments in the UCL Queen Square Institute of Neurology, working closely with the Departmental Administrator (DA). You will provide senior-level administrative support to the DA, Principal Investigators, staff, and students. Your responsibilities will include daily operations, financial, HR/student administration, and research governance tasks. You'll manage procedures for financial transactions, procurement, and grant applications, using systems like MyFinance, Axiom, WorkTribe, and MyHR. You'll also line manage the Departmental Administrative Assistant, providing guidance and support and overseeing their day-to-day work, and will lead ad hoc projects for the Heads of Research Department and/or DA. The post is to undertake specific maternity cover, the duration of which is dependent upon completion of the cover, return of the post holder, or cessation of the funding, whichever is the soonest. The post is available from 09 November 2026 and is to 17 December 2027 or until the return of the substantive post holder, whichever is earliest. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 40% of time on site. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). Informal enquiries regarding the role can be addressed to Judith Jolleys ( j.jolleys@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You will have a good standard of education to A-Level or NVQ Level IV administration, or equivalent professional experience. An interest in financial management, along with experience using financial systems and handling research grants, is essential. You will bring substantial administrative experience from a large, complex organisation, such as a university or NHS, and have a proven track record in designing, implementing, and maintaining efficient management information systems and procedures. The role involves interaction with many people within and external to UCL and requires a proactive, highly organised, and efficient individual with a professional attitude, willingness to help, and the ability to work collaboratively and problem solve effectively. This role does not meet the eligibility requirements for a Skilled Worker Visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require right to work in the UK to carry out this role. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Gold award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49269": {
      "title": "Education Administrator (Taught Programmes)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03300 About us: UCL is a radically different university. Founded in 1826 in the heart of London, we were the first university in England to welcome students of any religion and the first to welcome women on equal terms with men. Today UCL has over 40,000 students and 12,500 staff and is one of the world’s top 10 universities. We have a culture of promoting diversity and sustainability and 30 Nobel laureates among our current and previous alumni. Ranked 8th in the QS World University Rankings for Arts and Humanities 2026, and with 85% of our research graded 4* and 3* in the recent Research Excellence Framework (REF 2021), UCL Arts & Humanities (A&H) is recognised globally for both its teaching and research excellence. Alongside the traditional arts and humanities departments of English, Philosophy, Greek & Latin, and the School of European Languages, Culture & Society (SELCS), we are also proud to have the Slade School of Fine Art, Hebrew & Jewish Studies, Information Studies, the School of Slavonic and East European Studies (SSEES), European & International Social & Political Studies (EISPS), Arts and Sciences (UASc), and many interdisciplinary degrees at all levels of study as part of our Faculty. About the role: The Faculty of Arts and Humanities are recruiting for one full-time Education Administrator (Taught Programmes) to support particular areas of our taught programmes portfolio (both Undergraduate and Postgraduate Taught programmes). The postholder will provide specialist administration to designated taught degree programmes, ensuring effective procedures are adopted and documented for dealing with all aspects of programme administration. They will assist in achieving a high quality and timely service to current and prospective students and staff related to the designated taught degree programmes and beyond. They will also provide support for all matters relating to the teaching and learning activities of students, from enquiry to completion, including supporting committees and processes relating to assessment, student, and programme records. The post holder will be part of a wider Faculty Education and Student Experience (EASE) team, line managed through the Faculty, as part of our Faculty’s Professional Services (PS) staff community. They will be expected to spend time physically located with the academic programme team(s) they are supporting, on a regular basis. This will be discussed, agreed and periodically reviewed with the Line Manager. This role does not meet the eligibility requirements for a Skilled Worker Visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require right to work in the UK to carry out this role. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 40% on site , in line with UCL’s Hybrid Working Guidance . Location: This position will involve supporting programmes delivered at both the Bloomsbury and UCL East (Stratford) campuses, and travel between the campuses will be required. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. About you: The successful candidate will be educated to GCSE standard or equivalent with experience of administrative work in a busy office environment. Applicants will be able to demonstrate good organisation skills, with an ability to prioritise and organise their own variable workload, and be able to use their good interpersonal skills to interact effectively and regularly with a wide range of staff and students . Clear written and verbal communication skills, combined with good computer skills and a working knowledge of the MS Office suite of applications (Word, Excel, Outlook, Teams, etc), are also key requirements of the role. Experience of providing administrative support in a Higher Education (HE) setting, including the use of student records systems and virtual learning environments , are desirable. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Alex Craven, Head of Education & Student Experience, on a.craven@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the staffing team on ah-operations@ucl.ac.uk. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days of holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holidays, and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We, therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49256": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant x2",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; People and Culture & PS Hub (B30) Reference: B30-00272 About us: UCL’s Professional Services (PS) Hub brings together customer-facing and transactional services across HR, Finance, Estates, IT and Business Operations. We are committed to delivering high-quality, efficient services with a strong focus on customer experience. Within the PS Hub, the Business Operations team provides a centre of excellence for administrative and executive support, working across UCL’s Vice-Provost and Vice-President Offices to support the effective delivery of their priorities. About the role: We are seeking two organised and proactive Executive Assistants to provide high-level support to one Vice-Provost or three Directors across UCL’s Vice-Provost and Vice-President Offices. Reporting to the Executive Support Manager, you will manage complex diaries and inboxes, coordinate national and international travel, and liaise with senior colleagues and external partners. You will organise meetings, committees, events and away days, prepare agendas, take notes and monitor actions, while handling confidential information with discretion. You will also support specialist projects, recruitment and staff induction, and work collaboratively with other Executive Assistants to provide continuity of support. The role is based in London, with hybrid working available in line with UCL’s arrangements and operational requirements. If you have any questions about the application process or the role please contact Passant Khalifa (Senior Recruiter) at p.khalifa@ucl.ac.uk About you: You will have experience providing senior-level administrative and secretarial support to multiple leaders within a complex organisation, including managing busy diaries and organising committee meetings and events. You will bring excellent written communication skills, advanced proficiency in Microsoft Word, PowerPoint and Excel, and meticulous attention to detail. You will be able to manage competing priorities, maintain high standards under pressure and adapt confidently to changing requirements at short notice. A proactive and collaborative approach is essential, alongside the ability to build strong working relationships, influence colleagues at all levels and exercise sound judgement when handling complex or sensitive matters. Experience within higher education or a research environment would be an advantage What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49274": {
      "title": "Finance Business Partner - INTERNAL OPPORTUNITY FOR UCL EMPLOYEES ONLY",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00409 About us: We currently have one vacant position for Finance Business Partner within the Finance Business Partnering team. The team forges strong relationships with stakeholders to enable expert advice, critical challenge and timely decision support to enable the best financial performance. The team is responsible for building trusted relationships and providing professional financial advice to UCL stakeholders. About the role: As one of our Finance Business Partners you will build partnerships and maintain strong relationships with senior managers and their teams. You will be expected to lead on financial and resource matters; influence decision making; improve the understanding of business performance; support business strategy development; deliver analysis and insight on business opportunities; and provide options and scenarios for business decisions. This high profile and vital role provides wide ranging support on strategic and financial matters across their business area and the wider University as relevant. This will include drawing on finance experts to support complex or large projects to ensure the technical finance support is focused on the appropriate areas. This is a new and exciting job which will be fundamental in supporting the finance function to provide an excellent service to the University. There will be the opportunity to drive change and improve decision making across the University. A full range of duties can be found on the attached job description. About you: To do this job you'll need to: Be a fully qualified accountant (CCAB) with evidence of up-to-date technical knowledge Have experience of working in a Business Partnering role within a complex organisation Have demonstrable negotiation, relationship building, and conflict resolutions skills Possess excellent communication skills, ensuring clarity of messages, ideas and facts Have experience of delivering financial analysis and producing reports to non-finance staff, demonstrating measurable outcomes Please ensure you carefully review our job description before applying and that your application reflects the essential and desirable criteria as much as possible. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49241": {
      "title": "Global Engagement Manager",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement) (B09) Reference: B09-01434 About us: At UCL, our Global Engagement team helps put UCL's knowledge to work in the world, supporting our faculties to deliver meaningful impact through international partnerships and collaboration. Working within the Office of the Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement), the team brings together expertise in international research and education, global partnership development, geopolitical analysis, strategic planning and communications. We work closely with academics, professional services colleagues and external partners to strengthen UCL's international relationships and support activities that deliver positive change across the world. About the role: We are seeking a Global Engagement Manager to join our Global Engagement team at UCL, based in Bloomsbury, London. This is an exciting opportunity to play a key role in supporting UCL's international partnerships, projects and strategic initiatives. Working closely with Senior Global Engagement Managers and Strategic Global Initiatives Managers, the postholder will coordinate partnership activities, manage projects and develop relationships with external organisations across a range of international regions. Initially, the postholder will primarily support two regional portfolios: Americas & Australasia, and Japan, South Korea & Singapore. There will also be opportunities to contribute to projects and initiatives beyond these regions, supporting the team's wider international engagement activities. The role involves supporting the development and implementation of international partnerships, coordinating high-profile meetings, visits and delegations, and producing research, briefings and reports to inform strategic engagement. Responsibilities also include maintaining partnership records, supporting the development of agreements and providing practical advice to colleagues across UCL on international engagement opportunities. The successful candidate will work with a wide range of internal and external stakeholders, helping ensure that UCL's global partnerships and initiatives are delivered effectively and contribute to the university's wider strategic ambitions. About you: We are looking for an organised, proactive and collaborative individual with experience of developing and implementing international partnerships, ideally within higher education, research, innovation, government or policy environments. Strong relationship-building and communication skills are essential, alongside experience of independently supporting multiple projects and working effectively with a range of internal and external stakeholders. The successful candidate will have excellent written communication and analytical skills, with the ability to undertake research, interpret complex information and contribute to strategic reports, proposals and planning documents. An ability to manage competing priorities, adapt to changing circumstances and work collaboratively within a high-performing team will be important. An understanding of internationalisation within higher education, experience of preparing complex documents or agreements, and additional language skills would be advantageous. A good undergraduate degree or equivalent professional experience is required, alongside a willingness to travel and occasionally work outside standard hours where necessary. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49202": {
      "title": "Head of KE Strategy and Engagement",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Strategy, Project and Change Management; London; £68,284-£78,386​; Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement) (B09) Reference: B09-01436 About us: UCL is one of the world’s leading universities, with a strong commitment to ensuring that our research, expertise and ideas create meaningful economic and societal benefit. UCL Innovation & Enterprise (I&E), part of Research, Innovation & Global Engagement (RIGE), brings together activity spanning knowledge exchange, business and innovation partnerships, entrepreneurship, translational and industrial research, consultancy, commercial research contracts, and the commercialisation of intellectual property. Our Knowledge Exchange (KE) activity plays an important role in connecting UCL’s expertise with organisations, communities and partners beyond the university. We work across UCL to strengthen the environment for KE, build capability and engagement, and ensure our strategic approach responds to institutional priorities, government policy and funder expectations. About the role: We are seeking a Head of Knowledge Exchange Strategy & Engagement to join UCL Innovation & Enterprise on a 12-month fixed-term basis to provide maternity cover. The role is also available as a secondment opportunity for internal UCL candidates who apply. This is a senior strategic role leading the development, implementation and monitoring of UCL’s knowledge exchange and innovation strategy. Working across I&E, RIGE, faculties and the wider UCL community, the postholder will help ensure our strategic priorities, resources and delivery activity are aligned and supported by effective governance, planning and reporting. A key focus will be embedding I&E’s strategic planning framework and strengthening how data, evidence and insight are used to inform decision-making and demonstrate impact. This includes oversight of institutional KE monitoring and reporting, including HE-BCI, HEIF and KEF, alongside the production of strategic reports, funder submissions, committee papers and external consultations. The role will also provide leadership for programmes and initiatives that build KE capability and engagement across UCL, oversee a strategic portfolio of projects and investment, manage resources and budgets, and lead and develop a team of specialist staff. About you: We are looking for an experienced strategic leader who can operate confidently across a large and complex organisation. You will bring significant experience of developing and implementing organisation-wide strategy or planning frameworks, supported by effective governance and a strong understanding of how evidence and insight can inform organisational decision-making. You will have experience of leading and developing teams, setting priorities and enabling delivery through others, alongside the ability to provide strategic direction and oversee complex programmes, projects or portfolios. Experience of developing approaches to monitoring, evaluation, data capture and reporting will also be important. Excellent stakeholder management, influencing and communication skills are essential. You will be comfortable working with senior colleagues, navigating competing priorities and ambiguity, and translating complex information into clear and credible reports, briefings and strategic advice. Knowledge of the UK higher education policy landscape for innovation, enterprise and knowledge exchange, and an understanding of how the wider policy environment shapes organisational strategy, would be particularly valuable. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49222": {
      "title": "Head of Student Lifecycle Services (Progression & Awards)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Student Well-being and Registry; London; £68,284-£78,386​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05765 About us: Student and Registry Services (SRS) provides essential services across the student lifecycle, supporting UCL faculties and departments to deliver a high-quality student experience. Within SRS, Student Lifecycle Services is responsible for the operational management and assurance of taught student records from enrolment through to progression, award and the production of official academic records. As part of Student Operations, Student Lifecycle Services is led through two complementary Head of Student Lifecycle Services roles, with each postholder accountable for a defined service portfolio while sharing responsibility for a coherent, resilient and student-centred end-to-end student lifecycle service. We are looking for an experienced student lifecycle professional to join us as Head of Student Lifecycle Services (Progression and Awards). This is a senior leadership role with responsibility for services that are fundamental to the integrity of UCL’s academic administration and the student experience. About the role: As Head of Student Lifecycle Services (Progression and Awards), you will provide strategic leadership for UCL’s results, progression, awards and official academic records services across undergraduate, postgraduate taught, affiliate and other taught student populations. You will lead the Student Lifecycle Managers within your portfolio, ensuring high-performing, inclusive and resilient teams, and provide strategic oversight of the annual results, progression and awards cycle. You will act as the senior business process owner, ensuring academic regulations and decisions are translated into accurate, timely and controlled student outcomes and official documentation. Working collaboratively across faculties, departments and professional services, you will lead service improvement, strengthen governance and resilience, and shape the development of student lifecycle systems and processes. You will use data, user feedback and sector insight to drive continuous improvement and ensure services remain student-centred, compliant and fit for the future. Please note, this role is offered on a full-time basis with hybrid working available, currently a minimum of 1-day per week in our London Kings Cross office (subject to change) plus onsite events when required Job Ref: B01-05765 Closing Date: 30-10-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) About you: You will be an experienced student lifecycle professional with substantial senior experience of results, progression, awards or official academic records within a complex higher education environment. You will have a strong understanding of the UK higher education student lifecycle and the academic, policy and regulatory environment affecting progression and awards. You will have experience of leading high-volume, deadline-critical services and managing managers, alongside a strong track record in strategic service leadership, service redesign and organisational or digital change. You will be confident owning complex processes, controls, risks and data quality, and using management information and user feedback to improve services and inform decisions. A collaborative and inclusive leader, you will have excellent influencing and communication skills, strong strategic judgement and the ability to build effective relationships with senior stakeholders across organisational boundaries. You will be comfortable making evidence-based decisions in complex circumstances and remaining calm and accountable during high-pressure operational periods. Experience of SITS or another enterprise student information system, and of working with product, agile delivery or technology teams, would be desirable. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49233": {
      "title": "HR People Partner",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; HR and Wellbeing; London; £56,431-£66,437​; People and Culture & PS Hub (B30) Reference: B30-00261 About us: UCL is London’s Global University – a diverse intellectual university committed to excellence, innovation, and inclusion. Our People and Culture division is a dynamic and service-driven team of over 160 professionals who collaborate closely with academic faculties and professional service divisions to ensure our people strategies meet the university’s needs. We are proud of our values-led culture and our commitment to removing barriers and actively promoting diversity and inclusion across our community. About the role: We have two full-time HR People Partner opportunities available on a hybrid basis, working from our Bidborough House, King's Cross office for a minimum of two days per week (including Monday as our HR anchor day, plus one additional day). One role will provide partnering support to a designated faculty. As their HR People Partner, you will influence and support leaders to deliver strategic people priorities, acting as a trusted advisor across your stakeholder group. You will help leaders navigate complex employee relations matters and use people data to inform decision-making. A key part of the role will be translating UCL's people strategy into practical solutions that enhance staff development, engagement, recruitment, and retention. Working across a broad range of strategic people priorities, you will support organisational change, workforce and succession planning, performance management and culture initiatives. You will act as the link between your stakeholders and our Centres of Expertise, bringing insight, support, and constructive challenge to ensure effective outcomes. You will also champion inclusive leadership and diverse talent pipelines, helping to foster a culture of wellbeing, continuous development, and high performance. You will develop a deep understanding of your faculty's culture, challenges, and ambitions, enabling you to provide informed guidance and tailored people solutions that make a meaningful impact. The second role is a unique opportunity for an experienced HR People Partner who thrives in variety and complexity. Supporting different areas of the organisation according to business need, the postholder will bring broad HR expertise, quickly understand local challenges, and confidently influence senior stakeholders to deliver effective people solutions. Both roles are excellent opportunities to build strong partnerships with senior academic and professional services leaders while supporting the delivery of strategic people priorities. If you have any queries regarding the role or the application process, please contact Passant Khalifa via p.khalifa@ucl.ac.uk About you: You are an experienced HR professional who thrives in complex matrix environments and can demonstrate impact through strategic thinking, sound judgement, and effective problem-solving. You have successfully managed complex employee relations cases, built credibility with senior stakeholders, and delivered people initiatives that improve engagement and organisational effectiveness. You are skilled at influencing and coaching leaders, building productive relationships, and using evidence-based insights to support decision-making. A confident communicator, you are comfortable operating in ambiguity and balancing risk with organisational priorities. You are committed to equity, diversity, and inclusion and understand how to embed these principles into everyday practice. You enjoy working with autonomy while collaborating closely with colleagues and centres of expertise, and you will ideally have experience within a large public sector, higher education, or similarly complex organisation. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion ."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49168": {
      "title": "Laboratory Management Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Laboratory Technical and Lab Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11315 About us: Our mission is to maximise and advocate for the holistic health of all children, young people and the adults they will become, through world-class research, education and public engagement. The UCL GOS ICH, together with its clinical partner Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children, forms the largest concentration of children’s health research outside North America. The 2024-29 GOS ICH strategy focuses on its five scientific programmes. GOS ICH’s activities include active engagement with children and families, to ensure that our work is relevant and appropriate to their needs. GOS ICH generates the funding for our research by setting out our proposals in high quality applications to public, charitable and industrial funding bodies and disseminates the results of our research by publication in the medical and scientific literature, to clinicians, policy makers and the wider public. The Institute offers world-class education and training across a wide range of teaching and life learning programmes which address the needs of students and professional groups who are interested in and undertaking work relevant to child health. GOS ICH holds an Athena SWAN Charter Gold Award. Please only attach relevant documents to your application (qualifications, cover letters, supporting statements) and avoid attaching large files e.g. research papers, thesis, publications etc. About the role: We are looking for a Laboratory Management Assistant to join the Laboratory Management Team at the UCL GOS Institute of Child Health. This is a varied, practical role supporting the smooth day-to-day running of laboratory facilities across ICH and Zayed Centre for Research (ZCR). Working as part of the wider Laboratory Management Team, you will support laboratory operations, communal equipment, inspections, health and safety activities, Estates matters, laboratory moves and sustainability initiatives. You will also provide purchasing and procurement support, including obtaining quotations, raising purchase orders, liaising with suppliers and helping to maintain accurate purchasing and financial records. The role also includes maintaining Laboratory Management information and records, supporting meetings and committees, and working closely with the CL3 Laboratory Manager on routine checks, documentation, equipment, stock and general laboratory organisation. You will work with a wide range of colleagues across ICH, GOSH and UCL, so a helpful, professional and collaborative approach is important. This is an on-site role based at UCL GOS Institute of Child Health. The role is a secondment cover for 12 months in the first instance. About you: You will have a relevant biological or biomedical science degree, or equivalent, together with experience of working in a research laboratory environment. You should be comfortable working with laboratory equipment and troubleshooting routine problems and have a good understanding of laboratory health and safety. You will be organised, accurate and able to manage a varied workload, using your own initiative while knowing when to escalate more complex issues. Good communication and interpersonal skills are essential, as you will work closely with laboratory users, suppliers and colleagues across Professional Services. Experience of supporting purchasing or procurement activities, such as raising orders and liaising with suppliers, is important, along with an awareness of the need to follow organisational procurement and financial procedures. You should also have strong Microsoft Office skills and a positive, practical and helpful approach to problem-solving. Experience of laboratory safety systems and previous administrative experience within a laboratory environment would be an advantage. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49197": {
      "title": "Laboratory Technician",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Laboratory Technical and Lab Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11360 About us: The UCL Division of Surgery and Interventional Science is a collaborative and cross-disciplinary environment where clinicians, engineers, allied health professionals and researchers are constantly striving for innovative approaches to treatment. This includes developing highly effective methods which go beyond the boundaries of conventional wisdom and treating each patient as an individual. As a world leader in medical and biomedical research we are able to see at first-hand the directions in which medicine and science are moving and where patient and industry needs are growing. This knowledge ensures that we are producing and teaching some of the most innovative master’s courses for clinicians and scientists across the world. Additionally, our cutting-edge research expertise ensures that the latest techniques are taught using the most advanced equipment. Finally, we offer innovative master’s courses led by world leading experts, providing a truly research-based learning experience delivered by some of the greatest minds in science and medicine today. The Division reaching across three London campuses (Bloomsbury, Royal Free and Stanmore) brings together three major research departments; Targeted Intervention, Surgical Biotechnology and Orthopaedics and Musculoskeletal Science. Within these departments are world class research centres, specialising in many different surgical and interventional specialties. The Division also offers teaching and training in a wide range of subjects with intercalated BSc options for UCL medical students, direct MBBS teaching, undergraduate and postgraduate taught and research programmes for both clinicians and non-clinicians. The Division holds a silver award for the Athena Swan Charter which recognizes advancement of gender equality and aims for representation, progression and success for all. Further information on the Division can be found at https://www.ucl.ac.uk/surgery/ About the role: We are looking for someone with the skills, knowledge, and ability to provide our students with training, teaching and support for the use of a variety of imaging and physiochemical characterization equipment as well as the provision of teaching, training and support with related sample preparations, the setup and clean-up of teaching laboratories for our taught BSc and MSc courses and the contribution to laboratory-based teaching. The successful candidate will assist with the maintenance and running of the equipment to a high standard and ensure that all health and safety regulations are complied with. The postholder will be required to assist academic staff with student teaching and also to be willing to participate in university and external public engagement events. Other • The advert will close on 28th October 2026 at 23:59 GMT, however we may close applications early if we receive a high volume of applications. Early application submission is recommended. • Interview Date(s) TBC • If you have any queries about the role, please contact Catalina Moreno on m.catalina.moreno@ucl.ac.uk • If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR Team on l.oppong@ucl.ac.uk • The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. Closing an advert early: In the event we get a high number of applications, we may close the advert early before the published closing date. As a minimum we will keep all adverts open for 2 weeks. ​​​​​​​ About you: The post holder must have good general education to BSc, MSc levels or equivalent or having acquired substantial relevant experience. Experience in operating and maintaining research equipment. Research background with experience in cell/tissue culture and molecular biology and/or materials characterisation and/or advanced microscopy Training and background in health and safety relating to laboratories in the biomedical sciences area. Ability to teach laboratory techniques to students and advise on appropriate use of methodology and methodical problem solving would be advantageous. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49119": {
      "title": "Lecturer or Associate Professor in Computational Humanities and Social Science",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Academic; UCL East; See advert text; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03274 About us: University College London (UCL) is a world-leading multidisciplinary university, consistently ranked among the top higher education institutions globally. As London’s Global University, UCL is committed to fostering an inclusive and diverse academic community that promotes innovation, creativity, and social impact. The UCL Department of Information Studies (DIS) in the Faculty of Arts and Humanities launched the Information, Data & Society BSc programme in 2024. The department also offers postgraduate qualifications for the library, archive, and information professions, publishing, information and data science, and digital humanities, and collaborates in the delivery of an inter-faculty BSc in Information Management for Business. We have a strong international reputation for research and are deeply committed to our teaching quality. About the role: The Information, Data, and Society BSc attracts students from around the world with a wide range of technical abilities and interdisciplinary interests. The successful candidate will be able to design and deliver teaching that is accessible, engaging, and relevant to students who are not technical specialists, and who approach data and digital systems from diverse cultural, social, and critical perspectives. Experience of undergraduate teaching in small and large groups, as well as in a pastoral role would be welcome. Candidates are strongly advised to read the programme prospectus before applying ( https://www.ucl.ac.uk/study/prospective-students/undergraduate/courses/information-data-and-society-bsc ). A fuller view of modules offered on the Programme can be found by searching the module codes for the degree on the UCL module catalogue . The role includes undergraduate teaching, dissertation supervision at UG, PhD, and potentially also MA level, they may be asked to develop and lead a new module aligned with their expertise in addition to teaching on existing modules, including through taking on strategic or developmental roles related to departmental governance and administration. We seek an ambitious scholar with a commitment to interdisciplinary teaching and collaboration. The successful candidate will join a department committed to critical, applied inquiry into the complex interplay of information, data, and society, and will help shape the future of teaching and research at UCL. This post is NOT aimed at candidates whose primary expertise lies in computer science, AI/ML, business analytics, digital marketing, or health informatics. We seek a scholar whose technical skills are embedded within humanities or social science research, rather than oriented toward optimisation, modelling, clinical applications, or media production. Candidates who do not have a completed and awarded PhD in a relevant subject will not be shortlisted. About you: The Department of Information Studies at UCL invites applications for a Lectureship or Associate Professorship in Computational Humanities and Social Sciences (Grade 8 or 9). We seek a scholar whose work brings an applied computational and quantitative methodological approach to research questions about society in the humanities or social sciences, with a special focus on aspects relating to data and information, in line with the nature of the Department of Information Studies. The successful candidate will combine a high degree of technical competence with a compelling and substantive research agenda. The successful candidate will also demonstrate their ability to contribute to the teaching and development of digital and quantitative methods courses across our undergraduate programme in Information, Data, and Society BSc at UCL East as set out in the Programme Prospectus. We invite candidates to outline in their cover letter which 2-3 of the following undergraduate modules they could contribute to, either via team teaching or as module lead (we do not expect candidates to cover all listed modules): INST0065 Data Visualisation INST0082 Analysing Textual Data at Scale INST0086 Foundations of Statistical Methods and Computing INST0091 Introduction to Programming INST0106 Research Design We welcome applicants from disciplinary backgrounds including information studies, social sciences, digital and computational humanities, cultural analytics, or adjacent fields. Candidates should demonstrate computational research that engages meaningfully with societal topics in the humanities and social sciences, while being methodologically rigorous. Candidates should be building a record of high-quality publications and an ambitious research trajectory, centred on the themes of information, data and society. IMPORTANT INFORMATION Any queries about the role not already covered in this document can be addressed to Dr Daniel Wilson: daniel.c.s.wilson@ucl.ac.uk Queries relating to the recruitment process and/or reasonable adjustments can be addressed to Terrie Fiawoo: t.fiawoo@ucl.ac.uk As part of your application please attach (under the other documents section) only the following: Personal Statement (Max 2 pages) Research Statement (Max 2 page) A CV (Max 4 pages) What we offer: This post is offered at either Lecturer Grade 8; £56,431-£62,903 or Associate Professor Grade 9: £68,284-£74,166 (including London Allowance) dependent upon experience. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; people with disabilities; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49112": {
      "title": "NIHR Clinical Lecturer in Primary Care under the Prevention & Public Health Theme",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Clinical; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11378 About us: UCL is one of the World's top universities, based in the heart of London. The Research Department of Primary Care and Population Health (PCPH) is a multidisciplinary academic primary care teaching and research centre, working collaboratively to improve the health of individuals and populations. We research the entire life cycle of complex interventions, starting with understanding health problems from patients’ perspectives, and using a range of research approaches to develop, evaluate and implement interventions. We are part of the UCL Institute of Epidemiology & Healthcare (IEHC) and the NIHR School for Primary Care Research, and have strong links to both the NIHR Schools of Public Health and Social Care with extensive opportunities for collaboration/funding/training and access to local and national primary care networks and datasets. About the role: This NIHR funded post provides up to four years’ post-doctoral training for a general practitioner who can demonstrate outstanding potential to develop their career as an independent researcher, educator and leader in the field of prevention and public health research and teaching. They will be required to develop a coherent programme of research in an area relating to primary care prevention and public health. Application Details: Salary will be on the Non-Consultant Clinical Pay Scale CL8, at point 12 (£85,596) with an additional Market Supplement (£17,991): Total £103,587 plus £4,678 London Allowance (all pro-rata, per annum) This is a fixed-term contract with external funding until 31/8/2031. Appointment is dependent on award and confirmation of a PhD (or equivalent). This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. This appointment is subject to: Eligibility to be fully registered with the General Medical Council Registered on the NHS performers list as a General Medical Practitioner Disclosure and Barring Service assessment Application Process: We may close applications early if we receive a high volume of applications. Early application submission is recommended. As a minimum we will keep all adverts open for 2 weeks. A full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page along with guidance notes. Please use the personal statement section to explain how you meet each of the essential and desirable criteria outlined in the person specification. We would like you to submit with your application: 1. CV with full publication list 2. A personal statement no longer than 2 sides of A4 (Arial 11 font) indicating: How you propose to develop your own research programme (under prevention and public health theme) over the next 4 years; and How you propose to contribute to our undergraduate and postgraduate teaching programmes. Interviews: In-person interviews are scheduled for Monday 25th January 2027 at the Royal Free Campus Contract Details: If you have any informal queries about the post or difficulties accessing the online recruitment system, please contact Professor Joe Rosenthal (Email: j.rosenthal@ucl.ac.uk ). About you: Candidates must be registered medical practitioners who have completed GP vocational training, obtained the MRCGP or equivalent and are eligible for the NHS GP Performers List. Candidates must have submitted their PhD/MD thesis at the time of application and must have been fully awarded by the deadline for taking up post which is 1/9/2027. Candidates must be able to demonstrate outstanding potential to develop their career as an independent researcher, educator and leader in the field of prevention and primary care. Candidates who have already completed one NIHR Clinical Lectureship may not apply for another. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan and expenses Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver Award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49171": {
      "title": "Operations Coordinator - Internal UCL Applicants Only",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03296 About us: Founded in 1915, the UCL School of Slavonic and East European Studies (SSEES) is one of the UK's largest institutions for research and teaching on Russia, the Baltics, and Central, Eastern and South-East Europe. Our mission is to create in-depth knowledge of this area in regional, comparative, transnational, and global contexts, and to promote and disseminate an understanding of our region to the wider world; to develop multidisciplinary, interdisciplinary, and multi-method approaches to knowledge creation and to teaching; and to enhance the development of area studies at UCL and among the wider academic community. We offer taught and research programmes of study, both within our four core disciplines (Economics and Business; Politics and Sociology; Languages and Culture; and History), as well as through interdisciplinary programmes (for example, our BA History, Politics & Economics). The SSEES student community comprises approximately 1200 students spread across undergraduate (approx. 850 students), postgraduate taught (approx. 270 students) and postgraduate research (approx. 80 students) levels. About the role: The Operations Coordinator will play a key role in ensuring the smooth and effective day-to-day running of the Department, overseeing a range of operational activities. This includes managing departmental premises, including room and desk allocations, cleaning and maintenance; coordinating health and safety matters as the Departmental Safety Officer; supporting financial processes, including invoice and purchase order management and routine financial reporting; and overseeing general provisioning, such as stationery, photocopier agreements and furniture purchases. The postholder will help ensure that departmental facilities, resources and administrative processes are managed efficiently and effectively, supporting the wider needs of the Department. This role is available to UCL Internal Candidates only, on a fixed term or secondment basis for 12 months. Queries regarding this role should be addressed to Madalina Rau, HR Officer (ssees.people@ucl.ac.uk). We anticipate that interviews will be carried out on the morning of Wednesday 4 November. About you: The successful candidate will be educated to degree level or have equivalent professional experience, with experience in an operational role within a Higher Education Institution. They will have excellent IT skills, including proficiency in Microsoft Office applications such as Word, Excel and Teams, and the ability to use these effectively to support departmental operations. They will also demonstrate strong leadership and interpersonal skills, with the ability to lead and motivate colleagues, supervise the work of other staff members, and ensure that tasks are delivered efficiently and to consistently high standards. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Hybrid Final Salary Pension Scheme Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49236": {
      "title": "Policy Fellowship Coordinator",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement) (B09) Reference: B09-01437 About us: UCL is one of the world’s leading universities, with a long-standing commitment to ensuring that excellence in research goes hand in hand with making a positive contribution to society. The Office of the Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement) brings together functions, services and resources that support UCL’s research and engagement ambitions, working across institutional boundaries to enable responsible impact locally, nationally and internationally. UCL Public Policy is UCL’s institutional programme for strengthening engagement between researchers and policymakers and increasing the contribution of UCL’s academic expertise to public policy. The team supports capacity building across UCL, develops relationships with policymakers, coordinates knowledge exchange and policy engagement activity, and helps communicate and disseminate UCL research to policy audiences. About the role: We are seeking a Policy Fellowship Coordinator to join UCL Public Policy and lead the delivery of our Policy Fellowships Programme. This is a maternity cover opportunity, funded through Research England’s Policy Support Fund, with the maternity cover expected to run until 7 December 2027. Ideally, we are looking for someone who can start asap, allowing for a handover and shadowing period during December /January with the current postholder. Part-time, full-time and job-share arrangements will be considered. The role will oversee the full lifecycle of UCL Public Policy’s fellowship schemes, including Researchers in Residence, the London Local Authority Policy Fellowship, Policy Residency and CSAP affiliate schemes. You will coordinate programme delivery and administration, build relationships with fellows, policymakers and partners, and ensure a high-quality experience for those participating in the programmes. You will also plan and deliver workshops, training and engagement activities that bring researchers and policymakers together, while supporting wider capability building in policy engagement across UCL. Alongside programme delivery, you will coordinate monitoring, evaluation and reporting, maintain accurate programme data and help communicate the reach and impact of the programme. About you: You will bring experience of working in UK public policy or a related engagement environment, alongside strong programme management and delivery skills. You will be comfortable managing multiple activities in a complex environment and working across areas such as funding, data, contracts and programme evaluation. Strong relationship-building skills will be essential, with experience of engaging researchers, policymakers and senior external stakeholders and developing partnerships that support successful programme delivery. You will also be confident designing and delivering workshops, meetings, training or other capability-building activities. You will be highly organised and able to manage competing priorities, relationships and complex queries at pace. Excellent written and verbal communication skills are important, including the ability to distil complex information and produce clear, engaging briefings and reports for different audiences. A proactive and collaborative approach will be key, alongside the ability to work independently, develop ideas and adapt to changing priorities. Experience of designing or scaling policy fellowship or other knowledge exchange programmes, or experience within higher education, would be particularly valuable. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49092": {
      "title": "Post Graduate Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07947 About us: The Centre for Advanced Spatial Analysis is offering an opportunity to assist on its Urban Spatial Science Dissertation module as Post Graduate Teaching Assistant. Both full and part time students are eligible to apply. Please see the Job Description, at the bottom of this page, for full details. About the role: Post holder can be expected to take on some or all of the following duties: • Teaching assistance through the facilitation of seminars and/or workshops; • Class preparation and meeting with lecturers/module organisers; • After-class contact time with students, including time-tabled office hours; • Marking students' work; • Giving feedback to students. Please note that PGTA roles are dependent on student numbers and these are to be confirmed for individual modules. In the event of student numbers being lower than expected, the work may be not required or reduced. PGTA will be notified if work is withdrawn on this basis. Please note that this is an internal opportunity for UCL students. Only if there is no suitable UCL PhD student available, applications from external PhD students at other universities would be considered. For any queries about the role or about the application process please contact Sandra Starosta (s.starosta@ucl.ac.uk). To apply please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below and complete application form. Please remember to attach your CV. About you: You must be registered as a PhD student at the start of the academic year 2026/2027 to be eligible to apply. Any PGR student wishing to undertake teaching or assessing responsibilities at UCL must have attended the Arena One: Gateway workshop. You must have the right to work in the UK. Other requirements are: • Working towards a postgraduate research degree (PhD/EngD). • A desire to gain teaching experience in a built environment discipline. • Disciplinary knowledge and, if applicable, specialist skills of the subject area of the programme unit/module. • Ability to communicate clearly, both orally and in writing, with students and staff. • Excellent organisational and time-management skills. • Ability to be flexible in terms of research workload and other commitments. • Ability to work independently for short periods and as part of a team, recognising when advice / input needs to be sought. • Available for the three hour mandatory UCL Arena One Gateway Workshop, if not previously attended. • Commitment to UCL’s policy of equal opportunities and the ability to work harmoniously with colleagues and students of all cultures and backgrounds. • The ability to meet deadlines. What we offer: The appointment will be made on UCL’s salary structure at grade 6. PGTA will be paid for training, preparation time, attending lectures and meetings, office hours and marking. The estimated total number of working hours is listed in the Job Description, at the bottom of this page. Payments will be spread evenly for the duration of the module. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more about the benefits on offer. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy (https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/hr-policies-procedures-and-advice/z-policies/prevention-bullying-harassment-and-sexual-misconduct) and Personal Relationships Policy (https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/hr-policies-procedures-and-advice/z-policies/personal-relationships-policy)"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49096": {
      "title": "Post-doctoral Research Fellow in Psychiatric Epidemiology (EPICare)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11208 About us: An exciting opportunity has arisen for a Postdoctoral Research Fellow to join the NIHR-funded EPICare Programme, a major national research programme developing, implementing and evaluating the first psychosis registry and clinical dashboard for Early Intervention in Psychosis (EIP) services in England. The programme aims to provide timely insights into EIP service care, and patient outcomes to support clinical decision-making and improvements in EIP care. The Postdoctoral Research Fellow will lead the evaluation of the clinical effectiveness of the EPICare psychosis registry, contributing to Work Package 5 of the EPICare programme which is funded by a NIHR Programme Grant for Applied Research. The postholder will assess the accuracy and reliability of the demographic, treatment and clinical outcomes data captured within the digital registry using epidemiological methods. They will also develop analytical protocols and use the national registry to answer research questions pertaining to the real-world effectiveness of NICE-mandated treatments in EIP care, and the inequalities and variance in EIP treatment and fidelity to NICE EIP standards. The postholder may also contribute original research questions to the project. This work package will provide a crucial evaluation of the EPICare psychosis registry and aims to answer key research questions to enable national improvements in both patient care quality and EIP service delivery. The postholder will generate actionable insights into the EPICare registry using epidemiological causal inference methods under the supervision of Prof. James Kirkbride and in collaboration with EPICare stakeholders. They will contribute to dissemination efforts including through publishing in peer-reviewed journals, delivering presentations and engaging with the diverse range of EPICare team members (academics, clinicians, lived experience advisory groups members, technical experts). The UCL Division of Psychiatry, located within the Faculty of Brain Sciences, is a world-renowned centre of excellence in mental health research. Our mission is to improve mental health through cutting-edge research and education. By leveraging insights from basic science, we address clinical problems with the aim of improving patient outcomes and public health. Moreover, we take pride in delivering innovative teaching that is directly relevant to clinical practice and informed by our research. Join our team and help us drive forward the field of mental health research and education. You will join a multidisciplinary team of research academics, epidemiologists, statisticians, health economists, clinicians, implementation scientists and people with lived experience, working collaboratively to generate evidence that improves outcomes for people experiencing first episode psychosis. This is an excellent opportunity to contribute to research with direct clinical, service and policy impact while developing your own independent research profile within a supportive academic environment. About the role: Duties and responsibilities The postholder will work under the supervision of Professor James Kirkbride and in collaboration with investigators across the EPICare programme to apply epidemiological causal inference methods and modelling techniques (e.g. inverse probability weighting, growth mixture modelling, group-based trajectory modelling) to longitudinal health registry data. The Research Fellow will lead the design and development of statistical analysis protocols and the clinical effectiveness analyses of the registry data to address key questions around the EIP treatments and interventions and to assess external validity of the registry. They will also collaborate with EPICare team members to support cost-effectiveness assessments of the EPICare registry, and with colleagues working on other programme work packages to support work on the registry’s design and implementation. Other duties for the postholder include disseminating findings through high-quality scientific manuscripts, technical reports and presentations to academic, clinical and policy audiences; supporting junior EPICare team members; and contributing to the overall EPICare programme through effective communication and demonstrating high ethical and research standards. Appointment details: The post is graded at Grade 7, the annual salary for which ranges from £46,261 to £54,019 (including London Allowance) The appointment is available from 1 November 2026 and is funded for up to 30 months subject to satisfactory completion of a 9-month probation period Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be at Grade 6b (salary £39,807 to £42,891 per annum) with payment at Grade 7 being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD Thesis About you: You will be an enthusiastic and collaborative researcher with a strong quantitative background in epidemiology, biostatistics or a very similar discipline, with an outstanding early career track record in your field. You will have a passion for improving mental health through high-quality epidemiological research. You will have experience working with large observational datasets and applying advanced statistical methods to answer clinically relevant research questions. You should have excellent analytical and problem-solving skills, experience writing scientific publications, and the ability to communicate complex findings clearly to academic and non-academic audiences. You will be comfortable working both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary research team, contributing to the delivery of an ambitious national research programme. The ideal candidate will have a high level of experience and knowledge in causal inference, longitudinal data analysis, mental health epidemiology or health services research, alongside a commitment to producing research that improves clinical practice and reduces inequalities in care for people with psychosis. Application process: A full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page You should include the following documents in your application: (1) Maximum of 2-page CV, (2) Maximum of 2-page personal statement You should use the personal statement section to explain how you meet each essential and desirable criteria outlined in the person specification Please do not upload any additional documents (transcripts, your publications, or other reports). Shortlisted applicants will be required to undertake tasks at interview that test their original thinking, knowledge, expertise and data management Interviews will be held in mid-October (exact date TBC, online via Microsoft Teams. Contact details: If you have any queries about the role, please contact Proffesor James Kirkbride at j.kirkbride@ucl.ac.uk If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact dop.hr@ucl.ac.uk What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance The full range of staff benefits can be found here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/pay-benefits/staff-benefits Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. The Division of Psychiatry prides itself for operating in an all-inclusive environment. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and there is a commitment to advancing the careers of everyone, regardless of gender or role. We aim to provide a family friendly environment where both women and men feel able to take the time they need for family. The Athena SWAN Charter recognises commitment to advancing women's careers in science, technology, engineering, maths and medicine (STEMM) employment in academia and the Division is delighted to have an Athena Swan Silver Award since 2022. All staff are invited to contribute to EDI initiatives within the Division to contribute to improving working conditions and opportunities for all. Mentoring is a crucial part of supporting career progression and mentoring schemes are available for staff in the Division. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/psychiatry/about/equality-diversity-and-inclusion https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49132": {
      "title": "Project Manager (Maternity Leave Cover)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11317 About us: The UCL Institute for Global Health (IGH) is at the heart of UCL’s Grand Challenge of Global Health. The Institute’s vision is for a world where international policy on global health is informed by world-class research. IGH aims to support the development of robust solutions to aspects of the world’s major global health challenges through scholarly outputs, education, public engagement, translational research, and ultimately by influencing public policy and professional practice. The Chellaram NOURISH project (Nutritional Optimisation of Underutilised and staple crops through Resilient Integrated Systems for Health) is a five-year, multi-million-pound programme funded by the Chellaram Foundation. Led by UCL in partnership with the Indian Institute of Technology Delhi, the University of California, Davis, and partners in India, the project follows a ‘lab-to-community’ pathway: discovery science identifies and breeds more nutritious crop varieties (Workpackages 1–3), which are then translated into low-cost, community-designed growing systems (Workpackage 4) and rigorously evaluated for their impact on nutrition, health, livelihoods and value for money (Workpackage 5). We are looking for an experienced Project Manager to join our team. About the role: This role sits within Workpackages 4 (Engineering & Co-design) and 5 (Impact, Process & Economics). This post provides Maternity Leave cover for the substantive postholder, maintaining continuity of operational and financial management across Workpackages 4 and 5 during their leave. The Project Manager will lead the day-to-day operational and financial management of Workpackages 4 and 5 of the Chellaram NOURISH project, turning community-designed, low-cost growing systems into a rigorously evaluated model for improving nutrition, health and livelihoods in urban India. Working closely with Professor Jolene Skordis and the wider project leadership, the postholder will keep delivery on track against agreed workplans and milestones, manage relevant budgets, and identify, contract and manage relationships with the project's key India-based partners, including the local implementation and evaluation partners. The postholder will also provide lighter-touch project support across the Chellaram NOURISH project's other workpackages as required. This is an excellent opportunity for an experienced Project Manager with an interest in global health, nutrition or international development to play a central role in a high-profile, multi-country research and community programme. The role includes periodic travel to India to support partner oversight, community engagement and fieldwork. This is a part-time, Maternity Cover role (i.e. 0.5 FTE, 18.25 hours per week) and it is available until 30 th June 2027. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Salary range: £44,861 - £55,099 per annum (including London Allowance), depending on experience, pro-rata for part-time staff. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Prof Jolene Skordis on j.skordis@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the IGH HR Team on IGH.HR@ucl.ac.uk . About you: It is essential that the successful candidate is educated to degree level or has equivalent relevant experience. They will also have: Substantial experience of project or programme management, ideally within an international research, development or scientific programme Experience of financial management of project/research budgets, including monitoring expenditure and managing subcontracts Experience of identifying, contracting and managing relationships with international partners and stakeholders, including in low- and middle-income countries Strong organisational skills, with the ability to manage multiple, competing priorities and deadlines Strong financial and numerical skills, with meticulous attention to detail Willingness and ability to travel to India periodically for project meetings, partner oversight and community engagement What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49156": {
      "title": "Research Assistant - Internal Applicants Only",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07938 About us: University College London is a world-leading university. The UCL Department of Security and Crime Science undertakes internationally recognised multi-disciplinary research and is dedicated to equipping current and future professionals working in the crime and security field with the skills to meet the challenges of the 21st century. We are committed to bringing disciplines together and having real world impact. About the role: This is an opportunity to join a UCL research team working on a project examining how existing interventions could help prevent and respond to Sadistic Online Exploitation Networks (SOENs), an emerging form of technology-facilitated violence against women and girls. The research brings together evidence from fields including violence against women and girls, child exploitation, online harms, cyber offending, safeguarding, youth violence and violent extremism. As a Research Assistant, you will contribute to the development of a systematic Evidence and Gap Map identifying interventions relevant to the harms, drivers and mechanisms associated with SOENs. You will undertake key elements of the evidence synthesis, including literature searching, screening, data extraction and coding, quality appraisal, and contribute to the development of the Evidence and Gap Map and associated research outputs. You will work as part of a multidisciplinary research team within the Department of Security and Crime Science. The findings will be used to identify gaps in current evidence and promising opportunities for future intervention development, adaptation and evaluation. Internally SCS applicant only x 4 the role is hybrid, with the option to work fully remotely. About you: You will have completed a research-based Master’s degree in a relevant discipline or be currently studying towards a PhD in a relevant discipline. You will have experience of systematic or structured evidence review methods and be confident working with academic research literature. You will have strong analytical and organisational skills, excellent attention to detail, and the ability to work accurately and consistently with research protocols. You should also be able to work effectively as part of a collaborative research team and deliver high-quality work to agreed deadlines. Please see the job description and person specification for full details. What we offer: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards- and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Athena Swan Status Statements • Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion her: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49250": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11414 About us: The UK Dementia Research Institute (UK DRI) is a globally leading multidisciplinary research institute investigating the spectrum of neurodegenerative disorders causing dementia, driving a step change in our understanding of neurodegeneration, and accelerating the discovery, development and delivery of interventions that will help diagnose, treat, and ultimately prevent dementia. The UK DRI at UCL is embedded within the UCL Queen Square Institute of Neurology, an autonomous academic research department and a leading centre for neuroscience research. About the role: We are seeking a Research Fellow to investigate mechanisms of proteostasis collapse in ageing and neurodegenerative disorders within the Bourdenx Lab. Our research integrates systems biology with spatial and single cell omics and computational approaches to understand why specific neuronal populations selectively degenerate in dementia. You will specifically focus on regulatory mechanisms of diverse forms of autophagy in neuronal systems. You will use molecular and cell biology techniques, including the generation, maintenance and characterisation of novel iPS cells, their differentiation into neurons and mechanistic studies. The post is available from 01 November 2026 and is funded by the Medical Research Council to 31 October 2027 in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). For informal enquiries about the role please contact Dr Mathieu Bourdenx (email: m.bourdenx@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You will have a PhD in molecular or cellular biology or a related discipline and a strong foundation in cell-biology. Experience with iPSC and in genome-editing techniques, with high-content microscopy and image acquisition/processing and in advanced scientific programming for bioinformatics (Python, R) and programming good practises (version control, etc) is essential. Expertise in developing and applying agentic AI systems for biological knowledge extraction and regulatory networks, knowledge of neuroscience, and an understanding of the mission of the UKDRI, an interest in its research work and a commitment to supporting high-quality research is also a requirement. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. What we offer: Appointment as Research Fellow is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be as Research Assistant (salary £40,603 - £43,749 per annum) with appointment as Research Fellow being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits; visit This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Gold award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49101": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11377 About us: Our mission is to maximise and advocate for the holistic health of all children, young people and the adults they will become, through world-class research, education and public engagement. The UCL GOS ICH, together with its clinical partner Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children, forms the largest concentration of children’s health research outside North America. The 2024-29 GOS ICH strategy focuses on its five scientific programmes. GOS ICH’s activities include active engagement with children and families, to ensure that our work is relevant and appropriate to their needs. GOS ICH generates the funding for our research by setting out our proposals in high quality applications to public, charitable and industrial funding bodies and disseminates the results of our research by publication in the medical and scientific literature, to clinicians, policy makers and the wider public. The Institute offers world-class education and training across a wide range of teaching and life learning programmes which address the needs of students and professional groups who are interested in and undertaking work relevant to child health. GOS ICH holds an Athena SWAN Charter Gold Award. Please only attach relevant documents to your application (qualifications, cover letters, supporting statements) and avoid attaching large files e.g. research papers, thesis, publications etc. About the role: The successful candidate will join the ECHILD team within the Child Health Informatics Group (CHIG) at the UCL Great Ormond Street Institute of Child Health, contributing to a programme of research using linked population-level health, education and social care data. ECHILD comprises records for approximately 20 million children and young people in England, including linked mother-child data, providing a unique resource for research into child health, development, healthcare utilisation, health inequalities and life-course outcomes. The post is strategically aligned with HDR UK’s Driver Programmes in Social and Environmental Determinants of Health and Molecules to Health Records. Working closely with multidisciplinary colleagues across UCL and partner organisations, the postholder will contribute to the design, conduct and dissemination of high-quality research using large-scale administrative datasets and electronic health records. Research activities will span areas including child health, population health, developmental trajectories, health services research, population genomics and precision medicine. This role offers an excellent opportunity to contribute to methodologically rigorous and policy-relevant research within a highly collaborative environment. The successful candidate will apply and develop quantitative methods, contribute to scientific publications and research funding applications, and support the wider development of child health data science and population health research across UCL, ECHILD and HDR UK. Experience with one or more of the administrative databases within ECHILD and/or UK primary care data would be an advantage. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 40% (two days a week) on site. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. This post is funded for 18 months in the first instance. Key requirements: Experience/qualifications in the following: • PhD in epidemiology, biostatistics, health data science, public health, population health, or equivalent experience • Experience of conducting quantitative research using large-scale healthcare, administrative and/or electronic health record datasets, including the management and analysis of complex linked data • Strong understanding of epidemiological study design and statistical methods for longitudinal population health research • Knowledge of data governance, confidentiality and information security requirements for sensitive data Required skills: • Excellent statistical programming skills (e.g. R, Python or Stata) • Ability to contribute to the development of research questions and deliver high-quality research collaboratively within multidisciplinary teams • Ability to apply advanced analytical approaches, such as causal inference methods • Ability to work independently and manage competing priorities • Ability to conduct high-quality research and produce publications About you: You will hold a PhD in epidemiology, biostatistics, health data science, public health, population health, or a related discipline (or have equivalent experience), with a strong track record of conducting quantitative research using large-scale healthcare, administrative and/or electronic health record data. You will have experience managing and analysing complex longitudinal datasets and a sound understanding of epidemiological study design and advanced statistical methods. You will be proficient in statistical programming using tools such as R, Python or Stata, and committed to high standards of reproducible and open research. We are seeking a collaborative and intellectually ambitious researcher with an interest in using population-scale data to address important questions in child health, population health and precision medicine. You will be able to contribute to the development of research questions, apply advanced analytical approaches, and communicate findings through high-quality publications and presentations. Experience of working with linked health and administrative datasets is essential. An interest in integrating genomic and molecular data with routinely collected healthcare and administrative records to advance precision medicine and precision public health would be particularly welcomed. Above all, you will be a highly motivated and organised individual who can work effectively both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team, while embodying UCL's Ways of Working and commitment to sustainability. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49135": {
      "title": "Research Fellow (Maini Lab)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11383 About us: Applications are invited for a Postdoctoral Research Fellow in the Institute of Infection, Immunity and Transplantation, UCL funded by a Wellcome Discovery Team Award to Prof.s McKeating, Issa, Quaglia and Maini. Work within the Maini lab aims to translate advances in the understanding of pathogens, cancer and the immune system into improved diagnosis and therapy. The lab is housed in the newly built Pears Building in Hampstead. Its close links to the adjacent Royal Free London hospital provide an excellent interface between science and medicine. Mala strengthens this translational focus by her own clinical links and those of her clinical colleagues. The Institute provides an effective infrastructure for immunology/virology research and a lively and sociable environment, always striving for excellence. There is an established track record for post-docs gaining personal fellowships, with Mala having successfully supported seven of these just in the last couple of years; independently minded and talented investigators will be encouraged and supported in seeking such fellowship support. About the role: The successful candidate will join a dynamic group of basic and clinical scientists all working on integrated aspects of the immunopathogenesis and immunotherapy of hepatitis B, liver cancer and SARS-CoV-2, with a number of leading national and international collaborators. The lab’s central research goal is to re-direct the immune response to control hepatitis B virus (HBV) and resultant hepatocellular carcinoma by reconstituting successful T and B cell responses, working with patient samples, murine and in vitro 2D/3D models. Our careful delineation of the unique immune cellular interactions and microenviromental niche within the liver environment is informing the development of tailored therapeutic approaches. This post-holder will work on a cutting-edge programme of work funded by a Wellcome Team Discovery award, collaborating with other group members at UCL and the University of Oxford. This post will be predominantly focused on a detailed in situ examination of viral/host interactions in human HBV-infected liver using cutting-edge multiparameter transciptome and proteome approaches. The project is providing exciting edge new insights into the topology of key immune effectors and suppressors in relation to HBV-infected hepatocytes and zonal hypoxia.The new post-holder will build on spatial transcriptomic data generated and analysed by the team of post-docs based in the McKeating lab in Oxford to complement this with targeted multiplexed cyclic immunoflourecence microscopy liver staining together with flow cytometric phenotyping and functional experiments. This is a fixed term position available for 1 year in the first instance. About you: The post holder will be an enthusiastic postdoctoral researcher, expected to perform high quality research under the supervision of the group leader, Mala Maini, working closely with her senior post-docs, PhD students and technicians in the lab. They will also work with co-investigator Prof Quaglia (liver histopathologist at the RFL) and collaboratively with co-investigators Prof McKeating and Prof Issa and their post-docs in the University of Oxford. We are seeking highly motivated individuals with the ability to generate their own research strategies and pursue them with determination, flexibility and creativity. They should have experience in human cellular immunology, ideally in viral immunity, with skills in tissue immunostaining and large data analysis. Please see the attached job description and person specification for full details. Please also read the attached Candidate Guidance document. If you have any queries about the role or application process, technical issues, or need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, please contact the staffing team at hr.ii@ucl.ac.uk. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49219": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in 6G Wireless Communications Coding and Standardisations (EPSRC)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07946 About us: The UCL Electronic and Electrical Engineering Department invites applications for one (1) postdoctoral research position in 6G wireless communications technologies. The successful candidate will work on the EPSRC project, Fluid antenna system synergises reconfigurable intelligent surface for 6G . This project builds upon the pioneering effort of the investigators on fluid antenna systems - an entirely new antenna concept that permits extraordinary reconfigurability that can be translated into enormous capacity and reliability gains for mobile communication systems and aims to tackle the fundamental challenges in terms of design and optimisation and performance analysis for unleashing the great potential of fluid antennas for 6G mobile communications. About the role: We are looking for a talented postdoctoral researcher to join our team and help us fulfil the project’s goals, producing quality research. The main purpose of the role is undertaking research, design and development in the design of 6G fluid antenna communication systems. This will involve new fundamental transceiver design and analysis by developing optimisation algorithms in the context of fluid antenna communications systems. The researcher will participate in the design, performance optimisation, evaluation, and validation of wireless communications systems using fluid antenna technologies as described above and according to the work packages of the associated EPSRC project. The post is available for 12 months in the first instance. Further funding to support the post may be available. About you: Applicants should have a PhD degree (or about the submit) in a relevant subject area, or similar experience demonstrated via publications in international journals / conferences. The researcher should have strong background in optimisation for Fluid Antenna Systems (FAS). Experience in physical layer security and covert communications is also essential. A strong background in wireless communication system and optimisation techniques is also important. Knowledge in integrating FAS with other promising 6G technologies, as well as in evaluating and validating hardware measurement data in wireless channels and systems is also desirable. If the successful candidate has not yet been awarded their PhD, appointment will be made as a Research Assistant (Grade 6B). Payment at Grade 7 will be backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis including corrections, once the PhD has been awarded. Application details: To apply for the role, click the 'Apply Now' button at the bottom or top of the page Applications close on 29th October at 23:59 A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page Informal enquiries regarding this post can be addressed to Prof Kai-Kit Wong kai-kit.wong@ucl.ac.uk . For questions regarding the application process please contact Rebecca Thomas at becca.thomas@ucl.ac.uk . What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49196": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Building Physics",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07955 About us: Our aim is to help to build a globally sustainable energy system, by bringing to bear multiple disciplinary perspectives to observe, analyse, model and interpret energy use and energy systems. The Smart Energy Research Group (SERG) is a thriving research group that primarily conducts data-driven research relating to energy and residential buildings. We have a range of UK and international research projects and evaluation studies funded by research councils, government and industry. About the role: We are recruiting an EDOL Research Fellow to undertake energy-demand research using data from the Energy Demand Observatory and Laboratory (EDOL), with a particular focus on residential energy use and the thermal performance of homes. As a core member of the EDOL team, the postholder will also help design, develop and deliver research-ready data resources, including robust processes for data quality, linkage, analysis and management. The successful candidate will work collaboratively across the EDOL research programme with colleagues at UCL and project partners at the Universities of Oxford and Exeter, and will engage with stakeholders including DESNZ, other government departments, industry and academic organisations. They will conduct and publish research, present findings, contribute to policy debate and support the development of future research activity. This post is available from 1 December 2026 and is funded until 31 December 2027 in the first instance; further funding to support the post may become available. UCL welcomes applications from international applicants and has licence to sponsor individuals who require a visa. This is dependent on the post and candidate meeting eligibility requirements for visa sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. About you: The successful applicant will have a PhD in a relevant field, or be close to completion, and demonstrable expertise in using smart meter data, with and without supplementary data such as internal temperatures, to characterise the thermal performance of homes. They will have strong quantitative data-analysis skills, including programming in Python, and will have completed Safe Researcher Training to become an Accredited Researcher under standard UK data protocols. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit Work at UCL | Work at UCL - UCL – University College London to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49182": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Epidemiology & Statistics",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11379 About us: The Research Department of Behavioural Science and Health is dedicated to research on how human behaviour impacts health and health outcomes. Our research and teaching reflect our interdisciplinary approach to health and wellbeing, focusing on a broad range of public health issues, such as alcohol and tobacco use, heart disease, cancer, and obesity. Further information can be found at: www.ucl.ac.uk/epidemiology-health-care/research/behavioural-science-health The position will involve working in Professor Daisy Fancourt’s Social Biobehavioural Research Group, whose research focuses on how social connections and behaviours impact people’s health. By conducting cutting edge interdisciplinary research, UCL-SBB aims to determine how our health is influenced by both social ‘assets’ and ‘deficits’. These include social relationships, arts, culture and leisure (assets), as well as loneliness, isolation, and social restrictions (deficits). About the role: The postholder will work across three projects, within which the focus of the work can be guided by the postholder’s research interests. The first, a £1 million NIHR Advanced Fellowship (CLASP), is exploring whether community leisure activities can reduce youth anxiety and depression using epidemiological methods. The second project involves working with the EpiArts Lab, a partnership with the University of Florida, that uses epidemiological approaches with US cohort study data to explore whether arts engagement has long-term benefits for health. The third project explores the impact of Students' Union UCL activities and services on student wellbeing, belonging, skills, enjoyment, and other outcomes. Application Details: This is a fixed term contract with external funding until 31 May 2028 in the first instance. Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent on award and confirmation of a PhD (or equivalent). If this is not the case, initial appointment will be at Research Assistant Grade 6B, point 26 (£39,807 - £42,891 per annum) with payment at Grade 7, point 32 (£46,261 - £54,019 per annum) being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. Salaries are inclusive of London Allowance. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. Application Process: A full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Please use the personal statement section to explain how you meet each of the essential and desirable criteria outlined in the person specification Contact Details: If you have any queries regarding the vacancy or the application process, please contact Dr Jess Bone ( bone@ucl.ac.uk ). If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact Maria Kristensen ( kristensen@ucl.ac.uk ). About you: The candidate should hold a PhD in statistics, medical statistics, epidemiology, quantitative social science, or other relevant discipline, and be a highly motivated individual with experience in analysing survey and cohort data using cross-sectional and longitudinal approaches, as well as causal inference methods. The candidate should also have proven data management experience, excellent oral and written communication skills, and a strong ability to work to deadlines. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan and expenses Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver Award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49187": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in lung function mapping with advanced MRI methods",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07950 About us: The UCL Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering hosts world-leading research groups across multiple sites, spanning physics, engineering, medicine, physiology, computer science, and mathematics. Our diverse staff and students create a dynamic multidisciplinary environment for research and learning, complemented by a range of undergraduate and graduate degree programmes. This project aims to develop next-generation functional lung MRI methods using proton imaging, directly applied to patients with chronic lung disease. You will receive hands-on training in MRI physics, respiratory physiology, or advanced computational analysis, while working in close collaboration with clinicians, physicists, and data scientists. This is a unique chance to work at the interface of physics and medicine , and to help translate cutting-edge science into tools that could transform NHS care. About the role: In this role, you will: Design and optimise advanced MRI protocols for lung imaging. Build data analysis pipelines to deliver quantitative imaging biomarkers. Collaborate with leading experts across disciplines. Contribute to taking innovative imaging methods from the lab into the clinic. Contract: up to 2 years fixed term For informal inquiries, please contact Dr Mina Kim ( mina.kim@ucl.ac.uk) About you: What you bring: A PhD (in MRI Physics, Biomedical Engineering, Medical Physics, or a related field. Prior experience in MRI protocol development or image analysis. Strong quantitative and programming skills. Curiosity, ambition, and a genuine interest in pulmonary imaging. Excellent communication skills, both in writing and presenting. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49190": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Medical Image Analysis & Machine Learning",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07951 About us: UCL’s Department of Computer Science (CS) is a top-ranked Computer Science Department in the UK. In the 2021 Research Excellence Framework (REF) evaluation, UCL Computer Science was ranked second in the UK for research power and first in England. The UCL Hawkes Institute is a multi-disciplinary research grouping at the intersection of UCL’s engineering and health activities that focuses on advancing healthcare technology. The institute capitalises on UCL’s unique combination of research excellence in computational, engineering and medical sciences. About the role: This post will develop and evaluate novel artificial intelligence algorithms that allow the use of MRI data that are saved in the standard way to provide accurate and precise quantification of the distribution of iron load in the brain. Depending on the start date, this role is funded for 4 months. The salary range is £46,261 - £48,679 About you: The successful candidate is expected to have a good PhD in Imaging Science, with significance experience in developing and evaluating advanced artificial intelligence algorithms for quantitative susceptibility MRI (QSM). What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our long-term commitment and 'beacon' status in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49143": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Microscopy of Pathogenic Bacteria",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07962 About us: In our atomic force microscopy (AFM) laboratory, headed by Prof. Bart Hoogenboom, we have pioneered AFM methods and their application, from atomic-resolution imaging in liquid, via double-helix resolution imaging of DNA and high-speed imaging of protein assembly on membranes, to more recently some of the highest resolution images ever of living bacteria. Based on this microscopy expertise, we have initiated various programmes aiming to understand fundamental barriers that protect bacteria against antibiotics. We have a highly diverse group where lab members’ various backgrounds and expertise complement each other to together do great science on topics of biomedical importance. This is further enhanced by tight, cross-disciplinary collaborations with multiple other labs with complementary expertise. The London Centre for Nanotechnology (LCN) is an interdisciplinary enterprise between University College London, King’s College London and Imperial College London. In bringing together world-class infrastructure and leading nanotechnology research activities, the Centre aims to attain the critical mass to compete with the best facilities abroad. The LCN has strong relationships with the broader nanotechnology and commercial communities. As the world’s only such facility to be located in the heart of a metropolis, the LCN has superb access to corporate, investment and industrial partners. It is at the forefront of training in nanotechnology and has a strong media presence aimed at educating the public and bringing transparency to this emerging science. About the role: The postholder will develop and apply atomic force microscopy (AFM), in conjunction with other methods, to identify the architecture of the outer membrane of pathogenic bacteria, to define the principles of its assembly and growth, and to discover how antibiotics (can) overcome the outer membrane to kill the bacteria. This will build on recent advances in the lab in discovering bacterial cell envelope architectures and their disruption by antibiotics, e.g., https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2112237118 and https://doi.org/10.1038/s41564-025-02133-1 . Embedded in a larger, Wellcome-Trust funded research programme with the University of Oxford, this role represents an excellent opportunity for the postholder to develop research at the interface of biophysics, biochemistry and microbiology, in an environment where own initiative is strongly encouraged. The start date can be flexible, up to 1 st October 2027. At present, the end date is set as 31 st March 2029, with the possibility of an extension subject to funding. About you: The successful candidates will have a PhD in a relevant area of Life or Physical Sciences (or at least have submitted their thesis by the start date of the post) with an outstanding track record of research and publications. Experience in advanced microscopy is essential, and specific experience in in-liquid atomic force microscopy and/or microbiology would be most beneficial. They must have demonstrated ability for creative, original independent research. The person must be adept at multi-tasking and organizing their work to meet deadlines. They must have a proven track record of working in multi-disciplinary teams What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our long-term commitment and 'beacon' status in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49263": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Urban AI",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07935 About us: The Bartlett Centre for Advanced Spatial Analysis (CASA) is globally recognised as a leading academic department, researching and teaching the science of cities. A truly interdisciplinary centre, with staff hailing from backgrounds in subjects as diverse as Geography, Transport Studies, Mathematics, Physics, Computer Science, Statistics, Planning, Architecture, the Humanities and the Social Sciences, for more than 20 years we have been combining theory with novel data, sensors, computational models, analysis and cutting edge visualisation to generate new knowledge and insights addressing problems with a spatial dimension and an urban and regional focus. Our work in inherently applied and looks to influence urban planning, policy and design in both the public and private spheres. Educating only postgraduates, our degree programmes have gained a reputation for excellence and inclusivity, with student cohorts also reflecting a hugely diverse range of academic backgrounds. Further information can be found on our website at https://www.ucl.ac.uk/bartlett/casa/ About the role: The Bartlett Centre for Advanced Spatial Analysis (CASA) is recruiting a Research Fellow in Urban AI to contribute to an Innovate UK-funded collaborative project – “Increasing EV charging capacity on the strategic road network”. The project aims to develop an intelligent buffered ultra-rapid EV charging solution for grid-constrained motorway service areas. The role will focus on developing data-driven AI and spatial modelling approaches to understand and predict EV charging demand, particularly to optimise charging and energy management, and to assess the potential transferability of the solution across England’s Strategic Road Network. Working within the UCL research team and in close collaboration with industry partners, the research fellow will integrate large-scale EV charging, energy, transport, grid, and geospatial data to develop and validate models at both the charging-session and site/network scales. The research will contribute to a proof-of-concept AI-driven energy management solution and provide evidence to support the future planning, operation and deployment of buffered ultra-rapid charging infrastructure. The Research Fellow in Urban AI will: undertake research and analysis using EV charging, energy, transport and geospatial data to support the objectives of the project; undertake data acquisition, processing and integration, working with EV charging, energy, mobility and geospatial datasets from multiple sources; develop and test deep reinforcement learning or alternative AI-based approaches for intelligent charging and energy management; explore innovative approaches to intelligent charging and energy management that account for user behaviour and activity patterns. The post is available from 1st January 2027 and is funded until 31st December 2027 in the first instance. Starting salary offered will be £46,261- £51,246 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance, due to limited amount of funding available. Please note: Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon PhD award. If not yet awarded, initial appointment will be at Grade 6B (£39,807 - £42,891 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance), with backdating to Grade 7 upon submission of PhD thesis. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services staff for more information. Find out about the UCL Ways of Working: www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible. For any queries about the role please contact Prof Chen Zhong (c.zhong@ucl.ac.uk). A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. To apply for the vacancy please click on the ‘Apply Now’ button below. About you: The successful candidate will hold a PhD (or close to completion) in ML/DL, GeoAI, urban informatics, Spatial Data Science or a closely related quantitative discipline. They will also have: research experience in spatial modelling, machine learning or deep learning, with applications to mobility, transport, energy or related domains; experience in working with and analysing large and complex spatial datasets; knowledge of, or experience with, emerging data-driven approaches and service innovations in EV charging, including AI-enabled modelling, charging flexibility and intelligent energy-management strategies; strong technical skills in AI/ML modelling (e.g., Python), data driven modelling; ability to acquire, process, integrate and analyse large and heterogeneous datasets from multiple sources, in particular, spatiotemporal data; ability to understand and incorporate relevant energy-system and operational constraints or policy into modelling and analysis; ability to produce well-structured, documented and reproducible research outputs, including code, data-processing workflows and supporting documentation, and to prepare and maintain repositories for sharing through platforms such as GitHub; ability to work independently, identify research opportunities and develop innovative methodological approaches beyond established project specifications; ability to communicate effectively across disciplines and collaborate with researchers and stakeholders from industry. For full list of essential and desirable criteria, please see a job description and person specification at the bottom of this page. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: - 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days); - Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme; - Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE); - Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan; - Immigration loan; - Relocation scheme for certain posts; - On- Site nursery; - Onsite gym; - Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay; - Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service; - Discounted medical insurance. Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy (https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/hr-policies-procedures-and-advice/z-policies/prevention-bullying-harassment-and-sexual-misconduct) and Personal Relationships Policy (https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/hr-policies-procedures-and-advice/z-policies/personal-relationships-policy)"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49138": {
      "title": "Research Fellows (Maini Lab)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11393 About us: Applications are invited for Postdoctoral Research Fellow positions in the Institute of Infection, Immunity and Transplantation, UCL funded by the Maini lab’s Wellcome Discovery Award and CRUK Discovery Award (Co-applicants Meyer and Childs). Work within the Maini lab aims to translate advances in the understanding of pathogens, cancer and the immune system into improved diagnosis and therapy. The lab is based within UCL’s Institute of Infection, Immunity and Transplantation, housed in the newly built Pears Building in Hampstead. Its close links to the adjacent Royal Free London hospital provide an excellent interface between science and medicine. Mala strengthens this translational focus by her own clinical links and those of her clinical colleagues. The Institute provides an effective infrastructure for immunology/virology research and a lively and sociable environment, always striving for excellence. There is an established track record for post-docs gaining personal fellowships, with Mala having successfully supported seven of these just in the last couple of years; independently minded and talented investigators will be encouraged and supported in seeking such fellowship support. About the role: Successful candidates will join a dynamic group of basic and clinical scientists all working on integrated aspects of the immunopathogenesis and immunotherapy of hepatitis B, liver cancer and SARS-CoV-2, with a number of leading national and international collaborators. The lab’s central research goal is to re-direct the immune response to control hepatitis B virus (HBV) and resultant hepatocellular carcinoma by reconstituting successful T and B cell responses, working with patient samples, murine and in vitro 2D/3D models. Our careful delineation of the unique immune cellular interactions and microenviromental niche within the liver environment is informing the development of tailored therapeutic approaches. Our new Wellcome and CRUK Discovery awards build on this to fund exciting new programmes of work. The Wellcome Discovery award funds work to characterise the HBV-specific T and B cell responses in blood and liver associated with viral control or infection resolution and to test preclinical strategies to recapitulate these successful responses with novel vaccine-delivered immunotherapies. The CRUK Discovery award funds a programme of collaborative work with our oncology colleagues Prof Meyer and Dr Childs, led by Maini lab senior research associate Dr Diniz, to profile and track changes in the intratumoral immune niche sustaining or constraining long-term responses to checkpoint inhibitor therapy and incorporate these findings into biomarker and immunotherapeutic vaccine development. About you: Post holders will be enthusiastic postdoctoral researchers, expected to perform high quality research under the supervision of the principal investigator, Prof Mala Maini, working closely with her senior research associate Dr Mariana Diniz, post-docs, PhD students and technicians in the lab. We are seeking highly motivated individuals with the ability to generate their own research strategies and pursue them with determination, flexibility and creativity. They should have experience in human cellular immunology, ideally in viral/tumour adaptive immunity and tissue/liver immunology. One post requires expertise in antigen-specific T cells, vaccinology and in vivo work, one in tissue and/or antigen-specific B cells and one in tumour-associated neutrophils and immunostaining. Please see the attached job description and person specification for full details. Please also read the attached Candidate Guidance document. All applications must include a supporting statement, telling us, using examples, how you meet the essential criteria listed in the job description. Applications without a supporting statement will be rejected. If you have any queries about the role or application process, technical issues, or need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, please contact the staffing team at hr.ii@ucl.ac.uk. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=48984": {
      "title": "Section Manager",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07891 About us: UCL is a world-leading, multidisciplinary university with a vibrant community of over 13,000 staff and 38,000 students from more than 150 countries. We offer degree programmes across a broad range of disciplines including Arts and Humanities, the Built Environment, Brain Sciences, Engineering, Education, Law, Life Sciences, Mathematical & Physical Sciences, Medical Sciences, Population Health Sciences, and Social and Historical Sciences. The UCL Department of Computer Science is recognised as one of the UK’s top research and teaching departments, with a strong global reputation. We are currently experiencing a period of significant growth and offer a range of undergraduate and postgraduate programmes alongside an extensive research portfolio. Our research has lasting impact across society, and we collaborate widely across UCL and with other institutions through large-scale, interdisciplinary initiatives. About the role: 1 Year Secondment We are seeking to appoint a Section Manager for a 1 year Secondment to support the development and delivery of operational infrastructure within the Machine Learning Departmental Section. The SectionManager (SM) will play a key role in ensuring the smooth running of teaching, research, and wider departmental activities by taking ownership of the day-to-day and strategic operations within their Section. You will lead on the management and development of Section-wide resources, including staff, finances, health and safety, IT, estates, communications, and general administration. The role is responsible for maintaining effective systems and processes and ensuring the consistent application of UCL, Faculty, and Departmental policies. This post will be funded until 31st October 2027 in the first instance About you: You will be a highly organised, proactive, and solutions-focused professional with experience of operational and/or project management in a complex environment. You will have excellent leadership, communication, and stakeholder management skills, and a strong track record of delivering high-quality services. You will be confident managing multiple priorities and comfortable navigating organisational structures and change. Experience in higher education or a research-intensive environment is advantageous, but not essential. You will thrive in a collaborative setting, demonstrate a commitment to continuous improvement, and be motivated by supporting academic excellence. Crucially, you will bring both strategic insight and practical capability to ensure your Section operates efficiently and effectively. To apply, please submit your CV together with a written statement demonstrating how you meet the essential criteria for the role. You can find more information about our research and teaching at our website. UCL Computer Science | Faculty of Engineering What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: We are really proud to be the first Computer Science Department in the UK that holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our long-term commitment and 'beacon' status in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our achievements and our commitment to EDI here: Equity, Diversity and Inclusion | Faculty of Engineering As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : Equality, Diversity & Inclusion: Think differently, do differently. | Office of the President and Provost (Equality, Diversity & Inclusion) - UCL – University College London"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49149": {
      "title": "Senior Administrator - Internal Candidates only",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-President (Advancement) (C16) Reference: C16-00323 About us: UCL Advancement is a dynamic and collaborative team, responsible for engaging our alumni and supporter community, and developing philanthropic partnerships to support UCL’s objectives. UCL has launched Here, it will happen , our biggest philanthropic and engagement campaign yet, in our bicentennial year, that sets out our ambitions to solve global health, societal, climate and education challenges in UCL’s next century. Here, we have the determination to double down on our discoveries, translate our innovations into impact and inspire the citizens of the future. The campaign will unite some of the world’s best researchers, with a committed donor and alumni community, to fuel the next 200 years of societal impact at UCL. This role sits within the Executive Support function of the Advancement Planning & Performance (APP) team in UCL Advancement. The APP team oversees the implementation of Advancement's strategic plan, including the launch and delivery of the philanthropic campaign, through coordinated and high-level strategic and operational support in line with the department's priorities and institutional relationships. The Executive Support team supports the Vice-President (Advancement) (VPA) across the full range of their activities as a senior leader of the university. The team consists of a Senior Executive Officer (SEO), Senior Administrator and Diary Manager. Led by the SEO, the team provides strategic, logistical and administrative support to the VPA to ensure they can lead on all areas of Advancement activity. The team works closely with other parts of UCL, including the President & Provost’s (P&P) Office and Executive Assistants across the university, to ensure effective briefing and support in many aspects of the VPA role. The Executive Support remit is fast-moving and high-profile, managing both internal and external engagements. The post will suit an experienced administrator and/or project coordinator with a positive and professional attitude, a willingness to help and confidence to manage effective relationships with key stakeholders. The ideal candidate will demonstrate excellent team working and collaboration skills and an ability to remain calm and logical under pressure. The APP team is evolving, along with wider growth across Advancement, so a willingness to come on this journey with us is key. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. Job purpose The Senior Administrator will play an important role in bringing robust support to the Executive office of the Vice-President (Advancement) and Executive team. Support the delivery of Advancement’s strategic objectives by effectively coordinating meetings, briefings and key activities ensuring leaders are equipped to make informed and timely decisions. Anticipate requirements, exercise sound judgment and contribute to continuous improvement of processes and ways of working, enhancing the effectiveness of administrative support across Advancement. Due to the nature and scale of our office and the role, direction of work may come from outside of the APP team, such as the Associate Director of Operations. About the role: Duties & Responsibilities Briefings & Document Management As a critical priority, ensure all briefings, papers and speaking notes for the VPA are filed in the correct daily folders and work proactively with stakeholders to chase any missing or partial documentation, with a clear understanding of how the VPA prepares for meetings and accesses information so that she can use her time most effectively. Develop and continuously improve processes to ensure that briefings and documents are managed accurately and consistently, using feedback to drive enhancements and increase effectiveness. Executive Operations and Diary Management Under the guidance of the Senior Executive Officer and in close partnership with the Diary Manager, carry out high-quality operations, administrative and project coordination tasks to underpin the activities of the Office of the Vice-President (Advancement). Provide cover for the Diary Manager when they are on leave and manage VPA’s internal and external diary appointments. Stakeholder Engagement & Data Management Develop and maintain a strong understanding of the VPA’s key stakeholder landscape and relationships, including the critical role of the President and Provost’s Office in advancing institutional objectives across the institution. Maintain and update all VPA contact records and interactions within the institutional CRM, maintaining data integrity. Travel & Event Logistics Arrange and book comprehensive travel logistics, including flights, hotels and local transport for VPA conferences and training trips. Coordinate domestic travel arrangements for VPA development trips in close collaboration with the Global Visits Manager. Over time, develop a strong understanding of the VPA’s extensive travel schedule and her travel preferences and requirements, proactively providing informed advice and recommendations to support effective travel planning and decision-making. Research and manage room bookings, catering and logistics for departmental and Executive meetings and away days. Financial & Compliance Administration Process VPA expenses accurately and efficiently, using correct budget codes and investigating any anomalies in a timely manner. Process and manage all administration related to gift and hospitality declarations for VPA, ensuring strict alignment with institutional policy. Office Administration & Process Improvement Carry out other general administrative duties for VPA such as ordering updated business cards and maintaining stock of OVPA-branded stationary for written correspondence. Use appropriate tools, techniques and software to stay on top of priorities and keep track of progress to ensure all requests are managed, with a focus on pre-empting needs so they can be addressed accordingly. Build strong working relationships with peers across the department, with an eye for continuous improvements to administrative systems and processes that help colleagues work as effectively as possible with the VPA and the Executive Support team. Some out-of-hours working may be required to support the VPA while travelling. Time off in lieu (TOIL) will be considered where appropriate. About you: Qualifications, experience and knowledge Experience in a senior administrative or project coordination role, ideally within a complex and fast-paced environment such as higher education, government or large organisations. High degree of proficiency in Microsoft Office products, especially Excel. Experience in administering and optimising overall functionality of office processes and digital platforms and tools to enhance cross-team collaboration and operational efficiency. Experience coordinating high-level meetings, and away days, ensuring all logistical elements are taken care of, from venue arrangements to catering and guest management. Skills and abilities An excellent team player, with a proactive, service-oriented mindset and can-do approach, comfortable working across multiple functions in a matrix structure, and adaptable to diverse ways of working. Able to work independently and take ownership of tasks, with a demonstrated capacity to support high-level, multi-faceted institutional projects, demonstrating an understanding of how administrative work supports strategic objectives. Highly organised with the ability to manage multiple priorities and to balance diverse tasks while maintaining a high level of efficiency. Strong attention to detail, identifying discrepancies and ensuing no details are overlooked A high degree of professionalism and discretion in managing sensitive information, maintaining confidentiality and having the confidence to engage with senior and high-profile stakeholders. Ability to respond to verbal and written queries in a concise and logical way, ensuring critical information is clearly and accurately communicated to support decision-making. Good interpersonal skills to tactfully and assertively communicate with others, and to develop effective, professional working relationships across UCL and externally. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49142": {
      "title": "Senior Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11374 About us: The High-Dimensional Neurology Group, led by Professor Parashkev Nachev within the Department of Translational Neuroscience & Stroke, applies large-scale machine learning to routinely acquired clinical data, particularly brain imaging, to build generative and predictive models of the brain in health and disease and translate them into tools that improve the care of patients with acute neurological disorders such as stroke. The group works closely with clinical services at the National Hospital for Neurology and Neurosurgery and with University College London Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust. It also has access to some of the largest curated clinical neuroimaging datasets in the UK. About the role: We are seeking a Senior Research Fellow to work on an EPSRC-funded project developing a radically new brain-imaging modality using low-frequency ultrasound and full-waveform inversion (FWI), with the aim of generating high-resolution images of the living adult human brain. You will develop machine-learning methods to make FWI faster and more robust, using large-scale clinical imaging data to build generative models of the skull and brain and provide anatomically informed priors for image reconstruction. You will investigate the trade-offs between reconstruction speed and accuracy and evaluate the resulting images against clinically important questions in acute stroke. Working across machine learning, imaging and clinical research, you will play a key role in developing technology with the potential to deliver safe, fast and portable brain imaging for stroke and other acute neurological conditions, including outside hospital settings. The role is available from 01 November 2026 and is funded by the Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council (EPSRC) until 31 October 2027 in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). Informal enquiries regarding the role can be addressed to Professor Parashkev Nachev (email: p.nachev@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You will have a PhD in machine learning, computer science, computational physics, applied mathematics or a closely related discipline, or equivalent relevant research experience, together with a strong track record of research in machine learning for imaging or inverse problems. You will have substantial experience developing and evaluating deep generative models using large-scale scientific or imaging data, as well as experience with physics-driven inverse problems, learned priors or physics-informed methods. Strong Python and deep learning programming skills, including experience with GPU/HPC environments, are essential. You will be able to work independently and take ownership of research within a collaborative, multi-disciplinary project. Excellent communication skills and the ability to work effectively with researchers from different disciplines, including engineers, physicists and clinicians, are also important. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. What we offer: Starting salary offered in the range £56,431 - £64,644 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Gold award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49121": {
      "title": "Senior Reward Analyst",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; HR and Wellbeing; London; £43,981-£54,019​; People and Culture & PS Hub (B30) Reference: B30-00273 About us: Since our founding 200 years ago, UCL has blazed its own trail, fostering a vibrant, diverse community of world-class academics, staff, students, and alumni who aren’t afraid to think big and think differently. Together, we question the status quo, challenge assumptions, and tackle some of the world’s most complex problems head-on. Located in the heart of London, UCL is consistently ranked among the top ten universities in the world. With more than 16,000 staff and 50,000 students from 150 different countries, and an annual turnover of more than £1.2 billion a year, we are London’s leading multi-disciplinary university. UCL’s Reward Policy Team develops and delivers the university’s approach to pay, benefits and recognition. We provide expert advice, policy development and analysis to support transparent, fair and financially sustainable reward decisions. The team is also delivering a multi- year programme of reward transformation, including enhancements to pay progression, family- friendly benefits, recognition of exceptional contribution and career development. Our work plays an important role in helping UCL attract, retain and recognise talented colleagues while enhancing the overall staff experience. About the role: As a Senior Reward Analyst , you will provide specialist analytical and operational support across UCL’s reward and pay- planning activities. You will analyse complex workforce and pay data, undertake salary modelling and costing, and support annual pay reviews, promotions and salary benchmarking. You will also contribute to UCL’s gender, ethnicity, disability and equal pay reporting, maintain reward and grading data, and provide clear insights and advice to stakeholders. Working closely with People & Culture colleagues, Finance, senior leaders and academic departments, you will help ensure reward processes are accurate, consistent and aligned with policy, while contributing to the continuous improvement of UCL’s reward practices. If you have any questions about the role or application process please contact Passant Khalifa via p.khalifa@ucl.ac.uk About you: You will be a highly analytical and numerate professional with experience of working with large and complex datasets and an excellent understanding of job evaluation, grading structures, reward frameworks, gender and equal pay principles . You will have advanced Excel skills, including complex formulas, data manipulation and modelling, and be confident translating detailed analysis into clear insights for different audiences. You will bring strong problem-solving, organisational and communication skills, with the ability to build effective relationships and manage multiple priorities and tight deadlines. You will be comfortable navigating ambiguity, exercising sound judgement and handling confidential and sensitive information with accuracy and discretion. Experience of reward activities such as pay reviews, salary modelling, promotions or grading, and a CIPD or equivalent qualification, would be advantageous. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Find out more at https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49238": {
      "title": "Senior Translational Research Manager - Devices, Diagnostic and Digital",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; £68,284-£78,386​; Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement) (B09) Reference: B09-01433 About us: UCL is one of the UK’s leading universities and a world-class multidisciplinary research and teaching institution. UCL Innovation & Enterprise brings together specialist teams working with researchers, students and external partners to accelerate innovation and turn research into real-world impact. The Translational Research Office (TRO) is an integral part of UCL Innovation & Enterprise and UCL’s biomedical research community. Its expert team of applied scientists and business developers supports researchers to realise their translational ambitions through strategic advice, funding support and connections with industry. The Translational Research Group provides practical, tailored support to researchers navigating the translational pathway, helping to develop robust projects that can attract funding, investment and ultimately support NHS adoption. About the role: We are seeking an experienced Senior Translational Research Manager to manage and develop a portfolio of biomedical device, diagnostic and digital/AI translational projects. Working with both early-career and established academics, the role will provide expert guidance throughout the translational pathway, from developing project plans and identifying appropriate funding opportunities through to supporting competitive funding applications and project managing successful awards. The role will proactively identify research with translational potential and help build a sustainable pipeline of funded projects. This includes advising Principal Investigators, maintaining knowledge of the translational funding landscape, managing complex funded projects and supporting areas such as budgets, contracts, milestones and deliverables. Working across UCL and its partner hospitals, the postholder will build relationships with researchers, Clinical Trials Units, funders, industry and other external partners. They will also contribute to UCL’s wider translational strategy, helping progress innovative research towards clinical application and impact. About you: We are looking for someone with extensive experience of biomedical device, diagnostic, digital and/or AI development and translational science, alongside a strong track record of progressing projects through the translational pathway. Knowledge and experience of the regulatory pathways relevant to these technologies is essential. You’ll bring strong practical project management experience, including managing complex projects, budgets, timescales and deadlines, as well as experience of developing effective partnerships and working with a range of stakeholders. Excellent communication, influencing and interpersonal skills are important, with the ability to work effectively with multidisciplinary teams of clinical researchers, scientists and external partners. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, the ability to manage competing priorities independently and a collaborative approach to delivering complex projects will also be key. Knowledge of major national and international research funders and a recognised project management qualification would be advantageous. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49108": {
      "title": "Solution Architect",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; IT and Digital; London; £54,931-£64,644; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05287 About us: Information Services Division (ISD) is the primary provider of IT services to UCL. We support and enhance learning, teaching, research and administrative processes by providing information- and technology-related services to over 50,000 staff and students of UCL and associated institutions. Our goal is to be the top IT services group in the HE sector and we are growing our team’s capability in AI, experience/UX, agile development, security, cloud, service management and partnering. We are modernising our technology foundations, digitising the processes of the university to transform experience for students and staff, and partnering across the university to drive differentiation in UCL education and research. The Solution Architecture Team is part of a wider team of Architects including Enterprise, Platform, Data, Integration and AI Architecture teams who are responsible for defining and governing architecture, driving solution design, and maintaining the repository to include current and future states. This is a fantastic opportunity to join UCL’s Information Services Division (ISD) as a Solution Architect to help deliver excellence whilst continually evolving and progressing at this globally recognised top 10 university. About the role: We are seeking a Solution Architect with a proven ability to operate at the intersection of strategy, technology, and delivery, and who is motivated by shaping meaningful change within a complex and intellectually vibrant organisation. This is a high-impact role for an experienced architect or similar who wants to influence how UCL designs, builds, and evolves its digital services across programmes, platforms, and product areas. You will lead solution design in close collaboration with stakeholders, fellow architects, delivery teams, and external partners. Translating strategic priorities into pragmatic, resilient solutions, you will help shape architectural standards, patterns, and governance while remaining close to delivery. Acting as a trusted advisor to product owners, you will balance enterprise-level outcomes with hands-on architectural depth to address complex challenges with broad institutional impact. *If we get a high number of applications, we may close the advert early before the published closing date. As a minimum we will keep all adverts open for 2 weeks* About you: What you’ll bring: Proven experience working within large, complex organisations, with the ability to understand strategic drivers and translate them into practical, deliverable solution designs that meet real business and user needs The capability to shape end-to-end solution architectures, balancing strategic intent with delivery constraints to produce resilient, scalable, and maintainable outcomes Experience within multidisciplinary product teams, including product owners, business analysts, technical leads, software engineers, test engineers, user experience and accessibility practitioners, DevOps, security, operations, and external partners The ability to operate at both strategic and detailed levels, providing clear direction while engaging hands-on with design decisions, trade-offs, and implementation approaches A pragmatic mindset, with strong analytical skills and the ability to navigate complexity, ambiguity, and competing priorities in a matrix environment Solid technical breadth across modern technology stacks, including cloud platforms, integration patterns, APIs, identity, and application architectures Experience translating architectural principles and standards into actionable guidance for delivery teams, supporting consistency without creating unnecessary friction Strong communication and influencing skills, with the ability to explain complex technical concepts clearly and build alignment among diverse stakeholders A collaborative approach and interest in contributing to architectural communities of practice, knowledge sharing, and continuous improvement Experience or qualifications in TOGAF and/or SAFe (or equivalent frameworks) are advantageous, but not essential. What we offer: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London's Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world's talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL's workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49178": {
      "title": "Strategic Planning Partner",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Vice-Provost (Strategy) (B24) Reference: B24-00097 About us: UCL is London's Global University, where big ideas become real-world impact. Delivering that across 11 Faculties, several campuses and a large, highly devolved community takes careful, joined-up planning. That's our job. The Office of the Vice-Provost (Strategy) develops UCL's institutional strategy and makes sure our overarching goals are reflected in plans at every level of the university. We're five closely connected teams: Strategic Planning – runs UCL's planning processes, including Faculty and Vice-Provost Office planning, student number planning and tuition fee setting. Academic Strategy Development – shapes UCL's long-term academic strategy, starting with a university-wide conversation on 'Academic Ambition' and linked closely to long-term space planning and Phase 2 of UCL East. Data & Insight – provides the management information and insight that supports better decisions across UCL. Strategy Implementation – tracks how institutional strategy is being delivered. UCL East – runs the operations and governance of our newest campus, provides shared specialist facilities and plans its future. We work closely with colleagues right across UCL, including the Vice-Provost and Vice-President offices, UCL Estates, UCL Finance and the Office of the General Counsel, on everything from the Estates Masterplan to the next phase of UCL East. You'll join the Strategic Planning team, which supports the Provost & President and the University Management Committee. We coordinate academic plans, give advice and constructive challenge to senior leaders, and help make sure local plans fit with UCL's academic, financial, estates and digital strategies, with good data informing every decision. About the role: In an organisation as large and devolved as UCL, good strategic planning is hard, and that's what makes it rewarding. As a Strategic Planning Partner, you'll connect UCL's institutional priorities with the Faculties that deliver them. You'll be part of a team of Partners, each looking after a portfolio of Faculties. You'll get to know your Faculties well: their priorities, their pressures and how they work. As a trusted adviser, you'll offer analysis, insight and, when it's needed, honest challenge, helping Faculties turn their ambitions into plans they can deliver. Each Faculty is different, so you'll balance a consistent, university-wide service with an approach that fits each one. What you'll be doing Partnering with a portfolio of Faculties to deliver UCL's strategic priorities through good planning, problem-solving and stakeholder engagement Building a detailed understanding of each Faculty's priorities, challenges and operating context to inform planning and decision-making Providing strategic analysis, advice and constructive challenge so that Faculty and institutional decisions are well informed Supporting the Integrated Planning Cycle, with a particular focus on your partner Faculties Leading student number planning for your Faculties, including setting student number and offer targets Leading the tuition fee review for your Faculties, with analysis and recommendations that inform strategic and financial decisions Supporting new course initiation and approval with planning insight and stakeholder coordination, so that new courses fit institutional priorities and planning assumptions Providing secretariat support to Faculty Recruitment and Admissions Groups and other sub-groups of the Recruitment and Admissions Committee, coordinating recruitment planning and performance monitoring, and raising key risks and opportunities early Working with the Data & Insight team to identify and deliver the data that supports good planning at Faculty level Improving the processes, systems, data and tools behind strategic planning Working with fellow Partners to keep our planning service consistent, coordinated and high quality across the university You'll also lead on one or more projects or specialist areas within the Strategic Planning team and the wider Office, and help shape how the Office develops. You'll be building an important service at a globally leading university, using modern approaches to analysis, problem-solving, service design and delivery. Like all UCL colleagues, you'll follow and promote the UCL Ways of Working. We'd also welcome your involvement in wider university life, such as volunteering as a fire marshal or first aider, joining a committee, helping at student open days or supporting EDI initiatives. This is a hybrid role. You can normally work remotely for up to 40% of your week if you'd like to, with your time on campus split between Bloomsbury and UCL East. Campus remains the contractual place of work About you: You're a natural planner and problem-solver. You enjoy getting to grips with complex issues and working with others to design practical solutions. You're proactive and spot problems before they grow. You communicate with confidence and build credibility with people at all levels, from academic departments to senior leadership, and you're comfortable offering honest challenge when it matters. You'll also bring an entrepreneurial streak: you're willing to try new ideas and experiment with new products and services that help strategy happen in a complex organisation. Higher education experience is welcome but not required. A genuine interest in universities and the sector is what counts. Essential criteria Educated to degree level, or equivalent experience in report writing, data analysis and presentations Knowledge and experience of effective strategy delivery and performance management, and of converting ambitious goals into practical programmes of action Experience of co-designing solutions to complex delivery issues Experience of analysing data from a range of sources and in a range of formats to identify issues and design solutions Experience of communicating clearly and concisely in a range of formats to different audiences Experience of providing challenge and critique in the delivery of significant projects and priorities Experience of building constructive working relationships with colleagues at all levels within an organisation Knowledge of, or interest in, working in a university setting and/or the higher education sector more broadly Excellent analytical and problem-solving skills Ability to show initiative and be proactive in identifying and dealing with issues as they arise Strong communication skills, including the personal credibility to present information in a variety of formats to different audiences Influencing, negotiation and persuasion skills with senior and less senior staff, and with external contacts, clients and suppliers Strong organisational and project-planning skills Ability to support senior governance forums through effective planning, coordination and administration, including preparing papers, advising chairs on meeting management, and making sure actions are accurately recorded and followed up Clearly and proactively setting out expectations and goals to teams, colleagues and partners, empowering them to work toward solutions (UCL Ways of Working) Proactively influencing people and ideas to reach outcomes that benefit the organisation (UCL Ways of Working) Desirable criteria Specific training in strategy delivery, performance management or programme management Experience of supporting academic departments and/or Faculties with student number planning, tuition fee setting and new course initiation Creativity, innovation and an entrepreneurial mindset Knowledge of, or interest in, working in a public-purpose organisation or sector, such as government or an NGO When you apply, please copy each criterion into your supporting statement and explain beneath it how you meet it, with examples. We'll score your application against each one. What we offer: 41 days of holiday (27 days' annual leave, 8 bank holidays and 6 closure days). Hybrid working, with up to 60% of the week remote. An excellent defined benefit pension scheme. Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, on-site nursery and discounted gym membership. Wellbeing support, learning and development, and staff discounts. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London's Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We're committed to being fair and inclusive and to being a place where everyone belongs, and we particularly encourage applications from people likely to be underrepresented in UCL's workforce."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49333": {
      "title": "Deputy Site Manager, UCL Wickford Store",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Libraries, museums, theatres and open science; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-Provost (Faculties) (B25) Reference: B25-00300 About us: UCL is London’s global university. LCCOS – Library, Culture, Collections and Open Science – provides outstanding staff, services and resources to support the world-class research, education and enterprise that is undertaken in UCL, and connects the world with UCL through innovative programming and engagement activities. LCCOS is acknowledged as an international leader in Open Science and Scholarship. The LCCOS family comprises 4 museums and allied object collections, 14 libraries, the UCL Student Centre, Special Collections facilities, multiple additional learning spaces and a substantial off-site storage facility. We also provide comprehensive services to healthcare staff through our partnerships with 6 NHS Trusts. LCCOS holds the UK Customer Service Excellence (CSE) Award, The National Archives (TNA) Archives Service Accreditation, and Arts Council England Museum Accreditation. The combined staff in UCL LCCOS totals around 450. About the role: UCL Library Services holds printed collections of approximately two million volumes covering a range of disciplines and housed at various sites. Approximately 25% is kept at UCL Library Services’ Store in Wickford, Essex. This material includes important research materials and stock relegated from the sites plus UCL’s paper records. The Store is a large warehouse (28,700 square feet) with modern office space. It is central to the management of important research collections and provides a daily delivery of requested items to all UCL site libraries. It is not open to users. The Wickford Store team is part of the Collection Strategy team and consists of 6.28 FTE staff. It is open Mon – Fri (daytime only). The post holder works closely with the Head of Retrospective Cataloguing who is also based at Wickford 4 days per week, and the Stores Service Manager based in the Science Library. The postholder supervises and manages a team of Store Assistants for the daily running of the stores delivery service to ensure that service delivery targets are met. The postholder deputises as required for the Wickford Site Manager in the overall running of the Wickford Store and assists with all aspects of daily site management including opening and closing, maintenance and Health & Safety. • This is a part-time role (0.4 FTE – Mondays and Tuesdays only), job sharing with another member of staff. • This is a Grade 7 role with the FTE salary of 44,861 (0.4 Pro-rated, 17,944) • The post involves operating machinery and some moving and lifting of heavy books. Full training will be provided. • This is an on-campus role. It is not suitable for remote working. This role does not meet the eligibility requirements for a Skilled Worker Visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require right to work in the UK to carry out this role. About you: Energetic and enthusiastic person with experience of working in a library setting, preferably with some element of service provision. Self-motivated, hardworking and a problem solver, you will thrive in a team-oriented environment and play a key role in leading the small team located at our off-site store in Wickford. We are looking for candidates who have the following skills and experience: • Excellent communication skills are required as you will be working with a wide range of internal customers including Library staff across the service, other UCL staff (especially Estates & Facilities) and external organisations e.g., recruitment agencies, local businesses, and contractors. • Line management or supervisory experience. • Strong organisational skills including the ability to manage and prioritise your workload and meet deadlines. • A commitment to your own and others’ continuing professional development. The advert will close on the published date at 23:59 GMT. Interviews will be held at Wickford Library Store. A job description and person specification can be accessed from the link at the bottom of this page. Further details To apply, please provide: • An up-to-date CV, including as a minimum detail of your current employment, salary and notice period, details of any former employment and your education history. • A supporting statement, showing how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria marked ‘Application’ in the Person Specification found within the Job Description for this post. Your statement should be provided using the free text box under ‘Questionnaire’. If you have any queries about the role or have any queries about the application process, please contact lccos-jobs@ucl.ac.uk, quoting the job title and reference number. Further information about UCL LCCOS can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/lccos/ The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. What we offer: Annual leave & Work life balance (Pro-rated for Part-Time Staff) • 27 days annual leave + 6 closure days (Christmas and Easter) as well as statutory holidays • Hybrid and flexible working • Ability to buy 5 additional annual leave days Children & Family (Pro-rated for Part-Time Staff) • Enhanced maternity, adoption, paternity leave • Carer’s leave • Workplace nursery Other benefits We also offer some great benefits some of which are defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE); cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan; on-site gym etc. As part of the UCL community you can access free lunch hour lectures, exhibitions and museums and collections. On campus UCL has the Bloomsbury theatre hosting a range of performances and a series of bars, cafes and other facilities, which UCL staff can use. Visit Reward and Benefits | Work at UCL - UCL – University College London to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department is working towards an Athena SWAN award. We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49378": {
      "title": "Alumni Relations Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07958 About us: The UCL School of Management is home to UCL’s business and management research and education programmes. The School has forged a reputation for world-leading research and teaching in the areas of innovation management, the creation and growth of technology-intensive organisations, business analytics, and entrepreneurship. The research undertaken at the UCL School of Management has been rated as ‘world-leading’ and ‘internationally excellent’ in the Research Excellence Framework 2021 (REF), placing us 2nd in the UK for Business and Management. The School is accredited by both the AACSB and AMBA, and is a member of the Chartered Association of Business Schools. The School is home to over 2,300 students, with more than 55% from outside the UK, who study across a range of undergraduate, postgraduate, executive and PhD programmes. These programmes prepare our students for leadership roles in the next generation of tech-intensive organisations and new ventures, as well as in finance and consulting firms. This is an exciting time to join the UCL School of Management, which, since 2025, has been undergoing a rapid expansion to deliver a new strategy that will see staff and student numbers double over the coming years. This growth includes new degree programmes, additional staff, and enhanced facilities at our Canary Wharf campus, supporting our mission to educate socially conscious entrepreneurial leaders equipped to tackle the world’s greatest challenges. Further information about the School can be found here. About the role: The UCL School of Management is seeking an enthusiastic, relationship-focused Alumni Relations Officer to help shape and strengthen our growing global alumni community. In this varied role, the postholder will be responsible for the planning and delivery of alumni communications, including social media and web content, developing and managing impactful volunteering programmes, and fostering a vibrant community by creating opportunities for alumni to connect both with the School and with each other. Key skills for this role include experience in alumni relations and community management, volunteer and event management, strong digital communication skills, and the ability to cultivate relationships with diverse internal and external stakeholders. This post may require some weekend, evening or early morning work. Salary: UCL Grade 7 £44,861 to £55,099 per annum (including London Allowance) If you have any queries about the role or need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible online format to apply for this job, please contact mgmt-hrsom@ucl.ac.uk . The UCL Ways of Working support colleagues' success and happiness at UCL by sharing expectations around how we work—please click here to find out more. In the event we get a high number of applications, we may close the advert early before the published closing date. As a minimum, we will keep all adverts open for 2 weeks. ​​​​ About you: We are looking for an enthusiastic and highly organised individual who is educated to degree level (or equivalent), or who has significant experience of working in Higher Education or a similar environment. You will have experience of establishing and managing effective relationships with a range of internal and external stakeholders to deliver high-quality projects, activities and events. You will be confident in developing and delivering effective communication strategies, with a strong understanding of digital communication best practice across websites, email marketing and social media. Experience of managing volunteers and alumni communities would be highly desirable, alongside excellent written and verbal communication skills and the ability to engage effectively with people at all levels, both within the University and externally. You will be able to work calmly and effectively under pressure while maintaining high service standards, and will demonstrate strong organisational and time-management skills, with the ability to prioritise your workload, meet tight deadlines and deliver consistently high-quality work. Please see the attached job description and person specification for full details. Please also read the attached Candidate Guidance document. When completing your supporting statement, please tell us, using examples, how you meet the essential criteria listed in the job description. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits, some of which are below: 41 days' holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and, for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion here."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49495": {
      "title": "Assistant Director- Capital projects",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Estates, Facilities, Accommodation and Safety; London; £80,000+; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00367 About us: The UCL Estate comprises over 240 buildings, more than 6.5 million square feet of academic accommodation and around 4500 owned student bedrooms. The location, scale, variety and complexity of the estate are unparalleled in the sector, including world-leading research, teaching, offices and student accommodation within our Bloomsbury and UCL East campus, at Stratford. Together, Campus Experience and Infrastructure and Estates Development (CE&I and ED) is responsible for managing all aspects of the UCL Estate from the strategic to the operational, including property asset management, master-planning, capital projects and seamless end to end operational support. The function comprises approximately. 350 FM and property professionals, project managers and customer facing operational colleagues. This key activity supports the student and campus experience for our 46,000 students and 13,000 staff across our sites. From providing student accommodation, security and catering, to delivering an estates masterplan and all forms of capital projects in a sustainable manner. We are a collective of technical professionals who work together to deliver a resilient, functional, and vibrant estate We have a proud history, and the ambition and drive to innovate. We strive to provide a coordinated and customer-oriented approach to both buildings and services, maintaining and transforming UCL’s Estate, so that it directly contributes to the creation of a truly inspirational environment for our academics, students and staff. We aim to provide the best possible experience, and responsive support, to our students and campus users in respect of the physical estate and all our campus services. About the role: We are seeking an Assistant Director (Capital Projects) to join our Estates team in a key leadership role to lead our Bloomsbury & Operations teams. The Capital Projects Programme team is responsible for managing the delivery of all capital works across the UCL Estate, from inception through to operational phases. This includes new builds, refurbishment, adaptation, as well as infrastructure and public realm works. Our projects are bespoke facilities designed and constructed to industry-leading standards. The AD leading the Bloomsbury will be responsible for Leading the development and delivery of a significant portfolio of medium valued(10m-150m) projects located centrally within the Estate with a focus on heritage, campus experience and complex interfaces with infrastructure and campus operations. The projects are a range of teaching space, campus experience and public realm projects. In this role, you will be responsible for working with stakeholders and project partners developing and delivering projects, with the support of your consultant and project management staff who will work under your guidance to deliver projects across the UCL Estate. The Assistant Director will be responsible to the Director Capital Program for: Leading and managing the delivery of a range of capital projects from inception through to operation including new build, refurbishments and improvements across the UCL estate with specific responsibility for major projects. Leading and managing a team of UCL Project Managers responsible for delivery of the projects within your portfolio. Responsible for creating a high-performing team with a focus on Customer, Strategy, Value for Money and People. Working with the strategy team at project origination to develop value-based Business Cases that deliver institutional strategic objectives, taking lead on the scoping and service provision to deliver the Business Case. Where relevant, working with external parities to establish Joint Ventures and working with these parties to represent UCL’s interests in the delivery of joint venture projects. Leading the procurement and subsequent management of consultants and design teams to support project delivery. Management of consultancy services and performance, through established KPI’s and supply-chain engagement. Lead the procurement and subsequent management of contractors to deliver the construction phase of projects, with particular focus on robust pre-construction planning, risk management and contract/contractor selection to UCL standards. Working with the Programme Management Office (PMO) and the UCL wide strategy leaders to set and deliver reporting and performance standards for projects in delivery. Leading stakeholder engagement for your projects in delivery, ensuring excellent customer service for academic and institutional partners. Meeting and exceeding where possible agreed targets and institutional objectives for completed schemes, including safety, accessibility and sustainability. Driving a zero-tolerance safety culture across the team and with all suppliers to secure delivery of a safe, healthy and secure environment for students, staff and visitors. As a key member of the Capital Projects Programme Leadership Team, coach and support colleagues in delivering programme wide priorities and opportunities. About you: Duties and responsibilities: Lead and coordinate a portfolio of capital projects from inception, design and construction through mobilisation, handover and post-project review, delivering agreed time, cost and quality outcomes and meeting UCL, statutory, health and safety, environmental and accessibility requirements. Lead and develop a team of internal Project Managers, providing direction, performance management, coaching and professional development, and promoting a customer-focused, responsive and collaborative culture. Ensure projects demonstrate best value for UCL, supported by robust risk management, governance, audit controls and transparent, evidence-based decision-making. Ensure projects support UCL’s strategic priorities, including high-quality teaching, learning, research and assessment environments, improved accessibility and an enhanced student and staff experience. Lead project initiation and the development and approval of Business Cases, working with Space & Feasibility and relevant stakeholders to define scope, services, costs, risks and delivery requirements. Establish effective project and programme reporting, ensuring compliance with UCL governance, financial regulations and gateway review requirements, and working with Portfolio Services, Performance Office, auditors and other stakeholders. Manage project budgets, cost plans, cashflows, forecasting, interim payments and contract variations, ensuring effective financial control and governance. Work collaboratively with Faculties, Divisions, Departments and project teams, as well as external advisers, consultants, architects, contractors and suppliers, to resolve complex project, construction and technical issues and maintain delivery within approved budgets. Build effective relationships with internal and external stakeholders, including statutory authorities, landlords and suppliers, ensuring projects align with institutional strategy, local plans and operational requirements. Promote consistent standards of project delivery and performance, providing oversight, challenge and coaching and driving continuous improvement across the portfolio. Contribute to UCL-wide strategies for effective capital project delivery, including procurement, consultant and contractor engagement, risk management, value for money, contingencies and cost planning. Ensure project teams and suppliers have the appropriate skills, resources and professional competencies to deliver successfully, including risk management, contract management and Soft Landings. Work collaboratively with Campus Experience and Infrastructure to ensure project design aligns with engineering, maintenance and operational strategies, standards and requirements. Champion operational readiness , ensuring a ‘no surprises’ approach from inception through to occupancy. Lead effective Soft Landings, stakeholder engagement, commissioning and handover, ensuring spaces are safe, fully operational and ready for use, with relevant information and asset registers complete. Ensure completed projects are effectively handed over to operational teams, including Residences where applicable, supporting safe, efficient and sustainable ongoing operation. Ensure proactive and effective communication throughout the project lifecycle, working with Estates Communications and relevant stakeholders to keep the wider UCL community informed. What we offer: In return, you’ll have the opportunity to make a significant impact within Student Lifecycle portfolio, working alongside talented colleagues. We offer a collaborative environment where your ideas are valued, your growth is supported, and your work directly contributes to the university’s bold strategic goals. Our people really are our greatest asset and we invest in them so that they can fulfil their potential. Equity, diversity and inclusion is important to us. We are committed to creating an environment where everyone is encouraged to give their best in a place where their unique experiences, perspectives and skills are seen as valuable assets. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Flexible working options Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department is working towards an Athena SWAN award. We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Work at UCL pages: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49481": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer Conservation of Contemporary Art and Media (0.4 FTE)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; UCL East; £43,981-£52,586; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-02953 About us: UCL History of Art is a successful medium-sized department, based within the Faculty of Social & Historical Sciences, with an excellent international reputation. Our academic staff bring a wide range of approaches to the study of art and visual culture, working across diverse geographical contexts, with particular strengths in image cultures, the materials and materiality of art, and visual technologies. Based at the UCL East campus in Stratford, the postholder will teach time-based media conservation on the MSc Conservation of Contemporary Art & Media . This innovative programme extends the Department’s teaching and research strengths while providing for the rapidly developing training needs of the next generation of conservators, and conservation professionals looking to develop a specialism in the field of contemporary art. The focus of the programme is on contemporary sculpture, installation art, complex mixed media works and time-based media works of art, and contemporary design. The MSc benefits from two dedicated spaces in the Marshgate Building at UCL East; an objects conservation laboratory supported by a part-time lab manager , and a media conservation studio supported by a part-time studio manager. The postholder will use the Media Conservation Studio for teaching with the support of the Media Conservation Studio Manager. The programme forms part of the School for the Creative and Cultural Industries , which supports interdisciplinary teaching, collaborations and experimental practice across Faculties, as well as with neighbouring institutions at the Olympic Park, including the Victoria and Albert Museum. About the role: The UCL History of Art Department is seeking to appoint a part-time Associate Lecturer (Teaching) in the Conservation of Contemporary Art & Media with specialist expertise in time-based media conservation to cover the research leave of a member of academic staff from 1 January 2027 for 4 months. The appointment is fixed-term, from 1 January to 30 April 2027 , at 0.4FTE (equivalent to 2 days per week). The postholder will contribute to teaching and assessment on the MSc Conservation of Contemporary Art and Media during the second term of the 2026/27 academic year. The role combines specialist teaching, practical supervision, student assessment and dissertation supervision within a part-time appointment. The role requires the postholder to be in person at the Marshgate UCL East Campus for teaching and supervision. Key responsibilities will include: Module teaching and coordination : Delivering the Spring Term teaching for HART192 Conserving Complexity: Conservation of Contemporary Artworks. This is the second term of a two-term, 30-credit, module. The Spring Term focuses on the conservation of time-based media artworks and comprises ten teaching sessions, including sessions delivered by visiting specialist practitioners and guest lecturers. The postholder will contribute their own specialist teaching and coordinate guest contributions to support a coherent learning experience. Practical supervision and assessment : Supporting students undertaking the examination and analysis of assigned time-based media artworks as part of the module HART0159 Examining and Analysing Artworks. Th i s will include supervision of students’ reports which were started in term one and form part of their assessment due at the end of term two, liaison with lenders, and marking these assessments during the Easter vacation. Dissertation supervision : Providing second-term supervision for up to three students undertaking dissertations in a field related to time-based media. The teaching, supervision and assessment responsibilities will be undertaken within the 0.4 FTE appointment, with the distribution of duties agreed in consultation with the programme team. The postholder will work closely with colleagues across the MSc programme and contribute to a collaborative, practice-led learning environment that integrates conservation theory, ethics and professional practice. About you: We are seeking a practitioner with substantial professional experience and specialist expertise in the conservation of time-based media artworks, alongside a commitment to teaching and sharing knowledge with the next generation of conservation professionals. You will have a strong understanding of the practical, theoretical and ethical challenges associated with conserving contemporary art, and an interest in critically evaluating and developing approaches to conservation practice. Relevant expertise may include the conservation of video, film, software-based art, media installations, or other complex time-based artworks. You will be able to draw on your professional experience to equip students with the practical, analytical, decision-making and communication skills required to work across a range of conservation contexts. You will also be able to support students in exploring the complex ethical and theoretical questions raised by contemporary artworks. We particularly welcome applicants who can bring perspectives from professional conservation practice, including work in museums, galleries, collections, conservation studios or independent practice, and who are committed to experimental, collaborative and inclusive approaches to teaching and learning. Previous experience of university teaching would be welcomed but is not essential, provided applicants can demonstrate the ability to communicate specialist knowledge effectively, support student learning and contribute to an inclusive teaching environment. We recognise that this role requires a distinctive combination of specialist knowledge, professional experience and teaching skills. We therefore would welcome discussions about flexible approaches to delivering the role, including the possibility of a job-share arrangement. We encourage applications from practitioners who may wish to combine this appointment with existing professional commitments. Right to Work in the UK This is a part-time position, and visa sponsorship is not available for this role. The successful candidate must have the right to work in the UK by the commencement of employment, with permission to undertake the advertised working hours. How to apply A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. When you are ready to apply, click the 'Apply Now' box. Please attach a CV and a covering letter to your application. The advert will close on Sunday 8 November 2026 at 23:59 GMT. Interview date: Wednesday 18 November, in person at the Marshgate Building, UCL East, Stratford. We can be flexible with arrangements, including offering online interviews where needed. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Pip Laurenson, Director of the MSc Conservation of Contemporary Art and Media on p.laurenson@ucl.ac.uk If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact Rosie Price-Cousins, Operations Administrator, on r.price-cousins@ucl.ac.uk What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Hybrid Final Salary Pension Scheme Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion : As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49508": {
      "title": "Associate Professor (Teaching)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11066 About us: UCL Medical School (UCLMS) is dedicated to excellence in undergraduate and postgraduate medical education, with a strong reputation for innovation in teaching and curriculum development. Our work is grounded in scientific and educational research that shapes future healthcare practice. Part of the UCL Faculty of Medical Sciences, UCL Medical School is one of six Divisions and Institutes within the Faculty. UCL has been educating doctors and scientists in since 1834.UCL is 9th in the world for Medicine and No.1 in London (QS World University Rankings by Subject 2025). We are top in the UK for research power in Life and Medical Sciences (Research Excellence Framework 2021). Our prestigious MBBS programme is known for producing graduates who go on to become influential clinicians, educators, and researchers across the globe. About the role: This is an exciting opportunity to provide strategic leadership in the design, development, and delivery of UCL’s MBBS programme, with a particular focus on shaping the future UCL Doctor as a clinician fluent in research-informed practice. As a key member of the Medical School leadership team, the postholder will lead and support research into pedagogical practices within the MBBS programme, generating evidence to enhance teaching and learning across the curriculum. They will take a central role in curriculum-focused educational research and oversee student research activities, including guidance on ethics applications. The postholder will coordinate the UCLMS research portfolio related to MBBS-facing educational scholarship in preparation for the REF 2029 submission and will be encouraged to pursue external research funding. The role also includes contributing to a range of leadership activities across the Medical School to strengthen its research and educational impact. Appointment details: The post is grade 9, the annual salary for which ranges from £68,284 - £76,244 per annum (including London Allowance). The pro rata salary at 0.8FTE ranges from £54,627.2 to £60,995.2 per annum (including London Allowance) This position is offered on a part-time basis (0.8 FTE), equivalent to 29.2 hours per week. The appointment is available from October 2026 on a fixed-term basis for 12 months, or until the return of the substantive post holder, whichever is sooner. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 40% on site. This role is available as a secondment to eligible UCL staff. About you: You will be an experienced academic with a strong background in medical education and pedagogical research, holding a higher academic degree (MD, MS, PhD, or equivalent) and ideally a postgraduate qualification in education such as FHEA. You will have experience in teaching at undergraduate and postgraduate levels, a proven record of high-quality research and publication, and a commitment to collaborative, multidisciplinary working. Skilled in leadership, organisation, and communication, you will be able to manage diverse priorities, support colleagues, and contribute to a positive academic culture. Above all, you will uphold the highest ethical and professional standards in research and education. Application process: The advert will close on 08/11/ 2026 at 23:59 BST , however we may close applications early if we receive a high volume of applications. Early application submission is recommended. Interviews are scheduled for TBC A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Please use the personal statement section to explain how you meet each essential and desirable criteria outlined in the person specification. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. Contact details: If you have any queries about the role, please contact our Recruitment team at uclms.recruitment@ucl.ac.uk If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the UCLMS Staffing team at uclms.recruitment@ucl.ac.uk What we offer : As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to equality, diversity and inclusion : As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49525": {
      "title": "Centre Reception and Administrative Assistant - part time",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11405 About us: The Max Planck UCL Centre for Computational Psychiatry and Ageing Research (MPC) is a research centre dedicated to studying the causes of psychiatric disorders as well as the causes of individual differences in cognitive development. The overarching goal of the MPC is to understand the cognitive and neural underpinnings of neuropsychiatric disorders. Computational models of differences and changes in brain-behaviour relations are the Centre’s major theoretical tool. The Centre’s findings will provide information on how cognitive functioning can be maintained into old age and on how psychiatric disorders can be better recognised and treated more efficiently. About the role: The Centre Reception and Administrative Assistant supports the activities of the Max Planck UCL Centre at Russell Square House by providing a reception and general office administration service, as well as support to the Director. With the Centre Research Coordinator, you will form the support team for the Centre in London and ensure operations are efficient and in line with UCL and funders regulations and financial requirements. As a key and first point of contact for the Centre’s visitors, staff and researchers, the role is busy and varied and offers the opportunity to gain experience in a wide variety of activities. The post is available immediately and is funded by The Max Planck UCL Centre for Computational Psychiatry and Ageing Centre for 12 months in the first instance. The post is offered part-time at 21.9 hours per week (0.6 Full Time Equivalent) on Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday, for 12 months in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). For informal enquiries about the role please contact Dominique Drai (email: d.drai@ucl.ac.uk ). For a full job description please visit UCL’s online recruitment portal ( https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs ) and search using vacancy reference B02-11405. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You will have a good standard of general education to A level/NVQ Level IV administration or equivalent relevant experience, and experience of working in a fast-paced environment. Experience of providing a successful administrative support function, ideally in a university or research environment and an understanding of data protection regulations is essential. Excellent oral and written communication skills, good computer skills, excellent organisational skills and the ability to work independently and proactively is also a requirement. This role does not meet the eligibility requirements for a Skilled Worker Visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require right to work in the UK to carry out this role. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits; v isit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on EDI initiatives; visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/ion/equality-diversity-inclusion for more information about what we’re doing. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49344": {
      "title": "Child Health Research PhD Studentships in the UCL Great Ormond Street Institute of Child Health (2027/28)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11451 About us: Up to four 3-year full time, funded postgraduate research studentships, funded by the Child Health Research Charitable Incorporated Organisation (CHR CIO) are available, commencing on 1st October 2027. All projects are hosted at the UCL Great Ormond Street Institute of Child Health , in the UCL Faculty of Population Health Sciences. Together with its partner, Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children NHS Trust, UCL GOS ICH forms the largest concentration of children’s health research in Europe. About the role: Project portfolio The Child Health Research PhD studentship portfolio offers a broad range of PhD project proposals ranging from fundamental to translational studies. This year, studentships can be funded across all topics relevant to the Institute's research, but specific funding is available for projects broadly related to epilepsy and developmental neuroscience. About you: Eligibility Applications are invited from committed individuals wishing to do basic, translational or clinical research for the benefit of child health. Applicants should have a good first degree (UK 1st class or upper 2nd class honours degree or equivalent from abroad) and, will typically have a Master’s degree (or equivalent work experience) in a relevant discipline by the time of registration. Clinical graduates are also welcome to apply, but a stipend can only be provided at non-clinical rates. This is a prestigious and competitive scheme, with over 30 applicants per studentship. How to apply The application process is a two-stage process involving: Submitting a studentship application. Applicants will be shortlisted in January, and shortlisted applicants will be interviewed online on 17 and 18 February 2027. Following the interview, successful applicants will be required to submit a graduate application and register on the Child Health PhD programme to take up the studentship. (Please do not submit a PhD application unless you are successfully interviewed and invited to do so). Application deadline : 17:00 GMT Friday 11th December 2026. For further information and how to apply, see PhD studentships in Child Health . What we offer: Funding The CHR PhD Studentships cover UK (Home) tuition fees, consumables and provide a stipend starting at £24,522 per annum. Subject to confirmation, funding may be available to cover Overseas fees for some places. Non-UK students can apply but may have to fund the difference between full-time Home and Overseas fees if further funding is not available. Research environment The Institute has one of the largest communities of PhD students in UCL, with nearly 200 registered students. We are committed to high quality postgraduate education and the Institute has a strong track record of training and support for its postgraduates. A high level of interaction exists across the Institute between basic scientists, clinicians, and population health scientists. Research in the Institute is grouped into the following five departments: Developmental Biology and Cancer Developmental Neurosciences Genetics and Genomic Medicine Infection, Immunity, Inflammation Population, Policy and Practice The great strength of UCL's research was reaffirmed in REF 2021 . GOS ICH was part of a UCL return to the Clinical Medicine sub-panel 1, and Public Health, Health Services and Primary Care. 93% of UCL's research was rated as either world-leading or internationally excellent. In assessment of research power, UCL’s performance was top in the UK for research power in the main panels of ‘medicine, health and life sciences’ and ‘social sciences’. The NIHR Great Ormond Street Hospital Biomedical Research Centre (BRC), underpins all translational research at GOS ICH and GOSH. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49541": {
      "title": "Clinical Research Fellow in Transational Oncology",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Clinical; London; £48,705 - £69,584; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11452 About us: The UCL Cancer Institute is a leading centre for cancer research, bringing together scientists and clinicians to translate research discoveries into more effective treatments for people with cancer. The Treatment Resistance Team focuses on understanding treatment resistance in advanced urological cancers and undertakes translational research incorporating clinical samples and molecular technologies. About the role: We are seeking a medically qualified Clinical Research Fellow in Translational Oncology to join the Treatment Resistance Team within the UCL Cancer Institute. The postholder will support translational research through the STAMPEDE-Life and PEACE studies. Key responsibilities include coordinating the collection, processing and analysis of research samples; obtaining informed consent from eligible patients; attending urology medical oncology clinics at University College London Hospitals NHS Foundation Trust (UCLH); maintaining high-quality clinical and research data; and contributing to the development of translational research protocols. The postholder will also have opportunities to contribute to research presentations, peer-reviewed publications, teaching and other activities of the research team. This is a one-year fixed-term appointment at CL7/CL8. Appointment level will reflect the responsibilities of the role and the successful candidate's relevant clinical and research experience. Appointment at CL8 will additionally require evidence of greater independence in the development and delivery of clinical or translational research and the ability to take responsibility for defined elements of research activity with appropriate senior oversight. The postholder will be required to hold and maintain an appropriate honorary clinical contract with UCLH for the duration of the appointment. About you: Applicants must have: • A primary medical qualification (MBBS or equivalent). • Full registration with the General Medical Council (GMC) and a current licence to practise in the UK. • Completion of Internal Medicine Training (IMT) years 1 and 2, or equivalent clinical experience. • MRCP (UK), or an appropriate equivalent postgraduate medical qualification. • Evidence of Good Clinical Practice (GCP) training, or willingness to undertake and maintain the required training. • Experience of collecting, managing and interpreting clinical data. • An interest in clinical and translational research in oncology, particularly prostate cancer. • Strong communication, organisational and team-working skills, including the ability to communicate sensitively and professionally with patients and families. Previous oncology or UK NHS experience and research experience relevant to oncology or translational medicine would be advantageous. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible and job share basis wherever possible."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49393": {
      "title": "Contracts Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement) (B09) Reference: B09-01440 About us: UCL is a world-leading research-intensive university, consistently ranked among the top universities globally. Our Research and Innovation Services (RIS) directorate plays a vital role in supporting UCL's academic community throughout the research and innovation lifecycle, providing specialist advice and expertise on research funding, collaborations and contracts. Within RIS, the Contract Services team provides a central service for research and innovation-related contracts, ensuring agreements comply with UCL policies, external regulations and funder requirements. The team plays an important role in enabling research collaborations while protecting UCL's interests and supporting the delivery of world-class research. About the role: We are seeking a Contracts Officer to join our Contract Services team within Research and Innovation Services. This is an opportunity to support UCL's research activities by drafting, reviewing and negotiating a range of research-related agreements. The role involves managing contracts including Material Transfer Agreements, Data Sharing Agreements and Confidentiality/Non-Disclosure Agreements, ensuring appropriate contractual protections are in place. Key responsibilities include advising stakeholders on contractual terms and risks, negotiating commercially acceptable outcomes and protecting UCL's intellectual property interests. Working closely with academic departments, research support teams and colleagues across UCL, the successful candidate will manage a portfolio of contract requests, maintain accurate records using Worktribe and contribute to the continuous improvement of contracting processes. The role is based at UCL's Bloomsbury Campus, with hybrid working arrangements available. About you: We are looking for a proactive, organised and detail-oriented individual with a relevant undergraduate or postgraduate degree, or equivalent qualification, and an understanding of contractual principles and associated risks. The ideal candidate will have knowledge of key contractual areas, including intellectual property, confidentiality, publication rights, warranties, liabilities and indemnities, alongside the ability to assess risks and approach contractual matters pragmatically. Strong communication and interpersonal skills are essential, as is the ability to manage competing priorities, work independently and maintain high standards of accuracy in a busy environment. A collaborative approach, sound judgement and the ability to build effective relationships with a range of stakeholders will be important. Experience of research contracts, research funding or research support within higher education or a commercial research environment would be advantageous. A recognised professional legal qualification, or progress towards one, is also desirable. We welcome applications from candidates who can demonstrate a commitment to equality, diversity and inclusion, continuous improvement and delivering a high-quality service. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49314": {
      "title": "Data Analyst",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement) (B09) Reference: B09-01438 About us: UCL is a world-leading research-intensive university, consistently ranked among the best universities globally. Our Research and Innovation Services (RIS) directorate plays a vital role in supporting UCL's research community, providing specialist expertise across research funding, contracts, compliance and the wider research and innovation lifecycle. The Planning, Insight and Improvement team works across RIS to strengthen research support services, drive continuous improvement and embed evidence-based decision-making. Through high-quality data, analysis and insight, the team helps ensure that UCL's research and innovation activities are supported by effective, consistent and sustainable services. About the role: We are seeking a Data Analyst to join our Planning, Insight and Improvement team within Research and Innovation Services. This role offers an opportunity to use data and analytics to inform strategic and operational decision-making across UCL's research and innovation activities. Working closely with colleagues across RIS, the postholder will analyse complex datasets, identify trends and opportunities for improvement, and translate findings into meaningful insights that support planning, performance monitoring and service delivery. Key responsibilities include extracting, transforming and validating data from multiple systems; developing and maintaining dashboards and management information reports; and analysing performance across research funding, contracts, compliance and financial activities. The role will also involve working with stakeholders to understand reporting requirements, improve data quality and governance, and develop effective approaches to data visualisation and reporting. Reporting to the Assistant Director, Planning, Insight & Improvement (Data & Insight), the successful candidate will contribute to UCL's wider Data & Insight Community of Practice, sharing knowledge and supporting the development of analytical capability across the institution. About you: We are looking for an analytical and collaborative individual with experience of working with complex datasets and translating data into clear, actionable insights. The successful candidate will have a degree or equivalent professional experience, ideally with a strong mathematical or statistical component, alongside knowledge of data management and processing using SQL, Snowflake, SAS or equivalent technologies. Experience of designing and developing dashboards, reporting tools and performance metrics is essential, together with knowledge of R or Python for analytics, natural language processing and data quality. Proficiency in data visualisation and management information tools such as Power BI, Tableau or similar is also important. Strong analytical skills, including experience of statistical methods, exploratory data analysis and data validation, will be complemented by the ability to communicate complex information clearly to a range of audiences. Excellent communication and stakeholder engagement skills are essential, alongside a collaborative approach to working with colleagues across different teams and disciplines. Experience within higher education or research support environments, together with project management skills and an understanding of how data can inform service improvements, would be advantageous. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49545": {
      "title": "Deputy Head of Faculty and Professional Services Finance",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Professional Services Leadership; London; £69,660-£79,964; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00421 About us: Are you an experienced finance leader looking for an opportunity to shape financial services across a world-leading university? UCL is seeking a Deputy Head of Faculty and Professional Services Finance to play a key leadership role in its newly established Faculty and Professional Services Finance team, which has been created following the consolidation of devolved finance teams. This is an exciting opportunity to help shape a developing finance function, strengthen financial governance and deliver a consistent, high-quality service across a complex, multifaceted organisation. UCL is a global top 10 university, employing around 17,000 people and supporting more than 50,000 students across 11 academic faculties and central professional services. Its work spans world-leading research, exceptional teaching and innovation, creating a diverse and intellectually ambitious environment in which finance plays a vital strategic role. About the role: Reporting to the Head of Faculty and Professional Services Finance, you will lead the delivery of front line finance services across an allocated portfolio of Faculties and/or Professional Services, providing senior financial expertise, strategic insight and operational leadership. Working closely with academic and professional services leaders, Finance Managers and central finance teams, you will be responsible for delivering effective financial management support across operational finance activity and the provision of accurate reporting, planning and analysis that enables effective financial performance and decision making. You will also play a key role in embedding new ways of working following the consolidation of devolved finance teams, driving greater consistency, improving data quality and strengthening financial controls across UCL. Key responsibilities will include: Strategic financial leadership: Provide expert advice on financial planning, resource allocation, performance and risk, helping senior stakeholders make informed decisions. Operational leadership: Lead finance service delivery across your portfolio, supporting Finance Managers, developing team capability and ensuring resources are deployed effectively. Financial performance: Oversee budgets, forecasts and management accounts, ensuring robust reporting, meaningful financial insight and effective performance monitoring. Governance and control: Strengthen financial compliance, risk management and internal controls, ensuring adherence to UCL’s financial regulations and policies. Continuous improvement: Lead initiatives to improve finance processes, service consistency, data quality and operational effectiveness. Strategic stakeholder engagement: Build trusted relationships with senior academic and professional services leaders, representing finance in institutional initiatives and governance discussions. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will be a fully qualified accountant (CCAB or CIMA) with post-qualification experience and a strong track record in financial leadership within a complex organisation. You will combine excellent technical financial knowledge with the ability to influence senior stakeholders, lead high-performing teams and translate complex financial information into clear, practical advice. You will also bring: Strong technical knowledge in budgeting, forecasting, financial control and risk management. A thorough understanding of financial governance, compliance and policy frameworks. Proven experience leading teams and managing competing priorities across multiple business areas. The ability to build credibility and effective relationships with senior stakeholders. A continuous improvement mindset, with experience strengthening processes, controls and ways of working. Strong analytical, communication and influencing skills, alongside excellent financial systems and advanced Excel capability. Experience within higher education or a similarly complex public sector organisation is highly desirable as understanding of research and teaching funding would be advantageous. Please read our job description, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure these are covered in your application. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent.We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49369": {
      "title": "EDI Officer - ELIGIBLE FOR UCL INTERNAL CANDIDATES ONLY",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; HR and Wellbeing; London; £43,981-£54,019​; People and Culture & PS Hub (B30) Reference: B30-00278 About us: UCL’s Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) Team, part of the People and Culture Division, works to advance equality and embed inclusion across the university. Drawing on diverse experiences and expertise, we provide advice and support to faculties and departments, helping to develop initiatives and share good practice that reflect UCL’s founding values. We work collaboratively with staff, students and external partners to embed EDI into everyday activity and influence positive change. About the role: We are seeking an EDI Officer to support the development, delivery and evaluation of evidence-based projects, action plans and positive action programmes across a range of protected characteristics. Based within the central EDI Team and reporting to an EDI Manager, you will support initiatives including EDI charters such as Athena Swan and act as a first point of contact for queries and requests. You will work with a wide range of stakeholders, coordinate events and networking activities, deliver training and presentations, and prepare reports, briefings and communications materials. You will also analyse and present staff and student data to inform projects, maintaining confidentiality and meeting data protection requirements. About you: You will have experience working on EDI issues and interventions, including positive action and delivering EDI-related training. You will also bring experience of planning, delivering and evaluating EDI projects, alongside demonstrable knowledge of the Equality Act 2010, the duties it places on UCL and current equality issues. Strong written and verbal communication skills are essential, with the ability to advise stakeholders sensitively on complex or contentious matters and produce high-quality resources. You will demonstrate integrity, sound judgement and respect for confidentiality, supported by excellent organisational, time management and problem-solving skills. You will be comfortable taking initiative, adapting to changing priorities and working independently where information is incomplete or circumstances are uncertain. Experience of proactively engaging stakeholders and building networks would be an advantage. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49543": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant x2",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; People and Culture & PS Hub (B30) Reference: B30-00272 About us: UCL’s Professional Services (PS) Hub brings together customer-facing and transactional services across HR, Finance, Estates, IT and Business Operations. We are committed to delivering high-quality, efficient services with a strong focus on customer experience. Within the PS Hub, the Business Operations team provides a centre of excellence for administrative and executive support, working across UCL’s Vice-Provost and Vice-President Offices to support the effective delivery of their priorities. About the role: We are seeking two organised and proactive Executive Assistants to provide high-level support to one Vice-Provost or three Directors across UCL’s Vice-Provost and Vice-President Offices. Reporting to the Executive Support Manager, you will manage complex diaries and inboxes, coordinate national and international travel, and liaise with senior colleagues and external partners. You will organise meetings, committees, events and away days, prepare agendas, take notes and monitor actions, while handling confidential information with discretion. You will also support specialist projects, recruitment and staff induction, and work collaboratively with other Executive Assistants to provide continuity of support. The role is based in London, with hybrid working available in line with UCL’s arrangements and operational requirements. If you have any questions about the application process or the role please contact Passant Khalifa (Senior Recruiter) at p.khalifa@ucl.ac.uk About you: You will have experience providing senior-level administrative and secretarial support to multiple leaders within a complex organisation, including managing busy diaries and organising committee meetings and events. You will bring excellent written communication skills, advanced proficiency in Microsoft Word, PowerPoint and Excel, and meticulous attention to detail. You will be able to manage competing priorities, maintain high standards under pressure and adapt confidently to changing requirements at short notice. A proactive and collaborative approach is essential, alongside the ability to build strong working relationships, influence colleagues at all levels and exercise sound judgement when handling complex or sensitive matters. Experience within higher education or a research environment would be an advantage What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49450": {
      "title": "Executive Education Marketing Officer (maternity cover)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; External Engagement, Communications, Marketing and Events; UCL East; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11391 About us: The UCL Global Business School for Health (GBSH) at UCL East campus is an innovative business school preparing future healthcare leaders to disrupt and reimagine global health systems. GBSH is dedicated to training and developing the next generation of leaders in the global health economy by building on UCL’s proud tradition of cross-disciplinary academic excellence in education and research. GBSH will develop talent from all sectors - policy makers, technologists and entrepreneurs, to challenge conventional thinking, to improve health outcomes and address health inequalities world-wide. With a vision for the public good, GBSH is dedicated to Health as the most important global issue in the 21st Century - impacting communities and economies worldwide. Its ambition is aligned to the UN Sustainable Development Goal of ‘Universal Health Care for All’ bringing together global perspectives from diverse healthcare systems, industry partners and sector networks. GBSH is an interdisciplinary academic entity that sits within UCL’s Faculty of Population Health Sciences aimed at innovating and improving health worldwide. Our academic staff represent a cross disciplinary group of world-leading academics dedicated to improving health and healthcare management. We are focused on engaging with businesses and practitioners as well as ensuring impactful research. As part of this mission, we offer a suite of applied training and education programmes through open enrolment and customised executive education programmes using a variety of modes of delivery including online hybrid and in-person residential opportunities. Our academic staff are committed to engaging with health professionals, industry and businesses to ensure this applied thought leadership approach. About the role: The Marketing Officer will be dedicated to Executive Education and implement activities that attract and convert executive candidates from around the world, communicating both GBSH and the Executive Education portfolio’s unique value proposition to a diverse audience. About you: The post holder will have a strong understanding of the rapidly developing digital landscape and the relationships between SEO, paid activity, organic activity, content and social media in respect to engagement and marketing and conversion at executive level. This role requires a commercial mindset and experience with marketing and converting leads in B2B and B2C markets. The post holder will coordinate the end-to-end marketing lifecycle for Executive Education, from creation to execution, enrolments and evaluation. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • On-site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity,paternityand adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit Rewards and Benefits to find out more. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research, Teaching and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s academic talent, from local to global. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We, therefore, particularly encourage applications from candidates currently under-represented in UCL’s academic, research and teaching workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQ+ people; and women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49380": {
      "title": "Feedback and Assessment Programme Manager (0.4 FTE 3-Year Fixed Term Contract)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Vice-Provost (Education and Student Experience) (B06) Reference: B06-00217 About us: The Office of the Vice Provost (Education and Student Experience) works across UCL to enhance the educational experience and support student success. The Feedback and Assessment Programme brings together colleagues from across UCL to improve staff and student experiences of feedback and assessment, strengthen assessment integrity and ensure that our assessment regulations, processes and reporting continue to meet the needs of our students, staff and the wider regulatory environment. Working in partnership with faculties, departments and professional services, the Programme brings together expertise across areas including assessment regulations, operations and platforms, pedagogic support and guidance, and data and reporting. This is an opportunity to contribute to an important programme of institutional improvement at one of the world's leading universities. About the role: We are looking for an experienced Feedback and Assessment Programme Manager to provide specialist programme management expertise across UCL's Feedback and Assessment Programme. You will work with academic and professional services colleagues to coordinate improvement activity, maintain momentum and support the delivery of institutional priorities relating to feedback, assessment and student academic experience. Working with a high degree of autonomy, you will bring together data, evidence, policy and stakeholder perspectives to support informed decision-making and sustained improvement. You will maintain programme plans, monitoring frameworks and risk registers, coordinate activity across multiple programme strands and identify and address barriers, risks and dependencies that could affect delivery. You will also play an important role in supporting governance and senior decision-making, preparing high-quality reports, briefing papers, analysis and presentations, and ensuring that agreed decisions are translated into deliverable activity. The role includes supporting recommendations arising from institutional reviews and enhancement initiatives, including UCL's work relating to AI and assessment. This is a highly collaborative role requiring you to build effective relationships across faculties and professional services, facilitate meetings and workshops, bring together diverse perspectives and influence colleagues to deliver shared objectives. You will also use evidence and data to identify opportunities for continuous improvement and help share effective practice across UCL. Please note, this is a part time (0.4 FTE/ 14.6hrs per week) role offered on a 3-year fixed term contract basis in the first instance. The role offers hybrid working with currently a minimum of 20-40% onsite per week in our Bloomsbury offices. Job Ref: B06-00217 Closing Date: 04-11-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) About you: You will be an experienced programme or project professional with a strong understanding of the higher education environment and an interest in improving student academic experience, feedback and assessment. You will have significant experience coordinating complex projects, programmes or strategic initiatives involving multiple stakeholder groups and organisational boundaries. You will be confident supporting senior committees and governance groups, developing programme plans and monitoring frameworks, managing risks and dependencies, and producing clear, high-quality reports and briefings for senior audiences. You will have strong analytical skills and the ability to interpret complex information and use evidence to support effective decision-making. Crucially, you will be an excellent relationship builder and communicator, able to bring together academic and professional services colleagues with different perspectives and priorities. You will be comfortable working independently, exercising sound judgement and managing competing priorities in a complex environment, while maintaining a clear focus on delivery and continuous improvement. Experience of working in higher education, particularly in areas relating to student experience, assessment, feedback, academic quality, educational enhancement or careers and employability , would be highly relevant to this role. You will bring a collaborative, evidence-informed approach and a commitment to supporting positive outcomes for students and staff. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49365": {
      "title": "Finance Change Lead",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Finance; London; £68,284-£78,386​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00394 About us: UCL is a world-leading, global top 10 university, with 11 academic faculties, around 17,000 staff and over 50,000 students. We deliver ground-breaking research and exceptional teaching, supported by professional services that enable our community to thrive. Our Finance function plays a vital role in supporting UCL’s strategic ambitions, financial sustainability and operational excellence. Bringing together Strategic and Operational Finance, we are committed to improving our processes, systems and ways of working to deliver an effective, consistent and forward-looking service across the university. About the role: We are seeking an experienced Finance Change Lead to drive meaningful, sustainable change across UCL’s Finance function. In this job you will lead the coordination and delivery of finance change initiatives, ensuring they are aligned with institutional priorities and deliver measurable improvements. Working collaboratively with finance colleagues, subject matter experts and wider professional services teams, you will develop user-centred solutions that address underlying challenges, simplify processes and embed best practice. You will lead initiatives that improve consistency, strengthen financial controls and governance, and deliver better experiences for colleagues across UCL. Your work will span change portfolio management, process and systems improvement, stakeholder engagement and change adoption. You will establish effective governance, oversee implementation and training plans, monitor risks and benefits, and provide senior leaders with clear insight into progress and priorities. A key priority will be supporting the new Faculty and Professional Services Finance team to establish consistent, efficient processes, sharing best practice while recognising where flexibility is needed. You will also provide strategic oversight of the finance change portfolio, ensuring effective prioritisation, governance and coordination across people, processes, policy, data and technology. This is an exciting opportunity to shape how Finance operates in a world leading University, helping colleagues navigate change and building a more connected, efficient and future-ready function. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will bring significant experience leading change programmes in a large, complex organisation, supported by a professional qualification in project or change management, or equivalent practical experience. You will have a strong understanding of finance processes, systems, controls and regulatory requirements, alongside experience managing change portfolios, project governance and cross-functional delivery. A confident and collaborative leader, you will be skilled at engaging stakeholders, influencing across organisational boundaries and bringing people together to deliver shared outcomes. You will be comfortable navigating complex interdependencies, making informed decisions, managing competing priorities and using data to identify risks and measure improvements. You will also demonstrate a proactive approach to problem-solving, a commitment to equality, diversity and inclusion, and the ability to communicate change clearly to both specialist and non-specialist audiences. A finance or accounting qualification and evidence of ongoing professional development would be advantageous. Please read our job description, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure these are covered in your application. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent.We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49367": {
      "title": "Finance Change Manager",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Finance; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00395 About us: UCL is a world-leading, global top 10 university, with 11 academic faculties, around 17,000 staff and over 50,000 students. We deliver ground-breaking research and exceptional teaching, supported by professional services that enable our community to thrive. Our Finance function plays a vital role in supporting UCL’s strategic ambitions, financial sustainability and operational excellence. We are committed to improving our processes, systems and ways of working to deliver an effective, consistent and forward-looking service across the university. About the role: We are seeking a proactive and collaborative Finance Change Manager to support the delivery of change initiatives across UCL’s Finance function. Reporting to the Finance Change Lead, in this job you will manage projects that improve finance processes, systems, data and ways of working, ensuring changes are well planned, communicated and successfully adopted. Working with Finance Systems, IT, HR and wider university teams, you will coordinate project activities, facilitate working groups and engage stakeholders to develop practical, user-centred solutions. An initial priority will be supporting the new Faculty and Professional Services Finance team to establish more consistent and efficient processes by identifying and sharing best practice, standardising approaches where possible while retaining flexibility where needed. You will also manage project plans, risks and dependencies, coordinate communications and training, monitor the benefits of change and contribute to continuous improvement across Finance. This is an exciting opportunity to make a tangible contribution to the future of Finance at one of the world’s leading universities, helping colleagues navigate change and delivering more effective, consistent and sustainable services across UCL. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: To do this job, you will need to have experience delivering projects or change initiatives within a large, complex organisation, alongside an understanding of project or change management methodologies. Knowledge of finance processes, systems or operations would be extremely beneficial, as would experience facilitating workshops and engaging diverse stakeholders. You will bring excellent organisational, communication and problem-solving skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities, coordinate activity across teams and explain complex information clearly to different audiences. You will be comfortable working with ambiguity, building relationships and influencing without formal authority to achieve shared goals. You will also demonstrate a commitment to continuous improvement, inclusive decision-making and equality, diversity and inclusion. Please read our job description, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure these are covered in your application. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent.We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49552": {
      "title": "Fire Safety Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Estates, Facilities, Accommodation and Safety; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00403 About us: With more than 200 buildings across its diverse estate, UCL offers a challenging and rewarding environment in which to support fire safety compliance and protect its staff, students, visitors and contractors. Reporting to the Fire Safety Manager, you will support the delivery of UCL's fire safety management systems, undertake fire risk assessments, audits and inspections, and provide expert advice on fire safety legislation and best practice. You will contribute to incident investigations, emergency evacuation planning, fire safety training and building projects, working closely with departments, Estates teams, contractors and external agencies. About the role: Reporting to the Fire Safety Manager, you will support the implementation and delivery of UCL's fire safety management systems, ensuring compliance with relevant legislation, recognised standards and best practice. You will undertake fire risk assessments, audits and inspections across the estate, identify non-compliances and work with colleagues to develop practical solutions and manage corrective actions. You will provide specialist fire safety advice to staff, managers and departments, including guidance on Personal Emergency Evacuation Plans (PEEPs), fire prevention and protection measures. You will also contribute to the development and delivery of fire safety training, including training for Fire Evacuation Marshals, and support investigations into fire incidents and unwanted alarm activations. Working closely with Estates colleagues, contractors and external agencies, including the London Fire Brigade, you will help ensure fire safety is effectively integrated into building maintenance and capital projects. You will also contribute to monitoring fire safety performance, maintaining accurate records and reports, reviewing procedures and keeping up to date with changes in legislation and industry guidance. This varied role will involve working across UCL's buildings, campuses and accommodation sites, offering an excellent opportunity to make a tangible contribution to fire safety across a large and complex university estate. About you: You will have relevant fire safety professional qualifications and experience in fire safety inspection and management, together with a thorough, up-to-date understanding of fire safety legislation, standards and best practice. You will be confident undertaking risk assessments, identifying hazards and advising on proportionate, practical and cost-effective solutions. With excellent communication, interpersonal and organisational skills, you will be able to engage effectively with stakeholders at all levels, influence positive change and build productive working relationships. You will be equally comfortable working independently with minimal supervision and collaborating as part of a team, managing competing priorities and responding flexibly to operational demands. You will also have good IT skills and the ability to prepare clear reports, maintain accurate records and communicate technical information effectively. A full, valid UK driving licence is essential, as the role requires independent travel between sites. You must also be able to access different areas of the estate, including stairs, roof areas, basements and tunnels, as required. Experience delivering fire safety training, a general health and safety qualification such as NEBOSH, knowledge of HTM 05, or experience working within a large and complex organisation would be advantageous. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan •Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49509": {
      "title": "Full Time PhD Studentship- Prostate Cancer Multi-omics: Risk Stratification and Outcome Prediction",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11400 About us: The UCL Cancer Institute is one of the world's leading centres for cancer research, bringing together internationally recognised scientists, clinicians, and researchers to advance our understanding of cancer and improve patient outcomes. Situated in the heart of London, the Institute combines cutting-edge laboratory research with strong clinical partnerships, creating an environment where scientific discoveries can rapidly translate into real-world impact. This PhD studentship is part of the ReIMAGINE[Life] programme, a major initiative funded by Prostate Cancer UK that aims to transform prostate cancer diagnosis and risk stratification through the integration of molecular, imaging, and clinical data. About the role: This fully funded four-year PhD studentship offers an exciting opportunity to explore how molecular characteristics present at diagnosis influence the long-term progression of prostate cancer. Working within the Treatment Resistance Team led by Professor Gerhardt Attard, you will analyse and integrate large-scale multi-omics datasets, including genomic, transcriptomic, epigenomic, and proteomic data, alongside imaging and clinical information. You will develop innovative computational approaches and predictive models to improve risk stratification, disease monitoring, and patient outcomes. The role provides extensive training in cancer genomics, machine learning, statistical analysis, and scientific programming while working in a highly collaborative multidisciplinary research environment. How to Apply Submit the following by 23rd November 2026- (using the apply button.) CV including: Contact details of two referees (one academic). A short statement explaining how your experience aligns with the project and person specification. Academic transcripts and certificates (PDF format). Include official English translations if applicable. Informal enquiries about this post (including arrangements to visit UCL Cancer Institute) are welcome and may be directed by email to Prof Gert Attard (g.attard@ucl.ac.uk) and/or Dr Stefano Lise (s.lise@ucl.ac.uk)”. About you: You are a highly motivated and academically outstanding graduate with a first-class degree (or equivalent) in bioinformatics, computational biology, computer science, mathematics, statistics, physics, engineering, or a related quantitative discipline. You have experience in Python and/or R programming and possess strong analytical and problem-solving skills. You are excited by the opportunity to apply computational methods to address important biomedical challenges and have a genuine interest in cancer genomics and translational research. You thrive both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary team, communicate effectively, and are eager to learn new computational and analytical techniques that will contribute to impactful scientific discoveries. The job does not meet the eligibility requirements for a Skilled Worker Visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require the right to work in the UK to carry out this role. What we offer: This is a full time fully funded 4year PhD studentship , successful candidates will receive a non-taxable annual stipend of £23805 pa covering tuition fees at Home rate and meet UCL PhD admissions criteria. Stipend will go up to £24,522 in year 2, £25,260 for year 3 and £26021 in year 4. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people. Our division holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49535": {
      "title": "Head of Delivery (Estates)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £68,284-£76,244; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00327 About us: The UCL Estate comprises over 240 buildings, more than 6.5 million square feet of academic accommodation and around 4500 owned student bedrooms. The location, scale, variety and complexity of the estate are unparalleled in the sector, including world-leading research, teaching, offices and student accommodation within our Bloomsbury and UCL East campus, at Stratford Together, Campus Experience and Infrastructure and Estates Development (CE&I and ED) is responsible for managing all aspects of the UCL Estate from the strategic to the operational, including property asset management, master-planning, capital projects and seamless end to end operational support. The function comprises approximately 350 FM and property professionals, project managers and customer facing operational colleagues This key activity supports the student and campus experience for our 46,000 students and 13,000 staff across our sites. From providing student accommodation, security and catering, to delivering an estates masterplan and all forms of capital projects in a sustainable manner. We are a collective of technical professionals who work together to deliver a resilient, functional, and vibrant estate We have a proud history, and the ambition and drive to innovate. We strive to provide a coordinated and customer-oriented approach to both buildings and services, maintaining and transforming UCL’s Estate, so that it directly contributes to the creation of a truly inspirational environment for our academics, students and staff. We aim to provide the best possible experience, and responsive support, to our students and campus users in respect of the physical estate and all our campus services About the role: The Head of Delivery will set and lead the delivery culture and standards of the Capital Programme’s construction projects in delivery With oversight from supplier selection through pre-construction, construction to handing over to operations and academic occupation, the Head of Delivery will set and lead the delivery standard for capital programme works on behalf of UCL to objectively; Deliver a safe and respectful working environment for the workforce and work site adjacencies seeking industry leading-best practice in delivery Establish a high-quality interface with public realm focused on respecting and not interrupting a high-quality campus experience Ensure rigorous regulatory compliance for the works in delivery, setting the standard for how works are inspected and by whom Set a delivery model and standard for delivering world-class quality Ensure a seamless handover to operational and academic occupants, leading culture of engagement with operational colleagues and end users in the project delivery model Oversee project reporting to ensure rigorous command of works in delivery from a programmatic and resourcing lens, setting the standard for how we monitor and report on construction works in progress Oversee HSE reporting and near-miss and incident investigation, ensure robust and measured responses are actioned within the works in progress and lessons learned cascaded across the programme in delivery Ensure works in delivery meet existing UCL Standards and working closely with the UCL Health and Safety, Engineering and Operations teams to highlight if/where UCL standards do not delivery construction industry best practice and conceive pathways to agree and close any gaps The Head of Delivery will work with all respective Capital Programme Assistant Directors and UPOs, who will retain ultimate responsibility for their projects in delivery The Head of Delivery will assess skills gaps within the Capital Programme team and work will propose relevant training to close any skills gaps About you: With a focus is on delivery assurance and how we put people to work to deliver world-class places and spaces, the Head of Delivery will work with Capital Programme leadership team, the SLT and the Health and Safety and Operations team to set and define the aspired delivery culture for the programme and will review and drive enhancement in existing ways of working. Key considerations will include; Safety Culture and Project delivery leadership behaviours Roles and Responsibilities of our UPOs and PMOs in regard to health and safety and project delivery assurance Competency assessment and training/coaching to raise qualitive performance and confidence across programme Pre-Start checklists and establishing methods to assure project set up Quality of our CPPs and RAMS Training, review standards Quality of our site safety inspections Raising and closing out inspection and audit actions Monitoring and reporting progress on site Incidence response and reporting Quality of our monitoring and reporting Quality of how we put people to work daily Site set-up standards Welfare arrangements Logistics arrangements Interfaces with live academic spaces Communication with stakeholders and project environment neighbourhood What we offer: In return, you’ll have the opportunity to make a significant impact within Student Lifecycle portfolio, working alongside talented colleagues. We offer a collaborative environment where your ideas are valued, your growth is supported, and your work directly contributes to the university’s bold strategic goals Our people really are our greatest asset and we invest in them so that they can fulfil their potential. Equity, diversity and inclusion is important to us. We are committed to creating an environment where everyone is encouraged to give their best in a place where their unique experiences, perspectives and skills are seen as valuable assets As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Flexible working options Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department is working towards an Athena SWAN award We are committed to advancing gender equality within our department. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ Work at UCL pages: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49247": {
      "title": "Head of Education & Student Experience (Specialist Teams)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £68,284-£78,386​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03319 About us: The Faculty of Social and Historical Sciences (SHS) represents areas of knowledge where humanities and social science meet. The interests and methods of the eight component departments (Anthropology, Archaeology, Economics, Geography, History, History of Art, Institute of the Americas, Political Science), together with the interdisciplinary Institute of Advanced Studies, offer excellent opportunities for innovative and collaborative research. Each of the departments carries out world leading research in its own field. There are more than 6000 students and over 1000 members of staff in the faculty, of whom three quarters are academic, teaching and research staff, and one quarter professional services. The faculty has recently been deploying a new strategic vision for the coming decade, and has ambitious plans to develop its research, teaching and innovation activities. It is committed to enhancing its research and teaching in relation to major global challenges, including climate change and sustainability, technological change and the data revolution, and inequality About the role: Leading services that shape the student experience This is an opportunity to lead teams whose work makes a real difference to the student experience. You will lead teams in areas including assessment, quality and governance, student casework, student experience and student success, driving continuous improvement and ensuring effective collaboration and communication between these teams and the Faculty’s programme teams who have responsibility for the day-to-day support for the delivery of the Faculty’s degree programmes You will: • Lead and develop the Assessment & Progression, Quality & Governance, Student Casework, Student Success & Experience teams, delivering challenging objectives in an environment that supports excellent wellbeing; • Lead and support change, helping the teams adapt to new ways of working and continuously improve how services are delivered; • Support the teams in working in partnership with students ensuring their voices help shape how services are designed and delivered; • Work closely in partnership with other staff, including academic staff, to identify opportunities and challenges, and shape responses; • Manage competing priorities across the team's portfolio, making sound decisions and keeping work on track. • Act as a key point of contact for Faculty and University stakeholders. This role combines operational leadership and strategic impact. You'll help the Faculty deliver excellent services that are effective, well-run and centred on students About you: You will be a collaborative leader who enjoys building relationships and bringing people together around shared goals. You will have experience of: • Leading teams in a Higher Education setting, with responsibility for areas such as casework, quality, assessment or student success (access and participation). • Giving guidance and support to experienced staff, helping them to identify development gaps and coaching them to address these; • Leading change, with the communication skills to bring colleagues with you. • Identifying continuous improvement opportunities, and managing projects to take advantage of these opportunities; Managing competing priorities in a busy, complex environment What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49325": {
      "title": "Head of Education and Student Experience, Faculty of Arts and Humanities",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £68,284-£78,386​; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03316 About us: UCL is a large, comprehensive university with over 18,000 staff and 50,000 students from 150 different countries. One of 11 Faculties, Arts and Humanities offers study at all levels across 10 departments spanning a wide range of disciplines. For more information see www.ucl.ac.uk/arts-humanities Arts and Humanities (A&H) is a collaborative, innovative and collegial Faculty, committed to the holistic support, growth and development of its staff and students. EDI and Wellbeing are firmly embedded into our strategic vision, as is our commitment to the highest quality education and student experience. The Faculty is made up of c. 5,600 students and 815 staff who work across both UCL campuses in Bloomsbury and the Olympic Park, Stratford. Many of our degree courses are highly competitive with demanding entry tariffs. With significant expertise in languages and area studies, study abroad and the international student experience are important elements of our education profile. The Faculty Education Team This is an exciting opportunity to join A&H as one of two Heads of Education and Student Experience at a pivotal point in the development of our Education and Student Experience service. Following completion of the EASE restructure, we are establishing new standardised processes and ways of working, while embedding a culture of continuous improvement across our teams The Faculty Education Team is responsible for delivering both our business-as-usual education and student experience service to support our course delivery as required by the relevant policies and practices - and alongside that work, our cross-faculty strategic activity such as Quality Assurance, complex student casework and regulatory compliance. The two Heads will work in close partnership to deliver the Faculty's strategic ambitions for education and student experience. While sharing responsibility for service leadership and improvement, each post-holder will lead a distinct portfolio of strategic activity. Both HESE roles will report to the Director of Education and Student Experience and line-manage a roughly balanced team of grade 8 Education Managers. About the role: As a key member of our Education and Student Experience (EASE) Team, you will be dedicated to supporting all who work/study in our subject areas. You will lead on supporting the successful operation of all our education courses by ensuring an effective service infrastructure is both in place and compliant with academic policy and regulations, policy, and practice. You will oversee all areas relating to student support and experience; manage a team of EASE professionals and be an active participant in cross-UCL committees and networks to further the aims of A&H. This will all be combined with commitment to the highest possible standards of service to students and colleagues. Please see the attached job description and person specification for full details. Please also read the attached Candidate Guidance document. When completing your supporting statement, please tell us, using examples, how you meet the essential criteria listed in the job description. The advert will close on Sunday 8 November at 23:59 GMT. Interviews for this role are scheduled to be held on Tuesday 17 November , in person at the Bloomsbury campus. This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 40% on site – during peak periods you may be required to be on campus to support staff and student events. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Ben Fowler, Director of of Education & Student Experience, benjamin.fowler@ucl.ac.uk If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the staffing team on ah-operations@ucl.ac.uk The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies-advice/ways-working to find out more. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. About you: The successful candidate will need to demonstrate the following criteria: Educated to degree level or have equivalent qualifications or substantial relevant experience Knowledge of Higher Education policy and current issues in Higher Education (within the UK, across Europe and globally). Clear understanding of the complexity of Higher Education in relation to (i) student learning, (ii) maintenance of academic quality and standards and (iii) the broader student experience Significant experience of managing quality assurance processes and systems and Performance Indicators, e.g. NSS/PTES/PRES scores to drive performance Significant experience of leadership within HE, including management of staff and resources, and building teams. Experience of finding solutions to a range of problems by applying sound judgment, empathy, and a strategic understanding of relevant issues. Experience of managing change (cultural, procedural and/or organisational) in a complex multi-stakeholder environment Excellent communication and negotiation skills to work with and through others. A clear understanding of equality and diversity issues as they relate to the student experience and how the issues can be addressed by the role-holder. Relevant and substantial experience of student support within an education setting and including a wide range of academic and pastoral support activities. Experience of the interface between these roles and central student services is also important. Please see the attached job description and person specification for full details. Please also read the attached Candidate Guidance document. When completing your supporting statement, please tell us, using examples, how you meet the essential criteria listed in the job description. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits some of which are listed below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian, and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49360": {
      "title": "Head of Finance Capability and Professional Development",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Finance; London; See advert text; Finance, Commercial and Estates (B28) Reference: B28-00396 About us: UCL is a world-leading, global top 10 university, with 11 academic faculties, around 17,000 staff and over 50,000 students. We deliver ground-breaking research and exceptional teaching, supported by professional services that enable our community to thrive. Our Finance function plays a vital role in supporting UCL’s strategic ambitions, financial sustainability and operational excellence. We are committed to building a skilled, collaborative and forward-looking finance profession that supports informed decision-making across the university. About the role: We are seeking an experienced and strategic Head of Profession – Finance to lead the development of finance capability, professional standards and career pathways across UCL. In this job, you will lead initiatives that strengthen professional capability, create meaningful career pathways and foster a culture of collaboration and continuous improvement across UCL. Your work will include overseeing finance training and apprenticeship provision, building partnerships with professional bodies and sector networks, and supporting workforce planning, talent management and professional standards. You will also lead initiatives that bring the finance community together, evaluate the impact of development programmes and ensure effective governance, quality assurance and use of resources. You will shape and deliver a Finance People Plan aligned with UCL’s institutional priorities, future operating model and People Plan. Leading a comprehensive programme of training and professional development, you will establish competency and career frameworks, develop a Finance Apprenticeship Programme and explore opportunities for a graduate trainee scheme. This is a terrific opportunity to shape the future of the finance profession at one of the world’s leading universities. A full job description is attached to this advert. About you: You will hold a recognised professional accountancy qualification, such as ACCA, CIMA or ICAEW, and bring substantial experience in financial management, finance operations or professional development within a large, complex organisation. You will have a strong understanding of professional qualifications, apprenticeship standards and training frameworks, with a proven track record of designing and delivering capability-building programmes. An accomplished leader and communicator, you will be confident influencing stakeholders at all levels, managing competing priorities and using data to drive improvement. You will bring experience of working with external training providers and professional bodies, alongside a commitment to equality, diversity and inclusion and a passion for developing people. Please read our job description, paying particular attention to the Essential and Desirable criteria, and ensure these are covered in your application. Please also take some time to outline why the role appeals to you. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent.We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49386": {
      "title": "Head of Operations",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; School of Education (B16) Reference: B16-02269 About us: Founded in 1902, UCL Institute of Education has been shaping policy and helping government, organisations and individuals navigate a changing society for the last 120 years. We embrace collaboration and excellence to create a future that is inclusive and just and have been ranked number one for education every year since 2014 in the QS World University Rankings by Subject. We are looking to appoint exceptional candidates to the role of Head of Operations – multiple posts available. The Head of Operations (HoO) is a strategic leader who partners with the Head of Department (HoD) to translate academic vision into operational delivery, aligning departmental goals with faculty and university priorities. You will lead day-to-day operations, manages budgets, contribute to resourcing decisions, and ensure high-quality support for teaching, research, and strategy. About the role: In this role you will anchor the leadership structure, working closely with the HoD day-to-day and ensuring operational consistency and strategic alignment. You will foster an inclusive, high-performing culture and drive collaboration, innovation, and continuous improvement. As a departmental leader, you will build strong cross-university relationships and contribute to strategic initiatives. About you: With relevant professional experience with a high standard of education, you will have excellent financial skills and experiencing in managing change. In addition, you will have evidence of successful line management and development of staff with proven team leadership and motivational skills. Your application form should address all the person specification points and should clearly demonstrate how your effectiveness, skills and experience meet each of the criteria. For further information please contact Lindsey Du Plooy: l.plooy@ucl.ac.uk What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro rata for part time staff) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49334": {
      "title": "Head of Workshops, Institute of Making",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07959 About us: University College London (UCL) UCL is a multi-disciplinary university with a population of over 18,000 staff and 51,000 students from 150 different countries. Degree programmes are provided in Arts and Humanities, Built Environment, Brain Sciences, Engineering Sciences, Education, Laws, Life Sciences, Mathematical & Physical Sciences, Medical Sciences, Population Health Sciences and Social and Historical Sciences. For more information see http://www.ucl.ac.uk/about The Institute of Making The Institute of Making is a unique organisation that explores, celebrates, and investigates materials and processes through a wide range of activities. From large-scale public events to ground-breaking academic research projects, our diverse outputs and many audiences are united by a shared passion for materials and making. On a day-to-day basis, we bring together students and staff from across UCL to provide a creative home in which to collaborate, innovate, contemplate and experience making from a wide range of disciplines. At the heart of the Institute of Making are our open-access facilities, which include multi-material workshops and the Materials Library. The Institute of Making opened in Bloomsbury in 2013 with a workshop offering a broad range of tools and machinery for inducted members to use. The Materials Library is an ever-expanding repository of some of the most extraordinary materials on earth, including shape memory alloys and self-healing materials, as well as some of the more ordinary materials that have also changed the world, like paper, beeswax, and copper. The Workshop and our Materials Library sit side-by-side, enabling users to experience first-hand the relationships between materials and tools that constitute processes of making. The space is also a stage for our expansive events programme and home to a multitude of research projects. In the autumn of 2023, we opened a second Institute of Making space on the Olympic Park in Stratford. Ten times larger than the Institute of Making Bloomsbury, the Stratford site has its own Materials Library and a suite of workshop facilities, including a large open-plan studio and multi-materials workshop. There is also a number of specialist rooms offering ceramics, CNCing, electronics, 3D printing and plastic processing, laser cutters, metalwork, textiles, and an experimental kitchen. The Institute of Making workshop team is responsible for establishing the safe and sustainable operation of the workshops and supporting our members in using the equipment and facilities across both the Bloomsbury and Stratford sites. The team’s main duties include supervising members’ access to the space, inducting new members, conducting tool training, advising members on design, materials, and processes, supporting the delivery of events, and contributing to the friendly, welcoming, imaginative, and open-minded atmosphere of the Institute of Making. About the role: Please note, this role is open to UCL internal applicants only. The post holder will oversee the portfolio of workshop-based activities across the entirety of the Institute of Making. They will lead a growing workshop team, including a Workshop Manager and several Workshop Technicians and Assistant Technicians, working across our Bloomsbury and Stratford sites. They will be responsible for the overall health and safety of the workshops in relation to equipment, staff, members, protocols, and training. They will also work closely with the Head of Events & Communications to ensure the safety of any visitor or participant in any workshop-based events. The post-holder will be collaborating internally with other area leads, managing the workshop team, and overseeing communications with members and potential members, as well as other UCL departments such as Estates, to ensure the smooth running of the facility. They will also answer any workshop or make related queries from externals such as other workshops, universities, makers, schools, researchers, or businesses and develop partnerships where appropriate. More generally, the Head of Workshops will be responsible for planning, implementing, and reviewing the strategy of the workshops and ensuring the overall alignment of the facilities with the work, ambitions, and ethos of the Institute of Making. About you: The successful internal candidate will need to demonstrate the following criteria: Provide strategic oversight of workshop activities, resources, staffing and budgets across both sites, ensuring operations remain responsive, effective and aligned with Institute priorities. Build strong relationships across the Institute, UCL and external partners, representing the workshop team and supporting collaborative projects, events and member engagement activities. Lead and support the workshop team, managing staffing, performance, training and resource allocation to ensure the effective delivery of services, projects and events. Oversee member communications, inductions and training, while promoting an inclusive workshop culture and supporting the growth and development of the membership community. Manage the day-to-day operation of both workshops, including administration, scheduling, maintenance, procurement and the effective use of facilities and resources. Ensure a safe and compliant workshop environment through effective health and safety leadership, staff training, risk management and adherence to UCL policies and procedures. Manage workshop budgets responsibly, identify funding opportunities and support income-generating activities that enhance workshop services and resources. Monitor and report on workshop performance, membership activity and operational outcomes, contributing to the Institute's annual reporting processes. Promote UCL policies, accessibility and inclusive practice, undertake required operational responsibilities, support occasional out-of-hours activities, and carry out other duties appropriate to the role. See the attached job description and person specification for full details. Please also read the attached Candidate Guidance document. When completing your supporting statement, please tell us, using examples, how you meet the essential criteria listed in the job description. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits some of which are listed below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian, and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49517": {
      "title": "HR People Partner",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; HR and Wellbeing; London; £56,431-£66,437​; People and Culture & PS Hub (B30) Reference: B30-00261 About us: UCL is London’s Global University – a diverse intellectual university committed to excellence, innovation, and inclusion. Our People and Culture division is a dynamic and service-driven team of over 160 professionals who collaborate closely with academic faculties and professional service divisions to ensure our people strategies meet the university’s needs. We are proud of our values-led culture and our commitment to removing barriers and actively promoting diversity and inclusion across our community. About the role: We have two full-time HR People Partner opportunities available on a hybrid basis, working from our Bidborough House, King's Cross office for a minimum of two days per week (including Monday as our HR anchor day, plus one additional day). One role will provide partnering support to a designated faculty. As their HR People Partner, you will influence and support leaders to deliver strategic people priorities, acting as a trusted advisor across your stakeholder group. You will help leaders navigate complex employee relations matters and use people data to inform decision-making. A key part of the role will be translating UCL's people strategy into practical solutions that enhance staff development, engagement, recruitment, and retention. Working across a broad range of strategic people priorities, you will support organisational change, workforce and succession planning, performance management and culture initiatives. You will act as the link between your stakeholders and our Centres of Expertise, bringing insight, support, and constructive challenge to ensure effective outcomes. You will also champion inclusive leadership and diverse talent pipelines, helping to foster a culture of wellbeing, continuous development, and high performance. You will develop a deep understanding of your faculty's culture, challenges, and ambitions, enabling you to provide informed guidance and tailored people solutions that make a meaningful impact. The second role is a unique opportunity for an experienced HR People Partner who thrives in variety and complexity. Supporting different areas of the organisation according to business need, the postholder will bring broad HR expertise, quickly understand local challenges, and confidently influence senior stakeholders to deliver effective people solutions. Both roles are excellent opportunities to build strong partnerships with senior academic and professional services leaders while supporting the delivery of strategic people priorities. If you have any queries regarding the role or the application process, please contact Passant Khalifa via p.khalifa@ucl.ac.uk About you: You are an experienced HR professional who thrives in complex matrix environments and can demonstrate impact through strategic thinking, sound judgement, and effective problem-solving. You have successfully managed complex employee relations cases, built credibility with senior stakeholders, and delivered people initiatives that improve engagement and organisational effectiveness. You are skilled at influencing and coaching leaders, building productive relationships, and using evidence-based insights to support decision-making. A confident communicator, you are comfortable operating in ambiguity and balancing risk with organisational priorities. You are committed to equity, diversity, and inclusion and understand how to embed these principles into everyday practice. You enjoy working with autonomy while collaborating closely with colleagues and centres of expertise, and you will ideally have experience within a large public sector, higher education, or similarly complex organisation. What we offer: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion ."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49372": {
      "title": "IMSS Research Fellow in Statistical Science",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07957 About us: The Department of Statistical Science at UCL is the longest established university statistics department in the world and has played a pioneering role in the development of the subject since its foundation in 1911. It is one of eleven departments in the UCL Faculty of Mathematical and Physical Sciences and has close links with many other departments, both within the Faculty and outside of it. We teach statistical science at all levels (undergraduate single/combined honours, service courses, MSc and PhD) and carry out research across a wide range of theoretical and applied areas. In the last Research Excellence Framework exercise (2021/22), over 97% of our output was classified as “world-leading” or “internationally excellent” in terms of originality, significance and rigor. We consistently score highly in the National Student Survey in the Postgraduate Taught Experience Survey. About the role: To carry out original independent research, under the direct supervision of the Principal Investigator for the selected project ( https://www.ucl.ac.uk/mathematical-physical-sciences/departments/statistical-science/research/project-vacancies ). Areas of research include data science, machine learning, biomedical and environmental statistics, causal inference and applied probability. The post-holder will produce work leading to conference presentations and publication in top journals for the relevant field. Appointees will be expected to contribute to the research environment of the department of Statistical Science through interaction with staff and students and other activities, e.g. by participating in and co-organising events such as the IMSS lecture. A strictly limited amount of teaching will also be required (around 20% of a typical academic teaching load to maximise time for research). Typical teaching contributions include support with tutorial/practical lectures, marking and MSc summer projects (co-)supervision. About you: The successful candidate must have a PhD in statistics, mathematics, or related discipline and a track record of international research excellence and publishing in the relevant discipline. The candidate must look carefully at the list of available projects ( https://www.ucl.ac.uk/mathematical-physical-sciences/departments/statistical-science/research/project-vacancies ) and liaise with the prospective principal investigator/supervisors . What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are: - 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) - Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) - Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan - On-Site nursery - On-site gym - Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay - Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service - Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49348": {
      "title": "Information Rights Manager",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Legal, Governance and Secretariat; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Office of General Counsel (B27) Reference: B27-00136 About us: The Data Protection and Information Rights team sits within UCL’s Office of General Counsel and is responsible for providing advice on a wide range of data protection and information rights information matters relating to the University. The Data Protection and Information Rights Team is headed by Alexandra Potts, the Director, Data Protection and Information Rights. Alexandra reports to the General Counsel, Natasha Lewis, who reports to the Provost, Dr Michael Spence. The Information Rights Manager is a business and technology focused role which requires ambition and focus to help drive through the transformation required by an evolving institution against a background of increasing regulatory focus, transparency, competition, globalisation and a rapidly evolving threat horizon, especially in the information security space. The Information Rights Manager will be a lead member of the University's Data Protection and Information Rights team within Office of General Counsel. The post holder will play a key role in managing UCL's day to day compliance with the UK GDPR, the Freedom of Information Act 2000 (FOIA), and the Environmental Information Regulations (EIRs). The Information Rights Manager will provide advice on all matters relating to data protection and freedom of information compliance to staff at all levels and will assist in providing training and guidance across UCL. The Information Rights Manager will report to the Head of Information Rights. About the role: Working within a team of data protection and freedom of information professionals, the Information Rights Manager is responsible for supporting and helping to advise the University’s Faculties,Schools, Institutes and Professional Service (PS) functions by providing expert, pragmatic, and targeted data protection and freedom of information support, guidance and training, across the University, in accordance with the relevant legislation, University policies, standard operating procedures and technical security standards. Drawing upon prior experience in delivering data protection initiatives he/she will take data protection, freedom of information and records management action items and work with academic and professional services stakeholders to ensure that these items are effectively delivered. Providing knowledgeable support on key data protection, freedom of information and records management processes, the Information Rights Manager will, under the direction of the Head of Information Rights, ensure that the attitudes and objectives of the baseline plan are translated into lasting and beneficial changes to the academic and business operation of the University. Duties and responsibilities The Information Rights Manager’s duties will include: Proactively support and evidence University data protection compliance within the University by working with academic and relevant Professional Services staff. Day-to-day responsibility for the provision of responses to subject access requests made to UCL including determining where exemptions should be applied to withhold information. The management of a dedicated support team for data protection and freedom of information. Day-to-day responsibility for the provision of responses to Freedom of Information/Environmental Information Regulations requests made to UCL including determining where exemptions should be applied to withhold information and considering the public interest test. Managing requests from regulatory bodies and law enforcement agencies, exercising judgement and escalating concerns as appropriate. Providing expert, targeted information security and data protection support, guidance and training across the University. Providing ongoing support as appropriate to relevant leadership and project managers as well as the Information Security Group to ensure that new projects are appropriately risk assessed. Providing leadership, direction and guidance in assessing and evaluating data protection risks and monitor compliance with security standards and appropriate policies. Understand how changing risk horizons may affect the University and provide expert direction on mitigation. Conducting regular meetings as appropriate with stakeholders to ensure data protection provisions are included within University contracts (including data sharing and processing agreements, contractual clauses, memoranda of advice, letters, policy documentation etc.) and to review University / IT Services changes to determine the impact, improvement and continual compliance with the GDPR, Data Protection Act 2018 and obtain evidence of compliance. Providing regular Data Protection and other Information Governance progress reporting and metrics to the Head of Information Rights. Maintaining awareness of the latest external data protection, freedom of information and records management issues and best practice. Actively supporting the aims and objectives of the Data Protection and Freedom of Information Office and act as an ambassador for the Data Protection and Information Rights team in every interaction with University stakeholders. Other relevant tasks deemed appropriate by the University. About you: Experience and knowledge Educated to degree level or equivalent. Relevant Information Rights management professional qualification or proven significant relevant experience. Proven track record of successfully managing a large numbers of requests and enquiries under the FOIA, DPA, EIRs in a similar role. Excellent analytical skills and sound judgement. Experience of building relationships and successfully collaborating with internal stakeholders. Experience of producing guidance, tools and resources for staff and members of the general public. Skills and abilities Experience of dealing with a range of issues relating to information rights on a self-standing basis and a willingness to react confidently and quickly to emerging issues. Excellent written and oral communication skills, with an ability to prepare reports, specifications and plans and present these to variable audiences. Proven ability to research, analyse and present information and argument in writing and verbally. In-depth and up to date working knowledge of the Freedom of Information Act 2000, the General Data Protection regulation and assorted data protection laws, the Environmental Information Regulations and the associated regulatory environment. Excellent organisational skills: ability to manage competing demands and time-critical schedules to meet deadlines. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: UCL is proud to have a diverse staff and student community. We particularly welcome applications from all sections of the community, regardless of age, disability, gender, gender identity or expression, race, religion or belief, or sexual orientation. We are committed to fostering an inclusive culture that promotes equality, values diversity and maintains an inclusive environment for all."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49294": {
      "title": "Learning Technologist",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11268 About us: The UCL Institute for Global Health (IGH) is at the heart of UCL’s Grand Challenge of Global Health. The Institute’s vision is for a world where international policy on global health is informed by world-class research. IGH aims to support the development of robust solutions to aspects of the world’s major global health challenges through scholarly outputs, education, public engagement, translational research, and ultimately by influencing public policy and professional practice. The UCL Faculty of Population Health Sciences (FPHS) brings together eight academic departments whose common aim is to explore and understand the underlying factors that cause ill-health and innovate to improve health worldwide. The Faculty is one of the two leading Population Health Schools in the UK and top among Russell Group universities as demonstrated by the Research Excellence Framework 2021 results. UCL is ranked 3 rd in the world for Public Health (ShanghaiRanking's Global Ranking of Academic Subjects 2021). We host more than 30 taught programmes, mainly for postgraduate students, and have around 400 students studying towards a PhD qualification. About the role: This is an exciting opportunity for a Learning Technologist to work in close collaboration with academic and professional services staff, taking a leading part in the design, development and implementation of the online education infrastructure and exceptional learning content for a flagship doctoral programme. The Learning Technologist is a dedicated role to support the new blended Professional Doctorate in Global Health (DrPH). This degree is being developed by the Institute for Global Health, in collaboration with the Faculty of Population Health Sciences, for its launch in the 2027/28 academic year. The DrPH is a well-recognised UK qualification designed to provide advanced public health, research, and leadership training to professionals pursuing careers across academia, public health agencies, and private sector roles. As the primary digital education specialist, your main purpose is to ensure an innovative, high-quality, and engaging online learning experience for the DrPH in Global Health, alongside other Institute for Global Health degrees and short courses. You will also serve as a champion for current and emerging educational technologies, providing expert guidance and training to programme staff. This role also involves a close partnership with central UCL teams to ensure consistent and coordinated support, and you will contribute your expertise to wider digital education strategies across the Faculty of Population Health Sciences. This is a full-time role (i.e. 36.5 hours per week) and it is available from March 2027 for two years. A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Salary range: £44,861 to £55,099 per annum (including London Allowance), depending on experience. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Sean Gainford on s.gainford@ucl.ac.uk If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the IGH HR Team on IGH.HR@ucl.ac.uk . About you: Qualifications: Education to degree level or equivalent experience Experience & Knowledge: Experience in designing and developing digital learning materials using associated platforms and software packages, including learning management systems, e-authoring tools, video, image, animation, design, and presentation applications. Experience of using Moodle or other virtual learning environments Knowledge of how web-based, multimedia, and mobile technologies can be used to enhance learning, teaching and assessment, especially in subject areas related to population health sciences. Experience providing training and effectively sharing technical knowledge to support others. Skills and Abilities A high level of skill in a variety of learning technologies for online learning. A methodical and accurate approach to work with excellent attention to detail. Ability to work collaboratively with colleagues and stakeholders and promote good working relationships. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49492": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching) in Applied Linguistics TESOL -Dissertation Supervisor",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; School of Education (B16) Reference: B16-02281 About us: Founded in 1902, UCL Institute of Education has been shaping policy and helping government, organisations and individuals navigate a changing society for the last 120 years. We embrace collaboration and excellence to create a future that is inclusive and just and have been ranked number one for education every year since 2014 in the QS World University Rankings by Subject. The Department of Culture, Communication and Media (CCM ) is a department of IOE, UCL’s Faculty of Education and Society. It is committed to excellence in teaching, research and consultancy in the areas of: Media and Cultural Studies, Art, Design and Museology; Academic writing; English education: Applied Linguistics; Music Education; Multimodality studies; Learning with Digital Technologies. About the role: In this post you will supervise MA dissertations in Applied Linguistics and TESOL and related areas. The main activities are as follows: Supervising 10 dissertations in Applied Linguistics, specifically in the areas of : language and identity, Discourse Analysis, Critical Discourse analysis, Language Policy, and Global Englishes. Supporting the running of the Dissertation module throughout the academic year, as required. Attending induction and standardisation meetings for the activities above as required. This post is available until 13 th August 2027 in the first instance. About you: You will have completed a doctorate in Applied Linguistics, TESOL, or a related discipline and have a good knowledge of foundational areas and paradigms in one or more of the following areas: language and identity, Discourse Analysis, Critical Discourse analysis, Language Policy, and Global Englishes. Your application form should address all the person specification points and should clearly demonstrate how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria. Informal enquiries can be directed to Miguel Perez-Milans: m.milans@ucl.ac.uk It is important that the criteria are clearly numbered and that you provide a response to each one. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro-rata for part-time staff) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49184": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching) in Bio ID (Maternity Cover)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; UCL East; £56,431-£66,437​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07888 About Us: The Bartlett is unrivalled in its breadth and depth of disciplines, programmes and departments. It is made up of 9 departments, which are constructed of 13 schools, Institutes, Centres and Units, providing teaching and research in a wide range of subject areas to over 4,000 students each year from across the world. The Bartlett is ranked #1 in the world for Architecture and the Built Environment and remains #1 in the UK for the eleventh year in a row, in the QS World University Subject Rankings 2025. The Bartlett School of Architecture is a world-leading place to study, teach and research architecture. The School is a key part of UCL, a top-world-ranking university, where architecture has been taught for over 175 years. With over 400 permanent staff, across four London sites, the school enjoys a rich reputation for cultivating innovative ideas in architecture. For further information about the School, please visit www.bartlett.ucl.ac.uk/architecture About the role: The Bartlett School of Architecture is seeking a Lecturer (Teaching) in Lab Bio-ID to support the delivery of teaching and research within the Bio-ID Laboratory at UCL Here East. You will deliver scientific skills teaching, practical sessions, tutorials and workshops, while supporting interdisciplinary student projects and research. You will also develop teaching materials, provide feedback and assessment, oversee laboratory health and safety, and manage the day-to-day operation of the lab, including equipment maintenance, procurement and inventory management. This is a full-time, fixed-term contract (1.0 FTE) for a period of 12 months to provide maternity cover. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Academic Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies/2021/jun/conditions-service-academic-staff for more information. About you: You will have a relevant postgraduate qualification or equivalent professional experience in biology, microbiology, biotechnology, biochemical engineering or a related discipline, alongside substantial hands-on wet lab experience. You will have strong knowledge of laboratory equipment and techniques, experience in experimental planning and biological research methods, and the ability to deliver practical teaching and supervise students and researchers. You will also have the skills to manage laboratory health and safety, assess and authorise risk assessments, and oversee lab operations, including equipment maintenance and procurement. Strong organisational, technical and record-keeping skills, as well as a collaborative approach and commitment to equality, diversity and inclusion, are essential. Application Process The advert will close on Monday 2nd November at 23:59 GMT , however we may close applications early if we receive a high volume of applications. Early application submission is recommended. As a minimum we will keep all adverts open for 2 weeks. Interview Date(s) are scheduled for: TBC A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Contact Details If you have any queries about the role, please contact brenda.parker@ucl.ac.uk. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR Team at bartlett.arch.hr@ucl.ac.uk. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our Faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49291": {
      "title": "Lecturer (Teaching) in Chemical Engineering",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £56,431-£66,437​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07909 About us: The Department of Chemical Engineering is routinely recognised among the very top departments in Europe and among the top 20 departments in the world in the QS World University Subject Rankings. The department has an extensive research portfolio across a wealth of areas, from the molecular scale to the scale of large industrial plants, at the heart of which lie chemical transformations by which raw materials are converted to products such as fuels, commodity and specialty chemicals, pharma-ceuticals, construction materials and consumer products. Our current awareness of the world’s finite material and energy resources demands that these products are obtained in a sustainable, efficient and environmentally acceptable fashion and that new energy solutions are implemented. Within this exciting and demanding context, the department develops innovative and impactful technological solutions that leverage advances in basic science addressing Global Grand Challenges in domains such as Advanced Manufacturing; Functional Materials; Health; and Energy, Water, CO2, Environment and Sustainability. UCL Department of Chemical Engineering is one of the top research and teaching departments in the UK and has world-class standing. The department offers undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, and has an extensive research portfolio across a wealth of areas, from molecular scale to complex systems. The department is one of the founding members of the Sargent Centre for Process Systems Engineering and is home to the EPSRC “Frontier Engineering” Centre for Nature Inspired Engineering (CNIE), and the Electrochemical Innovation Laboratory (EIL). For more information see www.ucl.ac.uk/chemeng About the role: The role holder will contribute to the development, planning and implementation of a high quality curriculum and will assist in the development of learning materials, preparing schemes of work and maintaining records to monitor student progress, achievement and attendance. The role holder will also be tasked with identifying, developing and implementing a range of teaching and learning initiatives, for example incorporating more blended learning into the curriculum and to pilot new assessment and feedback methods. Salary is £14,392.50-£16,043 per annum inclusive of London allowance (pro rata for 0.25 FTE). This is a fixed-term role for 3 months in the first instance. The role is available part time, extended term time only. This appointment is subject to the UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Academic Staff . About you: The successful candidate will be educated to degree level in Chemical Engineering, and will hold excellent knowledge of chemical engineering fundamentals. The post holder will have excellent people skills, with the ability to build good relationships with students, colleagues and external partners, whilst being able to work independently and as part of a team. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Enquiries should be addressed to Elton Rodrias ( elton.rodrias@ucl.ac.uk ). Any queries on the application process can be directed to Ms Katy Le Lion (Head of Operations) at chemeng.department.manager@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, please contact chemeng.safety@ucl.ac.uk . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our long-term commitment and 'beacon' status in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49349": {
      "title": "Legacy Giving Manager",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-President (Advancement) (C16) Reference: C16-00328 About us: We are UCL: a diverse community with the freedom to challenge and think differently. The world urgently needs solutions to diverse problems. UCL was founded 200 years ago on the generosity of a collective of people who believed in an institution that could drive forward those solutions. The world needed collective support then, and it needs it again now. In response, UCL has launched Here, it will happen, our biggest philanthropic and engagement campaign yet. Here, it will happen sets out our ambitions to solve global health, societal, climate and education challenges in UCL’s next century. Here, we have the determination to double down on our discoveries, translate our innovations into impact and inspire the citizens of the future. There has never been a more exciting time to join UCL Advancement. We are a team of exceptionally ambitious, creative and passionate individuals who work together to achieve remarkable things with a global impact, and we would love for you to be part of this exciting journey and our success. Here, it will happen. About the role: We are seeking a Legacy Giving Manager to join our Development Directorate at an exciting time for UCL Advancement, as we continue to grow our legacy fundraising programme and build lasting relationships with supporters who want to make a difference to future generations. Working closely with the Senior Legacy Giving Manager, you will play a key role in developing relationships with legacy prospects and supporters, encouraging legacy pledges and helping to secure future philanthropic income for UCL. You will manage legacy enquiries, develop tailored cultivation and stewardship activities, and contribute to engaging communications, events and supporter experiences. You will also support the delivery of the Gower Street Circle, UCL's legacy recognition programme, and collaborate with colleagues across Advancement and the wider university to identify opportunities to grow our legacy giving community. We are happy to consider applications to work on a part-time/flexible basis wherever possible (minimum 0.8 FTE – equivalent of 4 days per week, stated salary to be pro-rata). This role is eligible for hybrid working with a minimum of 20% on site. To find out more about the role, please contact Barry Hunt, Senior Legacy Giving Manager by email: b.hunt@ucl.ac.uk . Please use the attach cover letter upload option to tell us how you meet the essential and desirable criteria in the person specification. About you: We are looking for a confident and engaging relationship-builder who is passionate about philanthropy and the impact that legacy giving can have. You will bring experience of fundraising or income generation within higher education, the charity sector or a similar environment, alongside a proven ability to develop and maintain strong relationships with a wide range of stakeholders. An excellent communicator, you will be able to engage supporters with warmth, sensitivity and professionalism, adapting your approach to different audiences. You will be highly organised, comfortable managing competing priorities and able to identify opportunities, influence others and achieve positive outcomes. Experience of legacy fundraising or other forms of relationship-based fundraising would be particularly welcome, as would experience of delivering projects and working towards measurable objectives. We are pleased to accept applications from individuals who possess transferable skills and experience drawn from sectors outside of Higher Education. What we offer: We know that our people are our greatest asset and in return we provide an empowering environment where personal and professional growth is invested in. We offer agile working and flexibility around working hours. Working with some of the greatest intellects in the world, UCL also offers a generous pension scheme and holiday allowance, plus closure days and bank holidays. Visit www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49364": {
      "title": "Operations Administrator",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05697 About us: UCL is a world-leading, global university with more than 51,000 students from 150 countries and over 18,000 staff. We pursue academic excellence, break boundaries and make a positive impact on real-world problems. We are ambitious for change and committed to taking ever bolder and more impactful steps towards becoming a more diverse, equitable and inclusive university. Faculty Operations is a new division working across all 11 UCL Faculties. We help to improve faculty operations and ways of working, while preserving the academic excellence and flexibility that makes UCL distinctive. Working in partnership with Departments, Faculties and central services, we help translate UCL’s strategic priorities into practical operational delivery and bring greater consistency to the way we work. We are now looking for an organised and proactive Operations Administrator to join our Faculty Operations team and play an important role in delivering an effective, efficient and customer-focused operational service. About the role: As an Operations Administrator, you will provide day-to-day operational and administrative support, helping colleagues, students and visitors to have a positive experience at UCL. You will act as a key point of contact for routine operational queries, using your knowledge and judgement to resolve enquiries or connect colleagues with the right specialist teams and central services. You will work across a varied portfolio of activities, including maintaining administrative systems and records, organising meetings and committees, coordinating room and catering bookings, raising requisitions and invoices, and supporting people-related administration. You will also support internal events and communications, helping with everything from event logistics and liaising with speakers to drafting internal communications and maintaining SharePoint or website content. Alongside this, you will contribute to effective office and space management, including facilities, access, sustainability and workplace safety activities. This is a varied role where no two days are likely to be the same. You will have opportunities to support one-off projects and initiatives, contribute to process improvements and use data to help inform operational activity. You will also provide cover for colleagues and support the team during periods of increased workload. Please note, this role is offered on a full-time basis with hybrid working available at our London Kings Cross Office with currently a minimum of 2-days per week onsite (subject to change). This role does not meet the requirements for a Skilled Worker visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require a right to work in the UK. Job Ref: B01-05697 Closing Date: 03-11-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) Interviews: Interviews are intended to be scheduled on 11th Nov 2026 About you: We are looking for an experienced administrator who takes pride in providing an excellent service and enjoys working across a broad range of operational activities. You will be highly organised, proactive and able to manage competing priorities while maintaining accuracy and attention to detail. You will have excellent written and verbal communication skills and be confident building effective relationships with colleagues and stakeholders from a wide range of backgrounds. You will bring strong administrative, interpersonal and problem-solving skills, with the ability to work independently while also contributing positively to a collaborative team. You will be comfortable adapting to changing priorities and using your initiative to find practical solutions. You will have good IT skills, including experience using Microsoft Office, databases and web-based applications, and the ability to use and interpret data to support operational activities. Experience working in a Higher Education Institution or similarly complex organisation would be particularly valuable. Most importantly, you will share UCL’s commitment to inclusion and wellbeing, be comfortable working with people from different cultures and backgrounds, and demonstrate a methodical approach to organising and prioritising your work. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49280": {
      "title": "Operations Administrator - UCL Internal Applicants only",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07904 About us: The Department of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering is one of the largest Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering departments in the UK. The Department is proud to host internationally leading researchers covering a broad range of activities, spread over several sites. Our staff and students have a diverse range of interests and expertise, covering many areas of physics, biomedical engineering, medicine, physiology, computer science, and mathematics. Our focus is on excellent academic research in identifying new technologies and methods for diagnosis, treatment and management of diseases using medical imaging, physiological monitoring, and the development of implanted devices.We offer a range of undergraduate and postgraduate degrees and are home to over 700 students. These programmes include a range of departmental and cross-faculty modules, incorporating problem and scenario-based learning at all stages. This creates a highly engaging learning environment where students put their theoretical knowledge to practical application. About the role: To be responsible for delivering an effective and efficient day-to-day departmental operations service within the Medical Physics & Biomedical Engineering Department. The post holder will, where possible, make improvements to Medical Physics operations processes. A proactive, flexible and customer-focussed approach is required at all times to ensure a high level of service to staff, students and visitors About you: Deliver a range of departmental operations activities and applying operational knowledge using established processes and UCL systems, ensuring an efficient, effective and customer- focused service aligned to UCL policies. This includes using and interpreting data reports to support delivery of operations activities. For example, making room and catering bookings, coordinating general departmental posts and deliveries, and submitting service requests. • Act as the first point of contact for internal and external routine departmental operations queries. Respond or signpost to the appropriate departmental or faculty specialist functional teams and/or central UCL services and advise on departmental operations processes. • Organise departmental meetings including booking room, drafting agendas, taking minutes and following up on actions. • Maintain departmental administrative systems and records, ensuring efficiency and in accordance with UCL policies. • Provide flexible project administrative support as requested for one ‑ off and ad hoc tasks or projects, and departmental Athena Swan and EDI initiatives. • Contribute to the implementation of continuous improvement of processes and the rollout of new processes, procedures and systems. • Provide routine financial administrative support as agreed. For example, acting as named Purchasing card holder, submitting expense claims, raising requisitions, submitting new supplier requests, and assisting with monitoring of expenditure for agreed departmental finance accounts. • Provide routine people ‑ related administrative support as agreed. • Contribute to a positive and welcoming experience for interview candidates and visitors as required. For example, supporting the logistics for interviews and associated assessments and coordinating visitor access needs such as guest WiFi accounts and/or swipe cards What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact Medphys.HR@ucl.ac.uk The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working As the Service Simplification HRFRI Programme develops, the role may change and the postholder will be expected to adapt to any changes in processes, systems and ways of working Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49409": {
      "title": "OPM Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; £44,861-£55,099; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11429 About us: The Department of Imaging Neuroscience at UCL, home to the internationally renowned Functional Imaging Laboratory (FIL), has over 30 years of experience pioneering brain imaging techniques and advancing our understanding of human cognition and brain function. The Department hosts major collaborative research initiatives and provides an interdisciplinary environment with extensive national and international partnerships. Its state-of-the-art facilities include MRI and MEG scanners and wearable optically pumped magnetometer (OPM) technology, supporting innovative neuroscience research and the translation of cutting-edge imaging techniques into clinical practice. About the role: We are seeking a highly motivated individual to join Dr Tierney’s team at the FIL, contributing to the translation of next-generation brain imaging technology – optically pumped magnetoencephalography (OP-MEG) – from research into routine clinical practice. You will lead two core workstreams. The first involves acquiring and analysing prolonged OPM recordings from patients with epilepsy to improve seizure localisation and map critical functional brain networks. The second involves comparing conventional OPM systems operating within a magnetically shielded room (MSR) with a pioneering portable system that operates without one ( unshielded OPMs ). This research aims to overcome a key infrastructure barrier to the wider clinical adoption of OPM technology. The role offers an exciting opportunity to develop hands-on expertise in cutting-edge human neuroimaging within a collaborative research environment, contributing to advances that could improve patient care. The role is available from 01 December 2026 and funded by a grant from the Engineering and Physical Sciences Research Council (EPSRC) until 30 November 2029 in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). Informal enquiries regarding the role can be addressed to Dr Tim Tierney ( tim.tierney.12@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You will have a PhD, or be close to completing one, in Physics, Engineering or a related discipline, with experience in acquiring and analysing EEG, MEG or OPM data. You will have knowledge of control systems engineering, ideally including active shielding for OPM recordings, and strong programming skills in one or more languages such as MATLAB, Python, C, C++ or LabVIEW. You will also demonstrate creativity and independence in developing and deploying hardware solutions for brain imaging or stimulation systems. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills are essential, alongside the ability to work collaboratively in a multidisciplinary research environment, manage competing priorities and meet deadlines. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. What we offer: Starting salary offered in the range £47,186 - £55,099 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance. Appointment as Research Fellow is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be as Research Assistant (salary £40,603 - £43,749 per annum) with appointment as Research Fellow being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Gold award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49497": {
      "title": "ORE Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11359 About us: The UCL Eastman Dental Institute (EDI) is a world-leading, academic centre for postgraduate dentistry and translational research based in London, UK. The ORE Assistant is a member of the Professional Services Team. The Professional Services Team is led by the Institute Manager and is responsible for student recruitment, academic administration, human resources, marketing and communications, and finance. About the role: The ORE Assistant supports the development, implementation and delivery of the Overseas Registration Examination (ORE). This is a full-time, Grade 6 position offered on a fixed-term contract until 28 February 2031. The starting salary is £37,162–£43,749 per annum, inclusive of London Allowance. The salary offered on appointment will be determined based on experience. This role does not meet the requirements for a Skilled Worker visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require a right to work in the UK. UCL Terms and Conditions of Service: Research and Professional Services Staff A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. For an informal discussion about this role, please contact the recruiting manager, Kate Marr (kate.marr@ucl.ac.uk) If you are unable to apply online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR Manager, Khadija Bouzgan (hr-eastman@ucl.ac.uk). Find out about the UCL Ways of Working : www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working . About you: We are looking for an experienced Administrator with a service orientated outlook who is able to communicate effectively with a range of stakeholders. The successful candidate will have experience Servicing high-level committees, working groups and similar including meeting preparation as well as minute taking. They will be able to work in a busy and complex office environment. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49282": {
      "title": "Personal Assistant (PA) to Vice Deans and other senior staff",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07939 About us: University College London (UCL) UCL is a multi-disciplinary university with a population of over 13,000 staff and 42,000 students from 150 different countries. Degree programmes are provided in Arts and Humanities, Built Environment, Brain Sciences, Engineering Sciences, Education, Laws, Life Sciences, Mathematical & Physical Sciences, Medical Sciences, Population Health Sciences and Social and Historical Sciences. For more information see http://www.ucl.ac.uk/about UCL Engineering Engineering Sciences comprises ten academic departments that undertake research and teaching across a wide range of disciplines. Our scientists and engineers take discoveries from fundamentals to practice, producing innovative technological solutions to challenging problems. It is a dynamic and growing Faculty, with a strong vision and desire to use our talents to ‘Change the world’. For more information see www.ucl.ac.uk/engineering About the role: This role is fixed term until 30 June 2027 The PA will report into the Executive Assistant and is part of the administrative team collectively responsible for providing a high-quality administrative service to underpin the activities of the faculty office. The primary focus of the role will be managing diary arrangements efficiently, servicing committees and meetings as required, and supporting key activities within the faculty. The postholder will also provide secretarial and administrative support to Vice Deans and other senior academic staff and line manager as required. This will include diary management, arranging meetings and minute taking, organising and providing logistical support with events, supporting research and enterprise activities, and other administrative support. Please note: This role does not meet the eligibility requirements for a Skilled Worker Visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require right to work in the UK to carry out this role. About you: The successful candidate should be able to demonstrate: Proven experience of providing effective PA support in a complex organisation. Experience of working with limited supervision, taking responsibility for meeting service standards and agreeing targets and workloads. Ability to proof-read correspondence on behalf of others, ensuring accuracy and a close attention to detail. Proven consistent ability to meet tight individual and group deadlines and to manage one’s own workload through effective prioritising, time management and organisational skills. The ability to be discreet, sensitive and professional, and to maintain confidentiality when dealing with a variety of student and staff data. Please see the attached job description and person specification for full details. Please also read the attached Candidate Guidance document. All applications must include a supporting statement telling us, using examples, how you meet the essential criteria listed in the job description. Applications without a supporting statement will be rejected. Important note: Please do not attach any copies of certificates or research documents. If you are successful, these will be requested at a later date. If you have any queries about the role or application process, have any technical issues, or need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, please contact the staffing team at faceng.facultyhr@ucl.ac.uk What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits some of which are listed below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49403": {
      "title": "PhD Studentship",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11487 About us: The research group of Dr Ian Harrison ( Harrison Lab ) is seeking a PhD student to investigate how early-life surgery affects glymphatic function in the brain and whether these changes contribute to the development of dementia-related pathology later in life. The studentship will be primarily supervised by Dr Ian Harrison, based within UCL’s Division of Medicine at the Centre for Advanced Biomedical Imaging (CABI) . CABI is a world-leading facility for in vivo imaging of glymphatic function in preclinical models. The project will also be co-supervised by Dr Simon Beggs in UCL’s Department of Developmental Neuroscience. Located in a purpose-built facility in central London, CABI offers a range of specialised experimental imaging technologies and infrastructure. The successful candidate will join a multidisciplinary research environment at UCL, bringing together expertise in glymphatic biology, neurodegeneration and developmental neuroscience. With access to state-of-the-art imaging facilities and opportunities to develop advanced experimental skills, UCL provides an outstanding setting in which to investigate the mechanisms linking early-life experiences, brain health and neurodegenerative disease. About the role: The successful applicant will be a highly motivated and enthusiastic individual with, or nearing completion of, a master’s degree in neuroscience, biomedical sciences, physiology or a related discipline. They will have a strong interest in neuroscience, glymphatic function and neurodegenerative disease, and a desire to develop their research skills through an interdisciplinary PhD project. The student will investigate how early-life surgery affects glymphatic function in the brain and whether these effects contribute to the development or progression of dementia-related pathology later in life. The project will involve preclinical models and may incorporate a range of experimental approaches, including in vivo imaging, behavioural testing, histology and molecular analysis. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to develop expertise in these techniques while exploring potential mechanisms linking early-life experiences, brain clearance pathways and neurodegeneration. We are seeking a candidate with strong organisational, communication and interpersonal skills, a proactive approach to learning, and the ability to work both independently and collaboratively within a multidisciplinary research team. Previous research experience in neuroscience or a related field is essential. Above all, the candidate should demonstrate scientific curiosity, enthusiasm for the project and a willingness to learn new techniques and approaches. About you: We are seeking a curious, enthusiastic and highly motivated individual with a strong interest in neuroscience, brain ageing and neurodegenerative disease. The ideal candidate will be keen to explore how early-life experiences influence brain function and may contribute to the development of dementia-related pathology later in life. The successful applicant will have strong communication, organisational and problem-solving skills, a willingness to learn new experimental techniques, and the ability to work effectively both independently and as part of a collaborative, multidisciplinary research team. They will be able to think critically about scientific questions, demonstrate initiative and perseverance when faced with challenges, and communicate their findings clearly and effectively. A background in neuroscience, biomedical sciences, physiology or a related discipline is expected, and previous research experience in neuroscience or a related field is essential. Experience with animal models, imaging or laboratory-based research would be particularly valuable. Above all, we are looking for someone who is eager to develop as a researcher, embrace new challenges and contribute to research advancing our understanding of the mechanisms linking early-life experiences to neurodegenerative disease. What we offer: This is an opportunity to join a multidisciplinary team working across the Division of Medicine. As well as the opportunities this role presents, we also offer further benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49458": {
      "title": "Physics and Astronomy Post Graduate Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £36,433-£41,833; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07510 About us: Physics and Astronomy is recruiting a cohort of Postgraduate Teaching Assistants (PGTAs) to support education across the department starting from the next academic year (September 2026-27). The Physics and Astronomy Department is located in the heart of the historical area of Bloomsbury. Scientific research and study has been a strong feature of UCL since its inception in 1826 and the Department is currently one of the top ranked Physics departments in the country, and indeed the world. The department is ranked #4 in the UK for Physics Education and #25 in the world according to QS ranking. We have forged an internationally renowned reputation for being at the forefront of world leading research and providing high class teaching. Those joining us can expect to be part of a dynamic community of Physicists and Astronomers whose enthusiasm for science and innovation is exceptional. About the role: The purpose of this role is to support and work with the academic leads on small group teaching and learning across our programmes. Main duties of PGTAs are: Practical class teaching Laboratory class teaching Facilitate teaching sessions Maximising resources and teaching materials to aid students in their learning; Respond to queries referring to departmental tutors/module leads and/or central support services as appropriate; Monitor students’ online presence, engagement with their learning and their progress, returning data to the administrative office in a timely manner and following up where necessary by offering support and encouragement and/or raising with departmental tutors/module leads as appropriate; Marking in course student assessments Deliver formative feedback directly to students; Participation in regular meetings with the module leader to discuss arising issues and problems; Involvement in ad hoc meetings organized by module leaders or the PGTA Administrator/Rep.; To actively follow and promote UCL policies, including Equal Opportunities; To uphold confidentiality in regards to students records and marks; To engage with all training required to support the role. PGTAs will be given training and support to assist them in their roles. Please note this is primarily intended as an internal opportunity - only if there is no suitable PhD student at UCL identified would external applications from students at other universities be accepted and considered. This is the first round of our recruitment; there will be opportunities for additional work in Term 2 and Term 3. About you: The successful candidates will be pursuing a postgraduate degree, preferably associated with UCL Department of Physics and Astronomy or studying a related subject (e.g. Physics, Astrophysics, Natural Sciences etc.) Previous teaching experience is an advantage. To apply, please supply a CV and covering letter of application by 23.59 on Sunday, 2 August 2026. Please use the expression of interest form provided to indicate the type of work you are interested in. The covering letter must include the following: specification regarding the areas of teaching desired, relevant teaching experience, and a summary of your knowledge, skills and abilities as they relate to the person specification. Your CV should detail your completed degrees, details of any current employer, and details of recent past employment. All PGTAs must complete training within the UCL Arena One scheme, normally available in September. This will be in addition to guidance or mentoring provided by the department for their PGTAs. What we offer: The appointments will be made on the grade 6 scale , (pro rata) on a fixed-term basis. This exceeds the London Living Wage (LLW). PGTAs will be paid based on the requirements of the UCL PGTA Code of Practice for up to a maximum of 60 hours per module. Coursework marking and feedback is included in the standard contracted hours, whilst examination marking (where applicable) will be paid additionally and calculated on the number of scripts marked. Visit UCL Reward & Benefits to find out more about the benefits on offer. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49554": {
      "title": "Postgraduate Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £36,433-£41,833; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07407 About us: The Bartlett School of Environment, Energy and Resources (BSEER) is recruiting a cohort of Postgraduate Teaching Assistants (PGTAs) to support education across the department for the academic year 2026-27. BSEER is set within The Bartlett Faculty of the Built Environment , which has been continually ranked #1 in the world in the QS World University Rankings by Subject (Architecture and Built Environment) . As of 2026, we remain global #1 for the fourth year in a row and #1 in the UK for the twelfth year in a row. About the role: The purpose of this role is to support and work with the academic leads on small group teaching and learning across our programmes in our four Institutes; UCL Energy Institute , UCL Institute for Environmental Design and Engineering , UCL Institute for Sustainable Heritage , and UCL Institute for Sustainable Resources . Main duties of PGTAs are: tutorial class teaching regular meetings with the module lead/lecturers provision of feedback to students provision of weekly office hours attendance reporting coursework assessment examination marking PGTAs will be given training and ongoing support to assist them in their roles. Please note this is primarily intended as an internal opportunity - only if there is no suitable PhD student at UCL identified would external applications from students at other universities be accepted and considered. In the event we receive a high number of applications, we may close the vacancy ahead of the published closing date. As a minimum we will keep all adverts open for 2 weeks. Early application submission is therefore recommended. About you: To be eligible for PGTA work, you must be registered as a current PhD student, either full or part time. A document outlining all the available vacancies is attached; as part of your application, you will be asked to specify which roles you would like to be considered for. Please complete the online application form summarising your relevant technical/specialist skills and any previous teaching experience, relating to the specific modules you would like to be considered for. If you have any queries about the role or about the application process, or, if you need any reasonable adjustments to assist you in your application, please contact the BSEER HR Team at bseer-recruitment@ucl.ac.uk . What we offer: Visit UCL Reward & Benefits to find out more about the benefits on offer. The UCL Ways of Working supports all staff to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit Ways of Working | UCL Human Resources - UCL – University College London to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49487": {
      "title": "Postgraduate Teaching Assistant - SRI",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Teaching, Teaching Support and Examination Support; London; £36,433-£41,833; School of Education (B16) Reference: B16-02053 About us: Founded in 1902, UCL Institute of Education has been shaping policy and helping government, organisations and individuals navigate a changing society for the last 120 years. We embrace collaboration and excellence to create a future that is inclusive and just and have been ranked number one for education every year since 2014 in the QS World University Rankings by Subject. Post Graduate Teaching Assistant – SRI The Social Research Institute is one of the leading centres for social research in the UK. It is home to seven internationally renowned research units: Centre for Longitudinal Studies Centre for Time Use Research CLOSER EPPI Centre Quantitative Social Science Social Science Research Unit Thomas Coram Research Unit About the role: We are looking to recruit PGTA's to support a variety of modules within SRI for the next academic year, for up to 180 hours. In this post you will: • Assist in the preparation of materials to support teaching, including liaising with the Module Leader regarding handouts for sessions, preparing practical exercises, preparing notes for assignments, etc. • Undertake first and/or second marking (as required) for student formative and summative assessments; generating and providing detailed feedback for students. • Co-ordinate any attendance monitoring activities for the groups you are supporting. • Engage in the collection of evaluation data and student feedback from the groups you are supporting to enable module reviews and improvement. About you: In this role you will • Receive appropriate support in developing your teaching practice, mentoring and departmental guidance in relation to your seminar support role. • Receive guidance relating to small group facilitation, assessment and giving feedback. • You'll also have the option of attending an extended course, the UCL Arena One Teaching Associate Programme, and then applying for an Associate Fellowship of the Higher Education Academy. You'll currently be a PGR student or registered as a PGR student by the start of the relevant academic year, with demonstrable sound knowledge in a subject area relevant to the programme to be supported. PLEASE NOTE THAT UCL PGR students will be given priority in recruitment. Please ensure that you clearly specify on your application form the modules that you are applying for. Your application form should address all the person specification points and should clearly demonstrate how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro-rata for part-time staff) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s global university, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and for grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. For a full job description and to apply for this role, visit UCL’s online recruitment portal https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49467": {
      "title": "Professor in Process Intensification",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Academic; London; £80,000+; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07936 About us: The Department of Chemical Engineering is routinely recognised among the very top departments in Europe and among the top 20 departments in the world in the QS World University Subject Rankings. The department has an extensive research portfolio across a wealth of areas, from the molecular scale to the scale of large industrial plants, at the heart of which lie chemical transformations by which raw materials are converted to products such as fuels, commodity and specialty chemicals, pharma-ceuticals, construction materials and consumer products. Our current awareness of the world’s finite material and energy resources demands that these products are obtained in a sustainable, efficient and environmentally acceptable fashion and that new energy solutions are implemented. Within this exciting and demanding context, the department develops innovative and impactful technological solutions that leverage advances in basic science addressing Global Grand Challenges in domains such as Advanced Manufacturing; Functional Materials; Health; and Energy, Water, CO2, Environment and Sustainability. UCL Department of Chemical Engineering is one of the top research and teaching departments in the UK and has world-class standing. The department offers undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, and has an extensive research portfolio across a wealth of areas, from molecular scale to complex systems. Over the past few years, the department has been undergoing rapid growth, and is the home of the EPSRC “Frontier Engineering” Centre for Nature Inspired Engineering (CNIE) ( www.cnie.org.uk ), the Electrochemical Innovation Laboratory ( http://www.ucl.ac.uk/electrochemical-innovation-lab ), and several consortia supported by the European Commission. For more information see www.ucl.ac.uk/chemeng . About the role: The post holder will develop a programme of original research and produce publications, as well as other research output. The successful candidate will carry out and publish high quality research, securing research grant funding to support your research programme. The post holder will develop and lead both national and international partnerships with industry and academic institutions. The successful candidate will also be expected to teach at undergraduate and postgraduate level. The Professorial Banding Structure can be found here . This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Academic Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/policies/2021/jun/conditions-service-academic-staff for more information. About you: The successful candidate will be educated to an undergraduate and postgraduate degree level in Chemical Engineering, with a strong research expertise to make significant contributions at the forefront of international research. The post holder will have experience in supporting PhD/EngD students, early career researchers. Knowledge of the fundamental principles of the discipline and experience in teaching undergraduate and postgraduate in Chemical Engineering is essential. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Relocation Supplement UCL currently supports a relocation supplement of up to £10,000 may be payable where it is necessary to relocate from within the UK, and up to £20,000 if from outside of the UK, to take up an appointment at UCL. For more information, please go to the UCL website – Relocation scheme . Enquiries should be addressed to Professor Eva Sorensen (Head of Department) at e.sorensen@ucl.ac.uk . Any queries on the application process can be directed to Ms Katy Le Lion (Head of Operations) at chemeng.department.manager@ucl.ac.uk . Application process You can upload two documents with your application; please ensure this includes: A full CV with a complete publication list; A single document including: A list of major research achievements (max 1 page); A starred list of your top five publications together with a short description of their significance (max 100 words each); A description of the research plans you propose to pursue at UCL, which should include how the proposed research would contribute to the department’s research profile (max 2 pages); A description of your teaching experience to date (max 1 page). A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our long-term commitment and 'beacon' status in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49306": {
      "title": "Programme Grant Coordinator",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07963 About us: We are seeking to appoint a highly motivated Programme Grant Coordinator to support the EPSRC-funded Programme Grant: Fundamental Science in the Age of Quantum Computation (FSQ). FSQ is an ambitious five-year, multi-institutional research programme bringing together world-leading researchers from UCL, Cambridge, Leeds, Nottingham and Oxford, together with international academic and industrial partners including Google, IBM and Quantinuum. The programme aims to understand the fundamental limits of classical and quantum computation, develop new algorithms for both classical and quantum computers, and harness these advances to transform discovery science. The Programme Grant Coordinator will play a central role in ensuring the successful delivery of this nationally significant research programme. The postholder will work closely with the Programme Lead and investigators across all partner institutions to establish and manage the administrative, financial and governance structures necessary to support the Programme’s research, networking, impact and outreach. The London Centre for Nanotechnology (LCN) is an interdisciplinary enterprise between University College London, King’s College London and Imperial College London. In bringing together world-class infrastructure and leading nanotechnology research activities, the Centre aims to attain the critical mass to compete with the best facilities abroad. The LCN has strong relationships with the broader nanotechnology and commercial communities. As the world’s only such facility to be located in the heart of a metropolis, the LCN has superb access to corporate, investment and industrial partners. It is at the forefront of training in nanotechnology and has a strong media presence aimed at educating the public and bringing transparency to this emerging science. This role will be offered on a part-time basis at 0.4 FTE until 12th September 2031. There is flexibility around the days and hours worked and we will be happy to coordinate with other part time roles in UCL. This will be discussed with the successful candidate. About the role: The postholder will have responsibility for the operational, financial and administrative management of the Programme Grant, and will work closely with the Programme Lead and investigators to ensure its successful delivery. The postholder will establish and manage administrative structures and governance processes, oversee financial management and reporting, coordinate communications and impact activities, support recruitment and personnel matters, and facilitate engagement across a large network of UK and international collaborators. The role requires a high degree of initiative, independence and judgement, together with the ability to build effective relationships across institutional boundaries and support the delivery of a complex and high-profile research programme. About you: The ideal candidate will have excellent organisational, financial, and communication skills, with experience coordinating complex projects and supporting senior stakeholders. Comfortable working with budgets, reporting requirements, and governance processes, you will be able to build strong relationships and drive programme delivery in a fast-paced research environment. Please refer to the JD for more information. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Bronze award, in recognition of our long-term commitment and 'beacon' status in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49406": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Mauri Lab)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11430 About us: The post holder will be based in the Institute of Infection, Immunity and Transplantation (IIIT; https://www.ucl.ac.uk/immunity-transplantation/ ) on the Royal Free campus, in the Pears Building. The IIT brings together a critical mass of more than 25 world-class immunology research groups working at the forefront of translational immunology. Work within the IIT aims to translate advances in the understanding of the immune system to improved therapies for patients.The Institute provides an effective infrastructure for immunology/virology research and a lively and sociable environment, always striving for excellence. About the role: The Mauri group specialises in B regulatory cells in the context of autoimmunity. We have developed a novel IL-10 fate reporter mouse strain that allow us to track regulatory B cells to study their ontology and function in inflammatory disease models. The Mauri group uses these models to establish the contribution of B cells and B regulatory cells on autoimmunity, antibody response and age-related immune defects. The position holder will explore the role of IL-10 experienced B cells function in inflammatory disease models. The project will provide an important understanding of how the B cell compartment influences immune responses such as autoimmunity and aging. The position is available from November 2026 for 6 months at least. About you: The post-holder will be expected to perform the highest quality research under the direction of Professor Mauri and senior members of the team. Applicants should enjoy working effectively with other group members in a multidisciplinary team, including molecular, cellular immunologists and bioinformaticians. The successful candidate will be competent in immunology and cellular immunology techniques, including the dissection of the peritoneal cavity and spleen from mice and the preparation of single cell suspensions of these tissues. Please see the attached job description and person specification for full details. Please also read the attached Candidate Guidance document. If you have any queries about the role or application process, technical issues, or need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, please contact the staffing team at hr.ii@ucl.ac.uk. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49528": {
      "title": "Research Assistant (Part-time)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11460 About us: The mission of the UCL GOS Institute of Child Health is to maximise and advocate for the holistic health of all children, young people and the adults they will become, through world-class research, education and public engagement. The UCL GOS ICH, together with its clinical partner Great Ormond Street Hospital for Children, forms the largest concentration of children’s health research outside North America. The 2024-29 GOS ICH strategy focuses on its five scientific programmes. GOS ICH’s activities include active engagement with children and families, to ensure that our work is relevant and appropriate to their needs. GOS ICH generates the funding for our research by setting out our proposals in high quality applications to public, charitable and industrial funding bodies and disseminates the results of our research by publication in the medical and scientific literature, to clinicians, policy makers and the wider public. The Institute offers world-class education and training across a wide range of teaching and life learning programmes which address the needs of students and professional groups who are interested in and undertaking work relevant to child health. GOS ICH holds an Athena SWAN Charter Gold Award. About the role: This work is part of a project entitled “Protecting language function in children undergoing brain surgery” and funded by the Great Ormond street Hospital Children’s Charity. The project aims to optimise and develop a new collection of functional MRI (fMRI) tasks/techniques for language mapping (to identify brain regions responsible for language function) in children who are undergoing brain surgery and speak more than one language. The post therefore represents an excellent opportunity for a postgraduate-level researcher to further develop their skills in advanced MRI and to gain experience working in a multi-disciplinary environment involving cognitive neuroscientists, MRI specialists, paediatric neurologists and radiographers. The role involves the design and analysis of new fMRI tasks, but also volunteer recruitment and data collection. This is a part-time (0.5 fte) post available until 30/06/2027, in the first instance. About you: You have a postgraduate degree in MRI, Neuropsychology or similar, and have a strong interest in developmental neuroscience. You have excellent communication skills, both written and oral, and you are able to work effectively as part of a multidisciplinary team. Ideally, you will have experience of MRI and data governance, as well as experience of task-based functional MRI. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/ If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact Madhur Sharma on ich.hr@ucl.ac.uk quoting job reference"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49391": {
      "title": "Research Assistant / Data Scientist in Artificial Intelligence for Computational Cancer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; £37,162-£43,749; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11399 About us: This post is based in Professor Gert Attard’s Treatment Resistance group at the UCL Cancer Institute and Dr. Tapabrata Rohan Chakraborty’s Transparent and Reliable Lab (TRAIL) in UCL Dept of Medical Physics and Biomedical Engineering. The UCL Cancer Institute is a world-leading centre for cancer research, bringing together more than 400 scientists and clinicians who work collaboratively to understand cancer and translate discoveries into improved diagnostics, treatments and patient outcomes. Professor Attard’s research group utilises bioinformatic analysis, computational modelling, and digital histopathology to interrogate treatment resistance in prostate cancer, and he leads several of the world leading trials in prostate cancer, including STAMPEDE, that have changed clinical practice multiple times. Dr. Chakraborty’s research group TRAIL is a computational lab that develops state of the art explainable and trustable AI/ML methods applied to grand challenges in personalised healthcare and precision biomedicine, particularly multimodal computational cancer problems. UCL Medical Physics & Biomedical Engineering is a world-leading multidisciplinary department that develops innovative technologies spanning medical imaging, biomedical engineering, healthcare technologies, artificial intelligence and computational medicine. Cancer is a complex, multi-scale disease involving interacting processes across the molecular, cellular, tissue and organ levels. At the same time, advances in genomics, molecular profiling, pathology, imaging and clinical data collection have generated unprecedented volumes of multimodal cancer data. A major challenge is to integrate these diverse data sources into coherent computational frameworks that can generate biological insights, support clinical decision-making and accelerate therapeutic discovery. We are seeking a highly motivated and talented Data Scientist (Grade 7) in AI for computational cancer to join an ambitious and multidisciplinary research programme focused on constructing and validating transparent, interpretable, and biologically grounded models directly from multimodal cancer datasets. About the role: The successful candidate will lead the research, generation, and exploration of novel multi-modal AI models, intended to deepen our understanding the underlying relationships between different forms of computational cancer data - including digital histopathology, immunohistochemistry, and genomics - generated from tissue samples collected as part of prostate cancer clinical trials. The researcher will contribute to fundamental advances in our understanding of cancer biology while supporting the development of innovative approaches for patient stratification, therapeutic target discovery, treatment optimisation and precision oncology. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to publish in leading journals, present their work at major international conferences and contribute to the development of transformative AI technologies with significant scientific and clinical impact. This is an exceptional opportunity for an ambitious AI researcher to develop and apply cutting-edge AI technologies to challenging real-world problems in biomedicine. For more details about the Attard Group, please visit: Treatment Resistance | Faculty of Medical Sciences This post is funded for 1 year in the first instance, with potential of extension subject to availability of funds. Applications should include a CV and a Cover Letter: In the Cover Letter, please provide evidence of the essential and desirable criteria in the Person Specification part of the Job Description. About you: You will have a strong computational background, with an MSc or equivalent in Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning, Data Science, Computer Science, Engineering or a related discipline, together with experience developing and applying AI and machine learning methods to complex research challenges. You will possess excellent programming skills, particularly in Python and modern machine learning frameworks such as PyTorch or TensorFlow, and have experience in areas such as computer vision, natural language processing or multimodal AI. You will be an analytical and innovative researcher with excellent problem-solving skills and, ideally, experience applying AI to healthcare or computational cancer research. Knowledge of cancer biology, multimodal biomedical data, genomics, imaging or digital pathology would be advantageous. The successful candidate will be highly organised, able to work independently and committed to producing high-quality, reproducible research. You will have strong communication skills and enjoy collaborating with clinicians, biologists, data scientists and researchers from diverse backgrounds. Most importantly, you will be passionate about applying cutting-edge AI technologies to advance cancer research and improve patient outcomes. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49301": {
      "title": "Research Assistant- 0.4FTE",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £36,433-£42,891​; School of Education (B16) Reference: B16-02280 About us: Founded in 1902, UCL Institute of Education has been shaping policy and helping government, organisations and individuals navigate a changing society for the last 120 years. We embrace collaboration and excellence to create a future that is inclusive and just and have been ranked number one for education every year since 2014 in the QS World University Rankings by Subject. The Social Research Institute is one of the leading centres for social research in the UK. It is home to seven internationally renowned research units: Centre for Longitudinal Studies Centre for Time Use Research CLOSER EPPI Centre Quantitative Social Science Social Science Research Unit Thomas Coram Research Unit All of our centres are multidisciplinary, with staff drawn variously from economics, sociology, social policy, demography, psychology, anthropology and social statistics. We promote problem-solving interdisciplinary research on particular themes where we have outstanding scholarship and critical mass, e.g. gender, families, work, inequalities, migration, bio-social interactions. About the role: We are looking for research assistants to work on the project “Applied stereotypes, social networks, and self-fulfilling prophecies: How stereotypes reinforce social inequalities” (STERN) . STERN is a five-year research project, now starting its second year, funded by the European Research Council and led by Zsófia Boda. The team also includes a postdoctoral researcher and two PhD students. We are a multidisciplinary team, with backgrounds ranging from sociology to education to psychology. The team aims to develop a measurement of applied stereotypes – the way people apply the stereotypes they hold to others – and collect empirical data in secondary schools in England. The project will analyse how applied stereotypes of peers emerge, and how they affect educational outcomes and well-being of pupils. We are looking for two or three research assistants to support the preparation for, and implementation of, data collection planned for autumn 2027. Each position will be between 0.2 and 0.4 FTE (equivalent of one to two days per week), depending on the availability of the successful applicants. In this post you will work closely with other team members on a range of tasks, based on the needs of the project and, where possible, your individual interests and expertise. You will support the development of the questionnaire, including potentially developing new measures; the selection of the sample; recruitment of participating schools; and data collection. Research assistants who are interested in developing their own research are also welcome to participate in the academic activities of the research group, and they may they have the opportunity to conduct their own research projects using the project data. About you: You will have a master’s degree in a relevant social science discipline (sociology, social psychology, education, behavioural science, or related field), or equivalent research experience. You will also have experience in quantitative research methods and an interest in social inequalities, social networks, or stereotypes. Experience using statistical software for quantitative data analysis, with a willingness to develop proficiency in R, is essential. Your application form should address all Person Specification points and clearly demonstrate how your skills and experience meet each of the criteria. It is important that the criteria are clearly numbered and that you provide a response to each one. This opportunity is offered until 30th September 2027 in the first instance. For further information contact Zsófia Boda: zsofia.boda@ucl.ac.uk What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday: 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days (pro rata for part time staff) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme (pro-rata for part-time staff) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49477": {
      "title": "Research Data Steward",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Administration and Business Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11435 About us: The Unit of Lifelong Health and Ageing at UCL (LHA) is part of the Department of Population Science and Experimental Medicine (PSEM), in the UCL Institute of Cardiovascular Science. The UCL Institute of Cardiovascular Science is a world-class centre of excellence in pioneering novel, integrative strategies in preventative and therapeutic cardiovascular medicine, and forms part of the UCL Faculty of Population Health Sciences (FPHS). The FPHS is rich in whole-of-life data on health-related variables captured on thousands of people. About the role: This new post represents an exciting opportunity to join the existing data management team to take forward management of the research data for the oldest and longest running British birth cohort study, The MRC National Survey of Health and Development (NSHD). The NSHD (https://nshd.mrc.ac.uk/) has informed UK health care, education and social policy for more than 70 years by following individuals throughout their lives. These involve multiple surveys, together with other specialised forms of data collection (for example physical measurements, biological samples and imaging), and linkages to health and administrative records. The mission of LHA is to identify biological and social factors that affect lifelong health, ageing and the development of chronic disease, and to use findings to design and test interventions that enhance healthy ageing. This role provides an excellent opportunity for an individual interested in the effective management and sharing of survey and biomedical research data. With a good understanding of, and the skills required for, the preparation of complex longitudinal data for research purposes, you will optimise data pipelines and integration in the research relational database, and will process, de-identify, curate and document the research data collected by LHA, planning this work in consultation with the LHA Director and ensure that the work progresses to schedule. This post is available for 2 years in the first instance. About you: You will have an MSc in a quantitative science discipline or equivalent professional experience, along with proven experience in data collection, research data and metadata management from a longitudinal study, preferably of social science or health data. You must have experience in questionnaire design, managing data collection tools such as paper questionnaires and computer assisted interviews and be able to use statistical software such as SPSS and STATA to perform quality control checks and clean newly collected data. You will have experience of managing and documenting research data and metadata, supporting data sharing and data discoverability activities, preparing and publishing metadata to external data discovery platforms and maintaining the study's presence across key research infrastructure services. The suitable applicant will have an interest in data management which includes automating, streamlining and improving data pipelines using Python or any other programming language, designing and managing relational databases and be able to use WordPress to manage, update the content on the website. You will also have interest in FAIR principles and metadata management which will include generating metadata for newly collected data according to both in-house and external database schemas and standardising legacy metadata for key longitudinal variables. Experience using version control tools such as Git and working with large or complex research data holdings would be advantageous. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments (transcripts, your publications, or other reports) as these will not be considered by the selection panel. Shortlisted applicants will be required to undertake tasks at interview that demonstrate their expertise and skills. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, UCL offer some great benefits, some of which are: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 UCL closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49424": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £44,861-£55,099; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11439 About us: The Department of Neuromuscular Diseases is a hub for clinical and research excellence across the spectrum of spinal cord and neuromuscular diseases. Based within the department the Cortese Laboratory focuses on the discovery and modelling of novel causes of inherited neuropathies and other neurodegenerative diseases, with particular interest in repeat expansion disorders and conditions caused by variations in non-coding DNA. One of the main commitments of the lab is the further elucidation of the mechanism and development of treatment for RFC1 CANVAS. About the role: This is an exciting opportunity to join the Cortese Laboratory as a Research Fellow, contributing to the development of novel therapeutic strategies for RFC1 CANVAS and related repeat expansion disorders. Building on recent findings demonstrating the potential of CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing to restore RFC1 expression and function, you will investigate complementary therapeutic approaches, including gene reprovision, CRISPR-based activation of endogenous RFC1 and small-molecule strategies to increase RFC1 expression. You will also develop engineered cellular models and CRISPR-based reporter systems to identify genetic and pharmacological modifiers of RFC1 expression through targeted and high-throughput screening, with promising candidates validated in patient-derived and isogenic iPSC-derived neurons. Working closely with the ARUK UCL Drug Discovery Institute and the Genetic Therapy Accelerator Centre, you will contribute to an innovative translational research programme combining molecular and cellular biology, genome engineering and drug discovery. The role offers considerable scope to develop new experimental approaches, shape the direction of the project and advance potential therapeutic strategies for RFC1 CANVAS. The role is available from 01 November 2026 and is funded by an ERC award for one year in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). Informal enquiries regarding the role can be addressed to Professor Andrea Cortese ( andrea.cortese@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You will have a PhD or equivalent in Molecular Biology, Neuroscience, Genetics, Cell Biology or a relevant discipline, with strong background in molecular and cellular biology, and particular expertise in CRISPR genome engineering, molecular cloning and human iPSC models. You will also have a good publication track record and be able to independently design, optimise and troubleshoot molecular and cellular experiments, demonstrating scientific rigour, analytical thinking and problem-solving skills. You will have the ability to supervise students and junior researchers effectively and to contribute positively to a collaborative research environment, as well as excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to work effectively as part of a multidisciplinary team. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. What we offer: Appointment as Research Fellow is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be as Research Assistant (salary £40,603 - £43,749 per annum) with appointment as Research Fellow being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Gold award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles ."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49307": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £44,861-£55,099; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11119 About us: The mission of the Research Department of Epilepsy is to transform the lives of people living with epilepsy for the better by identifying, understanding and correcting or preventing the underlying mechanisms leading to the epilepsies and associated comorbidities in each individual person. Our vision is to build upon the excellence and breadth of our research, seamlessly integrating basic and clinical science. The post will be based in the Experimental Epilepsy Group which sits within the Department; research within the Group spans across fundamental mechanisms of vesicle fusion and synaptic transmission, ion channel biology, experimental models of epilepsy, the function of neural circuits, and neurological diseases modelled in rodents and flies. About the role: You will use molecular and cell biology tools to develop and characterize novel AAV-based gene therapies in iPSC-derived neurons. The project is central to a BBSRC-funded programme of research in collaboration with Royal Veterinary College aimed at optimizing gene therapy for refractory epilepsy, to test them in a clinical trial in drug-resistant epileptic dogs. The post is available immediately and is funded by the BBSRC until 31 August 2027 in the first instance. If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, or have any queries regarding the application process, please contact the Institute of Neurology HR Team ( ion.hradmin@ucl.ac.uk ). Informal enquiries regarding the role can be addressed to Professor Gabriele Lignani ( g.lignani@ucl.ac.uk ). A full job description and person specification for this role can be accessed below. To apply, please upload a current CV, complete the online application form, and use the supporting statement section or upload a cover letter to outline how you meet the essential and desirable criteria for the role. Please do not upload any additional attachments as these will not be considered by the selection panel. About you: You’ll have a PhD (or equivalent) in Neuroscience or a related discipline, coupled with knowledge of viral vectors and experience of iPSC-derived neuron protocols, molecular biology techniques, basic bioinformatic analysis, and neurological disease modelling. Excellent communication, interpersonal, and problem-solving skills and IT proficiency at advanced user level (i.e., with electrophysiological and imaging data processing and statistical analysis programs) is also essential for this role. This role meets the eligibility requirements for a skilled worker certificate of sponsorship or a global talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. Therefore, UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. What we offer: Appointment as Research Fellow is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be as Research Assistant (salary £40,603 - £43,749 per annum) with appointment as Research Fellow being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits, some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan On-site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Institute prides itself on operating in an all-inclusive environment irrespective of personal, physical, or social characteristics. Teamwork is highly valued, individual strengths are recognised and celebrated, and we are committed to advancing the careers of everyone. 12% of Institute staff are actively working on Equality, Diversity and Inclusion (EDI) initiatives; visit our EDI website for more information about our initiatives and priorities. The Institute also holds an Athena SWAN Gold award , in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality. As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce; these include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, LGBTQI+ and gender diverse people in all roles, and women in Grade 9 and 10 roles."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49388": {
      "title": "Research Fellow",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11382 About us: The UCL Cancer Institute is a world-leading centre for cancer research at University College London, bringing together over 400 scientists and 150 PhD students dedicated to advancing the understanding and treatment of cancer. As part of the CRUK City of London Centre, the Institute collaborates with leading research organisations including Barts, King's College London, and the Francis Crick Institute to drive innovation in cancer research. The Treatment Resistance Team, led by Prof Gerhardt Attard, focuses on identifying and overcoming treatment resistance in prostate cancer. The group has been integrating liquid and solid biopsy multiomic analysis to discover novel markers for patient stratification and to track tumour clone dynamics in patients progressing through multiple lines of treatment. The overall aims are to improve the management and detection of prostate cancer patients and to discover novel drivers of resistance in multiple cancer types. About the role: The main purpose of the job is to provide expertise and support for projects concerned with genomic, epigenomic and transcriptomic analysis of liquid biopsies and solid tumours within the Treatment Resistance Team. Applicants should have extensive hands-on experience at a professional level performing next-generation sequencing workflows and analysis with experience in long-read sequencing and single cell analysis workflows being desirable. Applicants should have a PhD or in the process of completing their PhD. Applicants must have demonstrable hands-on experience processing patients’ samples, using databases, working to SOPs, extraction of nucleic acids from liquid biopsies and tumour samples as well as basic knowledge of programming and prostate cancer. We are seeking a highly motivated candidate who is confident in working independently and where excellent organizational and record-keeping skills are essential. Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be at Research Assistant Grade 6B, with payment at Grade 7 being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. Applications should include a CV and a Cover Letter: In the Cover Letter please provide evidence of the essential and desirable criteria in the Person Specification part of the Job Description. (By including a Cover Letter, you can leave blank the 'Why you have applied for this role' field in the application form, which is limited in the number of characters it will allow.) The role is available for 18 months in the first instance with the possibility of extension. About you: You will be a highly motivated and organised researcher with a PhD, or be close to completing a PhD, in a relevant biological or biomedical discipline. You will have substantial hands-on experience in next-generation sequencing workflows, together with a strong understanding of genomic analysis and cancer biology. Experience working with patient-derived samples, nucleic acid extraction and sample management systems will enable you to contribute effectively to a range of translational research projects. You will be confident using programming tools such as R or Python to analyse sequencing data and will be familiar with working within regulatory and quality frameworks relevant to biomedical research. A methodical approach to record keeping, data management and laboratory processes will be essential. As a collaborative and proactive team member, you will have excellent organisational skills and the ability to manage multiple priorities while working both independently and as part of a multidisciplinary research team. You will be committed to maintaining high research standards and contributing positively to the wider activities and culture of the department. Experience of long-read or single-cell sequencing technologies would be advantageous. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49382": {
      "title": "Research Fellow (Staff Scientist)",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11381 About us: The UCL Cancer Institute is a world-leading centre for cancer research at University College London, bringing together over 400 scientists and 150 PhD students dedicated to advancing the understanding and treatment of cancer. As part of the CRUK City of London Centre, the Institute collaborates with leading research organisations including Barts, King's College London, and the Francis Crick Institute to drive innovation in cancer research. ( www.ucl.ac.uk ) We are seeking a highly motivated and skilled Research Fellow (Staff Scietist) to join Bayraktar lab on 2D/3D spatial and single cell transcriptomic analysis of cancer-nervous system interactions. Professor Omer Bayraktar is a newly appointed group leader at the UCL Cancer Institute and his team explores brain cellular diversity in the health and disease using cell atlassing technologies. The Bayraktar lab is known for developing computational tools for spatial transcriptomics and for creating multiomic atlases of human brain disorders spanning brain cancer, autism and neurodegeneration. About the role: The position is part of a Cancer Grand Challenge Project (Team InteroCANCEption) in Cancer Neuroscience ( https://www.cancergrandchallenges.org/interocanception ). An international consortium across 9 labs, this project has the ambitious goal to harness the nervous system to halt tumour progression. To this end, Bayraktar team and collaborators are characterising cancer-nervous system interactions in genetically engineered mouse models and patient tumours using cutting-edge cell atlassing technologies. Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be at Research Assistant Grade 6B, with payment at Grade 7 being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. Applications should include a CV and a Cover Letter: In the Cover Letter please provide evidence of the essential and desirable criteria in the Person Specification part of the Job Description. (By including a Cover Letter, you can leave blank the 'Why you have applied for this role' field in the application form, which is limited in the number of characters it will allow.) The role is available for 2 years in the first instance with the possibility of extension. About you: You will be a highly motivated researcher with a PhD, or equivalent experience, in neuroscience, biology, genetics, cancer biology, developmental biology or a related discipline. You will bring a strong background in neuroscience together with substantial hands-on laboratory experience in spatial transcriptomics, including both assay development and the application of imaging-based spatial omics technologies. You will have experience working with tissue samples and cell culture systems, ideally including human or mouse iPSC differentiation, and will be confident using fluorescence microscopy and spatial transcriptomics approaches to generate high-quality data. Basic competency in the analysis of single-cell, spatial or imaging datasets using Python, together with experience managing sample and metadata records, will enable you to contribute effectively across the experimental workflow. You will be an organised and collaborative scientist, capable of managing multiple priorities while working closely with experimental and computational colleagues. With excellent communication skills and the ability to contribute to reports, publications and presentations, you will thrive in a multidisciplinary research environment. Experience in molecular biology, tissue or organoid culture, viral work or gene editing would be advantageous. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49522": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Algal Biomineralisation and Excitability",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £44,861-£55,099; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11372 About us: The Division of Biosciences at UCL is one of the world's foremost centres for research and teaching in the biological sciences. We have an outstanding international reputation and a community of over 500 staff and more than 300 PhD students engaged in cutting edge research and collaboration. Further information can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/biosciences This is an exciting opportunity to join the McClelland lab, which studies how marine algae build mineral structures and how electrical signalling at the cell membrane shapes their physiology. You will join a small, interdisciplinary team that combines algal cell biology with electrophysiology, advanced imaging, structural biology, geochemistry and mathematical modelling. Ideas and methods move freely between disciplines. About the role: You will investigate biomineralisation and electrical excitability in marine algae, applying specialist expertise in one of three areas: electrophysiology (patch clamp and sharp electrode), fluorescence lifetime imaging (FLIM), or cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM). You will design and carry out experiments, analyse and publish results, and present your work within the group and at conferences. Alongside the core project, you will be encouraged to develop your own research ideas. You will get extensive support to apply for independent fellowship or grant funding, including mentoring and feedback on applications. Salary: £ 46,261 – £ 54,019 per annum, Grade 7. Depending on experience. This is a fixed-term post until 30 th September 2027, for up to 12 months depending on the start date. Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon PhD award. If not yet awarded, initial appointment will be at Grade 6B, with backdating to Grade 7 upon submission of PhD thesis. Visa sponsorship This role meets the requirements for a Skilled Worker certificate of sponsorship or Global Talent visa under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. UCL welcomes applications from international applicants who require a visa. For guidance on eligibility, see Gov.uk Skilled Worker Visa guidance Flexible working and conditions We will consider applications to work on a part-time, flexible, and job share basis wherever possible. UCL Terms and Conditions of Service: Research and Professional Services Staff About you: You will hold a PhD (or have submitted your thesis) in a relevant discipline, with extensive hands-on experience of algal biology, including culturing algae and manipulating them in experiments. You will also have extensive demonstrable expertise in at least one of: • electrophysiology, including patch clamp and sharp-electrode recording; • fluorescence lifetime imaging microscopy (FLIM); • cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM). You will have a publication record appropriate to your career stage. You will be able to work independently while contributing to a collaborative, interdisciplinary team, and you will communicate your work clearly to specialist and non-specialist audiences. Experience with calcifying or silicifying marine algae, quantitative data analysis or programming (e.g. Python, MATLAB), or writing funding applications would be an advantage. Key Applicant Information Closing date: 25/10/26 at 23:59 GMT (applications may close early if volumes are high, so early submission is recommended). Interviews scheduled for: TBC Job description and person specification available at the bottom of the advert. Queries about the role: contact Dr Harry McClelland [h.mcclelland@ucl.ac.uk]. Please use the Subject line: PDRA enquiry-xxxxx (replacing xxxxx with your name). Queries about the process, accessibility, or reasonable adjustments: contact the Faculty HR Team on fls.hr@ucl.ac.uk What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits: As well as exciting opportunities, UCL offers a range of great benefits. Find out more: UCL Rewards and Benefits . ‘Why UCL?’ the impact UCL has had in the world and the impact people can make working with us. The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – visit UCL Ways of Working to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy Our Faculty holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity, and Inclusion here: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion ."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49304": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Brain Vascular Biology and Brain Cancer Biology",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11358 About us: The UCL Cancer Institute is a £40 million investment in central London based at University College London (UCL), one of the world’s top universities and a founding member of the Francis Crick Institute. The Institute draws together over 400 scientists working together to develop world-class basic and translational cancer research Information about the UCL Cancer Institute can be found on our website https://www.ucl.ac.uk/cancer/ The Group of CNS Angiogenesis and Neurovascular Link / Brain Vasculature and Perivascular Niche headed by neurosurgeon-neuroscientist Associate Professor Dr Thomas Wälchli is one of the laboratories of the University College London (UCL) Cancer Institute in the Department of Oncology of the University College London (UCL). The overarching vision of the research of Associate Professor Dr Thomas Wälchli, MD-PhD, FMH neurosurgery is to understand how the brain vasculature is regulated during brain development, in the adult healthy brain, and in various vascular-dependent brain diseases such as brain tumours and brain vascular malformations. The UCL Cancer Institute is a £40 million investment in central London based at University College London (UCL), one of the world’s top universities and a founding member of the Francis Crick Institute. The Institute draws together over 400 scientists working together to develop world-class basic and translational cancer research. About the role: The Group of Brain Vasculature and Perivascular Niche focuses on vascular growth and the development of blood vessels ( angiogenesis ) in the brain , including endothelial cells and perivascular cells of the neurovascular unit/perivascular niche, and on brain vascular heterogeneity in development, health and disease: The main aims are: i) to understand the cellular and molecular underpinnings that govern the growth of the developing, adult, and diseased human brain vasculature at single-cell resolution, ii) to uncover the single-cell transcriptomic, genomic and epigenomic landscapes and spatial biology of the developing, adult and diseased human brain vasculature, iii) to elucidate how developmental programs regulate vascular growth/angiogenesis in the human brain tumour vasculature (patho-fetal axis / onco-fetal axis), iv) to unravel the onco-fetal programs of vascular growth/angiogenesis and immunosuppression/immunomodulation, and v) to translate these fundamental insights about the onco-fetal axis in the human brain (tumour) vasculature into clinical settings with the ultimate goal to identify novel therapeutic strategies for human brain tumours (and for other vascular-dependent diseases such as human brain vascular malformations). The role will be available for one year in the first instance with the possibility of extension. Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be at Research Assistant Grade 6B, with payment at Grade 7 being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD thesis. Applications should include a CV and a Cover Letter: In the Cover Letter please provide evidence of the essential and desirable criteria in the Person Specification part of the Job Description. (By including a Cover Letter, you can leave blank the 'Why you have applied for this role' field in the application form, which is limited in the number of characters it will allow.) About you: The successful candidates : Have a PhD in bioinformatics/single-cell genomics/computer science (AI), neuroscience, vascular biology, developmental biology, cancer biology, immunology, biology/biomedicine, pharmacy, biomedical engineering, or related fields with ample research experience Have strong skills in working in the fields of bioinformatics/single-cell genomics and/or spatial multi-omics, neuroscience, vascular biology, developmental biology, cancer biology and immunology and/or experience in biotech, intellectual protection, etc. are an extra-added value Have a proven publication record with at least one first author publication in peer-reviewed international journals. Have experience in: (i) integrating single-cell and spatial multi-omics; (ii) computational programming in R, Python (and other common computer languages); (iii) competence/interest in analysing and integrating complex single-cell and spatial multi-omics- and clinical datasets are essential . will be an ambitious researcher, motivated to apply his/her skills to diverse (medical) research projects and enjoy working in a collaborative, dynamic, vibrant and fast-paced team environment. Have excellent organizational and supervisory skills, you can work in a team as well as independently. Have a strong ability to multi-task, to meet timelines, to work accurately; you are stress-resistant. Have outstanding written and verbal communication skills in English. Are able to identify priorities, and to find solutions for complex or difficult tasks. Have excellent organizational and record-keeping skills Have experience in presenting complex scientific concepts and content both in writing and verbally Have a strong commitment to enhance our fundamental understanding of human brain vascular biology and to improving outcomes for brain tumor patients Demonstrate excellent collaborative interpersonal skills with an ability to work co-operatively in a highly inter- and multidisciplinary setting. Have the ability to assist other members of the team, supervise MSc and PhD students and collaborate with other research groups. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance For rewards and benefits at UCL please visit: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49436": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in CAR-T Analytics",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; School of Life & Medical Sciences (B02) Reference: B02-11421 About us: The UCL Division of Surgery and Interventional Science is a collaborative and cross-disciplinary environment where clinicians, engineers, allied health professionals and researchers are constantly striving for innovative approaches to treatment. This includes developing highly effective methods which go beyond the boundaries of conventional wisdom and treating each patient as an individual. As a world leader in medical and biomedical research we can see at first-hand the directions in which medicine and science are moving and where patient and industry needs are growing. This knowledge ensures that we are producing and teaching some of the most innovative master’s courses for clinicians and scientists across the world. Additionally, our cutting-edge research expertise ensures that the latest techniques are taught using the most advanced equipment. Finally, we offer innovative master’s courses led by world leading experts, providing a truly research-based learning experience delivered by some of the greatest minds in science and medicine today. The Division reaching across three London campuses (Bloomsbury, Royal Free and Stanmore) brings together three major research departments: Targeted Intervention, Surgical Biotechnology and Orthopaedics and Musculoskeletal Science. Within these departments are world class research centres, specialising in many different surgical and interventional specialties. The Division also offers teaching and training in a wide range of subjects with intercalated BSc options for UCL medical students, direct MBBS teaching, undergraduate and postgraduate taught and research programmes for both clinicians and non-clinicians. The Division holds a silver award for the Athena Swan Charter which recognizes advancement of gender equality and aims for representation, progression, and success for all. Further information on the Division can be found at https://www.ucl.ac.uk/surgery/ About the role: You will be employed on a Versus Arthritis Translational Award led by Professor John Counsell, developing in vivo CAR-T cell therapy for lupus and inflammatory arthritis using novel UCL lentiviral vector technologies, including targeted envelopes, and proprietary expression backbones. You will lead the in vivo preclinical work package, in which T cell-targeted vectors are administered intravenously to PBMC-humanised NSG mice to generate CD19 CAR-T cells directly within the animal. You will compare targeting envelopes and vector backbones for CAR-T generation efficiency, persistence, biodistribution and CD19+ target cell elimination. The post is funded from 1 November 2026 to 31 October 2027 at Grade 7, spine point 32. You will work closely with the Research Fellow leading vector development and ex vivo human cell validation, and with clinical collaborators in UCLH Rheumatology. Other • The advert will close on 4th November at 23:59 GMT, however we may close applications early if we receive a high volume of applications. Early application submission is recommended. • Interview Date(s) TBC • If you have any queries about the role, please contact Professor John Counsell on j.counsell@ucl.ac.uk • If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact the HR Team on l.oppong@ucl.ac.uk • The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. Closing an advert early: In the event we get a high number of applications, we may close the advert early before the published closing date. As a minimum we will keep all adverts open for 2 weeks. ​​​​​​​ About you: The postholder must have a PhD (awarded, or submitted/near completion) in molecular biology, bioengineering, gene therapy or a closely related discipline. Hands-on experience of lentiviral vector engineering, production, purification and titration. Experience of vector retargeting, including engineering of envelope glycoproteins and pseudotyping. Experience of CAR potency assays (antigen-specific cytotoxicity/killing assays, cytokine release). Publications and/or conference presentations in gene therapy, vector engineering or immunotherapy would be advantageous. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Silver award, in recognition of our commitment and demonstrable impact in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49375": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Computer-aided Design and Discovery of MOFs for Adsorption Applications",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; UCL East; See advert text; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07806 About us: UCL Department of Chemical Engineering is one of the top research and teaching departments in the UK and has world-class standing. The department offers undergraduate and postgraduate programmes, and has an extensive research portfolio across a wealth of areas, from molecular scale to complex systems. About the role: The successful candidate will develop and operate high-throughput computational screening workflows for MOFs, integrating molecular simulation, automated structure generation and large-scale materials databases. The post holder will lead computational research on the design, screening and optimisation of MOFs for target adsorption applications. In addition, the successful candidate will perform advanced molecular simulations of adsorption, including quantum calculations, grand-canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) and molecular dynamics (MD), to characterise and predict the performance of candidate materials. Salary is £47,186-£ 50,942 per annum inclusive of London allowance. This is a fixed-term role until 31st of December 2029 in the first instance. About you: The successful candidate will hold (or shortly expect to obtain) a Ph.D. in Machine Learning, Physics, Chemistry, Chemical Engineering, Materials Science or a related quantitative discipline (or equivalent industry experience), together with an undergraduate degree (2(i) minimum) or M.Sc. in a relevant field. The post holder will have demonstrable experience with atomistic/molecular simulation methods, alongside knowledge of nanoporous materials (e.g. MOFs, zeolites or porous polymers) and adsorption processes for gas capture, with experience or knowledge of material informatics and the analysis of large materials datasets. Demonstrable experience or knowledge of computational modelling and data-driven approaches, including machine learning methods, optimisation techniques, and relevant programming skills is essential. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance A job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. If you have any queries about the role, please contact p.moghadam@ucl.ac.uk . If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact chemeng.safety@ucl.ac.uk . The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our long-term commitment and 'beacon' status in advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49423": {
      "title": "Research Fellow In Gravel Coast Science",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; UCL SLASH (B03) Reference: B03-03330 About us: University College London (UCL) is one of the world's top universities. Based in the heart of London, it is a dynamic, outward-looking institution. At its establishment in 1826, UCL was radical and responsive to the needs of society, and this ethos – that excellence should go hand-in-hand with enriching society – continues today. The UCL Department of Geography is a leading UK and global department, with a heritage of excellence in research, renowned for its innovative and interdisciplinary research to understanding complex global challenges. It brings together expertise in physical and human geography to explore critical issues such as climate change, sustainable development, and social justice. The department fosters an interdisciplinary approach, equipping students and researchers with the skills to critically engage with the complex interactions between people, places, and environments. About the role: We are seeking a short-term postdoctoral researcher to support on-going work on the NERC funded Gravel barrier resilience in a changing climate (#gravelbeach) project. The project is expanding and improving our knowledge and understanding of gravel barrier systems, particularly in the context of the UK where we have a wealth of these systems and where they play a vital role in natural flood and coastal erosion risk protection. Applicants will have experience and proven skillsets in coastal science, gravel sedimentology, data science, and machine learning applications to morphosedimentary data. The full job description and person specification can be accessed at the bottom of this page. This is a part time (0.2 FTE), fixed term post with funding available for 10 months starting 2nd November 2026. Funding is available at Grade 7, spine point 32 -33 (including London Allowance). Informal enquiries may be made to Prof Helene Burningham ( h.burningham@ucl.ac.uk ) If you have any queries regarding the application process, or if you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online, please contact the department’s research administration team: geog.research@ucl.ac.uk About you: Applicants will have a PhD in costal science relating to gravelly shorelines as well as be able to demonstrate : Experience in collecting, processing, and analysing heterogeneous coastal sediments across multiple grain size classes Experience in collecting, processing, and analysing near-sensing data, including using e.g. RTK GPS, UAS, TLS Experience and expertise in coding data science and machine learning pipelines for the analysis and segmentation of near-sensing data Please see the full job description and person specification at the bottom of this page full more information. This appointment is subject to UCL Terms and Conditions of Service for Research and Professional Services Staff. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/human-resources/conditions-service-research-teaching-and-professional-services-staff for more information. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave plus 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: The Department is committed to the values of equality, diversity and inclusion and we have an active Equality, Diversity and Inclusion Committee, and networks for BME, LGBTQ, and disabled/neurodiverse staff and students. The Department recognises the impor tance of family - friendly working, and we try, as far as possible, to organise meetings and events with staff responsibilities as parents and carers in mind. In 2022, the Department was awarded an Athena SWAN Silver award in recognition of our advancement of gender equality: representation , progression, and success. Above all the Department aims to be a supportive environment for both its staff and students. More information can be found here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/social - historical - sciences/geography/about/equality - diversity - and - inclusion"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49530": {
      "title": "Research Fellow in Multisensory Design",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Research and Research Support; London; See advert text; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07977 About us: UCL’s Department of Computer Science (CS) is a top-ranked Computer Science Department in the UK. In the 2021 Research Excellence Framework (REF) evaluation, UCL Computer Science was ranked second in the UK for research power and first in England. The post is based within the Multi-Sensory Devices (MSD) Group, which conducts interdisciplinary research spanning Human-Computer Interaction, multisensory interfaces, haptics, sensory technologies, interactive systems and emerging applications in healthcare and human-centred computing. About the role: The post is based within UCL Computer Science's Multi-Sensory Devices (MSD) Group and will contribute to interdisciplinary research developing novel multisensory interactions, interfaces and experiences. The Research Fellow will design, develop and evaluate interactive prototypes integrating sensory modalities such as vision, sound, touch, smell and taste, including multisensory XR applications where relevant. The postholder will work across interaction design, software-hardware prototyping, sensory-device integration and empirical user research, collaborating with researchers and external partners to advance multisensory HCI research. Appointment at Grade 7 is dependent upon having been awarded a PhD; if this is not the case, initial appointment will be at Grade 6B with payment at Grade 7 being backdated to the date of final submission of the PhD Thesis. The role is funded until 31st August 2028 in the first instance. About you: You will have a PhD (or be near completion) in Human-Computer Interaction, Computer Science, Interaction Design, XR/VR or a closely related discipline, with strong expertise in user-centred interaction design and experimental research methods. You will have experience developing interactive applications or research prototypes, excellent programming and prototyping skills, and experience integrating software with interactive hardware, sensors or sensory devices. You will also have experience designing and conducting empirical user studies, analysing research data and communicating findings through high-quality scientific publications. We particularly welcome candidates with experience in multisensory interfaces, sensory technologies or XR/VR applications, who can work independently and collaboratively within interdisciplinary research teams. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer great benefits. Please visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. Our department holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our long-term commitment and 'beacon' status in advancing gender equality."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49452": {
      "title": "Research Fellow, SHIFTIN Project",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £44,861-£55,099; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07915 About us: We are The Bartlett, UCL’s global faculty of the built environment. Individually, our schools and sections lead their fields. In partnership, they develop new responses to pressing world issues. As a whole, they represent a world-leading, multidisciplinary faculty, united by the radical spirit of UCL. Our vision is of a world where everything that’s built aims to add to the well-being of people and the environment. The Bartlett is unrivalled in its breadth and depth of disciplines, programmes, and departments. It is made up 12 Schools, Centres, and Institutes, providing teaching and research in a wide range of subject areas to over 4,000 students each year from across the world. Further information about The School can be found online. About the role: University College London (UCL) wishes to appoint a Research Fellow who will support the delivery of the SHIFTIN project, by undertaking research on circular reconstruction, sustainable construction materials and resilient transport infrastructure, using network and/or complex systems analysis where appropriate. The post holder will be based at The Bartlett School of Sustainable Construction at the Bartlett Faculty of the Built Environment, a world-leading faculty ranked #1 in the world for Built Environment studies and #1 in the UK for its research environment. The post holder will carry out research and programme administration within the Bartlett School of Sustainable Construction, consistent with the vision and mission of The School. This is a full-time role on a 1-year fixed-term contract, with an opportunity for extension subject to funding. About you: The successful applicant will be expected to have strong analytical and problem-solving skills, including the ability to analyse complex experimental, monitoring or infrastructure-performance data using appropriate computational, systems and network, geospatial/GIS, uncertainty, AI or data-management methods ; strong organisational skills, including the ability to plan research activities, coordinate contributions, manage priorities, and deliver high-quality outputs to agreed deadlines. The successful applicant should have effective written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to communicate complex research clearly and accurately to academic, technical, policy and project audiences, good IT and data management skills relevant to computational research and a proven ability to work both independently and collaboratively with multidisciplinary partners, including researchers, industry organisations, infrastructure practitioners and public-sector stakeholders. The appointee is required to have a PhD in Civil Engineering, Structural Engineering, Materials Engineering, Transport Infrastructure Engineering, or a closely related discipline. They will also have experience of supporting or contributing to collaborative research projects or European Union-funded research programmes. The successful candidate will be able to work harmoniously with staff, project partners and stakeholders of all cultures and backgrounds and possess excellent stakeholder engagement and/or public engagement skills. Your application will be assessed based on how well the criteria set out in the person specification are met using examples to demonstrate this. Please read the Candidate Guidance document below. A cover letter is required which addresses the criteria in the person specification. For further details including a job description and person specification, please see the attachment at the bottom of the page. https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs . What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here: https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49384": {
      "title": "Research Finance Manager",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Finance; London; £56,431-£66,437​; Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement) (B09) Reference: B09-01430 About us: UCL is a world-leading research-intensive university, with one of the largest and most diverse portfolios of externally funded research and innovation in the UK. Research and Innovation Services (RIS) supports UCL's academic community across the research and innovation lifecycle, providing specialist expertise in research funding, contracts, compliance and award management. Within RIS, Award Services works in partnership with academic and Professional Services colleagues, collaborating organisations and funders to support the effective management of research funding throughout the project lifecycle. The Post Award Operations team plays a critical role in supporting the financial stewardship of UCL's extensive research portfolio, helping to ensure research funding is managed effectively, responsibly and in accordance with institutional and funder requirements. Through its work, the team helps safeguard funding, support world-leading research and enable researchers to focus on delivering impactful outcomes. About the role: Please note we have two opportunities available one a permanent role and one a fixed-term maternity cover which will be around 12 months. If you could indicate in your application if you are interested in the permanent role, maternity cover or both. As Research Finance Manager, you’ll lead a team of Research Finance Administrators responsible for the financial administration and management of a portfolio of research and innovation awards. This is an exciting opportunity to play a key role in supporting one of the UK's largest and most complex research portfolios. You'll work across a broad range of funders, disciplines and award types, helping to ensure effective financial management while supporting UCL's strategic research ambitions. As research funding continues to grow in scale and complexity, you'll contribute to strengthening post-award operations, enhancing financial oversight and improving the services that support our academic community. The role combines team leadership, financial oversight and stakeholder management, ensuring awards are actively monitored throughout their lifecycle and that financial risks, compliance requirements and operational issues are identified and addressed effectively. This includes oversight of reconciliations, claims, budgets and forecasts, financial reporting, funder requirements and the timely financial closure of awards. Alongside managing and developing a team, you'll provide expert advice on complex post-award matters and build strong relationships with academic departments, faculties, funders and colleagues across UCL. You'll also have the opportunity to contribute to service development and continuous improvement initiatives, helping to strengthen financial processes, controls, management information and ways of working across a service that is evolving to meet the changing needs of a growing research portfolio. About you: You’ll bring significant experience of post-award financial management within a research-intensive university, with experience managing a broad portfolio of research and innovation awards from a range of funders. This should include international awards and foreign currency management. Strong financial and analytical skills are essential, including experience of reconciliation, forecasting, identifying trends and exceptions, and resolving financial discrepancies. You’ll also understand the importance of funder terms and conditions, institutional policies and regulatory requirements within research funding. An experienced people manager, you’ll be comfortable setting service standards, developing colleagues and creating a supportive, high-performing team environment. You’ll be able to explain complex financial information clearly, provide specialist advice to a range of stakeholders and build trusted relationships with colleagues at all levels. Strong organisational and digital skills, attention to detail and the ability to manage competing priorities will be important, alongside a proactive approach to identifying solutions and continuously improving the service provided to UCL’s research community. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49316": {
      "title": "Research Manager (EU & International)",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Research and Research Support; London; £43,981-£54,019​; Vice-Provost (Research, Innovation and Global Engagement) (B09) Reference: B09-01441 About us: UCL is a world-leading research-intensive university, consistently ranked among the best universities globally. Our Research and Innovation Services (RIS) directorate plays a vital role in supporting UCL's academic community, providing specialist expertise and services across the research and innovation lifecycle, from funding applications and award management to contracts and compliance. The Award Services team works closely with researchers and professional services colleagues to support the development, submission and management of research funding applications. Our EU & International team provides specialist support for international funding opportunities, including European Commission programmes such as Horizon Europe and US federal funding, helping UCL researchers secure funding and deliver world-class research. About the role: We are seeking a Research Manager (EU & International) to join our Award Services team within Research and Innovation Services. This is a part-time position (0.6 FTE), offered on a fixed-term contract for up to 12 months. The role is based at UCL's Bloomsbury Campus, with hybrid working arrangements available. The role provides an opportunity to support UCL's internationally funded research portfolio, working closely with academic departments, researchers and professional services colleagues to facilitate successful funding applications and the effective setup of research awards. Key responsibilities include reviewing and supporting research funding proposals, providing specialist advice on research costing, funder requirements and financial compliance, and ensuring applications meet institutional and external funding requirements. Particular emphasis will be placed on European Commission funding, including Horizon Europe, and US federal funding programmes such as those administered by the National Institutes of Health (NIH). The postholder will also support the development of complex and strategic funding bids, liaise with external funders and collaborators, and work closely with colleagues across Research and Innovation Services to ensure a coordinated approach to research funding and award management. About you: We are looking for an organised, analytical and collaborative individual with a strong understanding of research and innovation funding and a commitment to delivering high-quality support to researchers and colleagues. The successful candidate will be educated to degree level or have equivalent professional experience, alongside knowledge of research funding processes and the ability to interpret detailed documentation, financial information and funder requirements. Experience of managing a varied workload, meeting tight deadlines and delivering excellent customer service is essential. Strong analytical and problem-solving skills, together with the ability to work independently, prioritise competing demands and communicate complex information clearly to a range of audiences, are equally important. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills and a collaborative approach to working with colleagues across different teams will be key to success in this role. The ability to adapt to changing requirements and contribute to improvements in processes and service delivery is also important. Experience of supporting research or innovation projects within higher education, knowledge of European Commission and US federal funding requirements, and familiarity with research management systems or costing software would be advantageous. What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (including 27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49470": {
      "title": "Senior Finance Officer",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Finance; London; £43,981-£54,019​; UCL BEAMS (B04) Reference: B04-07952 About us: The Department of Biochemical Engineering at UCL is one of the world's leading centres for bioprocessing research and education. We are seeking a talented finance professional to join our team and support world-class teaching, research and innovation activities. About the role: We are looking for a proactive and customer-focused Senior Finance Officer to provide comprehensive financial support across the department. The postholder will support budgeting, forecasting, reporting, compliance and financial operations while acting as a key point of contact for finance queries. About you: You will be a part-qualified accountant (ACCA, CIMA or ACA) with experience in finance operations, budgeting, forecasting and reporting. You will have strong analytical skills, excellent attention to detail and the ability to build effective working relationships with stakeholders. Key Responsibilities:- • Review and authorise financial transactions • Support budgeting and forecasting processes • Monitor expenditure and produce management information • Investigate and resolve finance queries • Support month-end and year-end activities • Ensure compliance with financial regulations • Provide guidance on finance procedures • Support and supervise finance administration activities What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: • 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) • Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme • Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) • Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan • Immigration loan • Relocation scheme for certain posts • On-Site nursery • On-site gym • Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay • Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service • Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/rewards-and-benefits to find out more. • If you need reasonable adjustments or a more accessible format to apply for this job online or have any queries about the application process, please contact biochem-hr@ucl.ac.uk • The UCL Ways of Working supports colleagues to be successful and happy at UCL through sharing expectations around how we work – please visit www.ucl.ac.uk/ways-of-working • As the Service Simplification HRFRI Programme develops, the role may change and the postholder will be expected to adapt to any changes in processes, systems and ways of working Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people. • Our department holds an Athena SWAN Gold award, in recognition of our commitment to advancing gender equality. You can read more about our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion here : https://www.ucl.ac.uk/equality-diversity-inclusion/"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49472": {
      "title": "Senior Technical Lead",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; IT and Digital; London; £68,284-£78,386​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05759 About us: Information Services Division (ISD) is the primary provider of IT services to UCL. We support and enhance learning, teaching, research and administrative processes by providing information- and technology-related services to over 50,000 staff and students of UCL and associated institutions. Our ambition is to be the leading IT services group in the HE sector and we are growing our team’s capability in experience/UX, agile development, security, cloud, service management and partnering. We are modernising our technology foundations, digitising the processes of the university to transform experience for students and staff, and partnering across the university to drive differentiation in UCL education and research. About the role: UCL's Data Safe Haven (DSH) is a service which provides a ISO 27001 technical certified solution for storing, handling and analysing identifiable data. The service currently resides on on-premise servers and uses Citrix technologies and Active Directory to provide a secure remote desktop to access this data. The service is due to be migrated to Azure where it will use Azure Virtual Desktop (AVD) and managed with Nerdio. File storage and supporting infrastructure will also need to be migrated to the new environment. The migration to the new system is time critical and the postholder will be dedicated to ensuring the migration is completed successfully on schedule liaising with users with varying requirements. *For full information relating to the role please revert to the formal job description attached to the role* About you: The postholder will require extensive experience across the following: Large scale IT migration projects Windows Server Entra ID Azure Technologies and AVD Enterprise storage solutions Unix platforms (Linux preferred) Any of the following technologies would be advantageious: GoAnywhere PostgreSQL/MySQL JFrog Artifact What we offer: As well as the exciting opportunities this role presents, we also offer some great benefits some of which are below: 41 Days holiday (27 days annual leave 8 bank holiday and 6 closure days) Additional 5 days’ annual leave purchase scheme Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme and season ticket loan Immigration loan Relocation scheme for certain posts On-Site nursery On-site gym Enhanced maternity, paternity and adoption pay Employee assistance programme: Staff Support Service Discounted medical insurance Visit https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/reward-and-benefits to find out more. Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation, and we want our community to represent the diversity of the world’s talent. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We therefore particularly encourage applications from candidates who are likely to be underrepresented in UCL’s workforce. These include people from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds; disabled people; LGBTQI+ people; and for our Grade 9 and 10 roles, women."
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49549": {
      "title": "Student Support and Wellbeing Services Administrator and Adviser",
      "description": "Department: Permanent; Student Well-being and Registry; London; £36,433-£42,891​; Vice-President (Operations) (B01) Reference: B01-05783 About us: Student and Registry Services is one of the larger Professional Services divisions at UCL, providing essential services to UCL’s 50,000 students, 160,000 applicants and for supporting academic departments and administrative staff in delivering a high-quality student experience. UCL Student Support and Wellbeing Services (SSWS) provides information, advice and welfare support for all registered students, including delivery of related student events. The UCL Student Support and Wellbeing Team (Disability, Mental Health & Neurodiversity Support Services) is responsible for the delivery of advice and support aimed to enable students who identify as disabled thrive and succeed at UCL. About the role: As a Student Support and Wellbeing Administrator and Adviser, you will provide essential administrative and advisory support across disability, mental health and wellbeing services. You will help manage a high volume of enquiries received by email and telephone, responding to students’ needs by providing clear, supportive information and signposting them to the appropriate teams and services where necessary. This is an excellent opportunity for someone who combines strong administrative skills with a genuine commitment to student-centred support. You will provide information and guidance on reasonable adjustments at UCL, offering constructive and empathetic advice to help students understand and implement the support available to them. This will include delivering same-day appointments to respond to students’ needs in a timely and accessible way. Through your work, you will play an important role in ensuring reasonable adjustments are effectively implemented, helping to remove barriers to teaching and learning and enabling students to engage fully with their studies and achieve their potential. This role is offered on a full-time basis with currently a minimum of 2-days per week at our Bloomsbury campus (subject to change). This role does not meet the requirements for a Skilled Worker visa certificate of sponsorship under UK Visas and Immigration legislation. UCL will not be able to sponsor individuals who require a right to work in the UK. If you have any queries about the role, please contact Kleo Kourmpi at: kleoniki.kourmpi@ucl.ac.uk Job Ref: B01-05783 Closing Date: 09-11-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) About you: You will have experience of working in an administrative role and an understanding of how to identify and remove barriers for disabled people. You will have knowledge of the Equality Act 2010 and an appreciation of its relevance within a public sector environment, alongside an understanding of confidentiality and GDPR, particularly when handling sensitive student information. Further training will be provided to support you in the role. You will take a practical, solution-focused approach to supporting individuals, identifying appropriate adjustments and exploring ways to improve accessibility, whether through removing physical barriers or recommending assistive technology. You will be a confident problem-solver, able to balance an understanding of the wider barriers affecting disabled students with the provision of tailored, individual support. An interest in, or understanding of, the complexities of the medical and social models of disability would be an advantage. Current or previous membership of the National Association of Disability Practitioners (NADP) is desirable but not essential. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women. Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    },
    "https://www.ucl.ac.uk/work-at-ucl/search-ucl-jobs/details?jobId=49551": {
      "title": "Work Related Learning Officer - (SLASH/IOE) - Maternity Cover",
      "description": "Department: Fixed-term; Student Well-being and Registry; London; £36,433-£42,891​; Vice-Provost (Education and Student Experience) (B06) Reference: B06-00221 About us: UCL Careers supports students, graduates and staff to explore their career options, develop their skills and prepare for their futures. As part of UCL’s Higher Education Development & Support Institute (HEDS), we work collaboratively with academic departments, employers and alumni to enhance the student experience and strengthen graduate outcomes. Our SLASH/IOE team supports four faculties: Arts & Humanities, Social & Historical Sciences, Laws and the UCL Institute of Education. Together, these faculties are home to around 18,000 students across a diverse range of disciplines. We work closely with colleagues across UCL to ensure students can access meaningful opportunities to explore different industries, develop workplace skills and prepare for life beyond university. About the role: We are seeking a proactive and highly organised Work Related Learning Officer to join our SLASH/IOE team on a maternity cover basis from January to December 2027. Working closely with the Work Related Learning Manager, you will help develop and deliver an engaging programme of activities that connects students with employers, alumni and the world of work. This varied role will see you coordinate a diverse programme of experiential learning opportunities, including careers and skills workshops, employer-set projects, hackathons, networking events and employer visits. You will build and maintain relationships with academic colleagues, employers, alumni and professional bodies, while proactively identifying new opportunities to enrich our provision and respond to changing labour market trends. You will play a key role in planning and administering activities, coordinating delivery partners, monitoring student engagement and gathering feedback to help evaluate and continuously improve the programme. You will also create engaging promotional content to drive participation across the faculties. Working collaboratively with Careers Consultants and colleagues across UCL Careers, you will help ensure activities complement existing careers provision and respond to the diverse needs and backgrounds of our students. You will report on the outcomes of your work and support the Work Related Learning Manager in the evaluation of the programme. This is an exciting opportunity to make a tangible contribution to the student experience, helping UCL students develop the knowledge, confidence and practical skills to shape their future careers. Please note, this is a fixed-term maternity cover role available from January - December 2027. The role offers hybrid working with a minimum of 2-days per week onsite at our Bloomsbury office (subject to change). Job Ref: B06-00221 Closing Date: 09-11-2026 – 23:59 (UK time) About you: You will have experience of coordinating projects and delivering programmes of events, training or work-related learning activities, with the organisational skills to manage competing priorities, meet deadlines and maintain high standards of delivery. You will be confident building relationships with a wide range of stakeholders, including external partners, and able to communicate effectively with different audiences both in person and online. You will bring excellent interpersonal and influencing skills, enabling you to engage employers, alumni and academic colleagues, develop productive partnerships and encourage participation in new initiatives. A proactive, resilient and solutions-focused approach will be essential, alongside the ability to work independently, respond flexibly to changing demands and deliver an excellent service to students and colleagues. You will also have the ability to undertake qualitative and quantitative research to evaluate activities, alongside good IT skills, including Microsoft Office and experience of using event or database management systems. Knowledge of the graduate labour market, experience within higher education careers or graduate recruitment, and familiarity with creating marketing materials, newsletters or website content would be advantageous. Above all, you will be motivated by helping students explore their career options and prepare for the world of work, with a commitment to inclusive practice, continuous improvement and UCL’s Ways of Working. What we offer: Standard UCL Benefits: 41 days’ holiday (27 days annual leave, 8 bank holidays, 6 closure days) Option to purchase 5 additional days’ annual leave Defined benefit career average revalued earnings pension scheme (CARE) Cycle to work scheme, season ticket loan, and immigration loan Relocation support for eligible posts On-site nursery and gym Enhanced maternity, paternity, and adoption pay Staff Support Service (employee assistance programme) Discounted medical insurance Full details: UCL Rewards and Benefits . Our commitment to Equality, Diversity and Inclusion: “As London’s Global University, we know diversity fosters creativity and innovation. We are committed to equality of opportunity, to being fair and inclusive, and to being a place where we all belong. We particularly encourage applications from candidates underrepresented in UCL’s workforce, including those from Black, Asian and ethnic minority backgrounds, disabled people, and – for Grade 9 and 10 roles – women.” Read more: UCL Equality, Diversity and Inclusion . Applicants should also familiarise themselves with UCL’s policies on: Prevention of Bullying, Harassment and Sexual Misconduct Policy and Personal Relationships Policy"
    }
  },
